const LAB_HELP_LIBRARY = [{"id":"start","title":"Start with the Lab","summary":"Your first mod, the main tabs and where to go next.","group":"Start here","tabs":["mods","scene"],"html":"<p>The Lab lets you make mods, build a test zoo, play the engine and investigate the original game. You can create a mod without writing engine code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-before-you-begin\">Before you begin</h2>\n<p>Use your own installed copy of Zoo Tycoon. Wait for <strong>Engine running</strong> before opening species lists or placing things. <strong>Ghidra connected</strong> is needed for original-game code investigation, but you can read Help while it connects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-make-your-first-mod\">Make your first mod</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>Mods</strong> and choose <strong>New mod</strong>. Give it a name and start with an empty studio.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Make something</strong>. Start with <strong>New animal</strong>, <strong>Build an item</strong> or a map.</li>\n<li>For an animal, choose Zoo, Marine or Dinosaur first, then a suitable <strong>Behaves like</strong> species. This provides its starting behavior and art slots.</li>\n<li>Open a drawing. <strong>Remake</strong> copies existing art, <strong>Stencil</strong> gives you a faded guide to paint over, and <strong>Blank</strong> starts empty. <strong>Import external art</strong> accepts PNG images and OBJ meshes.</li>\n<li><strong>Save</strong> the drawing. Set the animal&#x27;s <strong>Needs</strong>, <strong>Pictures</strong> and Inspector values, or the item&#x27;s relevant properties. Wait for saves to finish.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Test</strong> to load the mod in the Test Zoo. Build its home yourself, add the appropriate staff, then run time and observe it.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>Play Game</strong> and test ordinary purchasing, placement and gameplay. A picture in the editor alone does not prove it works in the game.</li>\n<li>Set an <strong>About</strong> picture. <strong>Publish → Check it</strong> checks the local package. <strong>Upload it</strong> publishes only after the server&#x27;s checks pass.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"animal-imports\">Follow the three animal import lessons</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"park-tutorial\">make objects</a>, or <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"habitat-tutorial\">build a habitat and theme pack</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-find-your-way-around\">Find your way around</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tab</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explore</td>\n<td>Find a system, action or game value in plain words.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Original Game</td>\n<td>Read and investigate your original zoo.exe.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Write Code</td>\n<td>Edit and build the engine when you need to change its implementation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test Zoo</td>\n<td>Arrange a controlled test, inspect animals and objects, and watch behavior.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Play Game</td>\n<td>Try the ordinary player workflow in the engine or original game.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mods</td>\n<td>Create, edit, check and publish mods; inspect game files and Lab scripts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Help</td>\n<td>Search these guides and watch the recorded tutorials. Press F1 to return here.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-read-try-then-come-back\">Read, try, then come back</h2>\n<p>Use the <strong>Open…</strong> buttons above a guide to go to the relevant Lab tab. Help remembers your article, search and reading position while you switch tabs. The contents list jumps to a section. Search looks through article text as well as titles.</p>\n<p>Guides and tutorial media are included in the app. The older overview is marked <strong>Earlier interface</strong>; the current studio lessons show the new Mods menu. Historical test screenshots may show older version numbers. Follow the current publishing and repository-picture guides for today&#x27;s workflow.</p>","toc":[{"id":"before-you-begin","title":"Before you begin","level":2},{"id":"make-your-first-mod","title":"Make your first mod","level":2},{"id":"find-your-way-around","title":"Find your way around","level":2},{"id":"read-try-then-come-back","title":"Read, try, then come back","level":2}],"text":"# Start with the Lab\n\nThe Lab lets you make mods, build a test zoo, play the engine and investigate the original game. You can create a mod without writing engine code.\n\n## Before you begin\n\nUse your own installed copy of Zoo Tycoon. Wait for **Engine running** before opening species lists or placing things. **Ghidra connected** is needed for original-game code investigation, but you can read Help while it connects.\n\n## Make your first mod\n\n1. Open **Mods** and choose **New mod**. Give it a name and start with an empty studio.\n2. Choose **Make something**. Start with **New animal**, **Build an item** or a map.\n3. For an animal, choose Zoo, Marine or Dinosaur first, then a suitable **Behaves like** species. This provides its starting behavior and art slots.\n4. Open a drawing. **Remake** copies existing art, **Stencil** gives you a faded guide to paint over, and **Blank** starts empty. **Import external art** accepts PNG images and OBJ meshes.\n5. **Save** the drawing. Set the animal's **Needs**, **Pictures** and Inspector values, or the item's relevant properties. Wait for saves to finish.\n6. Use **Test** to load the mod in the Test Zoo. Build its home yourself, add the appropriate staff, then run time and observe it.\n7. Open **Play Game** and test ordinary purchasing, placement and gameplay. A picture in the editor alone does not prove it works in the game.\n8. Set an **About** picture. **Publish → Check it** checks the local package. **Upload it** publishes only after the server's checks pass.\n\n[Follow the three animal import lessons](help:animal-imports), [make objects](help:park-tutorial), or [build a habitat and theme pack](help:habitat-tutorial).\n\n## Find your way around\n\n| Tab | Use it for |\n|---|---|\n| Explore | Find a system, action or game value in plain words. |\n| Original Game | Read and investigate your original zoo.exe. |\n| Write Code | Edit and build the engine when you need to change its implementation. |\n| Test Zoo | Arrange a controlled test, inspect animals and objects, and watch behavior. |\n| Play Game | Try the ordinary player workflow in the engine or original game. |\n| Mods | Create, edit, check and publish mods; inspect game files and Lab scripts. |\n| Help | Search these guides and watch the recorded tutorials. Press F1 to return here. |\n\n## Read, try, then come back\n\nUse the **Open…** buttons above a guide to go to the relevant Lab tab. Help remembers your article, search and reading position while you switch tabs. The contents list jumps to a section. Search looks through article text as well as titles.\n\nGuides and tutorial media are included in the app. The older overview is marked **Earlier interface**; the current studio lessons show the new Mods menu. Historical test screenshots may show older version numbers. Follow the current publishing and repository-picture guides for today's workflow.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"workspace","title":"Find your way around the Lab","summary":"Explore, Original Game, Write Code, Test Zoo, Play and panel layouts.","group":"Start here","tabs":["explore","scene"],"html":"<h2 id=\"help-section-explore-find-what-you-need\">Explore: find what you need</h2>\n<p>Try a plain question such as “selling animals”, “tank filter” or “where is the zoo saved”. Results connect functions, recorded actions, game configuration and engine source. Open a result&#x27;s code or its game files to investigate further. Rebuild the map after adding recordings or naming functions if Explore says its map is stale.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-original-game-understand-a-behavior\">Original Game: understand a behavior</h2>\n<p>Find a function on the left, read its decompiled code in the middle and use the details on the right to name it or record what you learned. Search names, game text, an address, or a field such as <code>field:0x3d6</code>. Ghidra works from your own executable. Decompiled code and guesses still need checking against observed behavior.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-write-code-change-the-engine\">Write Code: change the engine</h2>\n<p>Open the engine file, make the change and choose <strong>Build engine</strong>. Read the Build output and Problems list. A successful build restarts the Test Zoo on the new engine. Keep a reproducible test setup before rebuilding. A normal art or needs change belongs in Mods and usually does not require an engine build.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-test-zoo-make-a-controlled-experiment\">Test Zoo: make a controlled experiment</h2>\n<p>Pause, select a map and place a small set of animals or objects. Use the Inspector to inspect the selected thing, then run or advance time. Compare a known working stock item with your custom item in similar conditions. Use the outline to find an animal, enclosure or building again.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-play-game-check-what-a-player-experiences\">Play Game: check what a player experiences</h2>\n<p><strong>Play ZT1 Engine</strong> runs the current engine with your normal mod switches. <strong>Play original</strong> runs the installed original game for comparison. Mods can be switched on and off in the engine&#x27;s Settings without manually restarting Play. Optional replacements return to the original assets when switched off. A pack required by the current map, another enabled mod, or unique content placed in the zoo stays on and explains why. Leave that map or remove the dependent content before switching its pack off. Embedded Play is a separate game session from the Test Zoo: placing an animal in one does not place it in the other.</p>\n<p>The Mods studio&#x27;s <strong>Test</strong> and <strong>Play</strong> shortcuts start temporary mod tests. A label identifies the test; your normal switches are unchanged. <strong>End test</strong> returns the Test Zoo to your normal selection. Stopping a Play test ends its temporary selection; the ordinary Play button then uses your normal switches. The installed-mod list keeps downloaded mods visible whether they are on or off. Use the left and right arrow buttons in Settings to reach every installed mod; the counter shows your current page and the total number of pages.</p>\n<p>Downloading installs a mod switched off. <strong>Enable</strong> in the main-menu Mod Menu uses the same smart switch as Settings. After <strong>Load mods</strong> was off, enabling one mod activates that mod and its requirements; old selections are not silently brought back. <strong>Load mods → Off</strong> switches the selection off together, unless the current map or zoo requires one of its packs. Temporary tests have their own Load mods state and load only the selected mod and its requirements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-mods-use-the-right-workspace\">Mods: use the right workspace</h2>\n<p>The studio lists a mod&#x27;s animals, items, maps and files. Choose an entry to edit it and use the Inspector for its properties. <strong>About</strong> holds the name, description, version and cover. <strong>Publish</strong> holds checks, upload and theme-pack creation. The Mods workspace also provides <strong>Game files</strong> and <strong>Lab scripts</strong>; those are different from a playable mod&#x27;s drawings and definitions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-restore-a-hidden-panel\">Restore a hidden panel</h2>\n<p>In Original Game, Write Code and Test Zoo, use <strong>Layout</strong> to bring back a closed panel or reset the tab&#x27;s layout. Drag panel tabs onto a side to dock, into the middle to stack, or out to float. Drag edges to resize. A saved layout is remembered between sessions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-sharing-findings\">Sharing findings</h2>\n<p>The header&#x27;s <strong>Sharing</strong> control manages knowledge-base contributions. Review its dialog before turning it on. Findings about the executable are separate from publishing a mod. Your private recordings and zoos are not the mod repository.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"lab-reference\">Read the complete Lab reference</a> for the detailed inspectors, traces, formulas and recording tools.</p>","toc":[{"id":"explore-find-what-you-need","title":"Explore: find what you need","level":2},{"id":"original-game-understand-a-behavior","title":"Original Game: understand a behavior","level":2},{"id":"write-code-change-the-engine","title":"Write Code: change the engine","level":2},{"id":"test-zoo-make-a-controlled-experiment","title":"Test Zoo: make a controlled experiment","level":2},{"id":"play-game-check-what-a-player-experiences","title":"Play Game: check what a player experiences","level":2},{"id":"mods-use-the-right-workspace","title":"Mods: use the right workspace","level":2},{"id":"restore-a-hidden-panel","title":"Restore a hidden panel","level":2},{"id":"sharing-findings","title":"Sharing findings","level":2}],"text":"# Find your way around the Lab\n\n## Explore: find what you need\n\nTry a plain question such as “selling animals”, “tank filter” or “where is the zoo saved”. Results connect functions, recorded actions, game configuration and engine source. Open a result's code or its game files to investigate further. Rebuild the map after adding recordings or naming functions if Explore says its map is stale.\n\n## Original Game: understand a behavior\n\nFind a function on the left, read its decompiled code in the middle and use the details on the right to name it or record what you learned. Search names, game text, an address, or a field such as `field:0x3d6`. Ghidra works from your own executable. Decompiled code and guesses still need checking against observed behavior.\n\n## Write Code: change the engine\n\nOpen the engine file, make the change and choose **Build engine**. Read the Build output and Problems list. A successful build restarts the Test Zoo on the new engine. Keep a reproducible test setup before rebuilding. A normal art or needs change belongs in Mods and usually does not require an engine build.\n\n## Test Zoo: make a controlled experiment\n\nPause, select a map and place a small set of animals or objects. Use the Inspector to inspect the selected thing, then run or advance time. Compare a known working stock item with your custom item in similar conditions. Use the outline to find an animal, enclosure or building again.\n\n## Play Game: check what a player experiences\n\n**Play ZT1 Engine** runs the current engine with your normal mod switches. **Play original** runs the installed original game for comparison. Mods can be switched on and off in the engine's Settings without manually restarting Play. Optional replacements return to the original assets when switched off. A pack required by the current map, another enabled mod, or unique content placed in the zoo stays on and explains why. Leave that map or remove the dependent content before switching its pack off. Embedded Play is a separate game session from the Test Zoo: placing an animal in one does not place it in the other.\n\nThe Mods studio's **Test** and **Play** shortcuts start temporary mod tests. A label identifies the test; your normal switches are unchanged. **End test** returns the Test Zoo to your normal selection. Stopping a Play test ends its temporary selection; the ordinary Play button then uses your normal switches. The installed-mod list keeps downloaded mods visible whether they are on or off. Use the left and right arrow buttons in Settings to reach every installed mod; the counter shows your current page and the total number of pages.\n\nDownloading installs a mod switched off. **Enable** in the main-menu Mod Menu uses the same smart switch as Settings. After **Load mods** was off, enabling one mod activates that mod and its requirements; old selections are not silently brought back. **Load mods → Off** switches the selection off together, unless the current map or zoo requires one of its packs. Temporary tests have their own Load mods state and load only the selected mod and its requirements.\n\n## Mods: use the right workspace\n\nThe studio lists a mod's animals, items, maps and files. Choose an entry to edit it and use the Inspector for its properties. **About** holds the name, description, version and cover. **Publish** holds checks, upload and theme-pack creation. The Mods workspace also provides **Game files** and **Lab scripts**; those are different from a playable mod's drawings and definitions.\n\n## Restore a hidden panel\n\nIn Original Game, Write Code and Test Zoo, use **Layout** to bring back a closed panel or reset the tab's layout. Drag panel tabs onto a side to dock, into the middle to stack, or out to float. Drag edges to resize. A saved layout is remembered between sessions.\n\n## Sharing findings\n\nThe header's **Sharing** control manages knowledge-base contributions. Review its dialog before turning it on. Findings about the executable are separate from publishing a mod. Your private recordings and zoos are not the mod repository.\n\n[Read the complete Lab reference](help:lab-reference) for the detailed inspectors, traces, formulas and recording tools.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"art-and-animals","title":"Draw and import an animal","summary":"PNG, directional sheets, OBJ poses, needs, pictures and eggs.","group":"Making mods","tabs":["mods"],"keywords":"dino dinosaur marine sprites animation","html":"<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-a-suitable-base\">Choose a suitable base</h2>\n<p>Use <strong>Mods → Make something → New animal</strong>. Choose Zoo, Marine or Dinosaur, name it and choose <strong>Behaves like</strong> from that kind. The base supplies behavior, sounds and working slots until you replace them. Changing the kind label alone does not create a swimming behavior set or a dinosaur&#x27;s egg states.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-start-a-drawing\">Start a drawing</h2>\n<p>Open a real animation row and choose <strong>Draw</strong>. <strong>Remake</strong> copies the existing frames. <strong>Stencil</strong> overlays the source outline with adjustable inside opacity. <strong>Blank</strong> starts empty. The direction ring selects a view; the timeline selects a frame. Use <strong>Own copy…</strong> before changing art that is linked to another slot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-import-a-png-or-sprite-sheet\">Import a PNG or sprite sheet</h2>\n<p>Choose <strong>Import external art</strong> and select the PNG with the normal file picker. Choose one picture, a horizontal frame strip, or a directional grid. Set the cell count, directions, frame timing and visible height. Check the ground anchor and preview every supplied frame and view before <strong>Save</strong>.</p>\n<p>A repeated picture shows the same viewpoint in every direction. It does not create unseen angles. Keep transparent margins and the shadow separate from the visible animal where possible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-import-obj-art\">Import OBJ art</h2>\n<p>Select the OBJ together with its MTL and any texture images. Set the fitted height and inspect the rendered directions. For a pose sequence, supply multiple exported OBJ poses in order. Export Y-up meshes. The importer renders supplied models and poses; it does not rig or animate a single static mesh.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-finish-the-slots-and-pictures\">Finish the slots and pictures</h2>\n<p>Give walking, eating, resting and other actions appropriate art. <strong>Link other poses</strong> can reuse frames for a prototype, but those links are not separate animations. Check young and adult variants, shadows and water layers too. In <strong>Pictures → Use an animation frame…</strong>, make the buy icon, list image and plaque, then adjust them if needed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-set-specifics-and-needs\">Set specifics and needs</h2>\n<p>Use <strong>Needs</strong> for food, carer, biome, terrain, liked objects and neighbors. Use the Inspector for price, group size, space and other values. An enabled mod&#x27;s custom food can be selected in Needs. The ordinary carer must be able to reach and supply it.</p>\n<p>For marine animals, check surface and submerged movement, minimum adoption depth, preferred depth, salinity and water quality. For dinosaurs, prepare <strong>egg_idle</strong>, <strong>egg_hatch</strong> and <strong>egg_break</strong>, hatch timing and an enclosure appropriate to its behavior.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-prove-it-in-the-game\">Prove it in the game</h2>\n<p>Build a suitable home in Test Zoo, run time and observe feeding and movement. Then adopt the animal through Play&#x27;s ordinary creature panel: choose the icon, choose male or female, and place it. Test rejected placements as well as valid ones. Use the <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"testing\">test checklist</a> before calling a species finished.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"png-lesson\">PNG lesson</a> · <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"sheet-lesson\">Directional-sheet lesson</a> · <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"obj-lesson\">OBJ-pose lesson</a> · <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"aquatic-tutorial\">Swimming and marine tutorial</a></p>","toc":[{"id":"choose-a-suitable-base","title":"Choose a suitable base","level":2},{"id":"start-a-drawing","title":"Start a drawing","level":2},{"id":"import-a-png-or-sprite-sheet","title":"Import a PNG or sprite sheet","level":2},{"id":"import-obj-art","title":"Import OBJ art","level":2},{"id":"finish-the-slots-and-pictures","title":"Finish the slots and pictures","level":2},{"id":"set-specifics-and-needs","title":"Set specifics and needs","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-in-the-game","title":"Prove it in the game","level":2}],"text":"# Draw and import an animal\n\n## Choose a suitable base\n\nUse **Mods → Make something → New animal**. Choose Zoo, Marine or Dinosaur, name it and choose **Behaves like** from that kind. The base supplies behavior, sounds and working slots until you replace them. Changing the kind label alone does not create a swimming behavior set or a dinosaur's egg states.\n\n## Start a drawing\n\nOpen a real animation row and choose **Draw**. **Remake** copies the existing frames. **Stencil** overlays the source outline with adjustable inside opacity. **Blank** starts empty. The direction ring selects a view; the timeline selects a frame. Use **Own copy…** before changing art that is linked to another slot.\n\n## Import a PNG or sprite sheet\n\nChoose **Import external art** and select the PNG with the normal file picker. Choose one picture, a horizontal frame strip, or a directional grid. Set the cell count, directions, frame timing and visible height. Check the ground anchor and preview every supplied frame and view before **Save**.\n\nA repeated picture shows the same viewpoint in every direction. It does not create unseen angles. Keep transparent margins and the shadow separate from the visible animal where possible.\n\n## Import OBJ art\n\nSelect the OBJ together with its MTL and any texture images. Set the fitted height and inspect the rendered directions. For a pose sequence, supply multiple exported OBJ poses in order. Export Y-up meshes. The importer renders supplied models and poses; it does not rig or animate a single static mesh.\n\n## Finish the slots and pictures\n\nGive walking, eating, resting and other actions appropriate art. **Link other poses** can reuse frames for a prototype, but those links are not separate animations. Check young and adult variants, shadows and water layers too. In **Pictures → Use an animation frame…**, make the buy icon, list image and plaque, then adjust them if needed.\n\n## Set specifics and needs\n\nUse **Needs** for food, carer, biome, terrain, liked objects and neighbors. Use the Inspector for price, group size, space and other values. An enabled mod's custom food can be selected in Needs. The ordinary carer must be able to reach and supply it.\n\nFor marine animals, check surface and submerged movement, minimum adoption depth, preferred depth, salinity and water quality. For dinosaurs, prepare **egg_idle**, **egg_hatch** and **egg_break**, hatch timing and an enclosure appropriate to its behavior.\n\n## Prove it in the game\n\nBuild a suitable home in Test Zoo, run time and observe feeding and movement. Then adopt the animal through Play's ordinary creature panel: choose the icon, choose male or female, and place it. Test rejected placements as well as valid ones. Use the [test checklist](help:testing) before calling a species finished.\n\n[PNG lesson](help:png-lesson) · [Directional-sheet lesson](help:sheet-lesson) · [OBJ-pose lesson](help:obj-lesson) · [Swimming and marine tutorial](help:aquatic-tutorial)\n","videos":[]},{"id":"test-zoo-and-maps","title":"Build a test zoo and a custom map","summary":"Boundaries, entrances, land exhibits, marine tanks and saved maps.","group":"Making mods","tabs":["scene"],"keywords":"ground path shelter filter fauna","html":"<h2 id=\"help-section-start-small-and-pause\">Start small and pause</h2>\n<p>In <strong>Test Zoo</strong>, choose an existing freeform map, a flattened map, or <strong>New map</strong> with a blank size. Pause while building. A blank map begins in <strong>Edit the map</strong>, allowing you to arrange the boundary and entrance. Finish and save custom asset edits before laying out a map that uses them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-draw-the-zoo-boundary\">Draw the zoo boundary</h2>\n<p>Use the Zoo Fence for a closed boundary and place a zoo entrance where guests should enter. Connect its entrance to a path. Switch <strong>Edit the map</strong> off to check which ground counts as inside the zoo. The Terrain view and exhibit overlay help spot gaps. A decorative gate is not automatically the zoo entrance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-build-a-land-exhibit\">Build a land exhibit</h2>\n<p>Close the enclosure, add a gate and connect its outside to the staff path. Paint suitable ground and add water, plants, rocks, shelter or enrichment according to the animal&#x27;s Needs. Put the right carer outside with access to the gate. A low fence that contains one species may not contain a jumping dinosaur.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-build-a-marine-tank\">Build a marine tank</h2>\n<p>Make a complete tank-wall enclosure. Check the actual water depth, not just wall height. Add a reachable filter outside, appropriate water conditions, underwater objects and a marine specialist. Check allowed tank placement and rejected land or shallow-water placement. Observe natural surface and submerged swimming rather than forcing an animation preview.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-test-a-service-object\">Test a service object</h2>\n<p>Connect buildings and furniture to accessible paths and check their orientation. Hire the relevant staff. Run normal visitors and service cycles: a kiosk should serve a guest; a bench should be usable; a bin should accumulate and be emptied; a filter should clean, wear and be serviced. A placement preview proves only that the art loads.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-save-and-reopen\">Save and reopen</h2>\n<p>Choose <strong>Save as a map</strong>, select the destination mod and give the map a name and description. The map stores its ground, paths, fences, exhibits, objects and starting view. Live animals, guests and staff are created again during Play; they are not promised as saved map inhabitants.</p>\n<p>Reopen the saved map before publishing it. Check the boundary, entrance, objects and custom resources. Enable its mod and the game&#x27;s <strong>custommaps</strong> switch, then find it in Play&#x27;s <strong>Freeform</strong> list and adopt or hire the live actors again.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-useful-controls\">Useful controls</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In Test Zoo</th>\n<th>Control</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Place palette</td>\n<td>P</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bulldoze</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo</td>\n<td>Ctrl+Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turn the selected placement</td>\n<td>R</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change fence/path drag mode</td>\n<td>Tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop the current placement tool</td>\n<td>Right-click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Route test</td>\n<td>T</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Use these while the zoo view has focus. In a text field, keys edit text instead.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"habitat-tutorial\">Habitat, tanks, food, eggs and map tutorial</a> · <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"testing\">Testing checklist</a></p>","toc":[{"id":"start-small-and-pause","title":"Start small and pause","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-zoo-boundary","title":"Draw the zoo boundary","level":2},{"id":"build-a-land-exhibit","title":"Build a land exhibit","level":2},{"id":"build-a-marine-tank","title":"Build a marine tank","level":2},{"id":"test-a-service-object","title":"Test a service object","level":2},{"id":"save-and-reopen","title":"Save and reopen","level":2},{"id":"useful-controls","title":"Useful controls","level":2}],"text":"# Build a test zoo and a custom map\n\n## Start small and pause\n\nIn **Test Zoo**, choose an existing freeform map, a flattened map, or **New map** with a blank size. Pause while building. A blank map begins in **Edit the map**, allowing you to arrange the boundary and entrance. Finish and save custom asset edits before laying out a map that uses them.\n\n## Draw the zoo boundary\n\nUse the Zoo Fence for a closed boundary and place a zoo entrance where guests should enter. Connect its entrance to a path. Switch **Edit the map** off to check which ground counts as inside the zoo. The Terrain view and exhibit overlay help spot gaps. A decorative gate is not automatically the zoo entrance.\n\n## Build a land exhibit\n\nClose the enclosure, add a gate and connect its outside to the staff path. Paint suitable ground and add water, plants, rocks, shelter or enrichment according to the animal's Needs. Put the right carer outside with access to the gate. A low fence that contains one species may not contain a jumping dinosaur.\n\n## Build a marine tank\n\nMake a complete tank-wall enclosure. Check the actual water depth, not just wall height. Add a reachable filter outside, appropriate water conditions, underwater objects and a marine specialist. Check allowed tank placement and rejected land or shallow-water placement. Observe natural surface and submerged swimming rather than forcing an animation preview.\n\n## Test a service object\n\nConnect buildings and furniture to accessible paths and check their orientation. Hire the relevant staff. Run normal visitors and service cycles: a kiosk should serve a guest; a bench should be usable; a bin should accumulate and be emptied; a filter should clean, wear and be serviced. A placement preview proves only that the art loads.\n\n## Save and reopen\n\nChoose **Save as a map**, select the destination mod and give the map a name and description. The map stores its ground, paths, fences, exhibits, objects and starting view. Live animals, guests and staff are created again during Play; they are not promised as saved map inhabitants.\n\nReopen the saved map before publishing it. Check the boundary, entrance, objects and custom resources. Enable its mod and the game's **custommaps** switch, then find it in Play's **Freeform** list and adopt or hire the live actors again.\n\n## Useful controls\n\n| In Test Zoo | Control |\n|---|---|\n| Place palette | P |\n| Bulldoze | B |\n| Undo | Ctrl+Z |\n| Turn the selected placement | R |\n| Change fence/path drag mode | Tab |\n| Stop the current placement tool | Right-click |\n| Route test | T |\n\nUse these while the zoo view has focus. In a text field, keys edit text instead.\n\n[Habitat, tanks, food, eggs and map tutorial](help:habitat-tutorial) · [Testing checklist](help:testing)\n","videos":[]},{"id":"testing","title":"Check that a mod works","summary":"Art, care, staff, service states, ordinary Play and evidence.","group":"Making mods","tabs":["scene","play"],"html":"<p>Use several small tests. <strong>Problems</strong>, a local package check, repository approval and ordinary gameplay each answer different questions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-the-files-and-art\">Check the files and art</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Save edits and wait for compilation. Resolve the studio&#x27;s Problems links.</li>\n<li>Preview every direction and relevant frame. Look at shadows, anchors and layers.</li>\n<li>Check idle, used, empty, full, worn, broken and off states where the item has them.</li>\n<li>Run the species check for missing animation paths, layer counts and unsupported behavior steps. Compare warnings with the stock base to distinguish inherited gaps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-an-animals-home\">Check an animal&#x27;s home</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Give it suitable terrain, water, space, group size and preferred objects.</li>\n<li>Confirm the correct staff can reach it and supply its food.</li>\n<li>Observe normal eating, resting and movement over time, not one good screenshot.</li>\n<li>For swimming zoo animals, observe both land-to-water and water-to-land movement.</li>\n<li>For marine animals, observe surface and submerged movement, depth and water conditions; reject land and shallow placement where appropriate.</li>\n<li>For dinosaurs, observe egg placement, hatch and shell states, feeding and containment after hatching.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-objects-with-real-users\">Check objects with real users</h2>\n<p>Let guests buy from a building or use furniture, and let staff perform their inherited work. Check footprint, orientation, path access, price and service effects. For filters, compare water purity over a timed interval and test off, worn, broken and maintenance states. For fences and tank walls, check corners, heights, slopes and gates rather than only a straight section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-ordinary-play\">Check ordinary Play</h2>\n<p>Find the mod in its expected buy category. Confirm its name, image, price and allowed placement. Start from a newly loaded session after changing mods. Reopen a saved map and test again. If the mod replaces an existing item, compare with the mod off to confirm the replacement is scoped correctly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-saved-zoos-in-both-games\">Check saved zoos in both games</h2>\n<p>In ordinary Play, <strong>Save a zoo...</strong> defaults to <strong>Shared zoo (.zoo)</strong>. This\nstores a compatible original-game world and the engine&#x27;s own snapshot in one\nfile. <strong>Engine-only save (.zt1save)</strong> remains available. Enter a new filename\nwithout an extension to use the selected format&#x27;s extension.</p>\n<p>Open the shared file in the engine and check custom animals, objects, shows and\npaths with the required mods enabled. Then open a separate copy in the original.\nUnique engine mod items stay in the engine snapshot; the original sees the\ncompatible stock world. Custom paths and boundaries may use stock equivalents,\nand elevated engine paths are absent there. The receiving original needs the\nappropriate installed expansions and assets.</p>\n<p><strong>Saving again in the original discards the engine snapshot.</strong> Keep the engine\ncopy if you want to retain its custom features. The original resave can still be\nimported by the engine, with the state that the original understands.</p>\n<p>Current native saves use version 14; older released engines cannot read them.\nKeep pre-update backups. Original thoughts about named units and habitats are\nmapped, and view conversion covers the original&#x27;s rotations and zooms. Exact\ntask/visit-history continuation and scenario parity still have limits; a\nsuccessful load is not proof that every in-progress action resumed unchanged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-repository-sharing-separately\">Check repository sharing separately</h2>\n<p><strong>Publish → Check it</strong> validates the local package. <strong>Upload it</strong> sends it for server rules and malware checks. Confirm the listing is live, inspect its cover in the game&#x27;s repository before download, then download into a clean test setup and enable it. A theme pack should work with its separate components off.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-keep-evidence-and-limits\">Keep evidence and limits</h2>\n<p>Record what you changed, the steps, elapsed game time, expected result and observed result. Retain failed attempts alongside the correction. A prototype that shares poses or inherits work states should say so. The tutorial examples demonstrate workflows, not complete animation or long-term balance certification.</p>\n<p>The current Amberhorn example escaped an earlier low-glass enclosure after hatching; containment still needs a dedicated test. Marine show and animation diagnostics can also expose inherited engine gaps. Use those findings to guide testing rather than treating repository approval as proof of finished gameplay.</p>","toc":[{"id":"check-the-files-and-art","title":"Check the files and art","level":2},{"id":"check-an-animals-home","title":"Check an animal's home","level":2},{"id":"check-objects-with-real-users","title":"Check objects with real users","level":2},{"id":"check-ordinary-play","title":"Check ordinary Play","level":2},{"id":"check-saved-zoos-in-both-games","title":"Check saved zoos in both games","level":2},{"id":"check-repository-sharing-separately","title":"Check repository sharing separately","level":2},{"id":"keep-evidence-and-limits","title":"Keep evidence and limits","level":2}],"text":"# Check that a mod works\n\nUse several small tests. **Problems**, a local package check, repository approval and ordinary gameplay each answer different questions.\n\n## Check the files and art\n\n- Save edits and wait for compilation. Resolve the studio's Problems links.\n- Preview every direction and relevant frame. Look at shadows, anchors and layers.\n- Check idle, used, empty, full, worn, broken and off states where the item has them.\n- Run the species check for missing animation paths, layer counts and unsupported behavior steps. Compare warnings with the stock base to distinguish inherited gaps.\n\n## Check an animal's home\n\n- Give it suitable terrain, water, space, group size and preferred objects.\n- Confirm the correct staff can reach it and supply its food.\n- Observe normal eating, resting and movement over time, not one good screenshot.\n- For swimming zoo animals, observe both land-to-water and water-to-land movement.\n- For marine animals, observe surface and submerged movement, depth and water conditions; reject land and shallow placement where appropriate.\n- For dinosaurs, observe egg placement, hatch and shell states, feeding and containment after hatching.\n\n## Check objects with real users\n\nLet guests buy from a building or use furniture, and let staff perform their inherited work. Check footprint, orientation, path access, price and service effects. For filters, compare water purity over a timed interval and test off, worn, broken and maintenance states. For fences and tank walls, check corners, heights, slopes and gates rather than only a straight section.\n\n## Check ordinary Play\n\nFind the mod in its expected buy category. Confirm its name, image, price and allowed placement. Start from a newly loaded session after changing mods. Reopen a saved map and test again. If the mod replaces an existing item, compare with the mod off to confirm the replacement is scoped correctly.\n\n## Check saved zoos in both games\n\nIn ordinary Play, **Save a zoo...** defaults to **Shared zoo (.zoo)**. This\nstores a compatible original-game world and the engine's own snapshot in one\nfile. **Engine-only save (.zt1save)** remains available. Enter a new filename\nwithout an extension to use the selected format's extension.\n\nOpen the shared file in the engine and check custom animals, objects, shows and\npaths with the required mods enabled. Then open a separate copy in the original.\nUnique engine mod items stay in the engine snapshot; the original sees the\ncompatible stock world. Custom paths and boundaries may use stock equivalents,\nand elevated engine paths are absent there. The receiving original needs the\nappropriate installed expansions and assets.\n\n**Saving again in the original discards the engine snapshot.** Keep the engine\ncopy if you want to retain its custom features. The original resave can still be\nimported by the engine, with the state that the original understands.\n\nCurrent native saves use version 14; older released engines cannot read them.\nKeep pre-update backups. Original thoughts about named units and habitats are\nmapped, and view conversion covers the original's rotations and zooms. Exact\ntask/visit-history continuation and scenario parity still have limits; a\nsuccessful load is not proof that every in-progress action resumed unchanged.\n\n## Check repository sharing separately\n\n**Publish → Check it** validates the local package. **Upload it** sends it for server rules and malware checks. Confirm the listing is live, inspect its cover in the game's repository before download, then download into a clean test setup and enable it. A theme pack should work with its separate components off.\n\n## Keep evidence and limits\n\nRecord what you changed, the steps, elapsed game time, expected result and observed result. Retain failed attempts alongside the correction. A prototype that shares poses or inherits work states should say so. The tutorial examples demonstrate workflows, not complete animation or long-term balance certification.\n\nThe current Amberhorn example escaped an earlier low-glass enclosure after hatching; containment still needs a dedicated test. Marine show and animation diagnostics can also expose inherited engine gaps. Use those findings to guide testing rather than treating repository approval as proof of finished gameplay.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"publish","title":"Publish, download and update mods","summary":"Theme packs, cover pictures, server checks and actual game retrieval.","group":"Making mods","tabs":["mods","play"],"html":"<h2 id=\"help-section-prepare-one-mod\">Prepare one mod</h2>\n<p>Save the assets and map, then open <strong>About</strong>. Set the name, description, version and picture. Explain inherited art or behavior and known limits. For an update, increase the version and keep the same repository ID.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-combine-a-theme-pack\">Combine a theme pack</h2>\n<p>Choose <strong>Publish → Build a theme pack…</strong> and select two or more compatible mods. Give the pack its own name, version, description and image. The builder reports conflicting files and includes identical shared files once. Keep the original mods: the pack is a snapshot, not a live link to future source edits.</p>\n<p>Check and upload the resulting pack as one mod. Players should enable the pack with its component mods off to avoid duplicate definitions or replacements. Mods with identity-specific settings may need to stay separate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-upload-and-wait\">Check, upload and wait</h2>\n<p><strong>Check it</strong> validates the local package. <strong>Upload it</strong> uses your signed-in CodeRook account and sends the package to the repository. Wait for its own rules and malware scan to finish, then confirm <strong>Your uploads</strong> reports that it is in the repository. A local check alone does not publish it.</p>\n<p>The current Lab generates a small repository thumbnail automatically from the About picture. It travels inside the checked package. The game shows that cover before installation. Older published packages gain this preview after a new upload through the current Lab; a listing without a valid picture remains downloadable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-retrieve-it-in-the-actual-game\">Retrieve it in the actual game</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>Game Options → Mods &amp; Updates → Mod Menu → Mod repository</strong>.</li>\n<li>Select the mod or pack. Read its description and preview its cover.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Download</strong> and wait for completion.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>Your mods</strong>, switch it on and restart the game. A new download starts off.</li>\n<li>In the Lab&#x27;s embedded Play, stop and start Play to reload mods.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For mod maps, enable <strong>custommaps</strong> and look in Freeform. For the tutorial theme, keep the three separate component mods off.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-if-windows-cannot-replace-an-update\">If Windows cannot replace an update</h2>\n<p>A running game or Lab host can hold its archive open. Switch the mod off and restart Play and the Lab so the archive is unloaded, then retry the download. Keep a copy of editable creator sources before replacing a locally edited version. The Help instructions do not remove or overwrite those sources for you.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-local-zip-imports\">Local ZIP imports</h2>\n<p>Use the game&#x27;s <strong>Add a mod…</strong> picker for a supplied installable ZIP. This is separate from public publishing. For public sharing, use Publish and wait for repository approval.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"publication-tutorial\">Actual publishing checkpoints</a> · <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"preview-tutorial\">Repository pictures and clean download proof</a></p>","toc":[{"id":"prepare-one-mod","title":"Prepare one mod","level":2},{"id":"combine-a-theme-pack","title":"Combine a theme pack","level":2},{"id":"check-upload-and-wait","title":"Check, upload and wait","level":2},{"id":"retrieve-it-in-the-actual-game","title":"Retrieve it in the actual game","level":2},{"id":"if-windows-cannot-replace-an-update","title":"If Windows cannot replace an update","level":2},{"id":"local-zip-imports","title":"Local ZIP imports","level":2}],"text":"# Publish, download and update mods\n\n## Prepare one mod\n\nSave the assets and map, then open **About**. Set the name, description, version and picture. Explain inherited art or behavior and known limits. For an update, increase the version and keep the same repository ID.\n\n## Combine a theme pack\n\nChoose **Publish → Build a theme pack…** and select two or more compatible mods. Give the pack its own name, version, description and image. The builder reports conflicting files and includes identical shared files once. Keep the original mods: the pack is a snapshot, not a live link to future source edits.\n\nCheck and upload the resulting pack as one mod. Players should enable the pack with its component mods off to avoid duplicate definitions or replacements. Mods with identity-specific settings may need to stay separate.\n\n## Check, upload and wait\n\n**Check it** validates the local package. **Upload it** uses your signed-in CodeRook account and sends the package to the repository. Wait for its own rules and malware scan to finish, then confirm **Your uploads** reports that it is in the repository. A local check alone does not publish it.\n\nThe current Lab generates a small repository thumbnail automatically from the About picture. It travels inside the checked package. The game shows that cover before installation. Older published packages gain this preview after a new upload through the current Lab; a listing without a valid picture remains downloadable.\n\n## Retrieve it in the actual game\n\n1. Open **Game Options → Mods & Updates → Mod Menu → Mod repository**.\n2. Select the mod or pack. Read its description and preview its cover.\n3. Choose **Download** and wait for completion.\n4. Open **Your mods**, switch it on and restart the game. A new download starts off.\n5. In the Lab's embedded Play, stop and start Play to reload mods.\n\nFor mod maps, enable **custommaps** and look in Freeform. For the tutorial theme, keep the three separate component mods off.\n\n## If Windows cannot replace an update\n\nA running game or Lab host can hold its archive open. Switch the mod off and restart Play and the Lab so the archive is unloaded, then retry the download. Keep a copy of editable creator sources before replacing a locally edited version. The Help instructions do not remove or overwrite those sources for you.\n\n## Local ZIP imports\n\nUse the game's **Add a mod…** picker for a supplied installable ZIP. This is separate from public publishing. For public sharing, use Publish and wait for repository approval.\n\n[Actual publishing checkpoints](help:publication-tutorial) · [Repository pictures and clean download proof](help:preview-tutorial)\n","videos":[]},{"id":"troubleshooting","title":"Find and fix common problems","summary":"Missing art, locked downloads, animal care, maps and hidden panels.","group":"Start here","tabs":["mods","scene"],"html":"<h2 id=\"help-section-the-species-list-is-empty-or-shows-an-unexpected-animal\">The species list is empty or shows an unexpected animal</h2>\n<p>Wait for <strong>Engine running</strong>, then reopen the creator. Confirm the kind and Behaves like preset before creating the animal. If a marine list shows an unrelated land fallback, cancel and let the catalogue reconnect.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-drawing-looks-right-but-the-game-still-shows-old-art\">The drawing looks right but the game still shows old art</h2>\n<p>Save the drawing and wait for compilation. Confirm the correct slot and direction, and whether it is still linked to another drawing. Check that the intended mod is on and a competing replacement is off. Stop and start Play to reload loaded archives. A theme pack is a snapshot: rebuild it after changing a source mod.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-an-animal-will-not-place-or-is-unhappy\">An animal will not place or is unhappy</h2>\n<p>Check the actual depth, enclosure, terrain, space, group and correct carer. Marine animals need a suitable tank; a swimming zoo animal may also need dry land. Read the Inspector&#x27;s recommendations. Observe normal feeding and recovery over time rather than changing one value and stopping immediately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-a-dinosaur-escapes\">A dinosaur escapes</h2>\n<p>Check its inherited jumping and fence-breaking behavior, fence height and strength, and enclosure gaps. Test after hatching with suitable care. A successful egg placement does not prove containment. The Amberhorn tutorial&#x27;s earlier low-glass escape is a known unresolved example.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-a-building-or-staff-member-does-nothing\">A building or staff member does nothing</h2>\n<p>Confirm its inherited role, registry kind, footprint and path access. Rotate the entrance toward the path, provide a real guest or task, and let time run. A new appearance does not create a new staff duty or guest role. Check work and service states as well as idle art.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-saved-map-is-missing-animals\">The saved map is missing animals</h2>\n<p>Map designs save the environment, not live inhabitants. Adopt animals and hire staff again in Play. Reopen the map to check ground, fences and objects, then use a game save for ordinary ongoing gameplay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-map-is-not-in-freeform\">The map is not in Freeform</h2>\n<p>Confirm <strong>Save as a map</strong> completed in the intended mod, enable that mod and <strong>custommaps</strong>, then restart Play. Check that you are running the engine version with mod maps.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-repository-picture-is-missing\">The repository picture is missing</h2>\n<p>Set an About picture, raise the version and upload through the current Lab. Wait for server approval. The fixed game build reads the repository cover before download; an older installed build needs the engine update. The installed picture and repository thumbnail are separate sizes. Legacy uploads can still have no thumbnail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-download-says-it-cannot-unpack-an-update\">Download says it cannot unpack an update</h2>\n<p>Unload the enabled archive first: switch it off, stop Play and restart the Lab, then retry. Keep your editable sources safe when testing updates. See <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"publish\">publishing and updating</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-a-panel-vanished\">A panel vanished</h2>\n<p>Use <strong>Layout</strong> to reopen it or reset the tab. Help has its own article and contents navigation; opening it does not reset your dock layout.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-a-check-passes-but-behavior-is-still-wrong\">A check passes but behavior is still wrong</h2>\n<p>Local package rules, server scanning, creator Problems and species diagnostics check different things. Repeat the ordinary Play scenario and compare with the stock base. Note the map, mod version, actions and elapsed game time when reporting the bug.</p>","toc":[{"id":"the-species-list-is-empty-or-shows-an-unexpected-animal","title":"The species list is empty or shows an unexpected animal","level":2},{"id":"the-drawing-looks-right-but-the-game-still-shows-old-art","title":"The drawing looks right but the game still shows old art","level":2},{"id":"an-animal-will-not-place-or-is-unhappy","title":"An animal will not place or is unhappy","level":2},{"id":"a-dinosaur-escapes","title":"A dinosaur escapes","level":2},{"id":"a-building-or-staff-member-does-nothing","title":"A building or staff member does nothing","level":2},{"id":"the-saved-map-is-missing-animals","title":"The saved map is missing animals","level":2},{"id":"the-map-is-not-in-freeform","title":"The map is not in Freeform","level":2},{"id":"the-repository-picture-is-missing","title":"The repository picture is missing","level":2},{"id":"download-says-it-cannot-unpack-an-update","title":"Download says it cannot unpack an update","level":2},{"id":"a-panel-vanished","title":"A panel vanished","level":2},{"id":"a-check-passes-but-behavior-is-still-wrong","title":"A check passes but behavior is still wrong","level":2}],"text":"# Find and fix common problems\n\n## The species list is empty or shows an unexpected animal\n\nWait for **Engine running**, then reopen the creator. Confirm the kind and Behaves like preset before creating the animal. If a marine list shows an unrelated land fallback, cancel and let the catalogue reconnect.\n\n## The drawing looks right but the game still shows old art\n\nSave the drawing and wait for compilation. Confirm the correct slot and direction, and whether it is still linked to another drawing. Check that the intended mod is on and a competing replacement is off. Stop and start Play to reload loaded archives. A theme pack is a snapshot: rebuild it after changing a source mod.\n\n## An animal will not place or is unhappy\n\nCheck the actual depth, enclosure, terrain, space, group and correct carer. Marine animals need a suitable tank; a swimming zoo animal may also need dry land. Read the Inspector's recommendations. Observe normal feeding and recovery over time rather than changing one value and stopping immediately.\n\n## A dinosaur escapes\n\nCheck its inherited jumping and fence-breaking behavior, fence height and strength, and enclosure gaps. Test after hatching with suitable care. A successful egg placement does not prove containment. The Amberhorn tutorial's earlier low-glass escape is a known unresolved example.\n\n## A building or staff member does nothing\n\nConfirm its inherited role, registry kind, footprint and path access. Rotate the entrance toward the path, provide a real guest or task, and let time run. A new appearance does not create a new staff duty or guest role. Check work and service states as well as idle art.\n\n## The saved map is missing animals\n\nMap designs save the environment, not live inhabitants. Adopt animals and hire staff again in Play. Reopen the map to check ground, fences and objects, then use a game save for ordinary ongoing gameplay.\n\n## The map is not in Freeform\n\nConfirm **Save as a map** completed in the intended mod, enable that mod and **custommaps**, then restart Play. Check that you are running the engine version with mod maps.\n\n## The repository picture is missing\n\nSet an About picture, raise the version and upload through the current Lab. Wait for server approval. The fixed game build reads the repository cover before download; an older installed build needs the engine update. The installed picture and repository thumbnail are separate sizes. Legacy uploads can still have no thumbnail.\n\n## Download says it cannot unpack an update\n\nUnload the enabled archive first: switch it off, stop Play and restart the Lab, then retry. Keep your editable sources safe when testing updates. See [publishing and updating](help:publish).\n\n## A panel vanished\n\nUse **Layout** to reopen it or reset the tab. Help has its own article and contents navigation; opening it does not reset your dock layout.\n\n## A check passes but behavior is still wrong\n\nLocal package rules, server scanning, creator Problems and species diagnostics check different things. Repeat the ordinary Play scenario and compare with the stock base. Note the map, mod version, actions and elapsed game time when reporting the bug.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"animal-imports","title":"Three animal import workflows","summary":"One PNG, a directional sheet and OBJ poses through the current studio.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/59ff7ec94cbed08b5b.mp4","title":"Recorded tutorial checkpoints","caption":"Captioned native screenshots. This is a checkpoint walkthrough, not a continuous recording.","src":"help/media/59ff7ec94cbed08b5b.mp4","poster":"help/media/03f6a7750bb5e5af61.webp"}],"html":"<p>Recorded 26 October 2026. This tutorial uses the development version of the new ZT1 Lab Mods workspace. It creates three fictional zoo animals in one mod, Wildlife Import Tutorial.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Animal</th>\n<th>Input</th>\n<th>Game frames</th>\n<th>Custom needs</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Copperleaf Pika</td>\n<td>One transparent PNG</td>\n<td>One pose repeated across five directions</td>\n<td>Fruit; 2–4 animals; grass and deciduous forest floor; foliage, rocks and shelter</td>\n<td>$420</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indigo Springhare</td>\n<td>4-column, 5-row PNG sheet</td>\n<td>Four walking frames in each of five directions</td>\n<td>Grass; 2–6 animals; grassland and shelter</td>\n<td>$610</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jade Shellback</td>\n<td>Two numbered OBJ poses plus MTL</td>\n<td>Two supplied poses rendered in five directions</td>\n<td>Herbivore chow; 1–3 animals; grassland, rocks and shelter</td>\n<td>$900</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-before-starting\">Before starting</h2>\n<p>The Lab must show Engine running. Copy the supplied source-assets folder somewhere you can find with the file picker. There is no need to create internal project JSON or copy files into the mod&#x27;s .art folder. The Lab&#x27;s Import and Save controls perform the conversion.</p>\n<p>Create an empty mod from the picker. Use Make something → New animal → Zoo animal. Copperleaf Pika and Indigo Springhare start from Thomson&#x27;s Gazelle; Jade Shellback starts from Plains Zebra. These provide the initial care and behavior system.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-import-settings\">Import settings</h2>\n<p>For Copperleaf Pika: Draw beside walk → Blank → This direction → Import external art. Select copperleaf-pika.png. Choose One picture, height 38, Repeat for all five directions. This is a prototype with one viewpoint; repetition does not create unseen angles.</p>\n<p>For Indigo Springhare: choose indigo-springhare-sheet.png. Select Grid, columns 4, rows 5, directions N,NE,E,SE,S, height 42, frame time 120 ms. Preview every view and frame. The three other compass directions are mirrored by the game. A horizontal strip can instead be assigned to the selected direction with its column count.</p>\n<p>For Jade Shellback: select jade-shellback-01.obj, jade-shellback-02.obj and jade-shellback.mtl together. Choose Y-up with front +Z, height 32, 200 ms per pose. Files are sorted by name. The supplied model has two different leg poses; a single static OBJ stays still. Import is not automatic rigging or animation generation.</p>\n<p>Check the visible feet against the red ground point. Save builds the game&#x27;s palette and animation in the mod. Use Link other poses for the prototype&#x27;s adult slots and wait until linking finishes. The examples share their imported pose sets across eating, resting and other behavior states; they do not have a complete bespoke animation library. Crate poses remain shared with the game.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-needs-and-pictures\">Needs and pictures</h2>\n<p>Set food, biome, preferred ground, objects and animal compatibility in Needs. Set price, group size, space and hunger growth in the Inspector. Wait for Saved after changing values. Check inherited dislikes as well as the new biome. These examples use the fictional creature family and like its members.</p>\n<p>Pika: grass 50, deciduous floor 50; group 2–4, space 80, density 40, hunger growth 4. Springhare: grass 100; group 2–6, space 80, density 40, hunger growth 3. Shellback: grass 100; group 1–3, space 100, density 50, hunger growth 2. All require shelter.</p>\n<p>In Pictures → Use an animation frame, choose the animal&#x27;s own walk animation, SE, frame 1, and make the icon, list picture and plaque. Each picture can then be edited independently. Fill in About this mod and use the Test Zoo view or an image for its Mod Menu preview.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-test-and-use-it\">Test and use it</h2>\n<p>Test in the zoo loads the mod temporarily and restarts the Test Zoo. Your normal mod switches stay unchanged. The temporary-test label has an <strong>End test</strong> control to return to them. Testing does not yet build a home automatically. Build an enclosed habitat with the palette. Start with bare grass to check missing-needs warnings, then add the requested ground, companions, shelter, trees, rocks and keeper access. The finished tests used separate enclosures and paired animals.</p>\n<p>In Play, start a freeform zoo. Adopt Animals → filter Mods → select an icon → click male or female → place the animal in a closed land exhibit. Selecting the icon alone does not start adoption. The small ordinary-game pen in the final screenshot is a purchase/rendering check; the separate Lab habitats are the welfare test.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-publish-and-retrieve\">Publish and retrieve</h2>\n<p>The studio&#x27;s top Publish button opens Check it and Upload it. Check it validates the local package; a pass does not publish it. Upload it sends the package using the signed-in CodeRook account. Wait for the repository&#x27;s rules and malware checks to pass and confirm Your uploads says In the mod repository. For an update, raise the version and upload the same mod ID.</p>\n<p>Players use Game Options → Mods &amp; Updates → Mod Menu → Mod repository. Download a part, return to Your mods, switch it on, and restart. In Lab Play, stop and start Play instead of the game&#x27;s Restart. The repository button requires the official repository client; the test installation had it and successfully fetched the live Plasma Fence listing. We did not upload the tutorial prototype or replace that existing download. Local Add a mod can import the supplied mod ZIP.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-recorded-results\">Recorded results</h2>\n<p>All three were loaded, displayed and adopted in the ordinary game with their own names, icons and prices. Species diagnostics reported zero problems. After suitable Lab habitats were built, suitability was 87 for Pika, 81 for Springhare and 82 for Shellback, with happiness 95–100. Hunger decreased during the feeding stress check, and no tested animal was sick or escaped.</p>\n<p>The raw creator-process.mp4 is a silent one-frame-per-second capture of the Lab workspace, including pauses, bugs and retakes. Native file dialogs and the embedded game&#x27;s child window are not fully included in that capture; their process is described here and actual game screenshots are included below. The illustrated guide presents the verified path. Breeding, long-term survival, textured OBJ material variants and live publishing/download of this prototype are not certified by these tests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-illustrated-steps\">Illustrated steps</h2>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-1-choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio\">1. Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/03f6a7750bb5e5af61.webp\" alt=\"Step 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-2-make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system\">2. Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e4e859ffb925a154ba.webp\" alt=\"Step 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-3-name-the-species-copperleaf-pika-and-choose-thomsons-gazelle-as-its-starting-behavior\">3. Name the species Copperleaf Pika and choose Thomson’s Gazelle as its starting behavior.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/433fd0340ae6641943.webp\" alt=\"Step 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-4-choose-draw-on-walk-then-blank-the-direction-ring-shows-the-five-views-that-need-frames\">4. Choose Draw on walk, then Blank. The direction ring shows the five views that need frames.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/1e6ba5ee1edc650a7c.webp\" alt=\"Step 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-5-choose-one-picture-fit-visible-art-to-38-pixels-and-repeat-for-all-five-directions-for-this-simple-prototype-repetition-does-not-invent-new-viewpoints\">5. Choose One picture, fit visible art to 38 pixels, and Repeat for all five directions for this simple prototype. Repetition does not invent new viewpoints.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/5a65194403f7b60522.webp\" alt=\"Step 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-6-check-the-visible-feet-against-the-red-ground-point-the-importer-ignores-nearly-transparent-edge-pixels-when-fitting-the-art\">6. Check the visible feet against the red ground point. The importer ignores nearly transparent edge pixels when fitting the art.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0873493115ba25690e.webp\" alt=\"Step 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-7-use-link-other-poses-to-reuse-the-imported-art-for-the-prototypes-adult-behavior-slots-this-shares-frames-it-is-not-bespoke-eating-or-sleeping-animation\">7. Use Link other poses to reuse the imported art for the prototype’s adult behavior slots. This shares frames; it is not bespoke eating or sleeping animation.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/00e0922751d32415e8.webp\" alt=\"Step 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-8-create-indigo-springhare-in-the-same-mod-each-species-has-its-own-art-needs-and-price\">8. Create Indigo Springhare in the same mod. Each species has its own art, needs and price.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0ad9cad50af011b45b.webp\" alt=\"Step 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-9-for-indigo-springhare-select-grid-frames-across-directions-down-enter-4-columns-5-rows-nneeses-fit-to-42-pixels-with-120-ms-frames\">9. For Indigo Springhare, select Grid: frames across, directions down. Enter 4 columns, 5 rows, N,NE,E,SE,S; fit to 42 pixels with 120 ms frames.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/cba26b12c9c05967c8.webp\" alt=\"Step 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-10-check-the-four-imported-frames-and-all-five-directions-at-game-size-save-the-finished-conversion\">10. Check the four imported frames and all five directions at game size. Save the finished conversion.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/91d6dfacbbad7f9d16.webp\" alt=\"Step 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-11-select-both-numbered-obj-pose-files-and-their-mtl-together-choose-y-up-front-z-32-pixels-and-200-ms-per-supplied-pose-the-importer-renders-five-directional-views\">11. Select both numbered OBJ pose files and their MTL together. Choose Y-up, front +Z, 32 pixels and 200 ms per supplied pose. The importer renders five directional views.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/522a89181ffe37ddbd.webp\" alt=\"Step 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-12-jade-shellback-rendered-from-the-obj-meshes-two-supplied-poses-five-directions-mtl-colours-and-transparent-background-save-compiles-the-same-game-art-used-for-png-imports\">12. Jade Shellback rendered from the OBJ meshes: two supplied poses, five directions, MTL colours and transparent background. Save compiles the same game art used for PNG imports.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/30e3660ee72d782132.webp\" alt=\"Step 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-13-pictures-use-an-animation-frame-pick-the-drawn-walk-animation-se-frame-1-and-icon-list-picture-and-plaque-the-lab-sizes-and-compiles-them\">13. Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/22cab71528f3be70f2.webp\" alt=\"Step 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-14-the-three-picture-cards-now-show-jade-shellbacks-own-art-rather-than-the-base-animals-pictures-each-card-can-still-be-edited\">14. The three picture cards now show Jade Shellback’s own art rather than the base animal’s pictures. Each card can still be edited.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/a024a9c08886115302.webp\" alt=\"Step 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-15-use-needs-for-food-and-preferred-ground-the-inspector-sets-adoption-price-group-size-space-and-hunger-rate-values-save-into-the-mod-as-they-change\">15. Use Needs for food and preferred ground; the Inspector sets adoption price, group size, space and hunger rate. Values save into the mod as they change.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/ddf5721518fccad543.webp\" alt=\"Step 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-16-needs-includes-inherited-likes-and-dislikes-update-forest-objects-and-compatible-creature-families-as-well-as-the-biome-and-terrain\">16. Needs includes inherited likes and dislikes. Update forest objects and compatible creature families as well as the biome and terrain.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/17509819a637082f66.webp\" alt=\"Step 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-17-a-bare-grass-enclosure-exposes-missing-companion-shelter-and-habitat-needs-these-warnings-are-part-of-testing-the-new-values\">17. A bare grass enclosure exposes missing companion, shelter and habitat needs. These warnings are part of testing the new values.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/1d8e2d5486464ed65b.webp\" alt=\"Step 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-18-verified-final-copperleaf-pika-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values\">18. Verified final Copperleaf Pika art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/fba686d1be6a48991a.webp\" alt=\"Step 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-19-verified-final-indigo-springhare-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values\">19. Verified final Indigo Springhare art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/fc99d2fef2f7ad24cf.webp\" alt=\"Step 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-20-verified-final-jade-shellback-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values\">20. Verified final Jade Shellback art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/2e32372f39603f16a0.webp\" alt=\"Step 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-21-about-this-mod-supplies-the-name-author-version-and-description-shown-in-the-games-mod-menu\">21. About this mod supplies the name, author, version and description shown in the game’s Mod Menu.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/27b84fadeb9dbc9e12.webp\" alt=\"Step 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-22-the-games-mod-menu-shows-wildlife-import-tutorial-switched-on-with-its-author-description-and-preview\">22. The game’s Mod Menu shows Wildlife Import Tutorial switched on, with its author, description and preview.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/9494a7b3b3e348d2b5.webp\" alt=\"Step 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-23-live-repository-browsing-works-in-this-installation-have-means-a-part-is-installed-the-action-offers-download-again\">23. Live repository browsing works in this installation. Have means a part is installed; the action offers Download again.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e372f27eac9e20fdb0.webp\" alt=\"Step 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-24-adopt-animals-mods-lists-all-three-new-icons-and-their-custom-names-and-prices\">24. Adopt Animals → Mods lists all three new icons and their custom names and prices.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/1765575e1d9b32ce66.webp\" alt=\"Step 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-25-select-the-animal-then-its-male-or-female-button-then-place-it-all-three-imported-animals-were-adopted-through-the-ordinary-game-ui-this-small-pen-checks-purchase-and-rendering-the-separate-lab-habitats-test-welfare\">25. Select the animal, then its male or female button, then place it. All three imported animals were adopted through the ordinary game UI. This small pen checks purchase and rendering; the separate Lab habitats test welfare.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/bbf69c24266a9a8f1b.webp\" alt=\"Step 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-26-the-completed-mod-has-a-preview-and-description-package-checks-pass-with-no-listed-problems-upload-it-remains-a-separate-publishing-step\">26. The completed mod has a preview and description; package checks pass with no listed problems. Upload it remains a separate publishing step.</h3>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/22b55e9b972edc82f7.webp\" alt=\"Step 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-additional-swimming-and-marine-tests\">Additional swimming and marine tests</h2>\n<p>The updated pack now also includes Copperbank Hippo and Azure Reef Dolphin variants and the Aquatic Mod Test habitat map. See <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"aquatic-tutorial\">the aquatic tutorial</a>, illustrated guide and results. These use Remake/Recolour and preserve distinct land, water and tank poses. Basic placement, natural swimming, feeding, purity, salinity and minimum-depth checks passed; inherited marine trick/layer warnings remain.</p>","toc":[{"id":"before-starting","title":"Before starting","level":2},{"id":"import-settings","title":"Import settings","level":2},{"id":"needs-and-pictures","title":"Needs and pictures","level":2},{"id":"test-and-use-it","title":"Test and use it","level":2},{"id":"publish-and-retrieve","title":"Publish and retrieve","level":2},{"id":"recorded-results","title":"Recorded results","level":2},{"id":"illustrated-steps","title":"Illustrated steps","level":2},{"id":"1-choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio","title":"1. Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.","level":3},{"id":"2-make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system","title":"2. Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.","level":3},{"id":"3-name-the-species-copperleaf-pika-and-choose-thomsons-gazelle-as-its-starting-behavior","title":"3. Name the species Copperleaf Pika and choose Thomson’s Gazelle as its starting behavior.","level":3},{"id":"4-choose-draw-on-walk-then-blank-the-direction-ring-shows-the-five-views-that-need-frames","title":"4. Choose Draw on walk, then Blank. The direction ring shows the five views that need frames.","level":3},{"id":"5-choose-one-picture-fit-visible-art-to-38-pixels-and-repeat-for-all-five-directions-for-this-simple-prototype-repetition-does-not-invent-new-viewpoints","title":"5. Choose One picture, fit visible art to 38 pixels, and Repeat for all five directions for this simple prototype. Repetition does not invent new viewpoints.","level":3},{"id":"6-check-the-visible-feet-against-the-red-ground-point-the-importer-ignores-nearly-transparent-edge-pixels-when-fitting-the-art","title":"6. Check the visible feet against the red ground point. The importer ignores nearly transparent edge pixels when fitting the art.","level":3},{"id":"7-use-link-other-poses-to-reuse-the-imported-art-for-the-prototypes-adult-behavior-slots-this-shares-frames-it-is-not-bespoke-eating-or-sleeping-animation","title":"7. Use Link other poses to reuse the imported art for the prototype’s adult behavior slots. This shares frames; it is not bespoke eating or sleeping animation.","level":3},{"id":"8-create-indigo-springhare-in-the-same-mod-each-species-has-its-own-art-needs-and-price","title":"8. Create Indigo Springhare in the same mod. Each species has its own art, needs and price.","level":3},{"id":"9-for-indigo-springhare-select-grid-frames-across-directions-down-enter-4-columns-5-rows-nneeses-fit-to-42-pixels-with-120-ms-frames","title":"9. For Indigo Springhare, select Grid: frames across, directions down. Enter 4 columns, 5 rows, N,NE,E,SE,S; fit to 42 pixels with 120 ms frames.","level":3},{"id":"10-check-the-four-imported-frames-and-all-five-directions-at-game-size-save-the-finished-conversion","title":"10. Check the four imported frames and all five directions at game size. Save the finished conversion.","level":3},{"id":"11-select-both-numbered-obj-pose-files-and-their-mtl-together-choose-y-up-front-z-32-pixels-and-200-ms-per-supplied-pose-the-importer-renders-five-directional-views","title":"11. Select both numbered OBJ pose files and their MTL together. Choose Y-up, front +Z, 32 pixels and 200 ms per supplied pose. The importer renders five directional views.","level":3},{"id":"12-jade-shellback-rendered-from-the-obj-meshes-two-supplied-poses-five-directions-mtl-colours-and-transparent-background-save-compiles-the-same-game-art-used-for-png-imports","title":"12. Jade Shellback rendered from the OBJ meshes: two supplied poses, five directions, MTL colours and transparent background. Save compiles the same game art used for PNG imports.","level":3},{"id":"13-pictures-use-an-animation-frame-pick-the-drawn-walk-animation-se-frame-1-and-icon-list-picture-and-plaque-the-lab-sizes-and-compiles-them","title":"13. Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.","level":3},{"id":"14-the-three-picture-cards-now-show-jade-shellbacks-own-art-rather-than-the-base-animals-pictures-each-card-can-still-be-edited","title":"14. The three picture cards now show Jade Shellback’s own art rather than the base animal’s pictures. Each card can still be edited.","level":3},{"id":"15-use-needs-for-food-and-preferred-ground-the-inspector-sets-adoption-price-group-size-space-and-hunger-rate-values-save-into-the-mod-as-they-change","title":"15. Use Needs for food and preferred ground; the Inspector sets adoption price, group size, space and hunger rate. Values save into the mod as they change.","level":3},{"id":"16-needs-includes-inherited-likes-and-dislikes-update-forest-objects-and-compatible-creature-families-as-well-as-the-biome-and-terrain","title":"16. Needs includes inherited likes and dislikes. Update forest objects and compatible creature families as well as the biome and terrain.","level":3},{"id":"17-a-bare-grass-enclosure-exposes-missing-companion-shelter-and-habitat-needs-these-warnings-are-part-of-testing-the-new-values","title":"17. A bare grass enclosure exposes missing companion, shelter and habitat needs. These warnings are part of testing the new values.","level":3},{"id":"18-verified-final-copperleaf-pika-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values","title":"18. Verified final Copperleaf Pika art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.","level":3},{"id":"19-verified-final-indigo-springhare-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values","title":"19. Verified final Indigo Springhare art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.","level":3},{"id":"20-verified-final-jade-shellback-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values","title":"20. Verified final Jade Shellback art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.","level":3},{"id":"21-about-this-mod-supplies-the-name-author-version-and-description-shown-in-the-games-mod-menu","title":"21. About this mod supplies the name, author, version and description shown in the game’s Mod Menu.","level":3},{"id":"22-the-games-mod-menu-shows-wildlife-import-tutorial-switched-on-with-its-author-description-and-preview","title":"22. The game’s Mod Menu shows Wildlife Import Tutorial switched on, with its author, description and preview.","level":3},{"id":"23-live-repository-browsing-works-in-this-installation-have-means-a-part-is-installed-the-action-offers-download-again","title":"23. Live repository browsing works in this installation. Have means a part is installed; the action offers Download again.","level":3},{"id":"24-adopt-animals-mods-lists-all-three-new-icons-and-their-custom-names-and-prices","title":"24. Adopt Animals → Mods lists all three new icons and their custom names and prices.","level":3},{"id":"25-select-the-animal-then-its-male-or-female-button-then-place-it-all-three-imported-animals-were-adopted-through-the-ordinary-game-ui-this-small-pen-checks-purchase-and-rendering-the-separate-lab-habitats-test-welfare","title":"25. Select the animal, then its male or female button, then place it. All three imported animals were adopted through the ordinary game UI. This small pen checks purchase and rendering; the separate Lab habitats test welfare.","level":3},{"id":"26-the-completed-mod-has-a-preview-and-description-package-checks-pass-with-no-listed-problems-upload-it-remains-a-separate-publishing-step","title":"26. The completed mod has a preview and description; package checks pass with no listed problems. Upload it remains a separate publishing step.","level":3},{"id":"additional-swimming-and-marine-tests","title":"Additional swimming and marine tests","level":2}],"text":"# Three animals with the new Mods studio\n\nRecorded 26 October 2026. This tutorial uses the development version of the new ZT1 Lab Mods workspace. It creates three fictional zoo animals in one mod, Wildlife Import Tutorial.\n\n| Animal | Input | Game frames | Custom needs | Price |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Copperleaf Pika | One transparent PNG | One pose repeated across five directions | Fruit; 2–4 animals; grass and deciduous forest floor; foliage, rocks and shelter | $420 |\n| Indigo Springhare | 4-column, 5-row PNG sheet | Four walking frames in each of five directions | Grass; 2–6 animals; grassland and shelter | $610 |\n| Jade Shellback | Two numbered OBJ poses plus MTL | Two supplied poses rendered in five directions | Herbivore chow; 1–3 animals; grassland, rocks and shelter | $900 |\n\n## Before starting\n\nThe Lab must show Engine running. Copy the supplied source-assets folder somewhere you can find with the file picker. There is no need to create internal project JSON or copy files into the mod's .art folder. The Lab's Import and Save controls perform the conversion.\n\nCreate an empty mod from the picker. Use Make something → New animal → Zoo animal. Copperleaf Pika and Indigo Springhare start from Thomson's Gazelle; Jade Shellback starts from Plains Zebra. These provide the initial care and behavior system.\n\n## Import settings\n\nFor Copperleaf Pika: Draw beside walk → Blank → This direction → Import external art. Select copperleaf-pika.png. Choose One picture, height 38, Repeat for all five directions. This is a prototype with one viewpoint; repetition does not create unseen angles.\n\nFor Indigo Springhare: choose indigo-springhare-sheet.png. Select Grid, columns 4, rows 5, directions N,NE,E,SE,S, height 42, frame time 120 ms. Preview every view and frame. The three other compass directions are mirrored by the game. A horizontal strip can instead be assigned to the selected direction with its column count.\n\nFor Jade Shellback: select jade-shellback-01.obj, jade-shellback-02.obj and jade-shellback.mtl together. Choose Y-up with front +Z, height 32, 200 ms per pose. Files are sorted by name. The supplied model has two different leg poses; a single static OBJ stays still. Import is not automatic rigging or animation generation.\n\nCheck the visible feet against the red ground point. Save builds the game's palette and animation in the mod. Use Link other poses for the prototype's adult slots and wait until linking finishes. The examples share their imported pose sets across eating, resting and other behavior states; they do not have a complete bespoke animation library. Crate poses remain shared with the game.\n\n## Needs and pictures\n\nSet food, biome, preferred ground, objects and animal compatibility in Needs. Set price, group size, space and hunger growth in the Inspector. Wait for Saved after changing values. Check inherited dislikes as well as the new biome. These examples use the fictional creature family and like its members.\n\nPika: grass 50, deciduous floor 50; group 2–4, space 80, density 40, hunger growth 4. Springhare: grass 100; group 2–6, space 80, density 40, hunger growth 3. Shellback: grass 100; group 1–3, space 100, density 50, hunger growth 2. All require shelter.\n\nIn Pictures → Use an animation frame, choose the animal's own walk animation, SE, frame 1, and make the icon, list picture and plaque. Each picture can then be edited independently. Fill in About this mod and use the Test Zoo view or an image for its Mod Menu preview.\n\n## Test and use it\n\nTest in the zoo loads the mod temporarily and restarts the Test Zoo. Your normal mod switches stay unchanged. The temporary-test label has an **End test** control to return to them. Testing does not yet build a home automatically. Build an enclosed habitat with the palette. Start with bare grass to check missing-needs warnings, then add the requested ground, companions, shelter, trees, rocks and keeper access. The finished tests used separate enclosures and paired animals.\n\nIn Play, start a freeform zoo. Adopt Animals → filter Mods → select an icon → click male or female → place the animal in a closed land exhibit. Selecting the icon alone does not start adoption. The small ordinary-game pen in the final screenshot is a purchase/rendering check; the separate Lab habitats are the welfare test.\n\n## Publish and retrieve\n\nThe studio's top Publish button opens Check it and Upload it. Check it validates the local package; a pass does not publish it. Upload it sends the package using the signed-in CodeRook account. Wait for the repository's rules and malware checks to pass and confirm Your uploads says In the mod repository. For an update, raise the version and upload the same mod ID.\n\nPlayers use Game Options → Mods & Updates → Mod Menu → Mod repository. Download a part, return to Your mods, switch it on, and restart. In Lab Play, stop and start Play instead of the game's Restart. The repository button requires the official repository client; the test installation had it and successfully fetched the live Plasma Fence listing. We did not upload the tutorial prototype or replace that existing download. Local Add a mod can import the supplied mod ZIP.\n\n## Recorded results\n\nAll three were loaded, displayed and adopted in the ordinary game with their own names, icons and prices. Species diagnostics reported zero problems. After suitable Lab habitats were built, suitability was 87 for Pika, 81 for Springhare and 82 for Shellback, with happiness 95–100. Hunger decreased during the feeding stress check, and no tested animal was sick or escaped.\n\nThe raw creator-process.mp4 is a silent one-frame-per-second capture of the Lab workspace, including pauses, bugs and retakes. Native file dialogs and the embedded game's child window are not fully included in that capture; their process is described here and actual game screenshots are included below. The illustrated guide presents the verified path. Breeding, long-term survival, textured OBJ material variants and live publishing/download of this prototype are not certified by these tests.\n\n## Illustrated steps\n\n### 1. Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.\n\n\n\n### 2. Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.\n\n\n\n### 3. Name the species Copperleaf Pika and choose Thomson’s Gazelle as its starting behavior.\n\n\n\n### 4. Choose Draw on walk, then Blank. The direction ring shows the five views that need frames.\n\n\n\n### 5. Choose One picture, fit visible art to 38 pixels, and Repeat for all five directions for this simple prototype. Repetition does not invent new viewpoints.\n\n\n\n### 6. Check the visible feet against the red ground point. The importer ignores nearly transparent edge pixels when fitting the art.\n\n\n\n### 7. Use Link other poses to reuse the imported art for the prototype’s adult behavior slots. This shares frames; it is not bespoke eating or sleeping animation.\n\n\n\n### 8. Create Indigo Springhare in the same mod. Each species has its own art, needs and price.\n\n\n\n### 9. For Indigo Springhare, select Grid: frames across, directions down. Enter 4 columns, 5 rows, N,NE,E,SE,S; fit to 42 pixels with 120 ms frames.\n\n\n\n### 10. Check the four imported frames and all five directions at game size. Save the finished conversion.\n\n\n\n### 11. Select both numbered OBJ pose files and their MTL together. Choose Y-up, front +Z, 32 pixels and 200 ms per supplied pose. The importer renders five directional views.\n\n\n\n### 12. Jade Shellback rendered from the OBJ meshes: two supplied poses, five directions, MTL colours and transparent background. Save compiles the same game art used for PNG imports.\n\n\n\n### 13. Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.\n\n\n\n### 14. The three picture cards now show Jade Shellback’s own art rather than the base animal’s pictures. Each card can still be edited.\n\n\n\n### 15. Use Needs for food and preferred ground; the Inspector sets adoption price, group size, space and hunger rate. Values save into the mod as they change.\n\n\n\n### 16. Needs includes inherited likes and dislikes. Update forest objects and compatible creature families as well as the biome and terrain.\n\n\n\n### 17. A bare grass enclosure exposes missing companion, shelter and habitat needs. These warnings are part of testing the new values.\n\n\n\n### 18. Verified final Copperleaf Pika art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.\n\n\n\n### 19. Verified final Indigo Springhare art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.\n\n\n\n### 20. Verified final Jade Shellback art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.\n\n\n\n### 21. About this mod supplies the name, author, version and description shown in the game’s Mod Menu.\n\n\n\n### 22. The game’s Mod Menu shows Wildlife Import Tutorial switched on, with its author, description and preview.\n\n\n\n### 23. Live repository browsing works in this installation. Have means a part is installed; the action offers Download again.\n\n\n\n### 24. Adopt Animals → Mods lists all three new icons and their custom names and prices.\n\n\n\n### 25. Select the animal, then its male or female button, then place it. All three imported animals were adopted through the ordinary game UI. This small pen checks purchase and rendering; the separate Lab habitats test welfare.\n\n\n\n### 26. The completed mod has a preview and description; package checks pass with no listed problems. Upload it remains a separate publishing step.\n\n\n\n\n## Additional swimming and marine tests\n\nThe updated pack now also includes Copperbank Hippo and Azure Reef Dolphin variants and the Aquatic Mod Test habitat map. See [the aquatic tutorial](help:aquatic-tutorial), illustrated guide and results. These use Remake/Recolour and preserve distinct land, water and tank poses. Basic placement, natural swimming, feeding, purity, salinity and minimum-depth checks passed; inherited marine trick/layer warnings remain.\n"},{"id":"png-lesson","title":"PNG lesson: Copperleaf Pika","summary":"Import one transparent picture and fit its anchor.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","html":"<p>Source: source-assets/copperleaf-pika.png</p>\n<p>Create a zoo animal based on Thomson’s Gazelle. Draw walk → Blank → Import external art. Select the PNG, One picture, height 38, Repeat for all five directions. This repeats one viewpoint and is a prototype, not five newly invented angles.</p>\n<p>Save compiles the game art. For this prototype, Link other poses reuses that art for the other adult states; wait for it to finish. Add its own food, habitat and group values in Needs and the Inspector. Pictures → Use an animation frame makes its icon, list picture and plaque.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio\">Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/03f6a7750bb5e5af61.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system\">Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e4e859ffb925a154ba.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-name-the-species-copperleaf-pika-and-choose-thomsons-gazelle-as-its-starting-behavior\">Name the species Copperleaf Pika and choose Thomson’s Gazelle as its starting behavior.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/433fd0340ae6641943.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-draw-on-walk-then-blank-the-direction-ring-shows-the-five-views-that-need-frames\">Choose Draw on walk, then Blank. The direction ring shows the five views that need frames.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/1e6ba5ee1edc650a7c.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-one-picture-fit-visible-art-to-38-pixels-and-repeat-for-all-five-directions-for-this-simple-prototype-repetition-does-not-invent-new-viewpoints\">Choose One picture, fit visible art to 38 pixels, and Repeat for all five directions for this simple prototype. Repetition does not invent new viewpoints.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/5a65194403f7b60522.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-the-visible-feet-against-the-red-ground-point-the-importer-ignores-nearly-transparent-edge-pixels-when-fitting-the-art\">Check the visible feet against the red ground point. The importer ignores nearly transparent edge pixels when fitting the art.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0873493115ba25690e.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-use-link-other-poses-to-reuse-the-imported-art-for-the-prototypes-adult-behavior-slots-this-shares-frames-it-is-not-bespoke-eating-or-sleeping-animation\">Use Link other poses to reuse the imported art for the prototype’s adult behavior slots. This shares frames; it is not bespoke eating or sleeping animation.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/00e0922751d32415e8.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-use-needs-for-food-and-preferred-ground-the-inspector-sets-adoption-price-group-size-space-and-hunger-rate-values-save-into-the-mod-as-they-change\">Use Needs for food and preferred ground; the Inspector sets adoption price, group size, space and hunger rate. Values save into the mod as they change.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/ddf5721518fccad543.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-verified-final-copperleaf-pika-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values\">Verified final Copperleaf Pika art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/fba686d1be6a48991a.webp\" alt=\"Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"animal-imports\">Shared testing, normal game adoption and publishing instructions</a></p>","toc":[{"id":"choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio","title":"Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.","level":2},{"id":"make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system","title":"Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.","level":2},{"id":"name-the-species-copperleaf-pika-and-choose-thomsons-gazelle-as-its-starting-behavior","title":"Name the species Copperleaf Pika and choose Thomson’s Gazelle as its starting behavior.","level":2},{"id":"choose-draw-on-walk-then-blank-the-direction-ring-shows-the-five-views-that-need-frames","title":"Choose Draw on walk, then Blank. The direction ring shows the five views that need frames.","level":2},{"id":"choose-one-picture-fit-visible-art-to-38-pixels-and-repeat-for-all-five-directions-for-this-simple-prototype-repetition-does-not-invent-new-viewpoints","title":"Choose One picture, fit visible art to 38 pixels, and Repeat for all five directions for this simple prototype. Repetition does not invent new viewpoints.","level":2},{"id":"check-the-visible-feet-against-the-red-ground-point-the-importer-ignores-nearly-transparent-edge-pixels-when-fitting-the-art","title":"Check the visible feet against the red ground point. The importer ignores nearly transparent edge pixels when fitting the art.","level":2},{"id":"use-link-other-poses-to-reuse-the-imported-art-for-the-prototypes-adult-behavior-slots-this-shares-frames-it-is-not-bespoke-eating-or-sleeping-animation","title":"Use Link other poses to reuse the imported art for the prototype’s adult behavior slots. This shares frames; it is not bespoke eating or sleeping animation.","level":2},{"id":"use-needs-for-food-and-preferred-ground-the-inspector-sets-adoption-price-group-size-space-and-hunger-rate-values-save-into-the-mod-as-they-change","title":"Use Needs for food and preferred ground; the Inspector sets adoption price, group size, space and hunger rate. Values save into the mod as they change.","level":2},{"id":"verified-final-copperleaf-pika-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values","title":"Verified final Copperleaf Pika art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.","level":2}],"text":"# Copperleaf Pika: a PNG animal\n\nSource: source-assets/copperleaf-pika.png\n\nCreate a zoo animal based on Thomson’s Gazelle. Draw walk → Blank → Import external art. Select the PNG, One picture, height 38, Repeat for all five directions. This repeats one viewpoint and is a prototype, not five newly invented angles.\n\nSave compiles the game art. For this prototype, Link other poses reuses that art for the other adult states; wait for it to finish. Add its own food, habitat and group values in Needs and the Inspector. Pictures → Use an animation frame makes its icon, list picture and plaque.\n\n## Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.\n\n\n\n## Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.\n\n\n\n## Name the species Copperleaf Pika and choose Thomson’s Gazelle as its starting behavior.\n\n\n\n## Choose Draw on walk, then Blank. The direction ring shows the five views that need frames.\n\n\n\n## Choose One picture, fit visible art to 38 pixels, and Repeat for all five directions for this simple prototype. Repetition does not invent new viewpoints.\n\n\n\n## Check the visible feet against the red ground point. The importer ignores nearly transparent edge pixels when fitting the art.\n\n\n\n## Use Link other poses to reuse the imported art for the prototype’s adult behavior slots. This shares frames; it is not bespoke eating or sleeping animation.\n\n\n\n## Use Needs for food and preferred ground; the Inspector sets adoption price, group size, space and hunger rate. Values save into the mod as they change.\n\n\n\n## Verified final Copperleaf Pika art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.\n\n\n\n[Shared testing, normal game adoption and publishing instructions](help:animal-imports)\n","videos":[]},{"id":"sheet-lesson","title":"Sprite-sheet lesson: Indigo Springhare","summary":"Import a grid of walking frames and directions.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","html":"<p>Source: source-assets/indigo-springhare-sheet.png</p>\n<p>Create a zoo animal based on Thomson’s Gazelle. Draw walk → Blank → Import external art. Select Grid, columns 4, rows 5, row directions N,NE,E,SE,S, height 42 and 120 ms per frame. Preview every view and all four walking frames.</p>\n<p>Save compiles the game art. For this prototype, Link other poses reuses that art for the other adult states; wait for it to finish. Add its own food, habitat and group values in Needs and the Inspector. Pictures → Use an animation frame makes its icon, list picture and plaque.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio\">Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/03f6a7750bb5e5af61.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system\">Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e4e859ffb925a154ba.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-create-indigo-springhare-in-the-same-mod-each-species-has-its-own-art-needs-and-price\">Create Indigo Springhare in the same mod. Each species has its own art, needs and price.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0ad9cad50af011b45b.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-for-indigo-springhare-select-grid-frames-across-directions-down-enter-4-columns-5-rows-nneeses-fit-to-42-pixels-with-120-ms-frames\">For Indigo Springhare, select Grid: frames across, directions down. Enter 4 columns, 5 rows, N,NE,E,SE,S; fit to 42 pixels with 120 ms frames.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/cba26b12c9c05967c8.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-the-four-imported-frames-and-all-five-directions-at-game-size-save-the-finished-conversion\">Check the four imported frames and all five directions at game size. Save the finished conversion.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/91d6dfacbbad7f9d16.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-pictures-use-an-animation-frame-pick-the-drawn-walk-animation-se-frame-1-and-icon-list-picture-and-plaque-the-lab-sizes-and-compiles-them\">Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/22cab71528f3be70f2.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-verified-final-indigo-springhare-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values\">Verified final Indigo Springhare art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/fc99d2fef2f7ad24cf.webp\" alt=\"Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"animal-imports\">Shared testing, normal game adoption and publishing instructions</a></p>","toc":[{"id":"choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio","title":"Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.","level":2},{"id":"make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system","title":"Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.","level":2},{"id":"create-indigo-springhare-in-the-same-mod-each-species-has-its-own-art-needs-and-price","title":"Create Indigo Springhare in the same mod. Each species has its own art, needs and price.","level":2},{"id":"for-indigo-springhare-select-grid-frames-across-directions-down-enter-4-columns-5-rows-nneeses-fit-to-42-pixels-with-120-ms-frames","title":"For Indigo Springhare, select Grid: frames across, directions down. Enter 4 columns, 5 rows, N,NE,E,SE,S; fit to 42 pixels with 120 ms frames.","level":2},{"id":"check-the-four-imported-frames-and-all-five-directions-at-game-size-save-the-finished-conversion","title":"Check the four imported frames and all five directions at game size. Save the finished conversion.","level":2},{"id":"pictures-use-an-animation-frame-pick-the-drawn-walk-animation-se-frame-1-and-icon-list-picture-and-plaque-the-lab-sizes-and-compiles-them","title":"Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.","level":2},{"id":"verified-final-indigo-springhare-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values","title":"Verified final Indigo Springhare art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.","level":2}],"text":"# Indigo Springhare: a directional sprite sheet\n\nSource: source-assets/indigo-springhare-sheet.png\n\nCreate a zoo animal based on Thomson’s Gazelle. Draw walk → Blank → Import external art. Select Grid, columns 4, rows 5, row directions N,NE,E,SE,S, height 42 and 120 ms per frame. Preview every view and all four walking frames.\n\nSave compiles the game art. For this prototype, Link other poses reuses that art for the other adult states; wait for it to finish. Add its own food, habitat and group values in Needs and the Inspector. Pictures → Use an animation frame makes its icon, list picture and plaque.\n\n## Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.\n\n\n\n## Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.\n\n\n\n## Create Indigo Springhare in the same mod. Each species has its own art, needs and price.\n\n\n\n## For Indigo Springhare, select Grid: frames across, directions down. Enter 4 columns, 5 rows, N,NE,E,SE,S; fit to 42 pixels with 120 ms frames.\n\n\n\n## Check the four imported frames and all five directions at game size. Save the finished conversion.\n\n\n\n## Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.\n\n\n\n## Verified final Indigo Springhare art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.\n\n\n\n[Shared testing, normal game adoption and publishing instructions](help:animal-imports)\n","videos":[]},{"id":"obj-lesson","title":"OBJ lesson: Jade Shellback","summary":"Convert supplied model poses and materials to game art.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","html":"<p>Source: source-assets/jade-shellback-01.obj, source-assets/jade-shellback-02.obj and source-assets/jade-shellback.mtl</p>\n<p>Create a zoo animal based on Plains Zebra. Draw walk → Blank → Import external art. Select both numbered OBJ files and their MTL together. Use Y-up, front +Z, height 32 and 200 ms per supplied pose. The two meshes supply the leg poses; the importer does not rig or invent a walk.</p>\n<p>Save compiles the game art. For this prototype, Link other poses reuses that art for the other adult states; wait for it to finish. Add its own food, habitat and group values in Needs and the Inspector. Pictures → Use an animation frame makes its icon, list picture and plaque.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio\">Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/03f6a7750bb5e5af61.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system\">Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e4e859ffb925a154ba.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-select-both-numbered-obj-pose-files-and-their-mtl-together-choose-y-up-front-z-32-pixels-and-200-ms-per-supplied-pose-the-importer-renders-five-directional-views\">Select both numbered OBJ pose files and their MTL together. Choose Y-up, front +Z, 32 pixels and 200 ms per supplied pose. The importer renders five directional views.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/522a89181ffe37ddbd.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-jade-shellback-rendered-from-the-obj-meshes-two-supplied-poses-five-directions-mtl-colours-and-transparent-background-save-compiles-the-same-game-art-used-for-png-imports\">Jade Shellback rendered from the OBJ meshes: two supplied poses, five directions, MTL colours and transparent background. Save compiles the same game art used for PNG imports.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/30e3660ee72d782132.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-pictures-use-an-animation-frame-pick-the-drawn-walk-animation-se-frame-1-and-icon-list-picture-and-plaque-the-lab-sizes-and-compiles-them\">Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/22cab71528f3be70f2.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-three-picture-cards-now-show-jade-shellbacks-own-art-rather-than-the-base-animals-pictures-each-card-can-still-be-edited\">The three picture cards now show Jade Shellback’s own art rather than the base animal’s pictures. Each card can still be edited.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/a024a9c08886115302.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-verified-final-jade-shellback-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values\">Verified final Jade Shellback art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/2e32372f39603f16a0.webp\" alt=\"Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<p><a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"animal-imports\">Shared testing, normal game adoption and publishing instructions</a></p>","toc":[{"id":"choose-new-mod-enter-wildlife-import-tutorial-and-use-empty-build-it-in-the-studio","title":"Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.","level":2},{"id":"make-something-new-animal-choose-zoo-animal-this-selects-its-care-system","title":"Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.","level":2},{"id":"select-both-numbered-obj-pose-files-and-their-mtl-together-choose-y-up-front-z-32-pixels-and-200-ms-per-supplied-pose-the-importer-renders-five-directional-views","title":"Select both numbered OBJ pose files and their MTL together. Choose Y-up, front +Z, 32 pixels and 200 ms per supplied pose. The importer renders five directional views.","level":2},{"id":"jade-shellback-rendered-from-the-obj-meshes-two-supplied-poses-five-directions-mtl-colours-and-transparent-background-save-compiles-the-same-game-art-used-for-png-imports","title":"Jade Shellback rendered from the OBJ meshes: two supplied poses, five directions, MTL colours and transparent background. Save compiles the same game art used for PNG imports.","level":2},{"id":"pictures-use-an-animation-frame-pick-the-drawn-walk-animation-se-frame-1-and-icon-list-picture-and-plaque-the-lab-sizes-and-compiles-them","title":"Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.","level":2},{"id":"the-three-picture-cards-now-show-jade-shellbacks-own-art-rather-than-the-base-animals-pictures-each-card-can-still-be-edited","title":"The three picture cards now show Jade Shellback’s own art rather than the base animal’s pictures. Each card can still be edited.","level":2},{"id":"verified-final-jade-shellback-art-in-the-engine-with-its-completed-habitat-and-welfare-values","title":"Verified final Jade Shellback art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.","level":2}],"text":"# Jade Shellback: OBJ poses into game frames\n\nSource: source-assets/jade-shellback-01.obj, source-assets/jade-shellback-02.obj and source-assets/jade-shellback.mtl\n\nCreate a zoo animal based on Plains Zebra. Draw walk → Blank → Import external art. Select both numbered OBJ files and their MTL together. Use Y-up, front +Z, height 32 and 200 ms per supplied pose. The two meshes supply the leg poses; the importer does not rig or invent a walk.\n\nSave compiles the game art. For this prototype, Link other poses reuses that art for the other adult states; wait for it to finish. Add its own food, habitat and group values in Needs and the Inspector. Pictures → Use an animation frame makes its icon, list picture and plaque.\n\n## Choose New mod, enter Wildlife Import Tutorial, and use Empty: build it in the studio.\n\n\n\n## Make something → New animal. Choose Zoo animal; this selects its care system.\n\n\n\n## Select both numbered OBJ pose files and their MTL together. Choose Y-up, front +Z, 32 pixels and 200 ms per supplied pose. The importer renders five directional views.\n\n\n\n## Jade Shellback rendered from the OBJ meshes: two supplied poses, five directions, MTL colours and transparent background. Save compiles the same game art used for PNG imports.\n\n\n\n## Pictures → Use an animation frame. Pick the drawn walk animation, SE, frame 1, and Icon, list picture and plaque. The Lab sizes and compiles them.\n\n\n\n## The three picture cards now show Jade Shellback’s own art rather than the base animal’s pictures. Each card can still be edited.\n\n\n\n## Verified final Jade Shellback art in the engine, with its completed habitat and welfare values.\n\n\n\n[Shared testing, normal game adoption and publishing instructions](help:animal-imports)\n","videos":[]},{"id":"aquatic-tutorial","title":"Swimming and marine animals","summary":"Hippo land and water behavior, dolphin tanks and appropriate care.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/6527bb4c32650eccc8.mp4","title":"Recorded tutorial checkpoints","caption":"Captioned native screenshots. This is a checkpoint walkthrough, not a continuous recording.","src":"help/media/6527bb4c32650eccc8.mp4"},{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/50528d5a315111a6f1.mp4","title":"Actual gameplay motion","caption":"Native gameplay clip from the test run.","src":"help/media/50528d5a315111a6f1.mp4"}],"html":"<p>Recorded 26 October 2026. This extends the three external-import lessons with two water cases in Wildlife Import Tutorial. These are custom variants made with the studio&#x27;s Remake and Recolour controls, using the game&#x27;s hippo and dolphin animations. They are not two additional original PNG/OBJ animals or complete new animation libraries. Only the main swimming animation is recoloured; inherited land, resting, surface and other behavior art can retain its original appearance.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variant</th>\n<th>Kind and base</th>\n<th>Saved custom properties</th>\n<th>Main custom art</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Copperbank Hippo (copperba)</td>\n<td>Zoo animal, Hippopotamus</td>\n<td>Price 1350; hunger increment 7; group minimum 2</td>\n<td>swim: 14 frames, five directions, copper recolour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Azure Reef Dolphin (azureree)</td>\n<td>Marine animal, Bottlenose Dolphin</td>\n<td>Price 1450; hunger increment 5; preferred depth 3–6; minimum adoption depth 2; marine specialist 9552</td>\n<td>walk: 10 underwater swim frames, five directions, azure recolour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-create-the-water-variants\">Create the water variants</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the tutorial mod. Wait for Engine running and the base-species list to load. New animal → Zoo animal → Copperbank Hippo → Hippopotamus. Confirm the selected preset before Make it. If the marine list shows only Plains Zebra, cancel and let the catalog reconnect; do not continue with that fallback.</li>\n<li>In Animations, Draw beside swim. In the stencil browser choose Remake. This copies the real animation in every direction and frame. Recolour from #888080 toward #b96d37 with closeness 45, then Save. Preserve the separate land, water-idle, surface, submerged and shadow slots.</li>\n<li>Change the price and hunger increment in the inspector. Let each save finish before changing another field.</li>\n<li>New animal → Marine animal → Azure Reef Dolphin → Bottlenose Dolphin. Draw beside walk → Remake. Recolour from #808080 toward #268fbe, closeness 60, then Save. Set the custom price, hunger increment and preferred depth range.</li>\n<li>Review Needs: this dolphin uses a Marine Specialist, fish, salt water and marine behavior. The hippo uses a Zookeeper, grass chow and a mixture of fresh water, savannah and dirt. Swims and Lives under water are different properties; use a suitable base rather than changing only the kind label.</li>\n<li>The dolphin preset&#x27;s shadowidle link points at a missing source path. Use Link beside shadowidle and replace it with animals/dolphin/m/ssubswim/ssubswim. The engine already has an alias for the stock species; this explicit link also lets the creator&#x27;s check resolve the mod. Keep underwater shadows and surface underlays aligned with the corresponding animation frame counts.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-build-and-test-their-homes\">Build and test their homes</h2>\n<p>Use Test Zoo on a flat map and pause while building. Make a chain-link exhibit with fresh water and a dry bank. The hippo prefers about 70% fresh water, 15% savannah and 15% dirt. Water reeds are placed on land beside the pond, not on water tiles. Keepers are placed outside the enclosure and enter through its gate.</p>\n<p>Make a separate completed rectangle of tank walls. A completed tank fills with salt water. Add a filter on an adjacent outside tile, kelp and ocean floor rocks inside, and a marine specialist outside. In this run the tank had 288 tiles. Wall height and water depth are not interchangeable after adjustment: a five-unit wall gave four units of water depth.</p>\n<p>Place two hippos, one initially on land and one in fresh water, and a male and female dolphin in the tank. Run naturally and follow the animals. Do not treat a forced animation preview as proof of natural swimming. The land-spawned hippo entered water within the first minute and was back on land by three minutes. The dolphin trace selected surface and submerged targets between two and four height units.</p>\n<p>For feeding stress, set Hunger to 85 in the animal inspector and let the normal carers and behaviors run. The zookeeper supplied grass chow and the marine specialist supplied fish. A hippo briefly reached hunger 100 before recovering, so do not stop after the first measurement. Actual mouthful steps are saved in feeding-steps.json.</p>\n<p>The following are deliberate Test Zoo stress controls, not steps for authoring an animal or ordinary player requirements. The exhibit number is required:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>dirty 2 30: set tank 2 purity to 30. After ten simulated seconds its running filter raised purity to 40, suitability fell from 73 to 64, and the animal requested cleaner water.</li>\n<li>fill 2 fresh: the salt-water dolphin&#x27;s suitability fell to 23 and it requested different salinity. Restore with fill 2 salt.</li>\n<li>tankwalls 2 2 then fill 2 salt: actual water depth is one. A new dolphin was rejected with the deeper-tank message. Restore tankwalls 2 5 and fill 2 salt.</li>\n<li>Try a dolphin in the dry land exhibit: the game rejects it and requests a tank with water.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Restore good conditions after negative checks. Final sampled hippos had suitability 78 and happiness 100; the dolphins had suitability 72 and happiness 95–100. No sampled animal was sick or escaped. These are functional fixtures with remaining habitat recommendations, not perfectly balanced finished exhibits.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-verify-in-ordinary-play\">Verify in ordinary Play</h2>\n<p>Save as a map → Aquatic Mod Test → Into Wildlife Import Tutorial. This saves the habitat design; it does not carry the live animals and staff into a new freeform game. In Play, choose Play Freeform Game, scroll to Aquatic Mod Test and start it. Open Creatures, select Mods, select the animal icon, then select the male or female button before clicking the habitat. Selecting an icon alone only changes the information panel.</p>\n<p>Copperbank Hippo cost 1350 and Azure Reef Dolphin cost 1450 in the normal adoption panel. Both sexes were adopted into the appropriate homes. Native screenshots show the copper swim art and blue underwater dolphin art. Other poses still use inherited art. Native-swimming.mp4 is actual game capture at two frames per second, silent; native-frame-times.json records capture times. It plays for sixteen seconds and is a low-frame-rate evidence clip.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-what-passed-and-what-remains\">What passed, and what remains</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom zoo swimmer</td>\n<td>Natural entry, swimming/water idle, exit and land behavior observed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom marine mod</td>\n<td>Accepted in tank, routes within tank, surface/submerged targets observed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrong habitat</td>\n<td>Land placement and insufficient depth rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom needs</td>\n<td>Correct prices, hunger rates, carer and food loaded; salinity and purity changed suitability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filtering</td>\n<td>Running filter increased purity 30 → 40 over ten seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feeding</td>\n<td>Normal carers supplied food; mouthful events and hunger recovery observed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary Play</td>\n<td>Habitat map loaded and both sexes of both variants adopted and rendered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local package rules</td>\n<td>Passed: 19 files, 2.2 MB including authoring/work files; builder Problems 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hippo engine diagnostics</td>\n<td>0 problems, 147 animations, 85 behavior sets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dolphin engine diagnostics</td>\n<td>10 warnings remain, documented below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The dolphin retains six unsupported fendtrick steps and an undefined stand in bcgland, both present in the stock dolphin. Its surface_hoop_jump has 20 frames against a 26-frame shadow: stock adults have the same mismatch. The creator&#x27;s young fallback also uses that adult mapping, producing a third mismatch. The stock diagnostic had 23 missing young-art paths; the new variant&#x27;s inherited adult fallback has none. The marine mod therefore passes basic movement, placement and care tests but is not certified for shows, hoop tricks, juvenile visuals, breeding balance or every Marine Mania behavior.</p>\n<p>The raw Lab recording includes retakes and waits and does not fully capture the embedded native game. Separate native screenshots and the native clip supply that evidence. Failed form selectors, an unloaded preset list and the first malformed dirty command are retained in steps.json with verified:false. Some scene placements failed and were corrected; only successful placements are used for conclusions. A cached studio map/file count needed a reload; the map itself was already present in ordinary Play. No engine source changes or builds were needed for these cases. No public upload, commit or push was performed.</p>\n<p>See report.md for the compact results, events.json for logged checks, steps.json for original checkpoint times, and walkthrough.mp4 for the captioned tutorial.</p>","toc":[{"id":"create-the-water-variants","title":"Create the water variants","level":2},{"id":"build-and-test-their-homes","title":"Build and test their homes","level":2},{"id":"verify-in-ordinary-play","title":"Verify in ordinary Play","level":2},{"id":"what-passed-and-what-remains","title":"What passed, and what remains","level":2}],"text":"# Swimming and marine mod tutorial\n\nRecorded 26 October 2026. This extends the three external-import lessons with two water cases in Wildlife Import Tutorial. These are custom variants made with the studio's Remake and Recolour controls, using the game's hippo and dolphin animations. They are not two additional original PNG/OBJ animals or complete new animation libraries. Only the main swimming animation is recoloured; inherited land, resting, surface and other behavior art can retain its original appearance.\n\n| Variant | Kind and base | Saved custom properties | Main custom art |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Copperbank Hippo (copperba) | Zoo animal, Hippopotamus | Price 1350; hunger increment 7; group minimum 2 | swim: 14 frames, five directions, copper recolour |\n| Azure Reef Dolphin (azureree) | Marine animal, Bottlenose Dolphin | Price 1450; hunger increment 5; preferred depth 3–6; minimum adoption depth 2; marine specialist 9552 | walk: 10 underwater swim frames, five directions, azure recolour |\n\n## Create the water variants\n\n1. Open the tutorial mod. Wait for Engine running and the base-species list to load. New animal → Zoo animal → Copperbank Hippo → Hippopotamus. Confirm the selected preset before Make it. If the marine list shows only Plains Zebra, cancel and let the catalog reconnect; do not continue with that fallback.\n2. In Animations, Draw beside swim. In the stencil browser choose Remake. This copies the real animation in every direction and frame. Recolour from #888080 toward #b96d37 with closeness 45, then Save. Preserve the separate land, water-idle, surface, submerged and shadow slots.\n3. Change the price and hunger increment in the inspector. Let each save finish before changing another field.\n4. New animal → Marine animal → Azure Reef Dolphin → Bottlenose Dolphin. Draw beside walk → Remake. Recolour from #808080 toward #268fbe, closeness 60, then Save. Set the custom price, hunger increment and preferred depth range.\n5. Review Needs: this dolphin uses a Marine Specialist, fish, salt water and marine behavior. The hippo uses a Zookeeper, grass chow and a mixture of fresh water, savannah and dirt. Swims and Lives under water are different properties; use a suitable base rather than changing only the kind label.\n6. The dolphin preset's shadowidle link points at a missing source path. Use Link beside shadowidle and replace it with animals/dolphin/m/ssubswim/ssubswim. The engine already has an alias for the stock species; this explicit link also lets the creator's check resolve the mod. Keep underwater shadows and surface underlays aligned with the corresponding animation frame counts.\n\n## Build and test their homes\n\nUse Test Zoo on a flat map and pause while building. Make a chain-link exhibit with fresh water and a dry bank. The hippo prefers about 70% fresh water, 15% savannah and 15% dirt. Water reeds are placed on land beside the pond, not on water tiles. Keepers are placed outside the enclosure and enter through its gate.\n\nMake a separate completed rectangle of tank walls. A completed tank fills with salt water. Add a filter on an adjacent outside tile, kelp and ocean floor rocks inside, and a marine specialist outside. In this run the tank had 288 tiles. Wall height and water depth are not interchangeable after adjustment: a five-unit wall gave four units of water depth.\n\nPlace two hippos, one initially on land and one in fresh water, and a male and female dolphin in the tank. Run naturally and follow the animals. Do not treat a forced animation preview as proof of natural swimming. The land-spawned hippo entered water within the first minute and was back on land by three minutes. The dolphin trace selected surface and submerged targets between two and four height units.\n\nFor feeding stress, set Hunger to 85 in the animal inspector and let the normal carers and behaviors run. The zookeeper supplied grass chow and the marine specialist supplied fish. A hippo briefly reached hunger 100 before recovering, so do not stop after the first measurement. Actual mouthful steps are saved in feeding-steps.json.\n\nThe following are deliberate Test Zoo stress controls, not steps for authoring an animal or ordinary player requirements. The exhibit number is required:\n\n- dirty 2 30: set tank 2 purity to 30. After ten simulated seconds its running filter raised purity to 40, suitability fell from 73 to 64, and the animal requested cleaner water.\n- fill 2 fresh: the salt-water dolphin's suitability fell to 23 and it requested different salinity. Restore with fill 2 salt.\n- tankwalls 2 2 then fill 2 salt: actual water depth is one. A new dolphin was rejected with the deeper-tank message. Restore tankwalls 2 5 and fill 2 salt.\n- Try a dolphin in the dry land exhibit: the game rejects it and requests a tank with water.\n\nRestore good conditions after negative checks. Final sampled hippos had suitability 78 and happiness 100; the dolphins had suitability 72 and happiness 95–100. No sampled animal was sick or escaped. These are functional fixtures with remaining habitat recommendations, not perfectly balanced finished exhibits.\n\n## Verify in ordinary Play\n\nSave as a map → Aquatic Mod Test → Into Wildlife Import Tutorial. This saves the habitat design; it does not carry the live animals and staff into a new freeform game. In Play, choose Play Freeform Game, scroll to Aquatic Mod Test and start it. Open Creatures, select Mods, select the animal icon, then select the male or female button before clicking the habitat. Selecting an icon alone only changes the information panel.\n\nCopperbank Hippo cost 1350 and Azure Reef Dolphin cost 1450 in the normal adoption panel. Both sexes were adopted into the appropriate homes. Native screenshots show the copper swim art and blue underwater dolphin art. Other poses still use inherited art. Native-swimming.mp4 is actual game capture at two frames per second, silent; native-frame-times.json records capture times. It plays for sixteen seconds and is a low-frame-rate evidence clip.\n\n## What passed, and what remains\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Custom zoo swimmer | Natural entry, swimming/water idle, exit and land behavior observed |\n| Custom marine mod | Accepted in tank, routes within tank, surface/submerged targets observed |\n| Wrong habitat | Land placement and insufficient depth rejected |\n| Custom needs | Correct prices, hunger rates, carer and food loaded; salinity and purity changed suitability |\n| Filtering | Running filter increased purity 30 → 40 over ten seconds |\n| Feeding | Normal carers supplied food; mouthful events and hunger recovery observed |\n| Ordinary Play | Habitat map loaded and both sexes of both variants adopted and rendered |\n| Local package rules | Passed: 19 files, 2.2 MB including authoring/work files; builder Problems 0 |\n| Hippo engine diagnostics | 0 problems, 147 animations, 85 behavior sets |\n| Dolphin engine diagnostics | 10 warnings remain, documented below |\n\nThe dolphin retains six unsupported fendtrick steps and an undefined stand in bcgland, both present in the stock dolphin. Its surface_hoop_jump has 20 frames against a 26-frame shadow: stock adults have the same mismatch. The creator's young fallback also uses that adult mapping, producing a third mismatch. The stock diagnostic had 23 missing young-art paths; the new variant's inherited adult fallback has none. The marine mod therefore passes basic movement, placement and care tests but is not certified for shows, hoop tricks, juvenile visuals, breeding balance or every Marine Mania behavior.\n\nThe raw Lab recording includes retakes and waits and does not fully capture the embedded native game. Separate native screenshots and the native clip supply that evidence. Failed form selectors, an unloaded preset list and the first malformed dirty command are retained in steps.json with verified:false. Some scene placements failed and were corrected; only successful placements are used for conclusions. A cached studio map/file count needed a reload; the map itself was already present in ordinary Play. No engine source changes or builds were needed for these cases. No public upload, commit or push was performed.\n\nSee report.md for the compact results, events.json for logged checks, steps.json for original checkpoint times, and walkthrough.mp4 for the captioned tutorial.\n"},{"id":"park-tutorial","title":"Buildings, fences, people and staff","summary":"Original models and remakes, guest appearances and working filters.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/d65b1d0296a625ed5d.mp4","title":"Recorded tutorial checkpoints","caption":"Captioned native screenshots. This is a checkpoint walkthrough, not a continuous recording.","src":"help/media/d65b1d0296a625ed5d.mp4"},{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/1257810e10df0d02cc.mp4","title":"Actual gameplay motion","caption":"Native gameplay clip from the test run.","src":"help/media/1257810e10df0d02cc.mp4"}],"html":"<p>This follows the new ZT1 Lab studio tested on 26 October 2026. The supplied\nPark Workshop Tutorial mod has ten creations and the Workshop Garden freeform\nmap. Use the updated engine from this checkout; older released builds lack the\nitem builder and independent filter support added during these tests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-the-workflow\">Choose the workflow</h2>\n<p>In Mods, select a mod or create an empty one. Choose <strong>Make something → Build\nan item</strong>. Pick the category and then one of these workflows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Create a separate item</strong> gives a building, fence, staff member or filter a\n  new definition and its own name. Pick a working role to inherit its behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Customize an existing item</strong> keeps the source definition&#x27;s path. Your copy\n  replaces it while this mod is enabled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>People use <strong>Guest appearance</strong>. The game has man, woman, boy and girl kinds.\nAn original drawing for man still replaces that appearance; it does not add a\nnew guest role. Staff also inherit a role, such as keeper or maintenance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-original-art-through-the-real-importer\">Original art through the real importer</h2>\n<p>The <code>source-assets</code> folder contains newly constructed OBJ models and their\nshared <code>park-models.mtl</code>. The models were imported using the studio&#x27;s Windows\nfile picker. No compiled game art or editor project was inserted to bypass it.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <strong>Cedar Snack Kiosk</strong>, Building, separate item, with Burger Stand as\n   the working service role.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>idle → Draw → Blank</strong>, then the editor&#x27;s Import icon.</li>\n<li>In the Windows picker, select <code>cedar-snack-kiosk.obj</code> and\n   <code>park-models.mtl</code> together. Include texture images too when your model uses\n   them. This particular test uses material colours, not image textures.</li>\n<li>Set the rendered height to <strong>65 game pixels</strong>, inspect the generated views,\n   then convert and <strong>Save</strong>. The importer renders eight item directions.</li>\n<li>In the Inspector, set construction price <strong>375</strong> and guest price <strong>4.25</strong>.</li>\n<li>Link <strong>used</strong> to <code>objects/cedarsnackkiosk/base/idle/idle</code> for this static\n   prototype. A customer now keeps the original kiosk drawing on screen.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Repeat with these inputs:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Category / inherited role</th>\n<th>Files</th>\n<th>Height</th>\n<th>Sequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>River Rail</td>\n<td>Fence / Chain-link</td>\n<td>river-rail.obj + MTL</td>\n<td>36</td>\n<td>One pose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lagoon Keeper</td>\n<td>Staff / Zoo Keeper</td>\n<td>lagoon-keeper-01.obj, -02.obj + MTL</td>\n<td>42</td>\n<td>Two poses, 180 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explorer Guest</td>\n<td>Guest appearance / man</td>\n<td>explorer-guest-01.obj, -02.obj + MTL</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>Two poses, 180 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reef Pump</td>\n<td>Tank filter / Filter</td>\n<td>reef-pump-01.obj, -02.obj + MTL</td>\n<td>45</td>\n<td>Two poses, 180 ms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Set River Rail&#x27;s price to <strong>95</strong> and strength to <strong>350</strong>. Set Lagoon Keeper&#x27;s\nhiring/monthly pay to <strong>925</strong> and walking rate to <strong>38</strong>. Its male work slots\nreuse the imported walk as a prototype. Explorer Guest reuses its walk for a\nsmall set of common poses. Inspect every slot: other poses, female staff art,\nfence gates and damage states still link to the game.</p>\n<p>A sequence consists of already exported poses. The importer does not rig a\nstatic mesh or invent swimming, walking or work movements. PNG and sprite-sheet\nimport were covered in the companion animal tutorial; these five item imports\nspecifically exercised OBJ plus MTL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-customize-what-is-already-there\">Customize what is already there</h2>\n<p>Create <strong>Copper Lemonade Stand</strong>, Building, Customize existing, selecting\n<code>scenery/building/drkstnd.ai</code>. Use the actual catalog choice; a guessed\n<code>lemstand.ai</code> path is not the source used here.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>idle → Remake</strong>. The corrected importer includes the complete static\n   building and the animated vendor, with its default palette filled.</li>\n<li><strong>Recolour</strong> roof shades around <code>#c39a30</code> to <code>#b87333</code>, closeness <strong>30</strong>.</li>\n<li>Save, then remake and recolour <strong>used</strong> the same way. Test service states as\n   well as idle, so the look does not change while a guest is using it.</li>\n<li>Set construction price <strong>285</strong> and guest price <strong>3.75</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Repeat the customization workflow for:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Edited drawing</th>\n<th>Recolour / properties</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Copper Chainlink</td>\n<td>fences/chainlnk.ai</td>\n<td>f / idle</td>\n<td>#808080 → #bd7440, closeness 55; price 85, strength 275</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copper Maintenance</td>\n<td>staff/maint.ai</td>\n<td>m / walk</td>\n<td>#808080 → #bd7440, closeness 55; pay 425, rate 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Violet Guest</td>\n<td>Guest appearance / woman</td>\n<td>walk</td>\n<td>#a08080 → #8851ad, closeness 55</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cobalt Filter</td>\n<td>scenery/other/filter1.ai</td>\n<td>idle</td>\n<td>#808080 → #326aaf, closeness 55; price 240</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These remakes retain the source&#x27;s supplied views and frames. The editor now\nstarts on a direction containing art when a source has diagonal views only.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-give-the-filters-different-behavior\">Give the filters different behavior</h2>\n<p>Use each filter&#x27;s Inspector, not a shared stock-filter setting:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>Reef Pump</th>\n<th>Cobalt Filter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction cost</td>\n<td>320</td>\n<td>240</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seconds between cleanings</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cleaning while healthy</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cleaning while worn</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost per cleaning</td>\n<td>35</td>\n<td>30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Starting health / worn threshold</td>\n<td>10 / 5</td>\n<td>10 / 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seconds per health lost</td>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>200</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The original model is used for Reef Pump&#x27;s running state. Its off, worn and\nbroken art remains linked to stock states. Cobalt&#x27;s non-idle states also remain\nlinked. These are functional prototypes, not complete original animation sets.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-test-the-ecosystem-and-normal-game\">Test the ecosystem and normal game</h2>\n<p><strong>Test in the zoo</strong> enables the mod and reloads the engine. Workshop Garden\ncontains two 10 × 10 land enclosures, gates, two tanks, the buildings and paths.\nPlace each filter <strong>outside and adjacent to</strong> a completed tank; the current\ngame supports one filter per tank. Give keepers gate access and connect each\nservice building&#x27;s door to paths. A path that stops at a zoo boundary does not\nconnect guests to the rest of the zoo.</p>\n<p>The recorded tests dirtied both tanks equally. At 31 seconds, Reef was 47 and\nCobalt 29: both lost one purity point naturally, while Reef added 18. At 41\nseconds, Cobalt reached 41 after its +12 cycle. At worn health, the corresponding\nadditions were 8 and 6. Broken filters did not clean; draining tanks turned them\noff. Copper Maintenance restored both filters and performed fence fixing.\nLagoon Keeper visited its gated exhibit and supplied food.</p>\n<p>Guests bought from both buildings. The Explorer man consumed a hamburger and\nhunger became zero on the next status tick. The Violet woman consumed a drink\nand thirst became zero. Needs apply on a tick, so do not sample only the instant\nafter a transaction. Other inherited guest needs still matter.</p>\n<p>Use <strong>Save as a map</strong> to preserve the fixture. Map designs keep terrain,\nobjects, fences and filters, but omit live animals, guests and staff. Adopt and\nhire them again when starting the freeform map in normal Play.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Play with it</strong>, choose Workshop Garden from the freeform list. The normal\nbuy/hire panels list the new names and prices. Their <strong>Mods</strong> filter helps find\nthem. Staff were hired, the original kiosk purchased and placed, and a zebra\nadopted into River Rail through these ordinary game panels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-check-install-and-share\">Check, install and share</h2>\n<p><strong>About this mod → From the Test Zoo view</strong> sets its preview. <strong>Publish → Check\nit</strong> runs local package rules; it does not upload. This pack passed the local\nrules and had Problems 0, with an author metadata hint still to complete before\npublic upload. Nothing was publicly uploaded during this session.</p>\n<p>Install <code>Park-Workshop-Tutorial.zip</code> through the game&#x27;s <strong>Mod Menu → Add a\nmod…</strong>, switch it on and restart. In Lab Play, stop and start Play to reload it.\nKeep <code>Workshop-Authoring-Sources.zip</code> separately for the models and editable\nprojects; authoring files are deliberately excluded from the installable ZIP.</p>\n<p>Disable <strong>Lemonade Stands</strong> while testing this replacement stand: it changes the\nsame <code>drkstnd.ai</code> file and takes precedence when both are enabled. The test\nsession leaves Park Workshop Tutorial on and Lemonade Stands off for inspection.\nOther existing mod switches were retained.</p>\n<p>The captioned video presents corrected checkpoints. <code>creator-process.mp4</code> is\nthe raw Lab session, including repairs and waits. It does not reliably include\nnative picker windows or the embedded game&#x27;s child window; actual game images\nand <code>gameplay-motion.mp4</code> cover normal Play. Failed attempts remain in the raw\nevidence, labelled in the test report and check log.</p>","toc":[{"id":"choose-the-workflow","title":"Choose the workflow","level":2},{"id":"original-art-through-the-real-importer","title":"Original art through the real importer","level":2},{"id":"customize-what-is-already-there","title":"Customize what is already there","level":2},{"id":"give-the-filters-different-behavior","title":"Give the filters different behavior","level":2},{"id":"test-the-ecosystem-and-normal-game","title":"Test the ecosystem and normal game","level":2},{"id":"check-install-and-share","title":"Check, install and share","level":2}],"text":"# Buildings, fences, people, staff and tank objects\n\nThis follows the new ZT1 Lab studio tested on 26 October 2026. The supplied\nPark Workshop Tutorial mod has ten creations and the Workshop Garden freeform\nmap. Use the updated engine from this checkout; older released builds lack the\nitem builder and independent filter support added during these tests.\n\n## Choose the workflow\n\nIn Mods, select a mod or create an empty one. Choose **Make something → Build\nan item**. Pick the category and then one of these workflows:\n\n- **Create a separate item** gives a building, fence, staff member or filter a\n  new definition and its own name. Pick a working role to inherit its behavior.\n- **Customize an existing item** keeps the source definition's path. Your copy\n  replaces it while this mod is enabled.\n\nPeople use **Guest appearance**. The game has man, woman, boy and girl kinds.\nAn original drawing for man still replaces that appearance; it does not add a\nnew guest role. Staff also inherit a role, such as keeper or maintenance.\n\n## Original art through the real importer\n\nThe `source-assets` folder contains newly constructed OBJ models and their\nshared `park-models.mtl`. The models were imported using the studio's Windows\nfile picker. No compiled game art or editor project was inserted to bypass it.\n\n1. Create **Cedar Snack Kiosk**, Building, separate item, with Burger Stand as\n   the working service role.\n2. Open **idle → Draw → Blank**, then the editor's Import icon.\n3. In the Windows picker, select `cedar-snack-kiosk.obj` and\n   `park-models.mtl` together. Include texture images too when your model uses\n   them. This particular test uses material colours, not image textures.\n4. Set the rendered height to **65 game pixels**, inspect the generated views,\n   then convert and **Save**. The importer renders eight item directions.\n5. In the Inspector, set construction price **375** and guest price **4.25**.\n6. Link **used** to `objects/cedarsnackkiosk/base/idle/idle` for this static\n   prototype. A customer now keeps the original kiosk drawing on screen.\n\nRepeat with these inputs:\n\n| Item | Category / inherited role | Files | Height | Sequence |\n|---|---|---|---:|---|\n| River Rail | Fence / Chain-link | river-rail.obj + MTL | 36 | One pose |\n| Lagoon Keeper | Staff / Zoo Keeper | lagoon-keeper-01.obj, -02.obj + MTL | 42 | Two poses, 180 ms |\n| Explorer Guest | Guest appearance / man | explorer-guest-01.obj, -02.obj + MTL | 40 | Two poses, 180 ms |\n| Reef Pump | Tank filter / Filter | reef-pump-01.obj, -02.obj + MTL | 45 | Two poses, 180 ms |\n\nSet River Rail's price to **95** and strength to **350**. Set Lagoon Keeper's\nhiring/monthly pay to **925** and walking rate to **38**. Its male work slots\nreuse the imported walk as a prototype. Explorer Guest reuses its walk for a\nsmall set of common poses. Inspect every slot: other poses, female staff art,\nfence gates and damage states still link to the game.\n\nA sequence consists of already exported poses. The importer does not rig a\nstatic mesh or invent swimming, walking or work movements. PNG and sprite-sheet\nimport were covered in the companion animal tutorial; these five item imports\nspecifically exercised OBJ plus MTL.\n\n## Customize what is already there\n\nCreate **Copper Lemonade Stand**, Building, Customize existing, selecting\n`scenery/building/drkstnd.ai`. Use the actual catalog choice; a guessed\n`lemstand.ai` path is not the source used here.\n\n1. Open **idle → Remake**. The corrected importer includes the complete static\n   building and the animated vendor, with its default palette filled.\n2. **Recolour** roof shades around `#c39a30` to `#b87333`, closeness **30**.\n3. Save, then remake and recolour **used** the same way. Test service states as\n   well as idle, so the look does not change while a guest is using it.\n4. Set construction price **285** and guest price **3.75**.\n\nRepeat the customization workflow for:\n\n| Item | Source | Edited drawing | Recolour / properties |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Copper Chainlink | fences/chainlnk.ai | f / idle | #808080 → #bd7440, closeness 55; price 85, strength 275 |\n| Copper Maintenance | staff/maint.ai | m / walk | #808080 → #bd7440, closeness 55; pay 425, rate 40 |\n| Violet Guest | Guest appearance / woman | walk | #a08080 → #8851ad, closeness 55 |\n| Cobalt Filter | scenery/other/filter1.ai | idle | #808080 → #326aaf, closeness 55; price 240 |\n\nThese remakes retain the source's supplied views and frames. The editor now\nstarts on a direction containing art when a source has diagonal views only.\n\n## Give the filters different behavior\n\nUse each filter's Inspector, not a shared stock-filter setting:\n\n| Property | Reef Pump | Cobalt Filter |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Construction cost | 320 | 240 |\n| Seconds between cleanings | 30 | 40 |\n| Cleaning while healthy | 18 | 12 |\n| Cleaning while worn | 8 | 6 |\n| Cost per cleaning | 35 | 30 |\n| Starting health / worn threshold | 10 / 5 | 10 / 5 |\n| Seconds per health lost | 200 | 200 |\n\nThe original model is used for Reef Pump's running state. Its off, worn and\nbroken art remains linked to stock states. Cobalt's non-idle states also remain\nlinked. These are functional prototypes, not complete original animation sets.\n\n## Test the ecosystem and normal game\n\n**Test in the zoo** enables the mod and reloads the engine. Workshop Garden\ncontains two 10 × 10 land enclosures, gates, two tanks, the buildings and paths.\nPlace each filter **outside and adjacent to** a completed tank; the current\ngame supports one filter per tank. Give keepers gate access and connect each\nservice building's door to paths. A path that stops at a zoo boundary does not\nconnect guests to the rest of the zoo.\n\nThe recorded tests dirtied both tanks equally. At 31 seconds, Reef was 47 and\nCobalt 29: both lost one purity point naturally, while Reef added 18. At 41\nseconds, Cobalt reached 41 after its +12 cycle. At worn health, the corresponding\nadditions were 8 and 6. Broken filters did not clean; draining tanks turned them\noff. Copper Maintenance restored both filters and performed fence fixing.\nLagoon Keeper visited its gated exhibit and supplied food.\n\nGuests bought from both buildings. The Explorer man consumed a hamburger and\nhunger became zero on the next status tick. The Violet woman consumed a drink\nand thirst became zero. Needs apply on a tick, so do not sample only the instant\nafter a transaction. Other inherited guest needs still matter.\n\nUse **Save as a map** to preserve the fixture. Map designs keep terrain,\nobjects, fences and filters, but omit live animals, guests and staff. Adopt and\nhire them again when starting the freeform map in normal Play.\n\nIn **Play with it**, choose Workshop Garden from the freeform list. The normal\nbuy/hire panels list the new names and prices. Their **Mods** filter helps find\nthem. Staff were hired, the original kiosk purchased and placed, and a zebra\nadopted into River Rail through these ordinary game panels.\n\n## Check, install and share\n\n**About this mod → From the Test Zoo view** sets its preview. **Publish → Check\nit** runs local package rules; it does not upload. This pack passed the local\nrules and had Problems 0, with an author metadata hint still to complete before\npublic upload. Nothing was publicly uploaded during this session.\n\nInstall `Park-Workshop-Tutorial.zip` through the game's **Mod Menu → Add a\nmod…**, switch it on and restart. In Lab Play, stop and start Play to reload it.\nKeep `Workshop-Authoring-Sources.zip` separately for the models and editable\nprojects; authoring files are deliberately excluded from the installable ZIP.\n\nDisable **Lemonade Stands** while testing this replacement stand: it changes the\nsame `drkstnd.ai` file and takes precedence when both are enabled. The test\nsession leaves Park Workshop Tutorial on and Lemonade Stands off for inspection.\nOther existing mod switches were retained.\n\nThe captioned video presents corrected checkpoints. `creator-process.mp4` is\nthe raw Lab session, including repairs and waits. It does not reliably include\nnative picker windows or the embedded game's child window; actual game images\nand `gameplay-motion.mp4` cover normal Play. Failed attempts remain in the raw\nevidence, labelled in the test report and check log.\n"},{"id":"habitat-tutorial","title":"Habitat items, maps and theme packs","summary":"Gates, plants, rocks, shelters, paths, ground, tanks, food and dinosaur eggs.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/944edcf85fa6a78990.mp4","title":"Recorded tutorial checkpoints","caption":"Captioned native screenshots. This is a checkpoint walkthrough, not a continuous recording.","src":"help/media/944edcf85fa6a78990.mp4"}],"html":"<p>This records the new Mods menu workflow used on 26 October 2026. The source OBJ,\nMTL and PNG files were selected through native pickers, converted in the editor\nand saved through the normal compiler. Internal project JSON was not inserted to\ncreate the playable art. The source files and editable projects are supplied\nseparately from the installable mods.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-make-a-separate-item\">Make a separate item</h2>\n<p>Choose Mods → Make something → Build an item. Choose its role and the separate\nitem workflow, then give it a name and select the stock role and properties to\nstart from. This supplies behavior and working slots; it does not invent new AI.\nOpen a real animation row, choose Blank, then Import external art. Select OBJ and\nMTL together, set the height and preview all eight object directions. Wait for\nconversion, then Save. Check the actual slot names: a bin uses empty, half and\nfull, rather than idle. Link used states to suitable custom art when needed.</p>\n<p>Original imported examples: Fern Spire, Moonstone Rock, Cedar Refuge, Moonstone\nGate and fence body, Moonstone Entrance, Fern Feast, Cedar Enrichment, Moonstone\nBench and Moonstone Bin. Amberhorn uses two original body poses and separate egg,\nhatch and broken-shell meshes. Their simple art is intended to test the pipeline.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-customize-something-already-in-the-game\">Customize something already in the game</h2>\n<p>Select Customize an existing item. Open each relevant row with Remake, use\nRecolour across its frames and directions, and Save. The source item changes\nwhile the mod is on. Indigo Fern, Copper Granite, Clay Refuge, Brass Gate, Mint\nZoo Wall, Amber Greens and Amber Play Log demonstrate this route. Pearl Glass\nTank is a separate tank identity with remade stock layers; Copper Tank replaces\nan existing tank wall. Neither tank is an entirely original geometry set.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-paths-ground-needs-and-eggs\">Paths, ground, needs and eggs</h2>\n<p>Import the twenty-shape path strip as twenty horizontal cells with whole-canvas\nplacement. Moonstone Path contains its numbered connection and ramp resources.\nMint Brick Path remakes the stock twenty shapes. Import Violet Meadow as a square\nopaque texture; Moss Floor remakes another texture. These reskin existing ground\ntypes, retaining their terrain IDs and gameplay properties.</p>\n<p>In Amberhorn → Needs, choose Fern Feast as custom food, a scientist as carer,\nand the desired ground and object likes. Enable the mod to populate custom food\nchoices. Its meadow, water, foliage, rock, shelter and enrichment preferences were\nedited through this page; price, space, hatch time and other specifics use the\nInspector. Make egg_idle, egg_hatch and egg_break separately. Own copy… separates\na slot from shared linked art before editing it. Prototype body poses are reused\nfor other actions; this is not a complete animation set.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-build-and-check-a-map\">Build and check a map</h2>\n<p>Finish asset edits, then Test in the zoo and New map → My own size → 64 by 64.\nDraw the boundary and place an entrance, lay connected paths, enclosures, objects\nand tank walls. Complete and fill the marine tank, set its depth and salinity,\nthen put the filter beside it. Furniture must sit beside an accessible path;\norient the bench so its entry slots face the path. Save as a map into the mod,\nthen reopen it to verify the saved layout. Moonstone Habitat was confirmed as a\n64 by 64 blank-base design with five enclosures and fourteen placed objects.</p>\n<p>Adopt animals and hire carers for live testing. The map design deliberately\nomits live actors. Amberhorn hatched, reached suitability 81, received Fern Feast\nfrom the scientist and consumed it. Its rest behavior selected a custom enclosure\nobject. The original bench restored guest energy. The custom tank held a marine\nanimal and the Reef Pump raised purity from 30 to 47 over 35 simulated seconds.\nThese measurements do not certify a balanced zoo or every animal behavior.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-publish-a-theme-pack-as-one-mod\">Publish a theme pack as one mod</h2>\n<p>Open Publish → Build a theme pack…. Include Moonstone Habitat Kit, Park Workshop\nTutorial and Wildlife Import Tutorial. Enter a pack name, id, version and About,\nthen Build pack. On About, set the pack&#x27;s own image using Choose a picture… or\nFrom the Test Zoo view. Open Publish → Check it. Moonstone Zoo Theme passed with\nthree package files, six animals, twenty-nine other definition entries and three\nmaps. A public upload was not performed in this test.</p>\n<p>The pack is a snapshot. Keep the source mods and authoring projects to edit later;\nbuild a new snapshot after changes. The builder combines compatible mods,\ndeduplicates identical resources and reports differing files at the same path.\nMods with identity-specific settings currently stay separate. Use the pack&#x27;s\nordinary Upload button when you intend to publish that single listing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-retrieve-and-play\">Retrieve and play</h2>\n<p>Install Moonstone-Zoo-Theme.zip through Bring in a mod or the game&#x27;s Add a mod…\npicker. Switch the installed pack on and restart. Switch off its three components\nand any earlier copy of the same pack. In normal Play, select Moonstone Habitat\nfrom Freeform, then adopt or hire again. The actual ZIP import and map selection\nwere tested. Amberhorn was adopted for $650 and hatched using its original art.</p>\n<p>Containment failure: Amberhorn subsequently escaped the glass enclosure near\nthe wall. Its base species retains jumping and fence-related behavior. Do not\ntreat this map&#x27;s glass enclosures as certified dinosaur containment. Use suitable\nstrong dinosaur fencing and test the animal&#x27;s abilities and placement. The\nescape and consequent welfare/adoption-ban popup remain in the raw evidence.</p>\n<p>walkthrough.mp4 is a captioned sequence of actual checkpoints, not continuous\nscreen video. creator-process.mp4 is the raw Lab capture at about one frame per\nsecond, with repairs and waits retained; native dialogs and the embedded game\nare supplemented by separate actual screenshots. Browser rendering of the local\nHTML was unavailable; local references and media decoding were checked instead.</p>","toc":[{"id":"make-a-separate-item","title":"Make a separate item","level":2},{"id":"customize-something-already-in-the-game","title":"Customize something already in the game","level":2},{"id":"paths-ground-needs-and-eggs","title":"Paths, ground, needs and eggs","level":2},{"id":"build-and-check-a-map","title":"Build and check a map","level":2},{"id":"publish-a-theme-pack-as-one-mod","title":"Publish a theme pack as one mod","level":2},{"id":"retrieve-and-play","title":"Retrieve and play","level":2}],"text":"# Moonstone Habitat and theme packs\n\nThis records the new Mods menu workflow used on 26 October 2026. The source OBJ,\nMTL and PNG files were selected through native pickers, converted in the editor\nand saved through the normal compiler. Internal project JSON was not inserted to\ncreate the playable art. The source files and editable projects are supplied\nseparately from the installable mods.\n\n## Make a separate item\n\nChoose Mods → Make something → Build an item. Choose its role and the separate\nitem workflow, then give it a name and select the stock role and properties to\nstart from. This supplies behavior and working slots; it does not invent new AI.\nOpen a real animation row, choose Blank, then Import external art. Select OBJ and\nMTL together, set the height and preview all eight object directions. Wait for\nconversion, then Save. Check the actual slot names: a bin uses empty, half and\nfull, rather than idle. Link used states to suitable custom art when needed.\n\nOriginal imported examples: Fern Spire, Moonstone Rock, Cedar Refuge, Moonstone\nGate and fence body, Moonstone Entrance, Fern Feast, Cedar Enrichment, Moonstone\nBench and Moonstone Bin. Amberhorn uses two original body poses and separate egg,\nhatch and broken-shell meshes. Their simple art is intended to test the pipeline.\n\n## Customize something already in the game\n\nSelect Customize an existing item. Open each relevant row with Remake, use\nRecolour across its frames and directions, and Save. The source item changes\nwhile the mod is on. Indigo Fern, Copper Granite, Clay Refuge, Brass Gate, Mint\nZoo Wall, Amber Greens and Amber Play Log demonstrate this route. Pearl Glass\nTank is a separate tank identity with remade stock layers; Copper Tank replaces\nan existing tank wall. Neither tank is an entirely original geometry set.\n\n## Paths, ground, needs and eggs\n\nImport the twenty-shape path strip as twenty horizontal cells with whole-canvas\nplacement. Moonstone Path contains its numbered connection and ramp resources.\nMint Brick Path remakes the stock twenty shapes. Import Violet Meadow as a square\nopaque texture; Moss Floor remakes another texture. These reskin existing ground\ntypes, retaining their terrain IDs and gameplay properties.\n\nIn Amberhorn → Needs, choose Fern Feast as custom food, a scientist as carer,\nand the desired ground and object likes. Enable the mod to populate custom food\nchoices. Its meadow, water, foliage, rock, shelter and enrichment preferences were\nedited through this page; price, space, hatch time and other specifics use the\nInspector. Make egg_idle, egg_hatch and egg_break separately. Own copy… separates\na slot from shared linked art before editing it. Prototype body poses are reused\nfor other actions; this is not a complete animation set.\n\n## Build and check a map\n\nFinish asset edits, then Test in the zoo and New map → My own size → 64 by 64.\nDraw the boundary and place an entrance, lay connected paths, enclosures, objects\nand tank walls. Complete and fill the marine tank, set its depth and salinity,\nthen put the filter beside it. Furniture must sit beside an accessible path;\norient the bench so its entry slots face the path. Save as a map into the mod,\nthen reopen it to verify the saved layout. Moonstone Habitat was confirmed as a\n64 by 64 blank-base design with five enclosures and fourteen placed objects.\n\nAdopt animals and hire carers for live testing. The map design deliberately\nomits live actors. Amberhorn hatched, reached suitability 81, received Fern Feast\nfrom the scientist and consumed it. Its rest behavior selected a custom enclosure\nobject. The original bench restored guest energy. The custom tank held a marine\nanimal and the Reef Pump raised purity from 30 to 47 over 35 simulated seconds.\nThese measurements do not certify a balanced zoo or every animal behavior.\n\n## Publish a theme pack as one mod\n\nOpen Publish → Build a theme pack…. Include Moonstone Habitat Kit, Park Workshop\nTutorial and Wildlife Import Tutorial. Enter a pack name, id, version and About,\nthen Build pack. On About, set the pack's own image using Choose a picture… or\nFrom the Test Zoo view. Open Publish → Check it. Moonstone Zoo Theme passed with\nthree package files, six animals, twenty-nine other definition entries and three\nmaps. A public upload was not performed in this test.\n\nThe pack is a snapshot. Keep the source mods and authoring projects to edit later;\nbuild a new snapshot after changes. The builder combines compatible mods,\ndeduplicates identical resources and reports differing files at the same path.\nMods with identity-specific settings currently stay separate. Use the pack's\nordinary Upload button when you intend to publish that single listing.\n\n## Retrieve and play\n\nInstall Moonstone-Zoo-Theme.zip through Bring in a mod or the game's Add a mod…\npicker. Switch the installed pack on and restart. Switch off its three components\nand any earlier copy of the same pack. In normal Play, select Moonstone Habitat\nfrom Freeform, then adopt or hire again. The actual ZIP import and map selection\nwere tested. Amberhorn was adopted for $650 and hatched using its original art.\n\nContainment failure: Amberhorn subsequently escaped the glass enclosure near\nthe wall. Its base species retains jumping and fence-related behavior. Do not\ntreat this map's glass enclosures as certified dinosaur containment. Use suitable\nstrong dinosaur fencing and test the animal's abilities and placement. The\nescape and consequent welfare/adoption-ban popup remain in the raw evidence.\n\nwalkthrough.mp4 is a captioned sequence of actual checkpoints, not continuous\nscreen video. creator-process.mp4 is the raw Lab capture at about one frame per\nsecond, with repairs and waits retained; native dialogs and the embedded game\nare supplemented by separate actual screenshots. Browser rendering of the local\nHTML was unavailable; local references and media decoding were checked instead.\n"},{"id":"publication-tutorial","title":"Publishing and downloading: recorded run","summary":"Real upload, approval, download and ordinary-game checks, with retained failures.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"notice":"Recorded workflow from 26 October 2026. Publication state and version numbers in these screenshots are historical; use the current publishing and repository-picture guides for the refreshed releases. Prototype art and gameplay limits remain.","videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/08ecbc6f36d6a3cc6a.mp4","title":"Recorded tutorial checkpoints","caption":"Captioned native screenshots. This is a checkpoint walkthrough, not a continuous recording.","src":"help/media/08ecbc6f36d6a3cc6a.mp4","poster":"help/media/781c20ab9d78f797bc.webp"}],"html":"<p>26 October 2026. Actual Lab publish actions and native game downloads. The checkpoint video is a captioned sequence of real screenshots, not a continuous screen recording.</p>\n<p>Published: Wildlife Import Tutorial 1.0, Park Workshop Tutorial 1.0, Moonstone Habitat Kit 1.0, Moonstone Zoo Theme 1.1. The theme contains all three components; keep the separate components off when using it.</p>\n<p>Repository rule corrections: .zt1map map-design data and .cfg configuration data were added through the existing owner settings API. The program/script blocking and virus scan remain active. Before/after settings and rejected attempts are retained here.</p>\n<p>Known gaps: repository browsing has no remote picture delivery; Your mods does display installed picture.png. Updating an enabled archive can fail on Windows until the game and Lab host unload it. Small downloads round to 0 MB in the current game UI. Amberhorn containment remains unresolved.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-1-publish-in-lab-wildlife-import-tutorial-passes-the-server-checks\">1. Publish in Lab: Wildlife Import Tutorial passes the server checks.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/781c20ab9d78f797bc.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-2-publish-the-separate-park-mod-server-rules-were-corrected-to-accept-game-cfg-data\">2. Publish the separate park mod. Server rules were corrected to accept game .cfg data.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0bab43b7a3bb0efc07.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-3-actual-game-game-options-mods-updates-mod-menu-mod-repository-select-the-wildlife-mod-and-download\">3. Actual game: Game Options &gt; Mods &amp; Updates &gt; Mod Menu &gt; Mod repository. Select the wildlife mod and Download.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e4985e1034a83f4d4e.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-4-the-habitat-kit-passes-server-rules-and-virus-scanning-its-description-retains-the-containment-limitation\">4. The habitat kit passes server rules and virus scanning. Its description retains the containment limitation.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/74fd11618ee08b8ef6.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-5-download-the-park-mod-through-the-actual-game-it-appears-as-have-and-starts-switched-off\">5. Download the park mod through the actual game. It appears as Have and starts switched off.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/4dc68278bc35dcf4ed.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-6-your-mods-displays-the-downloaded-picture-correctly-repository-browsing-still-lacks-remote-image-previews\">6. Your mods displays the downloaded picture correctly. Repository browsing still lacks remote image previews.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/5516b49669f0e9a6ef.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-7-download-moonstone-habitat-kit-through-the-same-player-workflow\">7. Download Moonstone Habitat Kit through the same player workflow.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/ef57207aa979ec1e67.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-8-retained-failure-downloading-over-the-enabled-theme-copy-cannot-unpack-its-locked-archive\">8. Retained failure: downloading over the enabled theme copy cannot unpack its locked archive.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/ce30c0a9a6ff740a22.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-9-switch-the-old-pack-off-and-restart-both-game-and-lab-host-retry-download-the-theme-installs-successfully\">9. Switch the old pack off and restart both game and Lab host. Retry Download: the theme installs successfully.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/ce6c351a2319058b79.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-10-switch-the-downloaded-theme-on-and-restart-play-its-three-custom-maps-appear-in-freeform-moonstone-habitat-has-its-preview\">10. Switch the downloaded theme on and restart Play. Its three custom maps appear in Freeform; Moonstone Habitat has its preview.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/c9965ba85044efec1d.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-11-moonstone-habitat-opens-with-custom-paths-gates-fences-shelters-park-objects-and-marine-tank\">11. Moonstone Habitat opens with custom paths, gates, fences, shelters, park objects and marine tank.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/962b181f3de8851503.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-12-the-mods-adoption-filter-exposes-the-downloaded-custom-animals\">12. The Mods adoption filter exposes the downloaded custom animals.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/4c9ad24dfaadea56ee.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-13-adopt-amberhorn-for-650-its-original-custom-egg-is-placed-this-is-an-installation-smoke-check-not-a-containment-retest\">13. Adopt Amberhorn for $650: its original custom egg is placed. This is an installation smoke check, not a containment retest.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/e4d21a35bd7fbdd3b4.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-14-adopt-azure-reef-dolphin-for-1450-inside-the-custom-tank-placement-succeeds-in-the-downloaded-map\">14. Adopt Azure Reef Dolphin for $1,450 inside the custom tank. Placement succeeds in the downloaded map.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/58d5bce67f340e09e2.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-15-replace-the-accidental-terrain-texture-cover-through-about-from-the-test-zoo-view-theme-version-11-changes-its-cover-only\">15. Replace the accidental terrain-texture cover through About &gt; From the Test Zoo view. Theme version 1.1 changes its cover only.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/f40e647b0870a5b40c.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-16-the-actual-game-offers-update-for-version-11-download-it-with-the-old-archive-unloaded-the-repository-confirms-have\">16. The actual game offers Update for version 1.1. Download it with the old archive unloaded; the repository confirms Have.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/522a94b501a699b9f0.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-17-your-mods-displays-the-corrected-zoo-overview-cover-from-downloaded-version-11\">17. Your mods displays the corrected zoo-overview cover from downloaded version 1.1.</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/5821b33f0174c92239.webp\" alt=\"Actual checkpoint\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>","toc":[{"id":"1-publish-in-lab-wildlife-import-tutorial-passes-the-server-checks","title":"1. Publish in Lab: Wildlife Import Tutorial passes the server checks.","level":2},{"id":"2-publish-the-separate-park-mod-server-rules-were-corrected-to-accept-game-cfg-data","title":"2. Publish the separate park mod. Server rules were corrected to accept game .cfg data.","level":2},{"id":"3-actual-game-game-options-mods-updates-mod-menu-mod-repository-select-the-wildlife-mod-and-download","title":"3. Actual game: Game Options &gt; Mods &amp; Updates &gt; Mod Menu &gt; Mod repository. Select the wildlife mod and Download.","level":2},{"id":"4-the-habitat-kit-passes-server-rules-and-virus-scanning-its-description-retains-the-containment-limitation","title":"4. The habitat kit passes server rules and virus scanning. Its description retains the containment limitation.","level":2},{"id":"5-download-the-park-mod-through-the-actual-game-it-appears-as-have-and-starts-switched-off","title":"5. Download the park mod through the actual game. It appears as Have and starts switched off.","level":2},{"id":"6-your-mods-displays-the-downloaded-picture-correctly-repository-browsing-still-lacks-remote-image-previews","title":"6. Your mods displays the downloaded picture correctly. Repository browsing still lacks remote image previews.","level":2},{"id":"7-download-moonstone-habitat-kit-through-the-same-player-workflow","title":"7. Download Moonstone Habitat Kit through the same player workflow.","level":2},{"id":"8-retained-failure-downloading-over-the-enabled-theme-copy-cannot-unpack-its-locked-archive","title":"8. Retained failure: downloading over the enabled theme copy cannot unpack its locked archive.","level":2},{"id":"9-switch-the-old-pack-off-and-restart-both-game-and-lab-host-retry-download-the-theme-installs-successfully","title":"9. Switch the old pack off and restart both game and Lab host. Retry Download: the theme installs successfully.","level":2},{"id":"10-switch-the-downloaded-theme-on-and-restart-play-its-three-custom-maps-appear-in-freeform-moonstone-habitat-has-its-preview","title":"10. Switch the downloaded theme on and restart Play. Its three custom maps appear in Freeform; Moonstone Habitat has its preview.","level":2},{"id":"11-moonstone-habitat-opens-with-custom-paths-gates-fences-shelters-park-objects-and-marine-tank","title":"11. Moonstone Habitat opens with custom paths, gates, fences, shelters, park objects and marine tank.","level":2},{"id":"12-the-mods-adoption-filter-exposes-the-downloaded-custom-animals","title":"12. The Mods adoption filter exposes the downloaded custom animals.","level":2},{"id":"13-adopt-amberhorn-for-650-its-original-custom-egg-is-placed-this-is-an-installation-smoke-check-not-a-containment-retest","title":"13. Adopt Amberhorn for $650: its original custom egg is placed. This is an installation smoke check, not a containment retest.","level":2},{"id":"14-adopt-azure-reef-dolphin-for-1450-inside-the-custom-tank-placement-succeeds-in-the-downloaded-map","title":"14. Adopt Azure Reef Dolphin for $1,450 inside the custom tank. Placement succeeds in the downloaded map.","level":2},{"id":"15-replace-the-accidental-terrain-texture-cover-through-about-from-the-test-zoo-view-theme-version-11-changes-its-cover-only","title":"15. Replace the accidental terrain-texture cover through About &gt; From the Test Zoo view. Theme version 1.1 changes its cover only.","level":2},{"id":"16-the-actual-game-offers-update-for-version-11-download-it-with-the-old-archive-unloaded-the-repository-confirms-have","title":"16. The actual game offers Update for version 1.1. Download it with the old archive unloaded; the repository confirms Have.","level":2},{"id":"17-your-mods-displays-the-corrected-zoo-overview-cover-from-downloaded-version-11","title":"17. Your mods displays the corrected zoo-overview cover from downloaded version 1.1.","level":2}],"text":"# Publishing and downloading the new mods\n\n26 October 2026. Actual Lab publish actions and native game downloads. The checkpoint video is a captioned sequence of real screenshots, not a continuous screen recording.\n\nPublished: Wildlife Import Tutorial 1.0, Park Workshop Tutorial 1.0, Moonstone Habitat Kit 1.0, Moonstone Zoo Theme 1.1. The theme contains all three components; keep the separate components off when using it.\n\nRepository rule corrections: .zt1map map-design data and .cfg configuration data were added through the existing owner settings API. The program/script blocking and virus scan remain active. Before/after settings and rejected attempts are retained here.\n\nKnown gaps: repository browsing has no remote picture delivery; Your mods does display installed picture.png. Updating an enabled archive can fail on Windows until the game and Lab host unload it. Small downloads round to 0 MB in the current game UI. Amberhorn containment remains unresolved.\n\n## 1. Publish in Lab: Wildlife Import Tutorial passes the server checks.\n\n\n\n## 2. Publish the separate park mod. Server rules were corrected to accept game .cfg data.\n\n\n\n## 3. Actual game: Game Options > Mods & Updates > Mod Menu > Mod repository. Select the wildlife mod and Download.\n\n\n\n## 4. The habitat kit passes server rules and virus scanning. Its description retains the containment limitation.\n\n\n\n## 5. Download the park mod through the actual game. It appears as Have and starts switched off.\n\n\n\n## 6. Your mods displays the downloaded picture correctly. Repository browsing still lacks remote image previews.\n\n\n\n## 7. Download Moonstone Habitat Kit through the same player workflow.\n\n\n\n## 8. Retained failure: downloading over the enabled theme copy cannot unpack its locked archive.\n\n\n\n## 9. Switch the old pack off and restart both game and Lab host. Retry Download: the theme installs successfully.\n\n\n\n## 10. Switch the downloaded theme on and restart Play. Its three custom maps appear in Freeform; Moonstone Habitat has its preview.\n\n\n\n## 11. Moonstone Habitat opens with custom paths, gates, fences, shelters, park objects and marine tank.\n\n\n\n## 12. The Mods adoption filter exposes the downloaded custom animals.\n\n\n\n## 13. Adopt Amberhorn for $650: its original custom egg is placed. This is an installation smoke check, not a containment retest.\n\n\n\n## 14. Adopt Azure Reef Dolphin for $1,450 inside the custom tank. Placement succeeds in the downloaded map.\n\n\n\n## 15. Replace the accidental terrain-texture cover through About > From the Test Zoo view. Theme version 1.1 changes its cover only.\n\n\n\n## 16. The actual game offers Update for version 1.1. Download it with the old archive unloaded; the repository confirms Have.\n\n\n\n## 17. Your mods displays the corrected zoo-overview cover from downloaded version 1.1.\n\n\n\n"},{"id":"preview-tutorial","title":"Repository pictures before download","summary":"The fixed cover workflow, four clean-folder previews and a real theme download.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":["mods"],"legacy":false,"videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/36604102323fc5863d.mp4","title":"Recorded tutorial checkpoints","caption":"Captioned native screenshots. This is a checkpoint walkthrough, not a continuous recording.","src":"help/media/36604102323fc5863d.mp4","poster":"help/media/5fb1c253a3ee4f8996.webp"}],"html":"<p>Recorded 26 October 2026. These are actual native-game checkpoints, presented as a captioned walkthrough; they are not a continuous screen recording.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In Lab <strong>Mods</strong>, select your mod or theme pack.</li>\n<li>Set its cover under <strong>About</strong>, using your own image or <strong>From the Test Zoo view</strong>. Save edits and increase the version for an update.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>Publish</strong>, run <strong>Check it</strong>, then <strong>Upload it</strong>. The current Lab prepares the repository thumbnail automatically. No separate picture upload is required.</li>\n<li>Wait for the repository rules and malware scan to finish. A local check alone does not publish it.</li>\n<li>In the actual game open <strong>Game Options &gt; Mods &amp; Updates &gt; Mod Menu &gt; Mod repository</strong>. Select the listing: its cover appears before installation.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Download</strong>. After completion, open <strong>Your mods</strong>, switch it on and restart to load it. For a theme pack, keep its component mods off.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Older uploads need a version update through the current Lab to gain a repository thumbnail. A listing without a cover remains downloadable. This feature is in the current game build and the repository service; older installed game builds need the next engine release.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Start in the actual game with an empty mods folder. No installed copy can provide a cover.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/5fb1c253a3ee4f8996.webp\" alt=\"Start in the actual game with an empty mods folder. No installed copy can provide a cover.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mod repository: Moonstone Habitat Kit 1.1 shows its cover before download.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/f161562824d9163589.webp\" alt=\"Mod repository: Moonstone Habitat Kit 1.1 shows its cover before download.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Wildlife Import Tutorial 1.1: its own cover loads when the selection changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/22a5c66d1ed2cef3fa.webp\" alt=\"Wildlife Import Tutorial 1.1: its own cover loads when the selection changes.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Park Workshop Tutorial 1.1: repository artwork is supplied by the approved upload.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0143e0db66709427d5.webp\" alt=\"Park Workshop Tutorial 1.1: repository artwork is supplied by the approved upload.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Moonstone Zoo Theme 1.2: a theme pack has its own cover and one Download button.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/c4339dfd14a1995a44.webp\" alt=\"Moonstone Zoo Theme 1.2: a theme pack has its own cover and one Download button.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Legacy listing without a thumbnail: the previous picture clears correctly.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/7fce7d20d4a3e1f0fb.webp\" alt=\"Legacy listing without a thumbnail: the previous picture clears correctly.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Download finishes in the actual game. The theme is installed and switched off until enabled.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/8526ebbba95217e366.webp\" alt=\"Download finishes in the actual game. The theme is installed and switched off until enabled.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Testing finished: original mods settings restored, Play stopped and Test Zoo paused.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/674937852be5907c49.webp\" alt=\"Testing finished: original mods settings restored, Play stopped and Test Zoo paused.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>","toc":[],"text":"# Repository pictures: tutorial addendum\n\nRecorded 26 October 2026. These are actual native-game checkpoints, presented as a captioned walkthrough; they are not a continuous screen recording.\n\n1. In Lab **Mods**, select your mod or theme pack.\n2. Set its cover under **About**, using your own image or **From the Test Zoo view**. Save edits and increase the version for an update.\n3. Open **Publish**, run **Check it**, then **Upload it**. The current Lab prepares the repository thumbnail automatically. No separate picture upload is required.\n4. Wait for the repository rules and malware scan to finish. A local check alone does not publish it.\n5. In the actual game open **Game Options > Mods & Updates > Mod Menu > Mod repository**. Select the listing: its cover appears before installation.\n6. Choose **Download**. After completion, open **Your mods**, switch it on and restart to load it. For a theme pack, keep its component mods off.\n\nOlder uploads need a version update through the current Lab to gain a repository thumbnail. A listing without a cover remains downloadable. This feature is in the current game build and the repository service; older installed game builds need the next engine release.\n\n1. Start in the actual game with an empty mods folder. No installed copy can provide a cover.\n\n\n\n2. Mod repository: Moonstone Habitat Kit 1.1 shows its cover before download.\n\n\n\n3. Wildlife Import Tutorial 1.1: its own cover loads when the selection changes.\n\n\n\n4. Park Workshop Tutorial 1.1: repository artwork is supplied by the approved upload.\n\n\n\n5. Moonstone Zoo Theme 1.2: a theme pack has its own cover and one Download button.\n\n\n\n6. Legacy listing without a thumbnail: the previous picture clears correctly.\n\n\n\n7. Download finishes in the actual game. The theme is installed and switched off until enabled.\n\n\n\n8. Testing finished: original mods settings restored, Play stopped and Test Zoo paused.\n\n\n\n"},{"id":"modding-reference","title":"Modding reference","summary":"Package structure, builders, external art, maps, sharing and compatibility.","group":"Reference","tabs":[],"legacy":false,"source":"../../docs/MODDING.md","html":"<p>A mod changes or adds to Zoo Tycoon without touching the game&#x27;s own files. Players switch mods on and off in the <strong>Mod Menu</strong> (main menu › Game Options › Mods &amp; Updates › Mod Menu), and every mod is off until they switch it on.</p>\n<p>There are three ways to get a mod into the game:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Who can use it</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Add a mod</strong></td>\n<td>Only you, on your own PC</td>\n<td>Mod Menu › <strong>Add a mod...</strong> in the game, or the Lab&#x27;s <strong>Your mods</strong> › <strong>Bring in a mod</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The mods folder</strong></td>\n<td>Only you</td>\n<td>Put the mod&#x27;s folder in the mods folder (Mod Menu › <strong>Folder...</strong> shows where)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The mod repository</strong></td>\n<td>Everyone with the official game</td>\n<td>The Lab&#x27;s <strong>Your mods</strong> › <strong>Upload to the mod repository</strong>. Players download it from Mod Menu › <strong>Mod repository</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-what-a-mod-is\">What a mod is</h2>\n<p>A mod is a folder with a <code>mod.json</code> and its files:</p>\n<pre><code>My Plasma Fence/\n  mod.json          what it is (below)\n  picture.png       optional: its picture in the Mod Menu (367 x 276)\n  content.ztd       game files: a zip with the game&#x27;s own paths inside\n  shaders/          optional: shaders (a look for the art)\n  hd/               optional: HD art (bigger frames for the zoomed-in map)\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>mod.json</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;plasma-fence&quot;,\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Plasma Fence&quot;,\n  &quot;author&quot;: &quot;Your name&quot;,\n  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;1.0&quot;,\n  &quot;about&quot;: &quot;A glowing fence for the dinosaur exhibits.&quot;,\n  &quot;replaces&quot;: [&quot;fences/dzbar&quot;],\n  &quot;picture&quot;: &quot;picture.png&quot;,\n  &quot;settings&quot;: []\n}\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>id</strong> is the mod&#x27;s name in the mod repository. Use lower-case letters, numbers, <code>.</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>. Once you&#x27;ve uploaded a mod, its id is yours.</li>\n<li><strong>version</strong>: raise it each time you change the mod, so players see there&#x27;s an update.</li>\n<li><strong>replaces</strong>: the game files or folders it changes, which the Mod Menu lists under &quot;It changes&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>picture</strong>: an image in the mod&#x27;s folder that the Mod Menu shows beside it, 367 x 276 (the Lab&#x27;s mod Overview sets one for you, cut to that size).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lab Upload automatically prepares a small <code>repository-preview.png</code> from this\npicture for browsing in <strong>Mod repository</strong>, before the player downloads the mod.\nIt is included in the ordinary upload and appears after the package passes the\nrepository&#x27;s checks and malware scan. Keep the original <code>picture.png</code> for the\ninstalled mod. No separate image upload is needed. Older published packages need\na version update uploaded with the current Lab to gain this preview. A missing\nor invalid cover leaves the listing usable without a picture.\n- <strong>settings</strong>: the mod&#x27;s own options, shown in Game Options. See <code>mods/zt1-toon/README.md</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-kinds-of-mod\">Kinds of mod</h2>\n<p><strong>Game files: new animals, objects, scenery, fences, buildings.</strong> These go in a <code>.ztd</code>, which is a zip with the game&#x27;s own paths inside: <code>animals/&lt;name&gt;/...</code>, <code>objects/&lt;name&gt;/...</code>, and each one&#x27;s <code>.ai</code> that says how it works. This is how every official download and every fan download for the 2001 game worked, so the same files load here.</p>\n<p>In game, the buy panels&#x27; filter (All, Zoo Tycoon, Dinosaur Digs, Marine Mania) has a <strong>Mods</strong> choice too. It lists only what your switched-on mods add or change, so a new animal or rock is easy to find. Turn it off with the <code>modfilter</code> switch in Game Options.</p>\n<p>The Lab helps:\n- <strong>Game files</strong> lists every path the game has, with each <code>.ai</code> readable.\n- Its <strong>animation maker</strong> makes a recoloured copy of an animal or object as a mod.</p>\n<p><strong>A new animal.</strong> In the Lab, open an animal&#x27;s <code>.ai</code> in Write Code › Game files and press <strong>New species from this</strong>. Give it a name and a colour: you get a new mod with the animal&#x27;s art copied from your own game under the new name, dyed, and its <code>.ai</code> as a <code>.uca</code> species file with the new name in <code>[1033] cName</code>. That file opens to change: its cost, herd size, what it likes, the line the buy panel shows, and its behaviour sets (a list of animations and sounds; <code>fPlaySetProb</code> picks one by chance). Then design its look in the mod&#x27;s <strong>Art</strong> page.</p>\n<p><strong>The Lab&#x27;s mod studio.</strong> The Mods tab is one workspace for a mod: its contents down the left by kind (animals, buildings, scenery, maps), the editor for what you pick in the middle, an <strong>Inspector</strong> on the right with the picked item&#x27;s values as plain controls (price, food and rest, group size, what it can do; a building&#x27;s capacity and prices), saved into the mod as you change them, and a <strong>Problems</strong> list at the bottom of what&#x27;s still missing. <strong>＋ Make something</strong> starts a new animal, a new map or a change to one of the game&#x27;s items.</p>\n<p><strong>A new animal from scratch.</strong> In the studio, <strong>New animal</strong>: its kind (a zoo animal, a Marine Mania animal or a dinosaur), a name, and an animal it behaves like (its needs, biome, behaviour and sounds, all yours to change in its species file). It starts with no art of its own: each of its animations is <em>linked</em> - its line in <code>[m/Animations]</code> is the whole path of an animation that exists (<code>walk = animals/zebra/m/walk/walk.ani</code>, the way the game&#x27;s own files share art) - so it works in the game at once. <strong>Draw</strong> any of them in the editor (layers, frames, onion skin, the linked animation as a faint guide; a Shadow layer is its ground shadow) for the five ways it faces, N, NE, E, SE and S - the game mirrors the other three. Saving makes the game&#x27;s art: one 256-colour palette for all the animal&#x27;s drawn animations, made from the colours you used. <strong>Link…</strong> points any line at any animation, yours or the game&#x27;s. Its <strong>Needs</strong> tab holds everything else the game weighs: its kind and keeper, its food, its biome and continent, the ground, plants, rocks and shelters it likes and the animals it lives with - or <strong>Copy needs from…</strong> another animal as a preset. A dinosaur hatches from an egg: its <code>egg_idle</code>, <code>egg_hatch</code> and <code>egg_break</code> animations are drawn or linked like any other. You needn&#x27;t draw from nothing. Every new drawing starts with <strong>Remake</strong> (an existing animal&#x27;s, person&#x27;s, building&#x27;s, tree&#x27;s or fence&#x27;s art copied in to change), <strong>Stencil</strong> (its outline over your canvas, its inside as faint as you like - draw your own in it, paint kept inside the outline if you want, or fill the outline as a base) or <strong>Blank</strong>. Then reshape it for all of them at once (<strong>Size</strong>, a <strong>Body</strong> longer or shorter along the way it faces, a <strong>Band</strong> of its height taller or shorter for a neck or legs) and <strong>Recolour</strong> a colour and its shades everywhere. The <strong>direction ring</strong> shows the frame in all eight ways it faces while you work.</p>\n<p><strong>Changing the game&#x27;s own things</strong> (a building&#x27;s capacity, an animal&#x27;s price, its colours). Open the file in Game files and press <strong>Change it in a mod</strong>: the mod gets a copy at the same path, which takes the game&#x27;s place while the mod is on. For art, the mod&#x27;s <strong>Art</strong> page lists the game&#x27;s art for what the mod changes. Change the palette or paint frames, and saving puts the copies in the mod.</p>\n<p><strong>The Art page</strong> (Mods › a mod › Art): pick colours on the animal or by shade, change them (one palette colours all of an animal&#x27;s animations), paint frames, fade a pattern along the body in every frame (the quagga&#x27;s stripes fading out towards its tail), or send the frames out as PNGs to paint in another program and bring them back.</p>\n<p><strong>A freeform map.</strong> In the Lab&#x27;s Test Zoo, start from a <strong>blank map</strong> (flat grass, the game&#x27;s Small, Medium or Large size or your own, 16 to 512 tiles a side) or one of the game&#x27;s maps. A blank map starts in <strong>Edit the map</strong>: draw the zoo&#x27;s boundary with the Zoo Fence and put the entrance where guests should come in (the wall opens across its doorway). Then paint the ground and water, shape hills, lay paths and place trees and buildings. The <strong>Terrain</strong> view shows the whole map from above. Then <strong>Save as a map</strong>: it goes into a mod as <code>freeform/&lt;id&gt;.scn</code> (the map it&#x27;s over&#x27;s <code>.scn</code>, with <code>[start] design=</code> and <code>[freeform] title=</code>) plus the design (<code>.zt1map</code>), a description and a preview. A blank map is saved with <code>[start] blank = W H</code> and no map of the game&#x27;s under it. Switched on, it&#x27;s in the game&#x27;s freeform map list. Players turn mod maps off with the <code>custommaps</code> switch.</p>\n<p><strong>Shaders</strong> change how the zoomed-in art looks. <code>mods/zt1-toon</code> is a complete example with a guide.</p>\n<p><strong>HD art</strong> adds bigger frames for the zoomed-in map. The Lab&#x27;s <strong>HD art</strong> experiment makes them with an AI upscaler.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-build-buildings-fences-people-and-objects\">Build buildings, fences, people and objects</h2>\n<p>In <strong>Mods → Make something → Build an item</strong>, choose Building, Fence, Staff\nmember, Guest appearance, Tank filter, or Scenery/object. Choose <strong>Create a\nseparate item</strong> for its own identity, or <strong>Customize an existing item</strong> to replace\nthe selected definition while this mod is enabled. Start from a working role;\nthe Inspector changes its prices, size and supported properties.</p>\n<p>Draw an existing animation slot with <strong>Blank → Import</strong> for original PNG or\nOBJ art, or use <strong>Remake → Recolour</strong> for a customized game asset. Select the\nOBJ, its MTL and any texture pictures together in the file picker. Multiple\nexported poses form a sequence; a single mesh is not automatically rigged or\nanimated. Items can use eight supplied views; a remake keeps its source views.\nRemaking a building includes its static background and default colour slots.\nCheck both idle and used states, not just its first frame.</p>\n<p><strong>Link…</strong> and <strong>Link other poses…</strong> explicitly reuse an animation. Reusing a\nwalk drawing for work or damage slots makes a prototype, not a finished set of\nmotions. Staff retain an existing duty such as keeper or maintenance. Guest\nappearances replace man, woman, boy or girl; they do not add a fifth guest role.\nIcons and poses that have not been drawn continue to use their inherited art.</p>\n<p><strong>Test in the zoo</strong> reloads enabled mods; <strong>Play with it</strong> starts the normal\ngame. Check the normal buy panel&#x27;s <strong>Mods</strong> filter, prices, placement, guest\nservice, enclosure gates and staff duties. Different tank filter definitions\nkeep their own footprint, cleaning delay, cleaning strength, wear, upkeep and\nservice settings; placed filter identities are saved with the zoo or map.</p>\n<p>If a replacement looks unchanged, check other enabled mods that change the\nsame file. An earlier mod folder can take precedence. For example, disable\n<strong>Lemonade Stands</strong> when testing <strong>Park Workshop Tutorial</strong>&#x27;s replacement of\n<code>scenery/building/drkstnd.ai</code>. Local <strong>Publish → Check it</strong> is a package rules\ncheck, not an upload or a complete behavior test.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-import-external-animal-art-in-the-new-studio\">Import external animal art in the new studio</h2>\n<p>In <strong>Mods</strong>, pick or create a mod, then <strong>Make something → New animal</strong>. Choose its kind, name and starting behavior. Under <strong>Animations</strong>, choose <strong>Draw</strong> beside an animation, then <strong>Blank</strong>. In the editor, <strong>This direction → Import external art</strong> opens the file picker.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Conversion controls</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One PNG picture</td>\n<td>One picture; visible height; selected direction or repeat</td>\n<td>A fitted frame, with its visible bottom at the ground point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal PNG strip</td>\n<td>Horizontal frame strip; column count; height; timing</td>\n<td>A sequence for the chosen direction, or the same sequence repeated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Directional PNG sheet</td>\n<td>Grid; columns; rows; row directions such as <code>N,NE,E,SE,S</code>; height; timing</td>\n<td>Frames across each row, assigned to its named direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OBJ mesh with MTL</td>\n<td>Select the OBJ and MTL together, plus any named texture images; Y-up front axis; height</td>\n<td>Five rendered views of the supplied mesh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several OBJ poses</td>\n<td>Select the numbered OBJ files together, with MTL/textures; timing</td>\n<td>Each file becomes one pose, sorted by filename, rendered in five directions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Use a regular grid with margins around each sprite. PNG transparency is retained; scaling uses nearest pixels. Check all directions, frames and the ground point before <strong>Save</strong>, which builds the game&#x27;s animation and palette inside the mod. Import replaces frames in the selected layer for the supplied directions; other matching layers are retained. Undo restores the previous drawing.</p>\n<p>A picture does not supply unseen angles: repeating it gives the same view in each direction. OBJ import converts polygon meshes and supplied poses; it does not rig a model or invent a walking cycle. Export Y-up OBJ poses from your modeling application. MTL diffuse colours and selected texture images are supported; advanced material/shader effects are not.</p>\n<p>For a quick prototype, <strong>Link other poses…</strong> reuses one drawn animation for the listed behavior slots, keeping crate and egg poses. Wait for linking to finish before editing needs. A finished animal needs appropriate separate walking, eating, resting and other poses.</p>\n<p>Under <strong>Pictures → Use an animation frame…</strong>, choose one drawn frame to make its icon, list picture and plaque at the game&#x27;s sizes. Each picture remains editable. Under <strong>Needs</strong>, set its food, habitat, terrain and compatibility; the Inspector sets its price, group size, space and other values.</p>\n<p><strong>Test in the zoo</strong> loads the mod in a temporary test session and restarts the Test Zoo. Your normal mod switches are unchanged. Use <strong>End test</strong> to return to them. Build an appropriate enclosure, add companions, shelter and keepers, and compare its recommendations before and after meeting its needs. <strong>Play with it</strong> starts a separate temporary Play test: <strong>Adopt Animals → Mods → choose the animal → male or female button → place it</strong>. Selecting the icon alone does not start adoption. Stopping that Play session ends its temporary selection; ordinary <strong>Play ZT1 Engine</strong> uses your normal switches.</p>\n<p>For a zoo animal that swims, start from an amphibious species and keep its land\nand water animation slots separate. Test natural entry into the pond and return\nto land, as well as feeding. A marine animal needs a completed tank with enough\nwater depth, the right salinity, a filter beside the tank and the appropriate\ncarer. Check its surface and submerged art with their underlays and shadows;\nmatching frame counts matter. A successful package check does not certify every\nmarine behavior or show trick. Use the Test Zoo&#x27;s species check for those deeper\ndiagnostics, and compare inherited warnings with the base species.</p>\n<p><strong>Save as a map</strong> preserves the habitat design for ordinary Play. Live animals\nand staff must be adopted again when starting that freeform map.</p>\n<p><strong>Publish → Check it</strong> checks the package locally. <strong>Upload it</strong> sends it to the repository for its own checks; a local pass does not mean it is published. Players download through the game&#x27;s <strong>Mod Menu → Mod repository</strong>, then switch the downloaded mod on under <strong>Your mods</strong> or <strong>Settings → Mods &amp; Updates</strong>. Downloaded mods remain listed while switched off. Settings has left and right arrow buttons and a page counter for the full installed list.</p>\n<p>Optional mod switches apply to the running engine without manually restarting Play. Turning off a replacement restores the stock asset. A pack that supplies the current custom map, or unique animals, objects, fencing, paths, food or filters in use, stays enabled and explains why. Leave that map or remove its dependent custom content first. The engine checkpoints the current zoo privately before refreshing assets and rolls back a change that would lose zoo contents or cash; it does not overwrite the player&#x27;s save.</p>\n<p>A mod can declare external requirements with <code>&quot;dependencies&quot;: [&quot;other-mod-id&quot;]</code> (or <code>&quot;requires&quot;</code>). Required mods must be installed; enabling the dependent mod enables them too. They stay enabled until their dependent mod is switched off. A self-contained theme pack already includes its component files: its <code>themePack.components</code> list records those components and does not require the separate component mods to be enabled.</p>\n<p><strong>Download</strong> installs a mod switched off; it is separate from <strong>Enable</strong>. The main-menu Mod Menu and Settings use the same smart switch. After <strong>Load mods</strong> was switched off, enabling one mod activates only that mod and its requirements. The old remembered selection is not reactivated. Temporary tests have their own master switch and load only the selected mod and its requirements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-habitat-items-and-theme-packs\">Habitat items and theme packs</h2>\n<p><strong>Make something → Build an item</strong> supports plants, rocks, shelters, enrichment,\nzoo entrances, tank walls, paths, ground textures and animal food as well as the\nbuilding, fence, person and filter workflows. Choose a separate item or customize\nan existing one. Imported object meshes provide eight directions. A path drawing\ncontains twenty numbered connection shapes, including its ramps; use whole-canvas\nplacement for path sheets and ground textures. Ground textures replace an existing\nterrain&#x27;s appearance and retain that terrain&#x27;s gameplay identity.</p>\n<p>An animal&#x27;s <strong>Needs → Custom food</strong> can select food from an enabled mod. Its normal\ncarer then supplies that food. Dinosaur egg idle, hatch and broken-shell slots are\neditable separately. <strong>Own copy…</strong> makes an independently editable drawing when\nseveral slots currently link to the same animation.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Publish → Build a theme pack…</strong>, include two or more compatible local mods,\ngive the pack its own name, id, version and description, and build it. On <strong>About</strong>,\nchoose its own image or take the Test Zoo view. Then check and upload the pack\nthrough the ordinary Publish controls as one mod. Players install and switch it\non once. Switch off the separate component mods while testing the pack.</p>\n<p>A pack is a snapshot. The original mods remain available to edit; build a new\nsnapshot after changing them. Different files at the same game path stop the build\nand name the conflicting mods. Identical shared files are included once. Mods\nwith identity-specific settings currently need to stay separate. Authoring project\nfolders are excluded from the playable pack; retain them separately for editing.</p>\n<p>Finish and save asset edits before laying out a map. Check the saved map by\nreopening it; <strong>Save as a map</strong> stores its design, while live animals, guests and\nstaff are created again during Play.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-rules\">The rules</h2>\n<p>These are the same in the Lab&#x27;s <strong>Check it</strong> and in the mod repository&#x27;s own checks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Game data only.</strong> That means <code>.ztd .ai .ani .pal .tga .bmp .png .webp .jpg .wav .ogg .mp3 .json .ini .txt .md .frag .lyt .uca .ucs .ucb .scn .zoo .lng</code>, and the extension-less frames inside animation folders. No programs or scripts (<code>.exe .dll .bat .js .ps1</code> ...), not even inside a <code>.ztd</code>.</li>\n<li>No paths that point outside the mod, and no links.</li>\n<li>At most 200,000 files.</li>\n<li>A <code>mod.json</code> with an id and a name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-sharing-it\">Sharing it</h2>\n<p>In the Lab: <strong>Mods</strong>, pick your mod, then <strong>Publish → Upload it</strong>. Set its cover\non <strong>About</strong> first, using your own image or the Test Zoo view.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You need a CodeRook account. Sign in with <code>cbx sign-in</code>, or paste a personal access token into the Lab&#x27;s <strong>Your uploads</strong> panel.</li>\n<li>The upload is checked automatically. This includes the rules above and a malware scan. Once the checks pass, it&#x27;s offered in every official game&#x27;s Mod Menu.</li>\n<li><strong>To update it</strong>, raise its version and upload it again.</li>\n<li><strong>To take it down</strong>, use <strong>Your uploads › Take it down</strong>. You can only change or remove mods you uploaded yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-saved-zoos-with-custom-content\">Saved zoos with custom content</h2>\n<p>Ordinary Play defaults to <strong>Shared zoo (.zoo)</strong>: one file holds an\noriginal-compatible stock world and the engine&#x27;s full native snapshot. The\nengine restores custom entities and elevated paths from its section, with their\nrequired mods enabled. Definitions and artwork still come from those mods.</p>\n<p>Unique engine-only animals, staff, objects, food and filters are omitted from\nthe original-compatible section. Custom paths and fences use stock counterparts;\ncustom terrain uses a stock terrain where needed. A replacement of a stock\ndefinition can use that definition&#x27;s original counterpart. The original needs\nthe relevant expansions/assets; arbitrary fan-mod compatibility is not promised.</p>\n<p>An original-game resave discards the engine snapshot. Save the original-game\ncopy separately if you want to keep engine-only features. <strong>Engine-only save\n(.zt1save)</strong> remains available. New native snapshots use version 14 and cannot\nbe read by older released engines. See <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"save-reference\">save compatibility and limits</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-old-mods-from-the-2001-game\">Old mods from the 2001 game</h2>\n<p>Fan-made downloads from 2001 to 2010 (new animals, objects, expansion-style packs) are <code>.ztd</code> files, the same kind the game itself uses. To use one you already have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In the game:</strong> Mod Menu › <strong>Add a mod...</strong>, then pick the <code>.ztd</code>, or a <code>.zip</code> of several.</li>\n<li><strong>In the Lab:</strong> Your mods › <strong>Bring in a mod</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It becomes a mod of its own, switched off, with a <code>mod.json</code> that says what it adds. It loads exactly as it did in the original game. We don&#x27;t re-host other people&#x27;s old downloads; bring in the ones you have.</p>","toc":[{"id":"what-a-mod-is","title":"What a mod is","level":2},{"id":"kinds-of-mod","title":"Kinds of mod","level":2},{"id":"build-buildings-fences-people-and-objects","title":"Build buildings, fences, people and objects","level":2},{"id":"import-external-animal-art-in-the-new-studio","title":"Import external animal art in the new studio","level":2},{"id":"habitat-items-and-theme-packs","title":"Habitat items and theme packs","level":2},{"id":"the-rules","title":"The rules","level":2},{"id":"sharing-it","title":"Sharing it","level":2},{"id":"saved-zoos-with-custom-content","title":"Saved zoos with custom content","level":2},{"id":"old-mods-from-the-2001-game","title":"Old mods from the 2001 game","level":2}],"text":"# Making mods for ZT1-Engine\n\nA mod changes or adds to Zoo Tycoon without touching the game's own files. Players switch mods on and off in the **Mod Menu** (main menu › Game Options › Mods & Updates › Mod Menu), and every mod is off until they switch it on.\n\nThere are three ways to get a mod into the game:\n\n| | Who can use it | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Add a mod** | Only you, on your own PC | Mod Menu › **Add a mod...** in the game, or the Lab's **Your mods** › **Bring in a mod** |\n| **The mods folder** | Only you | Put the mod's folder in the mods folder (Mod Menu › **Folder...** shows where) |\n| **The mod repository** | Everyone with the official game | The Lab's **Your mods** › **Upload to the mod repository**. Players download it from Mod Menu › **Mod repository**. |\n\n## What a mod is\n\nA mod is a folder with a `mod.json` and its files:\n\n```\nMy Plasma Fence/\n  mod.json          what it is (below)\n  picture.png       optional: its picture in the Mod Menu (367 x 276)\n  content.ztd       game files: a zip with the game's own paths inside\n  shaders/          optional: shaders (a look for the art)\n  hd/               optional: HD art (bigger frames for the zoomed-in map)\n```\n\n`mod.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"plasma-fence\",\n  \"name\": \"Plasma Fence\",\n  \"author\": \"Your name\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"about\": \"A glowing fence for the dinosaur exhibits.\",\n  \"replaces\": [\"fences/dzbar\"],\n  \"picture\": \"picture.png\",\n  \"settings\": []\n}\n```\n\n- **id** is the mod's name in the mod repository. Use lower-case letters, numbers, `.`, `_` and `-`. Once you've uploaded a mod, its id is yours.\n- **version**: raise it each time you change the mod, so players see there's an update.\n- **replaces**: the game files or folders it changes, which the Mod Menu lists under \"It changes\".\n- **picture**: an image in the mod's folder that the Mod Menu shows beside it, 367 x 276 (the Lab's mod Overview sets one for you, cut to that size).\n\nLab Upload automatically prepares a small `repository-preview.png` from this\npicture for browsing in **Mod repository**, before the player downloads the mod.\nIt is included in the ordinary upload and appears after the package passes the\nrepository's checks and malware scan. Keep the original `picture.png` for the\ninstalled mod. No separate image upload is needed. Older published packages need\na version update uploaded with the current Lab to gain this preview. A missing\nor invalid cover leaves the listing usable without a picture.\n- **settings**: the mod's own options, shown in Game Options. See `mods/zt1-toon/README.md`.\n\n## Kinds of mod\n\n**Game files: new animals, objects, scenery, fences, buildings.** These go in a `.ztd`, which is a zip with the game's own paths inside: `animals/<name>/...`, `objects/<name>/...`, and each one's `.ai` that says how it works. This is how every official download and every fan download for the 2001 game worked, so the same files load here.\n\nIn game, the buy panels' filter (All, Zoo Tycoon, Dinosaur Digs, Marine Mania) has a **Mods** choice too. It lists only what your switched-on mods add or change, so a new animal or rock is easy to find. Turn it off with the `modfilter` switch in Game Options.\n\nThe Lab helps:\n- **Game files** lists every path the game has, with each `.ai` readable.\n- Its **animation maker** makes a recoloured copy of an animal or object as a mod.\n\n**A new animal.** In the Lab, open an animal's `.ai` in Write Code › Game files and press **New species from this**. Give it a name and a colour: you get a new mod with the animal's art copied from your own game under the new name, dyed, and its `.ai` as a `.uca` species file with the new name in `[1033] cName`. That file opens to change: its cost, herd size, what it likes, the line the buy panel shows, and its behaviour sets (a list of animations and sounds; `fPlaySetProb` picks one by chance). Then design its look in the mod's **Art** page.\n\n**The Lab's mod studio.** The Mods tab is one workspace for a mod: its contents down the left by kind (animals, buildings, scenery, maps), the editor for what you pick in the middle, an **Inspector** on the right with the picked item's values as plain controls (price, food and rest, group size, what it can do; a building's capacity and prices), saved into the mod as you change them, and a **Problems** list at the bottom of what's still missing. **＋ Make something** starts a new animal, a new map or a change to one of the game's items.\n\n**A new animal from scratch.** In the studio, **New animal**: its kind (a zoo animal, a Marine Mania animal or a dinosaur), a name, and an animal it behaves like (its needs, biome, behaviour and sounds, all yours to change in its species file). It starts with no art of its own: each of its animations is *linked* - its line in `[m/Animations]` is the whole path of an animation that exists (`walk = animals/zebra/m/walk/walk.ani`, the way the game's own files share art) - so it works in the game at once. **Draw** any of them in the editor (layers, frames, onion skin, the linked animation as a faint guide; a Shadow layer is its ground shadow) for the five ways it faces, N, NE, E, SE and S - the game mirrors the other three. Saving makes the game's art: one 256-colour palette for all the animal's drawn animations, made from the colours you used. **Link…** points any line at any animation, yours or the game's. Its **Needs** tab holds everything else the game weighs: its kind and keeper, its food, its biome and continent, the ground, plants, rocks and shelters it likes and the animals it lives with - or **Copy needs from…** another animal as a preset. A dinosaur hatches from an egg: its `egg_idle`, `egg_hatch` and `egg_break` animations are drawn or linked like any other. You needn't draw from nothing. Every new drawing starts with **Remake** (an existing animal's, person's, building's, tree's or fence's art copied in to change), **Stencil** (its outline over your canvas, its inside as faint as you like - draw your own in it, paint kept inside the outline if you want, or fill the outline as a base) or **Blank**. Then reshape it for all of them at once (**Size**, a **Body** longer or shorter along the way it faces, a **Band** of its height taller or shorter for a neck or legs) and **Recolour** a colour and its shades everywhere. The **direction ring** shows the frame in all eight ways it faces while you work.\n\n**Changing the game's own things** (a building's capacity, an animal's price, its colours). Open the file in Game files and press **Change it in a mod**: the mod gets a copy at the same path, which takes the game's place while the mod is on. For art, the mod's **Art** page lists the game's art for what the mod changes. Change the palette or paint frames, and saving puts the copies in the mod.\n\n**The Art page** (Mods › a mod › Art): pick colours on the animal or by shade, change them (one palette colours all of an animal's animations), paint frames, fade a pattern along the body in every frame (the quagga's stripes fading out towards its tail), or send the frames out as PNGs to paint in another program and bring them back.\n\n**A freeform map.** In the Lab's Test Zoo, start from a **blank map** (flat grass, the game's Small, Medium or Large size or your own, 16 to 512 tiles a side) or one of the game's maps. A blank map starts in **Edit the map**: draw the zoo's boundary with the Zoo Fence and put the entrance where guests should come in (the wall opens across its doorway). Then paint the ground and water, shape hills, lay paths and place trees and buildings. The **Terrain** view shows the whole map from above. Then **Save as a map**: it goes into a mod as `freeform/<id>.scn` (the map it's over's `.scn`, with `[start] design=` and `[freeform] title=`) plus the design (`.zt1map`), a description and a preview. A blank map is saved with `[start] blank = W H` and no map of the game's under it. Switched on, it's in the game's freeform map list. Players turn mod maps off with the `custommaps` switch.\n\n**Shaders** change how the zoomed-in art looks. `mods/zt1-toon` is a complete example with a guide.\n\n**HD art** adds bigger frames for the zoomed-in map. The Lab's **HD art** experiment makes them with an AI upscaler.\n\n## Build buildings, fences, people and objects\n\nIn **Mods → Make something → Build an item**, choose Building, Fence, Staff\nmember, Guest appearance, Tank filter, or Scenery/object. Choose **Create a\nseparate item** for its own identity, or **Customize an existing item** to replace\nthe selected definition while this mod is enabled. Start from a working role;\nthe Inspector changes its prices, size and supported properties.\n\nDraw an existing animation slot with **Blank → Import** for original PNG or\nOBJ art, or use **Remake → Recolour** for a customized game asset. Select the\nOBJ, its MTL and any texture pictures together in the file picker. Multiple\nexported poses form a sequence; a single mesh is not automatically rigged or\nanimated. Items can use eight supplied views; a remake keeps its source views.\nRemaking a building includes its static background and default colour slots.\nCheck both idle and used states, not just its first frame.\n\n**Link…** and **Link other poses…** explicitly reuse an animation. Reusing a\nwalk drawing for work or damage slots makes a prototype, not a finished set of\nmotions. Staff retain an existing duty such as keeper or maintenance. Guest\nappearances replace man, woman, boy or girl; they do not add a fifth guest role.\nIcons and poses that have not been drawn continue to use their inherited art.\n\n**Test in the zoo** reloads enabled mods; **Play with it** starts the normal\ngame. Check the normal buy panel's **Mods** filter, prices, placement, guest\nservice, enclosure gates and staff duties. Different tank filter definitions\nkeep their own footprint, cleaning delay, cleaning strength, wear, upkeep and\nservice settings; placed filter identities are saved with the zoo or map.\n\nIf a replacement looks unchanged, check other enabled mods that change the\nsame file. An earlier mod folder can take precedence. For example, disable\n**Lemonade Stands** when testing **Park Workshop Tutorial**'s replacement of\n`scenery/building/drkstnd.ai`. Local **Publish → Check it** is a package rules\ncheck, not an upload or a complete behavior test.\n\n## Import external animal art in the new studio\n\nIn **Mods**, pick or create a mod, then **Make something → New animal**. Choose its kind, name and starting behavior. Under **Animations**, choose **Draw** beside an animation, then **Blank**. In the editor, **This direction → Import external art** opens the file picker.\n\n| Source | Conversion controls | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| One PNG picture | One picture; visible height; selected direction or repeat | A fitted frame, with its visible bottom at the ground point |\n| Horizontal PNG strip | Horizontal frame strip; column count; height; timing | A sequence for the chosen direction, or the same sequence repeated |\n| Directional PNG sheet | Grid; columns; rows; row directions such as `N,NE,E,SE,S`; height; timing | Frames across each row, assigned to its named direction |\n| OBJ mesh with MTL | Select the OBJ and MTL together, plus any named texture images; Y-up front axis; height | Five rendered views of the supplied mesh |\n| Several OBJ poses | Select the numbered OBJ files together, with MTL/textures; timing | Each file becomes one pose, sorted by filename, rendered in five directions |\n\nUse a regular grid with margins around each sprite. PNG transparency is retained; scaling uses nearest pixels. Check all directions, frames and the ground point before **Save**, which builds the game's animation and palette inside the mod. Import replaces frames in the selected layer for the supplied directions; other matching layers are retained. Undo restores the previous drawing.\n\nA picture does not supply unseen angles: repeating it gives the same view in each direction. OBJ import converts polygon meshes and supplied poses; it does not rig a model or invent a walking cycle. Export Y-up OBJ poses from your modeling application. MTL diffuse colours and selected texture images are supported; advanced material/shader effects are not.\n\nFor a quick prototype, **Link other poses…** reuses one drawn animation for the listed behavior slots, keeping crate and egg poses. Wait for linking to finish before editing needs. A finished animal needs appropriate separate walking, eating, resting and other poses.\n\nUnder **Pictures → Use an animation frame…**, choose one drawn frame to make its icon, list picture and plaque at the game's sizes. Each picture remains editable. Under **Needs**, set its food, habitat, terrain and compatibility; the Inspector sets its price, group size, space and other values.\n\n**Test in the zoo** loads the mod in a temporary test session and restarts the Test Zoo. Your normal mod switches are unchanged. Use **End test** to return to them. Build an appropriate enclosure, add companions, shelter and keepers, and compare its recommendations before and after meeting its needs. **Play with it** starts a separate temporary Play test: **Adopt Animals → Mods → choose the animal → male or female button → place it**. Selecting the icon alone does not start adoption. Stopping that Play session ends its temporary selection; ordinary **Play ZT1 Engine** uses your normal switches.\n\nFor a zoo animal that swims, start from an amphibious species and keep its land\nand water animation slots separate. Test natural entry into the pond and return\nto land, as well as feeding. A marine animal needs a completed tank with enough\nwater depth, the right salinity, a filter beside the tank and the appropriate\ncarer. Check its surface and submerged art with their underlays and shadows;\nmatching frame counts matter. A successful package check does not certify every\nmarine behavior or show trick. Use the Test Zoo's species check for those deeper\ndiagnostics, and compare inherited warnings with the base species.\n\n**Save as a map** preserves the habitat design for ordinary Play. Live animals\nand staff must be adopted again when starting that freeform map.\n\n**Publish → Check it** checks the package locally. **Upload it** sends it to the repository for its own checks; a local pass does not mean it is published. Players download through the game's **Mod Menu → Mod repository**, then switch the downloaded mod on under **Your mods** or **Settings → Mods & Updates**. Downloaded mods remain listed while switched off. Settings has left and right arrow buttons and a page counter for the full installed list.\n\nOptional mod switches apply to the running engine without manually restarting Play. Turning off a replacement restores the stock asset. A pack that supplies the current custom map, or unique animals, objects, fencing, paths, food or filters in use, stays enabled and explains why. Leave that map or remove its dependent custom content first. The engine checkpoints the current zoo privately before refreshing assets and rolls back a change that would lose zoo contents or cash; it does not overwrite the player's save.\n\nA mod can declare external requirements with `\"dependencies\": [\"other-mod-id\"]` (or `\"requires\"`). Required mods must be installed; enabling the dependent mod enables them too. They stay enabled until their dependent mod is switched off. A self-contained theme pack already includes its component files: its `themePack.components` list records those components and does not require the separate component mods to be enabled.\n\n**Download** installs a mod switched off; it is separate from **Enable**. The main-menu Mod Menu and Settings use the same smart switch. After **Load mods** was switched off, enabling one mod activates only that mod and its requirements. The old remembered selection is not reactivated. Temporary tests have their own master switch and load only the selected mod and its requirements.\n\n## Habitat items and theme packs\n\n**Make something → Build an item** supports plants, rocks, shelters, enrichment,\nzoo entrances, tank walls, paths, ground textures and animal food as well as the\nbuilding, fence, person and filter workflows. Choose a separate item or customize\nan existing one. Imported object meshes provide eight directions. A path drawing\ncontains twenty numbered connection shapes, including its ramps; use whole-canvas\nplacement for path sheets and ground textures. Ground textures replace an existing\nterrain's appearance and retain that terrain's gameplay identity.\n\nAn animal's **Needs → Custom food** can select food from an enabled mod. Its normal\ncarer then supplies that food. Dinosaur egg idle, hatch and broken-shell slots are\neditable separately. **Own copy…** makes an independently editable drawing when\nseveral slots currently link to the same animation.\n\nIn **Publish → Build a theme pack…**, include two or more compatible local mods,\ngive the pack its own name, id, version and description, and build it. On **About**,\nchoose its own image or take the Test Zoo view. Then check and upload the pack\nthrough the ordinary Publish controls as one mod. Players install and switch it\non once. Switch off the separate component mods while testing the pack.\n\nA pack is a snapshot. The original mods remain available to edit; build a new\nsnapshot after changing them. Different files at the same game path stop the build\nand name the conflicting mods. Identical shared files are included once. Mods\nwith identity-specific settings currently need to stay separate. Authoring project\nfolders are excluded from the playable pack; retain them separately for editing.\n\nFinish and save asset edits before laying out a map. Check the saved map by\nreopening it; **Save as a map** stores its design, while live animals, guests and\nstaff are created again during Play.\n\n## The rules\n\nThese are the same in the Lab's **Check it** and in the mod repository's own checks:\n\n- **Game data only.** That means `.ztd .ai .ani .pal .tga .bmp .png .webp .jpg .wav .ogg .mp3 .json .ini .txt .md .frag .lyt .uca .ucs .ucb .scn .zoo .lng`, and the extension-less frames inside animation folders. No programs or scripts (`.exe .dll .bat .js .ps1` ...), not even inside a `.ztd`.\n- No paths that point outside the mod, and no links.\n- At most 200,000 files.\n- A `mod.json` with an id and a name.\n\n## Sharing it\n\nIn the Lab: **Mods**, pick your mod, then **Publish → Upload it**. Set its cover\non **About** first, using your own image or the Test Zoo view.\n\n- You need a CodeRook account. Sign in with `cbx sign-in`, or paste a personal access token into the Lab's **Your uploads** panel.\n- The upload is checked automatically. This includes the rules above and a malware scan. Once the checks pass, it's offered in every official game's Mod Menu.\n- **To update it**, raise its version and upload it again.\n- **To take it down**, use **Your uploads › Take it down**. You can only change or remove mods you uploaded yourself.\n\n## Saved zoos with custom content\n\nOrdinary Play defaults to **Shared zoo (.zoo)**: one file holds an\noriginal-compatible stock world and the engine's full native snapshot. The\nengine restores custom entities and elevated paths from its section, with their\nrequired mods enabled. Definitions and artwork still come from those mods.\n\nUnique engine-only animals, staff, objects, food and filters are omitted from\nthe original-compatible section. Custom paths and fences use stock counterparts;\ncustom terrain uses a stock terrain where needed. A replacement of a stock\ndefinition can use that definition's original counterpart. The original needs\nthe relevant expansions/assets; arbitrary fan-mod compatibility is not promised.\n\nAn original-game resave discards the engine snapshot. Save the original-game\ncopy separately if you want to keep engine-only features. **Engine-only save\n(.zt1save)** remains available. New native snapshots use version 14 and cannot\nbe read by older released engines. See [save compatibility and limits](ORIGINAL_SAVE_COMPATIBILITY.md).\n\n## Old mods from the 2001 game\n\nFan-made downloads from 2001 to 2010 (new animals, objects, expansion-style packs) are `.ztd` files, the same kind the game itself uses. To use one you already have:\n\n- **In the game:** Mod Menu › **Add a mod...**, then pick the `.ztd`, or a `.zip` of several.\n- **In the Lab:** Your mods › **Bring in a mod**.\n\nIt becomes a mod of its own, switched off, with a `mod.json` that says what it adds. It loads exactly as it did in the original game. We don't re-host other people's old downloads; bring in the ones you have.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"save-reference","title":"Save between the engine and original","summary":"Shared .zoo files, engine-only saves, custom content and compatibility limits.","group":"Reference","tabs":["play"],"legacy":false,"source":"../../docs/ORIGINAL_SAVE_COMPATIBILITY.md","html":"<p>The local development build can import original saves and write the current\nedited zoo as an original-compatible <code>.zoo</code>. The same file also contains the\nengine&#x27;s full native snapshot. This has been tested in the Lab&#x27;s real original\ngame and ordinary engine Play; it is not yet a claim of complete save parity.\nPublication remains held for the release gates below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-choose-a-save-format\">Choose a save format</h2>\n<p>The ordinary <strong>Save a zoo...</strong> dialog offers:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Choice</th>\n<th>Contents</th>\n<th>Intended use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared zoo (<code>.zoo</code>, default)</td>\n<td>Original TZFB world, followed by the native version-14 snapshot and a validated footer</td>\n<td>Open the compatible world in the original, or restore the engine snapshot in ZT1 Engine.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine-only save (<code>.zt1save</code>)</td>\n<td>Native S1TZ version 14</td>\n<td>Continue in ZT1 Engine without producing an original-compatible world.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Selecting a format updates the default extension for a name without one.\n<strong>Load</strong> accepts both formats, individual filters and <strong>All files</strong>. Contents\ndetermine the loader: renaming a native file does not convert it.</p>\n<p><strong>Keep a separate copy before saving in the original game.</strong> The original\nignores the appended engine snapshot when loading, but discards it when saving.\nThat original resave can be imported again; engine-only content from the earlier\nfile cannot be recovered from the original resave.</p>\n<p>New native saves use version <strong>14</strong>. Read paths for native versions 1-13 remain;\nolder released engines cannot read a new version-14 snapshot. Retain old backups.\nVersion 14 stores a guest&#x27;s favourite species by definition file and keeps recent\nviewed exhibits, so registry reordering does not change the saved preference.\nCustom asset definitions and art still need their mods installed and enabled in\nthe engine: the snapshot stores zoo state, not a bundle of mod resources.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-what-goes-into-the-original-compatible-world\">What goes into the original-compatible world</h2>\n<p>The writer serializes the <strong>current</strong> terrain, paths, boundaries, habitats,\ntanks, objects, animals, guests, staff, food, dung, litter and filters. It also\nwrites mapped money/date/admission, finance, research, awards, marketing and show\nprograms. Added content and later edits are not replaced by an old zoo snapshot.</p>\n<p>Unique definitions supplied only by engine mods remain in the native section.\nThe compatible section omits their animals, staff, objects, filters and food.\nCustom paths and fences use a stock equivalent; custom ground uses stock grass\nwhere necessary. Generic engine dung on a submerged tank floor also remains\nnative-only: the original stock dung definition cannot be placed underwater\nand otherwise prevents filling. Dung on dry ground or a tank&#x27;s dry bank still\nexports. A mod replacing a stock definition can use that definition&#x27;s\nstock counterpart in the original. Elevated engine paths remain native-only.\nThis is a reduced compatible world, not identical mod gameplay in both engines.\nThe Amber Mossbun plus stock zebra test verifies the basic unique-animal case;\narbitrary old fan mods and every theme-pack combination are not certified.</p>\n<p>The receiving original must have the necessary stock assets/expansions. Runtime\ntests here use Marine Mania / Dinosaur Digs <strong>12.04.00.0009</strong>, which reads save\nversions through 106. They do not establish base-only executable acceptance of\nMarine Mania content. Older archives needing tanks or shows are promoted to a\nstock Marine Mania template; the original source archive remains in the native\nsnapshot. Fresh blank zoos use a stock template as well.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-state-coverage-and-limits\">State coverage and limits</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State</th>\n<th>Current coverage and remaining limits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>World and boundaries</td>\n<td>Live tiles, paths, objects, fences and tank walls. Correct owner tiles, original condition periods and shared tank edges. Less common asset/geometry combinations need wider testing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Animals</td>\n<td>Identity, sex, young/egg/crate state, mapped lifetime/status and need timers. Original AI stacks and every in-progress disease/breeding transition are not resumed exactly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Staff</td>\n<td>Identity, appearance, duties and mapped assignments. Original route/task stacks rebuild rather than resume byte-for-byte.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guests</td>\n<td>Names, appearance, needs, inventory, favourite species, arrival, mapped timers and leaving state. Original plain, named-unit and habitat thoughts restore newest-first. Recent original visits use grid coordinates; recent species use stock name IDs. The engine&#x27;s bounded recent-history sets do not preserve exact original visit ordering. Original occupied seats/buildings and in-flight routes rebuild. Native shop users safely replan; show spectators retain their controller state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Money and date</td>\n<td>Live cash/date/admission/rating; mapped monthly changes update annual and lifetime tables with the original row signs and calendar ring. Older monthly detail is not invented. Categories absent from an older format cannot be represented there.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shows</td>\n<td>Import scripts, species, frequency and admission into the engine controller; export current stock-compatible scripts and visible tricks for the original. Replaced species use the stock species ID. Programs with custom behavior or required-item definitions remain native-only. Active original performance progress and spectators rebuild rather than resume exactly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filters</td>\n<td>Real serviceable equipment, position, facing, health and last/current/lifetime costs. Construction month is mapped. Native saves retain decay and cleaning countdowns; the original format does not serialize those countdowns and resets them on load. Exported pump survives an original resave. Exact private viewed date/construction day remain partial.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managers</td>\n<td>Mapped research, awards and marketing. Unknown manager/unit data is retained where possible; stale original event queues are cleared on live export. Full scenario events/goals remain unfinished.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>View and ground</td>\n<td>Current camera centre and all four rotations convert both ways at original half/normal/double zoom. Arbitrary native zoom is retained natively and quantized to an original-supported zoom for interchange. Native pre-tank ground snapshots remain exact; original-only imports have no pre-excavation snapshot, so demolition uses surrounding ground height and grass. Original tank floors and terrain are retained.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The original archive is bounded and reparsed when read from native state. The\ncombined file has a versioned 36-byte <code>ZT1EXTRA</code> footer, native offset/length and\nCRC32 for each stream. Missing/truncated trailers and damaged streams fail\nvalidation instead of silently loading a reduced zoo. The archive excludes the\nnative extension, preventing recursive growth on repeated saves. Writes use a\ntemporary file and atomic replacement. These checks do not make unknown fields\nfully understood or promise lossless original AI continuation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-verified-through-the-lab\">Verified through the Lab</h2>\n<p>All game controls, C++ edits, builds and original executable inspection used the\nLab MCP. Files below are copies under\n<code>E:/zt1-shots/save-compatibility-2026-10-29/</code>; source saves were not overwritten.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Observed result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>marine3-shared-08.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Original loads a newly placed dolphin and Drink Stand: 3 animals, 11 buildings, 5 guests, 3 staff, 92 paths, 62 tank walls. Original resave retains the additions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>show-export-13.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Original identifies the show tank and displays Wave to Crowd, Spray Water, Continuous and $5.50.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dolphin-shared-17.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Engine and original display the new Flip/TailWalk program, Frequent and $7.25. <code>dolphin-og-resave-18.zoo</code> retains both tricks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dino-new-31-shared.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Newly placed T. rex egg, scientist and reinforced enclosure load in the original. <code>dino-og-resave-32.zoo</code> retains the young animal and scientist.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>custom-native-33.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Native snapshot restores Amber Mossbun and stock zebra; ordinary engine Play renders the custom animal with its temporary test mod. Original loads the same file with just the stock zebra. Normal mod switches are untouched.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>new-filter-37.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Original creates a real ZTTankFilter, not decoration. <code>filter-og-resave-38.zoo</code> retains <code>filter/filter1/f</code>; screenshot <code>original-filter-38.png</code> shows it facing the tank.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>base-litter-39.zoo</code></td>\n<td>Original loads exported litter. <code>base-litter-og-resave-40.zoo</code> retains it, and ordinary engine Play reimports one live litter pile with no missing records.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>blank-shared-46.zoo</code></td>\n<td>A wholly blank 75x75 Lab world saves without an imported archive. Native read-back retains zero placed objects/actors; the original loads the blank terrain with ambient wildlife. <code>blank-og-resave-48.zoo</code> parses completely.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Save dialog</td>\n<td>Shared default, engine-only selection, switching back, extension changes and cancellation verified. <code>engine-only-filter-28.zt1save</code> is native; <code>shared-filter-29.zoo</code> contains both streams.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The expanded save/import regression passes <strong>1,431/1,431</strong> across four real original saves:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fixture</th>\n<th>Checks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lab-sweep-marine3</code></td>\n<td>294/294</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lab-sweep-marine2</code></td>\n<td>678/678</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lab-sweep-marine</code></td>\n<td>234/234</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lab-show-zoo</code></td>\n<td>225/225</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Fresh Dinosaur Digs and base-game maps pass another <strong>160/160</strong> and <strong>118/118</strong>,\nfor <strong>1,709/1,709</strong> total (<code>final-save-regression-159.json</code>).\nTank-specific checks are explicitly skipped on maps without a tank. Financial\nchecks use admission income for older formats that lack the show-income row.\nEach fixture also checks twelve view combinations, missing definitions, failed\nreplacement, malformed/truncated state, and native reload after tank demolition.\nNormal Load validates a separate world and retains the current zoo on failure;\nmissing content is named. Original workers caught on ladders or inside exhibits\nrestart on accessible ground instead of being silently lost or rejecting the zoo.\nCoverage includes full native files, a different-size bootstrap, show scripts,\ncurrent finance transactions/year rollover, engine-only decks, CRC failures,\ntruncated/missing footers and stable repeated saves.</p>\n<p>The retained parser sweep covers 108 available files: 105 map sections, 99 full\nmanager sections and <strong>106,050/106,050</strong> exactly decoded entity payloads. Three\nold scenario versions are rejected by this original executable too; six patched\nscenario copies still stop at thoughts. This is parser coverage, not runtime\nacceptance of every zoo. Activity checks pass 77/77, behavior/parity 57/57 and\nbuilding/DRT 55/55. Marine management previously passed 30/30; show polish passes\n39/39 with 18/18 nested show-start checks. Full native/shared file reloads cover\nshow gathering/performance/return, four specialist climb stages, pump service,\nhatching, sleep, illness, a shop visit, helicopter flight and darted-dinosaur\ncontainment. Supported controllers resume; other tasks safely replan.</p>\n<p>Retained native files from versions 7, 8, 10, 11 and 12 load. Some old Lab\nfixtures used placeholder <code>road</code> paths and <code>grass</code> chow; known legacy keys map\nto stock equivalents if their own definitions do not exist. The old original\nimport&#x27;s generic <code>food</code> key is corrected using its exhibit&#x27;s occupants. These\ntargeted migrations do not bypass missing custom definitions in current saves.</p>\n<p>The exported marine fixture <code>final-show-42.zoo</code> ran in the original from January\n1 to January 6; its specialist supplied food and <code>final-show-og-resave-47.zoo</code>\nretains the orca, trainer, tanks and script. The fixture&#x27;s welfare warning\ninterrupted the wall-clock interval; this is not a completed long soak.\nOrdinary engine Load then restores two habitats, one food pile, the orca, its\ntrainer and seven guests from that original resave, with zero missing records.\n<code>final-checks-43.json</code> records all six save suites and the repeated activity/show\nchecks. <code>shared-file-validation-45.json</code> records nine file structure/CRC checks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-reference-and-release-gates\">Reference and release gates</h2>\n<p>SlendyMilky&#x27;s <a href=\"#\" data-help-web=\"https://github.com/AlexC1991/zt1-engine/issues/5#issuecomment-6443994223\" title=\"Open in your browser\">follow-up</a>\npointed to RustyZoo&#x27;s versioned reader/writer. The local reference is pinned to\n<code>4250545173284cdd4963a47cde7fbf6edf7073b4</code>. Its files are GPL-3.0; no Rust source\nwas vendored, copied into or linked with the engine. Independent C++ serialization\nwas checked against the original loaders and actual original-game load/resave.\nPreserving unknown bytes is distinct from decoding or restoring their meaning.</p>\n<p>Before public release, rebuild the packages from this final source, repeat the\nmatching-engine checks, test installation/upgrade/updater relaunch in isolation,\nand complete the owner&#x27;s packaged Lab test. Include stock land, marine/show and\ndinosaur zoos plus a modded zoo, new filenames, original resaves and backups.\nKeep scenario parity, exact original AI continuation and wider\nmod/asset coverage open. The older package inventory does not include this work.</p>","toc":[{"id":"choose-a-save-format","title":"Choose a save format","level":2},{"id":"what-goes-into-the-original-compatible-world","title":"What goes into the original-compatible world","level":2},{"id":"state-coverage-and-limits","title":"State coverage and limits","level":2},{"id":"verified-through-the-lab","title":"Verified through the Lab","level":2},{"id":"reference-and-release-gates","title":"Reference and release gates","level":2}],"text":"# Original save compatibility - 29 October 2026\n\nThe local development build can import original saves and write the current\nedited zoo as an original-compatible `.zoo`. The same file also contains the\nengine's full native snapshot. This has been tested in the Lab's real original\ngame and ordinary engine Play; it is not yet a claim of complete save parity.\nPublication remains held for the release gates below.\n\n## Choose a save format\n\nThe ordinary **Save a zoo...** dialog offers:\n\n| Choice | Contents | Intended use |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Shared zoo (`.zoo`, default) | Original TZFB world, followed by the native version-14 snapshot and a validated footer | Open the compatible world in the original, or restore the engine snapshot in ZT1 Engine. |\n| Engine-only save (`.zt1save`) | Native S1TZ version 14 | Continue in ZT1 Engine without producing an original-compatible world. |\n\nSelecting a format updates the default extension for a name without one.\n**Load** accepts both formats, individual filters and **All files**. Contents\ndetermine the loader: renaming a native file does not convert it.\n\n**Keep a separate copy before saving in the original game.** The original\nignores the appended engine snapshot when loading, but discards it when saving.\nThat original resave can be imported again; engine-only content from the earlier\nfile cannot be recovered from the original resave.\n\nNew native saves use version **14**. Read paths for native versions 1-13 remain;\nolder released engines cannot read a new version-14 snapshot. Retain old backups.\nVersion 14 stores a guest's favourite species by definition file and keeps recent\nviewed exhibits, so registry reordering does not change the saved preference.\nCustom asset definitions and art still need their mods installed and enabled in\nthe engine: the snapshot stores zoo state, not a bundle of mod resources.\n\n## What goes into the original-compatible world\n\nThe writer serializes the **current** terrain, paths, boundaries, habitats,\ntanks, objects, animals, guests, staff, food, dung, litter and filters. It also\nwrites mapped money/date/admission, finance, research, awards, marketing and show\nprograms. Added content and later edits are not replaced by an old zoo snapshot.\n\nUnique definitions supplied only by engine mods remain in the native section.\nThe compatible section omits their animals, staff, objects, filters and food.\nCustom paths and fences use a stock equivalent; custom ground uses stock grass\nwhere necessary. Generic engine dung on a submerged tank floor also remains\nnative-only: the original stock dung definition cannot be placed underwater\nand otherwise prevents filling. Dung on dry ground or a tank's dry bank still\nexports. A mod replacing a stock definition can use that definition's\nstock counterpart in the original. Elevated engine paths remain native-only.\nThis is a reduced compatible world, not identical mod gameplay in both engines.\nThe Amber Mossbun plus stock zebra test verifies the basic unique-animal case;\narbitrary old fan mods and every theme-pack combination are not certified.\n\nThe receiving original must have the necessary stock assets/expansions. Runtime\ntests here use Marine Mania / Dinosaur Digs **12.04.00.0009**, which reads save\nversions through 106. They do not establish base-only executable acceptance of\nMarine Mania content. Older archives needing tanks or shows are promoted to a\nstock Marine Mania template; the original source archive remains in the native\nsnapshot. Fresh blank zoos use a stock template as well.\n\n## State coverage and limits\n\n| State | Current coverage and remaining limits |\n| --- | --- |\n| World and boundaries | Live tiles, paths, objects, fences and tank walls. Correct owner tiles, original condition periods and shared tank edges. Less common asset/geometry combinations need wider testing. |\n| Animals | Identity, sex, young/egg/crate state, mapped lifetime/status and need timers. Original AI stacks and every in-progress disease/breeding transition are not resumed exactly. |\n| Staff | Identity, appearance, duties and mapped assignments. Original route/task stacks rebuild rather than resume byte-for-byte. |\n| Guests | Names, appearance, needs, inventory, favourite species, arrival, mapped timers and leaving state. Original plain, named-unit and habitat thoughts restore newest-first. Recent original visits use grid coordinates; recent species use stock name IDs. The engine's bounded recent-history sets do not preserve exact original visit ordering. Original occupied seats/buildings and in-flight routes rebuild. Native shop users safely replan; show spectators retain their controller state. |\n| Money and date | Live cash/date/admission/rating; mapped monthly changes update annual and lifetime tables with the original row signs and calendar ring. Older monthly detail is not invented. Categories absent from an older format cannot be represented there. |\n| Shows | Import scripts, species, frequency and admission into the engine controller; export current stock-compatible scripts and visible tricks for the original. Replaced species use the stock species ID. Programs with custom behavior or required-item definitions remain native-only. Active original performance progress and spectators rebuild rather than resume exactly. |\n| Filters | Real serviceable equipment, position, facing, health and last/current/lifetime costs. Construction month is mapped. Native saves retain decay and cleaning countdowns; the original format does not serialize those countdowns and resets them on load. Exported pump survives an original resave. Exact private viewed date/construction day remain partial. |\n| Managers | Mapped research, awards and marketing. Unknown manager/unit data is retained where possible; stale original event queues are cleared on live export. Full scenario events/goals remain unfinished. |\n| View and ground | Current camera centre and all four rotations convert both ways at original half/normal/double zoom. Arbitrary native zoom is retained natively and quantized to an original-supported zoom for interchange. Native pre-tank ground snapshots remain exact; original-only imports have no pre-excavation snapshot, so demolition uses surrounding ground height and grass. Original tank floors and terrain are retained. |\n\nThe original archive is bounded and reparsed when read from native state. The\ncombined file has a versioned 36-byte `ZT1EXTRA` footer, native offset/length and\nCRC32 for each stream. Missing/truncated trailers and damaged streams fail\nvalidation instead of silently loading a reduced zoo. The archive excludes the\nnative extension, preventing recursive growth on repeated saves. Writes use a\ntemporary file and atomic replacement. These checks do not make unknown fields\nfully understood or promise lossless original AI continuation.\n\n## Verified through the Lab\n\nAll game controls, C++ edits, builds and original executable inspection used the\nLab MCP. Files below are copies under\n`E:/zt1-shots/save-compatibility-2026-10-29/`; source saves were not overwritten.\n\n| Test | Observed result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `marine3-shared-08.zoo` | Original loads a newly placed dolphin and Drink Stand: 3 animals, 11 buildings, 5 guests, 3 staff, 92 paths, 62 tank walls. Original resave retains the additions. |\n| `show-export-13.zoo` | Original identifies the show tank and displays Wave to Crowd, Spray Water, Continuous and $5.50. |\n| `dolphin-shared-17.zoo` | Engine and original display the new Flip/TailWalk program, Frequent and $7.25. `dolphin-og-resave-18.zoo` retains both tricks. |\n| `dino-new-31-shared.zoo` | Newly placed T. rex egg, scientist and reinforced enclosure load in the original. `dino-og-resave-32.zoo` retains the young animal and scientist. |\n| `custom-native-33.zoo` | Native snapshot restores Amber Mossbun and stock zebra; ordinary engine Play renders the custom animal with its temporary test mod. Original loads the same file with just the stock zebra. Normal mod switches are untouched. |\n| `new-filter-37.zoo` | Original creates a real ZTTankFilter, not decoration. `filter-og-resave-38.zoo` retains `filter/filter1/f`; screenshot `original-filter-38.png` shows it facing the tank. |\n| `base-litter-39.zoo` | Original loads exported litter. `base-litter-og-resave-40.zoo` retains it, and ordinary engine Play reimports one live litter pile with no missing records. |\n| `blank-shared-46.zoo` | A wholly blank 75x75 Lab world saves without an imported archive. Native read-back retains zero placed objects/actors; the original loads the blank terrain with ambient wildlife. `blank-og-resave-48.zoo` parses completely. |\n| Save dialog | Shared default, engine-only selection, switching back, extension changes and cancellation verified. `engine-only-filter-28.zt1save` is native; `shared-filter-29.zoo` contains both streams. |\n\nThe expanded save/import regression passes **1,431/1,431** across four real original saves:\n\n| Fixture | Checks |\n| --- | --- |\n| `lab-sweep-marine3` | 294/294 |\n| `lab-sweep-marine2` | 678/678 |\n| `lab-sweep-marine` | 234/234 |\n| `lab-show-zoo` | 225/225 |\n\nFresh Dinosaur Digs and base-game maps pass another **160/160** and **118/118**,\nfor **1,709/1,709** total (`final-save-regression-159.json`).\nTank-specific checks are explicitly skipped on maps without a tank. Financial\nchecks use admission income for older formats that lack the show-income row.\nEach fixture also checks twelve view combinations, missing definitions, failed\nreplacement, malformed/truncated state, and native reload after tank demolition.\nNormal Load validates a separate world and retains the current zoo on failure;\nmissing content is named. Original workers caught on ladders or inside exhibits\nrestart on accessible ground instead of being silently lost or rejecting the zoo.\nCoverage includes full native files, a different-size bootstrap, show scripts,\ncurrent finance transactions/year rollover, engine-only decks, CRC failures,\ntruncated/missing footers and stable repeated saves.\n\nThe retained parser sweep covers 108 available files: 105 map sections, 99 full\nmanager sections and **106,050/106,050** exactly decoded entity payloads. Three\nold scenario versions are rejected by this original executable too; six patched\nscenario copies still stop at thoughts. This is parser coverage, not runtime\nacceptance of every zoo. Activity checks pass 77/77, behavior/parity 57/57 and\nbuilding/DRT 55/55. Marine management previously passed 30/30; show polish passes\n39/39 with 18/18 nested show-start checks. Full native/shared file reloads cover\nshow gathering/performance/return, four specialist climb stages, pump service,\nhatching, sleep, illness, a shop visit, helicopter flight and darted-dinosaur\ncontainment. Supported controllers resume; other tasks safely replan.\n\nRetained native files from versions 7, 8, 10, 11 and 12 load. Some old Lab\nfixtures used placeholder `road` paths and `grass` chow; known legacy keys map\nto stock equivalents if their own definitions do not exist. The old original\nimport's generic `food` key is corrected using its exhibit's occupants. These\ntargeted migrations do not bypass missing custom definitions in current saves.\n\nThe exported marine fixture `final-show-42.zoo` ran in the original from January\n1 to January 6; its specialist supplied food and `final-show-og-resave-47.zoo`\nretains the orca, trainer, tanks and script. The fixture's welfare warning\ninterrupted the wall-clock interval; this is not a completed long soak.\nOrdinary engine Load then restores two habitats, one food pile, the orca, its\ntrainer and seven guests from that original resave, with zero missing records.\n`final-checks-43.json` records all six save suites and the repeated activity/show\nchecks. `shared-file-validation-45.json` records nine file structure/CRC checks.\n\n## Reference and release gates\n\nSlendyMilky's [follow-up](https://github.com/AlexC1991/zt1-engine/issues/5#issuecomment-6443994223)\npointed to RustyZoo's versioned reader/writer. The local reference is pinned to\n`4250545173284cdd4963a47cde7fbf6edf7073b4`. Its files are GPL-3.0; no Rust source\nwas vendored, copied into or linked with the engine. Independent C++ serialization\nwas checked against the original loaders and actual original-game load/resave.\nPreserving unknown bytes is distinct from decoding or restoring their meaning.\n\nBefore public release, rebuild the packages from this final source, repeat the\nmatching-engine checks, test installation/upgrade/updater relaunch in isolation,\nand complete the owner's packaged Lab test. Include stock land, marine/show and\ndinosaur zoos plus a modded zoo, new filenames, original resaves and backups.\nKeep scenario parity, exact original AI continuation and wider\nmod/asset coverage open. The older package inventory does not include this work.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"lab-reference","title":"Complete Lab reference","summary":"Detailed tools, panels, recordings, traces, scripts and engine investigation.","group":"Reference","tabs":[],"legacy":false,"source":"../README.md","html":"<p><strong>Help:</strong> the tab beside Mods contains searchable guides, illustrated tutorials\nand nine bundled videos. Start with <strong>Start with the Lab</strong>, or search for an\nanimal, OBJ import, a tank, a map or publishing. Press <strong>F1</strong> from any Lab tab.\nThe library works offline and includes the earlier overview, labelled as an\nearlier interface. Current lessons cover the redesigned Mods studio.</p>\n<p>The friendly face of the Lab, built with Electron to the design in\n<code>re/lab/design/</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Item builder:</strong> Mods → Make something → Build an item creates separate\nbuildings, fences, staff, tank filters and scenery, or customizes a selected\ngame item. Draw with Blank and external PNG/OBJ import, or Remake existing art.\nGuest appearances target the game&#x27;s four existing guest kinds; staff inherit\nexisting duties. Check service, damage and work poses as well as idle/walk.\nSee <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"modding-reference#build-buildings-fences-people-and-objects\">the modding guide</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>From a checkout:</strong> double-click <strong><code>ZT1 Lab.cmd</code></strong>, or run:</p>\n<pre><code>npm start\n</code></pre>\n<p>It needs <code>build\\Release\\zt1-engine.exe</code> built first. The first time, it\ninstalls its packages (Electron) into <code>node_modules/</code>, which isn&#x27;t\ncommitted.</p>\n<p><strong>Installed:</strong> <code>dist/ZT1-Lab-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code> (a release&#x27;s third\ndownload), which is this app - Electron and\nall - with the engine and what the Lab needs inside it, and no admin\nprompt. It installs into <code>%LocalAppData%\\Programs\\ZT1-Lab</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ZT1 Lab.exe</code></td>\n<td>the app (the page inside <code>resources/app.asar</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tree\\engine\\</code></td>\n<td><code>zt1-engine.exe</code>, built for release, and the hook for the original game</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tree\\re\\</code></td>\n<td>the knowledge base and the Lab&#x27;s Python tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tree\\clangd\\</code></td>\n<td>the code intelligence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tree\\ghidra\\</code></td>\n<td>Ghidra itself, and <code>tree\\java\\</code> the JDK it runs on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tree\\python\\</code></td>\n<td>a Python of its own, with what the Lab&#x27;s tools need</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Nothing here asks the player to install Python or Java: <code>tree\\python</code>\nis an interpreter with <code>capstone</code>, <code>pefile</code> and <code>unicorn</code> beside it, and\n<code>tree\\ghidra</code> is Ghidra with the JDK it runs on. <code>tree\\ghidra</code> is the\nprogram, not a project of the game: a Ghidra project is built out of\n<code>zoo.exe</code> itself - the game, which is nobody&#x27;s to hand out - so the Lab\nmakes one from the player&#x27;s own copy.</p>\n<p>The installer asks for the engine&#x27;s checkout (and remembers it in\n<code>%LocalAppData%\\zt1-engine\\lab-roots.json</code>). With one, the Lab reads and\nedits the real tree - Write Code, Build engine, Game Files, Sync, the\ncode intelligence - exactly as running from a checkout does. Without\none, it runs on what it carries: the lab zoo, Explore, Original Game&#x27;s\nfunctions and code, the game&#x27;s own files, Play. What needs a checkout\nsays so when you click it.</p>\n<p><strong>One thing it does not carry, and will not: the game.</strong> <em>Zoo Tycoon</em> is\nnot free, and the Lab never ships it. It reads a copy you already own and\nhave installed - the original <code>zoo.exe</code> for Original Game, Play original\nand the traces, and the game&#x27;s own files for the Scene and Game Files. It\nfinds it by itself, or you point it at yours:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>re/private/original.json</code> — <code>{ &quot;exe&quot;: &quot;&lt;your zoo.exe&gt;&quot;, &quot;iso&quot;: &quot;&lt;a Marine Mania disc image, if your copy needs the disc&gt;&quot; }</code></li>\n<li><code>ZT1_EXE</code> for the Scene&#x27;s <strong>Try zoo.exe</strong></li>\n<li>Game Files and the Terrain painter find the install by themselves (the usual\n  <code>Microsoft Games\\Zoo Tycoon</code>, or the Complete Collection)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Everything the Lab <em>does</em> carry is ours or third-party under its own\nlicence: the engine (<code>tree\\engine</code>), the Lab&#x27;s own tools and its knowledge\nbase (<code>tree\\re</code>), clangd, an embeddable Python with <code>capstone</code>, <code>pefile</code> and\n<code>unicorn</code>, and Ghidra with the JDK it runs on. No part of the game is in\nthe installer, and <code>re/private</code> - your recordings, your game&#x27;s location -\nnever leaves your machine.</p>\n<p>Each of those carries its licence with it: the build writes\n<code>THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt</code> from the licence text each project ships (and says\nso when one does not), and <code>SOURCE-OFFER.txt</code> is the written offer of\ncorresponding source for the GPL-covered ones — the Temurin JDK and unicorn —\nbecause that source is not in the installer. Both sit beside <code>LICENSE.txt</code> in\nthe install; <code>engine-build-resources/notices.py</code> writes them, and\n<code>fetch_licenses.py</code> keeps the one text no package provides (unicorn&#x27;s GPLv2).</p>\n<p>The installer is built on the release machine. Its packing and the\nbackend it carries (a Python with <code>capstone</code>, <code>pefile</code> and <code>unicorn</code>, and\nGhidra with its JDK) are part of the release tooling, which isn&#x27;t in this\nrepository.</p>\n<p>The <strong>checkout</strong> needs none of that: it runs on the machine&#x27;s own\nPython 3, so that still needs <code>unicorn</code>, <code>capstone</code> and <code>pefile</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-shared-knowledge-base\">The shared knowledge base</h2>\n<p>Everything the Lab works out about the original&#x27;s <code>zoo.exe</code> - what\n<code>0x4378df</code> is, a struct&#x27;s fields, which functions ran in a recording - is\nthe same answer for every install, because it is all about one build of\none game. So it can be shared: <code>re/cloud/</code> is a Cloudflare Worker and a\ndatabase that moves findings between checkouts, and <code>re/knowledge.json</code>\nstays the copy that ships and that you review in git.</p>\n<pre><code>python re/cloud/sync.py pull        # bring in what others have found\npython re/cloud/sync.py contribute  # send what this checkout worked out\n</code></pre>\n<p>The released Lab and checkout include the shared endpoint, so no per-user\nsetup is needed. <code>re/private/cloud.json</code> or <code>ZT1_KB_API</code> can override it.\n<strong>Sharing</strong> in the Lab&#x27;s header turns it on (off until you do): the Lab then\nreceives and sends in the background - at start and every half hour, sending\nonly what changed - and its dialog shows when it last did and <strong>Share now</strong>.\nThe Lab can sync from its control port too, so an AI agent can\n(<code>lab_kb_cloud</code>). Anything contributed lands <strong>provisional</strong> until it is\nreviewed, and a contribution from a <code>zoo.exe</code> build other than the 2002\nComplete Collection is refused - its addresses would land on the wrong\nfunctions. Recordings never leave the machine; the addresses they found\ndo. See <strong><a><code>re/cloud/README.md</code></a></strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-explore-start-here\">Explore (start here)</h2>\n<p>The original game has no source code and no documentation. What&#x27;s known\nabout it is spread across the knowledge base, the recordings, Ghidra and our\nengine&#x27;s comments. <strong>Explore</strong> joins all of it into one map you can ask in\nplain words. Nothing is sent anywhere: the search runs in the Lab.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ask</strong> &quot;selling animals&quot;, &quot;show tank&quot;, &quot;where is the zoo saved&quot;,\n  &quot;cHungerCheck&quot; or an address like <code>0x4378df</code>. Each answer says what it is\n  (a function, a recorded action, a config key, a struct, our engine&#x27;s\n  code), why it matched, and where to go next:</li>\n<li><strong>Open its code</strong> shows the function in Original Game.</li>\n<li><strong>Open in Write Code</strong> jumps to the engine line.</li>\n<li><strong>Which game files set it</strong> runs a <code>has:</code> search for a key.</li>\n<li><strong>What&#x27;s missing</strong> lists every action recorded in the original, against\n  our engine:</li>\n<li><strong>Copied from the original</strong> and <strong>Partly traced</strong> come from what our\n    engine&#x27;s code cites, so they&#x27;re reliable.</li>\n<li><strong>Probably in our engine, not traced</strong> and <strong>Not found - check</strong> are\n    guesses from the action&#x27;s words, until someone checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Not in our engine</strong> only ever comes from a person&#x27;s checked verdict.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Open an action&#x27;s <strong>Details</strong> to see:\n  - our engine&#x27;s closest code;\n  - the functions that run only for that action, green where our engine\n    cites them (click one to read it);\n  - everything that ran in the recording.</p>\n<p>Checked it? Press <strong>Our engine does this</strong> or <strong>Not in our engine</strong>. The\n  verdict is saved in <code>re/lab/verdicts.json</code> with your name.\n- <strong>The map</strong> is <code>re/private/atlas.json</code>, built by <code>re/lab/atlas.py</code> from\n  the knowledge base, the recordings, Ghidra&#x27;s function list and texts, and\n  every comment and function in <code>src/</code>. When you record or name something,\n  Explore says the map is stale; <strong>Rebuild the map</strong> takes a few seconds.\n- From an AI agent (MCP): <code>lab_action exAsk &quot;&lt;question&gt;&quot;</code>, <code>exMissing [status]</code>,\n  <code>exOpenAction &quot;&lt;label&gt;&quot;</code>, <code>exVerdict &quot;&lt;label&gt;|&lt;status&gt;|&lt;file:line&gt;|&lt;note&gt;&quot;</code>\n  and <code>atlasBuild</code>.</p>\n<p>The usual loop for a modder:\n1. Play the original (Play › Play original) and record an action.\n2. Rebuild the map, and look the action up in Explore.\n3. Read its own functions (Open its code), write the feature (Write Code),\n   and cite the addresses in a comment.\n4. Rebuild: it turns green.</p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-code-11-with-zooexe-explores-right-side\">Code 1:1 with zoo.exe (Explore&#x27;s right side)</h3>\n<p>Our engine is meant to be the original, 1:1. <strong>Code 1:1 with zoo.exe</strong>\n(next to Recorded actions) shows how far it is, both ways round. It&#x27;s built\nfresh each time by <code>re/lab/fidelity.py</code>, and the answer is saved to\n<code>re/private/fidelity.json</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formula checks</strong> (<code>re/lab/formulas.json</code>) run the original&#x27;s function\n  in zoorun and ours on the same numbers, case by case:</li>\n<li>On the original&#x27;s side, zoorun lays the objects out from each case&#x27;s\n    inputs, and reads back ST0 for a float, eax, or a field.</li>\n<li>On our side, the engine&#x27;s <code>formula</code> command calls the very function\n    the game calls.</li>\n<li>The checks run again by themselves after every build that works, and\n    a toast says if any case differs.</li>\n<li>To add one, make our formula a function the game and the check both\n    call. Then add it to <code>formulas.json</code> and to the <code>formula</code> command in\n    <code>src/Lab.cpp</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The original&#x27;s gameplay, by system</strong>: every function the knowledge\n  base names, grouped by system (money, tasks, jobs, guests, animals,\n  staff…). Each is green if our engine cites its address and red if it\n  doesn&#x27;t. Click one to open it.</li>\n<li><strong>Our code, by file</strong>: every function in our gameplay, UI and drawing\n  code, and whether it says where it came from. A function counts as\n  marked when its comment or body has one of:</li>\n<li>a <code>zoo.exe 0x…</code> address (copied);</li>\n<li><code>measured:</code> (measured from the original&#x27;s behaviour, saying how);</li>\n<li><code>ours:</code> (the original has nothing like it);</li>\n<li><code>addition:</code> or a <code>Features::</code> switch (a new feature).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rest are listed to go through. <strong>#</strong> counts the <em>bare numbers</em>: a\n  number with a point in it on a line that doesn&#x27;t say where it&#x27;s from.\n  Those belong in <code>src/OriginalConstants.hpp</code> with their address, or come\n  from the game&#x27;s files (economy.cfg&#x27;s keys are read into\n  <code>Original::economy()</code>).\n- From an AI agent (MCP):\n  - <code>lab_action exMode fidelity</code>\n  - <code>exFidOpen &quot;o:&lt;system&gt;&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;f:src/&lt;file&gt;&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;x:&lt;check id&gt;&quot;</code>\n  - <code>fxRun</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-views\">The views</h2>\n<p>The tabs are named for what you do in each: <strong>Original Game</strong> studies the\noriginal zoo.exe; <strong>Write Code</strong>, <strong>Test Zoo</strong> and <strong>Play Game</strong> are our\nengine; then <strong>Mods</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tab</th>\n<th>Was</th>\n<th>What it&#x27;s for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Original Game</strong></td>\n<td>Functions + Code</td>\n<td>One window for the original zoo.exe. Left: find a function - search names, notes and the game&#x27;s text (with the functions using each), <code>field:0x3d6</code>, filter by system. Middle: its code - decompiled C, or raw instructions where Ghidra made no function. Right: name it and note what it does (saved to the knowledge base and Ghidra), what calls it, the fields it uses, where our engine copies it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Write Code</strong></td>\n<td>Engine code</td>\n<td>Our engine&#x27;s own source (and, on the left, the game&#x27;s files): write the feature, <strong>Build engine</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Test Zoo</strong></td>\n<td>Scene</td>\n<td>Our engine running a test zoo, as a scene editor and debugger (below).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Play Game</strong></td>\n<td>Play</td>\n<td><strong>Play ZT1 Engine</strong>: the whole game as our engine runs it now. <strong>Play original</strong>: the real zoo.exe, here in the same view, windowed and at the view&#x27;s size by our hook (see <em>The original game in Play</em> below).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mods</strong></td>\n<td>Experiments</td>\n<td>Three sections, switched at the top of its left column. <strong>Mods</strong>: make, test and share mods (see <em>Mods</em> below). <strong>Game files</strong>: every file the game has. <strong>Lab scripts</strong>: small C++ scripts that run in the Test Zoo without changing the engine - checking one of the original&#x27;s functions against ours, or making things such as the HD art mod (see <em>Making mods with Lab scripts</em>); edit, see what it will do in plain words, <strong>Build &amp; test</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Help</strong></td>\n<td>New</td>\n<td>Search the bundled guides, follow the current studio tutorials and play the recordings. Article history, section links and enlarged pictures keep longer lessons easy to navigate.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Laid out as you like:</strong> Original Game (Find · Code · Name &amp; details), Write\nCode (Files · Editor · Build) and Test Zoo are made of panels. Drag a panel&#x27;s\ntab onto another&#x27;s side to dock it there, onto its middle to stack them, or\nout to float it; drag the edges to resize. <strong>▦ Layout</strong> (top right) brings a\nclosed panel back or resets the tab to its default. Each tab&#x27;s layout is\nremembered.</p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-finding-things-the-lab-couldnt-before\">Finding things the Lab couldn&#x27;t before</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Game files, <code>has:text</code></strong> looks <em>inside</em> the game&#x27;s text files (.ai, .cfg,\n  .lyt…), the copies the game really uses (a patch&#x27;s over the original).\n  Spaces and case don&#x27;t count: <code>has:cpreattack=1</code> lists every animal with\n  <code>cPreattack = 1</code>, with the matching lines under each file, and opening one\n  jumps to that line. Combine it with words: <code>animals/trex.ai has:bpreattack]</code>.\n  (A section is written <code>[m\\BehaviorSet\\bPreattack]</code>, so search the name with\n  just its closing bracket.) An open game file&#x27;s bar says what our engine\n  makes of it: where our code names it, or - for a species&#x27; .ai, which goes\n  through the one loader - how many of its keys our engine reads (<code>107 of\n  its 193 keys: Class, Type…</code>, each a link to where it&#x27;s read).</li>\n<li><strong>Functions, <code>field:0x3d6</code></strong> lists every instruction in zoo.exe that\n  touches that offset - read or written, and the function it&#x27;s in (or the\n  bare code). It&#x27;s how you find who uses a value the species loader filled.\n  Add a size to keep to it: <code>field:0x3b0 byte</code> (or <code>word</code>, <code>dword</code>). The\n  function each is in comes from Ghidra (a function&#x27;s code can be in\n  pieces). Above the list, a form names that offset in a struct\n  (<code>ZTAnimal</code> + <code>0x3b0</code> = <code>rampaging</code>, bool) - saved to the knowledge base,\n  then shown as Known and suggested in Write Code.</li>\n<li><strong>Where used on a global</strong> (an address from 0x600000, a value in the\n  game&#x27;s data): the box has <strong>Name it</strong> - <code>0x641560</code> = <code>cDefenseRadius</code> -\n  saved to the knowledge base&#x27;s constants. From then on the decompiled C\n  shows the name beside it: <code>DAT_00641560 cDefenseRadius</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Find in the code</strong>: the Code panel&#x27;s header (it stays in view) has a Find\n  box - the lines holding the words lit, Enter for the next, Shift+Enter\n  back, <code>1 of 3</code>. It&#x27;s kept as you open other functions, and a function\n  newly opened goes to its first match.</li>\n<li><strong>Where used on text nobody uses</strong>: a string marked &quot;not used by any\n  function (yet found)&quot; has a <strong>Where used</strong> button. Its places are often in\n  code Ghidra never made a function of (reached through a table).</li>\n<li><strong>Raw instructions</strong>: open such an address and Code shows zoo.exe&#x27;s\n  instructions there (re/lab/zoobin.py, capstone), from where that code\n  starts (a guess: just after the code before it ends), the address marked,\n  and the text each instruction points at named (<code>&quot;Started preattack&quot;</code>).\n  The page is titled <code>Code at 0x…</code> with <strong>no function yet</strong>, and\n  <strong>Make it a function</strong> (in the header, always in view) turns that code into a Ghidra function from where it\n  starts, and Original Game then shows its decompiled C, callers and all. The\n  starts are kept in <code>re/made_functions.json</code>: the Lab&#x27;s Ghidra bridge makes\n  them again each time it starts (it opens zoo.exe read only), and\n  <code>ZT1_Apply</code> makes them for good in your own Ghidra project.</li>\n<li><strong>Engine code suggests the binary</strong>, beside clangd&#x27;s C++ suggestions:\n  inside <code>ZT_STEP(&quot;</code> or <code>ZT_FN(&quot;</code> type a name, part of what a function does\n  or an address, and pick from zoo.exe&#x27;s mapped functions (the knowledge\n  base&#x27;s first, then Ghidra&#x27;s named ones) - the address goes in. After <code>+0x</code>,\n  the mapped struct fields (<code>ZTAnimal+0x3</code> → zapCheck, egg, hatchIn…). Inside\n  <code>num(&quot;</code> / <code>set(m, &quot;</code> / <code>pushSet(m, &quot;</code>, the game&#x27;s own .ai keys and\n  behaviour sets, with how many files use each (<code>num(&quot;cpre</code> → cPreattack in\n  24 files). Hover an address for its name and summary, or a game key for\n  where it&#x27;s set. Name what you find (Functions › Name) and it&#x27;s suggested\n  from then on.</li>\n<li><strong>Scene › Watch</strong> (a panel): follow animals and the engine logs a row each\n  time one starts doing something else or goes a tile, in game time - paused,\n  playing or skipping time. A hunt reads like a script: <code>roar_up_left</code>,\n  <code>shake</code>, <code>snort</code>, <code>dustball</code>, <code>(gone)</code>. Pick an animal, then <strong>Follow</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Steps during a time skip</strong>: skipping 200 s keeps the steps the engine\n  reports on the way (the last 1500), so a rampage that starts at 140 s is\n  there to read afterwards. A skip from an agent (<code>lab_scene_time</code>) waits\n  till the engine says the skip is done.</li>\n<li><strong>Grow the database</strong> (top of Find, in Original Game): what zoo.exe says\n  about itself, beside what <code>re/knowledge.json</code> has, saved in one click. Each\n  save is under the tool&#x27;s name (&quot;Lab loader mapper&quot;), so it&#x27;s told apart\n  from what you name yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Config loaders</strong>: every key the game reads (<code>cHungerCheck</code>) and where it\n    goes, a struct&#x27;s field or a global, grouped by the function reading them.\n    The readers it looks for are in <code>re/lab/loaders.json</code> (int, string,\n    float). Name the struct a loader fills (<code>ZTKeeperType</code>) and <strong>Save</strong>: each\n    key becomes a field at its offset where nothing&#x27;s named yet, each global\n    is labelled with its key, and the loader itself is named\n    (<code>ZTKeeperType::loadConfig</code>, or <code>loadAnimalSettings</code> for a loader of\n    globals only). &quot;N in the base&quot; counts what&#x27;s mapped already.</li>\n<li><strong>Class tables</strong>: the 223 tables of functions (vtables) zoo.exe&#x27;s\n    constructors store. Open one to see each slot&#x27;s function and which named\n    classes share it: shared slots are the class they come from (ZTAnimal and\n    ZTGuest share 112 of 197). Name it and <strong>Save</strong>, and <code>kb.py show</code> then\n    says which tables a function is in (<code>ZTAnimal (slot 0x2c)</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>The game&#x27;s text</strong>: functions named by their own debug text\n    (<code>GXVidMan::flip</code>) and source files from the developers&#x27; paths\n    (<code>gx/gxvidman.cpp</code>). <strong>Save all</strong> gives provisional names where nothing&#x27;s\n    named; a function with only a file gets <code>gxvidman_4d64ab</code> till it has a\n    name. One text in several functions is numbered (<code>unlockSurface2</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Refresh the Lab&#x27;s Ghidra</strong> (top right of the window): the Lab&#x27;s Ghidra\n    reads a copy of your project (<code>E:\\zt1-ghidra-work</code>), so after ZT1_Apply,\n    or naming things in your own Ghidra, this copies your project over it and\n    restarts the bridge. Close your project in Ghidra first: it&#x27;s locked while\n    it&#x27;s open.</li>\n<li><strong>Naming a class table:</strong> let the census (Play › the bar&#x27;s readout) say\n    what its objects are (<code>helicopter</code>, <code>herbchow</code>). A type&#x27;s table has its\n    config loader at slot 0x60, so <code>kbClassCompare</code> names the type tables.\n    Comparing slots finds the base classes: a table no object uses that\n    shares most of its slots with a whole family (ZTStaff, ZTUnit). The census\n    uses the knowledge base&#x27;s class names.</li>\n<li>From an AI agent (MCP), <code>lab_action</code>: <code>kbTab loaders|classes|text</code>,\n    <code>kbLoaderBrief all</code> (every loader&#x27;s keys, a line each), <code>kbLoaderKeys</code>,\n    <code>kbLoaderSave &quot;0x4c34ae|ZTKeeperType&quot;</code> (add <code>|likely</code> for a guess), <code>kbClassSlots</code>,\n    <code>kbClassCompare &quot;0x62ed84 0x62f0b4&quot;</code> (each table&#x27;s slots shared with every\n    named class, its own, and its known functions), <code>ghidraRefresh</code>, <code>kbClassSave\n    &quot;0x62ff54|ZTAnimal&quot;</code>, <code>kbTextSave</code>, and <code>kbOrphans</code> / <code>kbMakeOrphans</code> (the\n    engine&#x27;s zoo.exe references that fall where Ghidra made no function, each\n    with where its function would start and what comes before it; check the\n    raw code before making one, because a piece of a bigger function or a\n    jump table can look like a start).</li>\n<li><strong>The Test Zoo survives a build</strong>: Build engine restarts the engine, and\n  the Lab then makes the zoo again - the map, every fence, animal and object\n  placed with the mouse, the pins, the Watch follows - and runs it back to\n  the same time. The engine log says so (&quot;made again after the build: 17\n  steps of set-up, back to 199 s&quot;). Loading a map starts the list afresh.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-scene\">The Scene</h2>\n<p>The Scene is our engine&#x27;s real world, the same code the game runs. You can\nbuild in it, watch its code run step by step, and try zoo.exe&#x27;s own code on\nanything in it.</p>\n<p><strong>Laid out as you like:</strong> every piece of the Scene tab is a panel. The pieces\nare the Scene view, Zoo, Inspector, Steps, Step, Our code, zoo.exe C, zoo.exe\nbinary, Compare, Errors, Try zoo.exe, Log and Console. You can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>drag a panel&#x27;s tab onto another panel&#x27;s side to dock it there, onto its\n  middle to put it in with that panel as a tab, or off to float it;</li>\n<li>drag the edges between panels to size them;</li>\n<li>close a panel with its ×.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>▦ Layout</strong> (top bar) brings panels back and switches the Scene view&#x27;s\nfloating pieces on and off: the tools, the map and view bar, play and time,\nthe status box (what&#x27;s under the cursor), and the exhibit names. It also\nsaves layouts by name, loads them, and resets to the default. The layout is\nkept between runs. <strong>Classic</strong> goes back to the fixed layout (<code>layout.js</code>,\nusing dockview-core).</p>\n<p><strong>The zoo</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maps:</strong> the map name (top right, or top left) loads any of the game&#x27;s\n  freeform maps, as it is or made flat (all grass, nothing in it). The game&#x27;s\n  files are what the engine reads, expansions included. The Lab is where\n  unfinished things get tried.</li>\n<li><strong>Save as a map</strong> (beside the map name): the zoo as a freeform map of your\n  own, in a mod - its name, description, size, and which mod. The engine\n  writes the design (<code>SaveGame::saveMap</code>: its ground, paths, walkways,\n  fences, exhibits and objects over the map it was loaded from, and the view\n  it starts on) and re/lab/mapmaker.py packs it: <code>freeform/&lt;id&gt;.scn</code> (the map\n  it&#x27;s over&#x27;s .scn, with <code>[start] design=</code> and <code>[freeform] title=</code>),\n  <code>freeform/&lt;id&gt;.zt1map</code>, its description and a 367x276 picture of the view\n  you&#x27;re looking at. Switched on, the mod&#x27;s map is in the game&#x27;s freeform map\n  list (the &quot;custommaps&quot; switch).</li>\n<li><strong>A blank map</strong> (the map list&#x27;s first entries): flat grass with nothing on\n  it, Small 75 x 75, Medium 125 x 125 or Large 150 x 150 as the game&#x27;s maps\n  are, or any size from 16 to 512 a side. It starts in <strong>Edit the map</strong>\n  (beside the map name; <strong>✎ Editing the map</strong> while on): walls, entrances\n  and scenery go anywhere, not only inside the zoo. Paint the ground and\n  paths, draw the zoo&#x27;s boundary with the Zoo Fence, and put down an\n  entrance - the Zoo Entrance, Dinosaur Digs&#x27; or Marine Mania&#x27;s (<code>aqgate</code>:\n  the game has no name for it), and their admission booths, all under\n  Buildings - where guests should come in: the wall opens across its\n  doorway. Switch editing off to see the zoo\n  as the game will (its inside is what the wall and entrance close in; the\n  Exhibits view and the Terrain view show the walls, the zoo wall in red).\n  Saved as a map, it&#x27;s packed with <code>[start] blank = W H</code> and no map of the\n  game&#x27;s under it; the game&#x27;s map view draws what you made.</li>\n<li><strong>Terrain</strong> (the view): the whole map from above - each tile its ground&#x27;s\n  colour, lit by its height, paths pale, objects as dots - to design a map\n  by. The tools work in the Scene view (the Lab&#x27;s own place tool switches\n  back to it).</li>\n<li><strong>Zoo outline</strong> (left): every exhibit, animal, staff member, guest,\n  building and kind of scenery. Click one to select it and centre on it. The\n  <strong>Mods</strong> tab beside it holds the mods.</li>\n<li><strong>Placing</strong> (<strong>＋ Place</strong>, or P): the buy panels&#x27; items by tab, with the\n  game&#x27;s icons, plus terrain paint and height tools. Each is put down with\n  the game&#x27;s own tool, so the same rules apply. Fences and paths have the\n  drag modes (Bend, Straight, Rectangle: Tab). Animals can be male or\n  female, objects turn (R), and the terrain brush has a size.</li>\n<li><strong>Other tools:</strong> <strong>Bulldoze</strong> (B), <strong>Undo</strong> (Ctrl+Z), and right-click to\n  stop a tool.</li>\n<li><strong>Animal</strong> (the view): a species&#x27; art on its own. Pick any of its\n  animations; <strong>Layers</strong> draws a Marine Mania animation&#x27;s layers together\n  (&quot;shadow&quot; + its art, &quot;under&quot; + its art, then the animation, at one anchor:\n  the red cross); ◀ ▶ hold every layer on one frame and step it. <strong>Check</strong>\n  goes through the species in bulk: art missing or not loading, layers missing\n  or with other frame counts, behaviour-set steps the engine skips (not done\n  yet), moves to places it doesn&#x27;t know, and animations or sets named but not\n  there. <strong>Check marine</strong> does it for every tank animal.</li>\n<li><strong>Route</strong> (T): can it get there? Pick <strong>Guest</strong> or <strong>Staff</strong> and click\n  where from, then where to (each click after is a new where to). Or pick\n  <strong>Picked animal</strong> and click where to. The search runs by that kind&#x27;s own\n  rules: guests on paths and not into exhibits, staff out through gates with\n  paths cheaper, an animal on its exhibit&#x27;s ground. It&#x27;s drawn on the ground:</li>\n<li>what it looked at, fainter first</li>\n<li>what was still open (yellow)</li>\n<li>tiles it couldn&#x27;t go onto (red)</li>\n<li>steps refused, such as a fence or cliff edge (red edges)</li>\n<li>the route (green), the start (blue) and the goal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The chip says how it went.\n- <strong>Routes</strong> (an overlay): where everyone is walking (animals orange, guests\n  blue, staff green, goals as diamonds), and the picked one&#x27;s last route\n  search drawn as above. Its inspector has a <strong>Route</strong> section: where it&#x27;s\n  going, and how its last search went. If it failed, it says why: no way\n  there, gave up at its limit, or off the map. Each search is also a step\n  (&quot;Route search done&quot;) you can stop on. <strong>Blocked</strong> is the engine&#x27;s older\n  staff overlay: where staff can&#x27;t walk.\n- <strong>Inspector</strong> (right): click anything (an animal, guest, keeper, building,\n  rock, exhibit, the ground) for its live values. Type a new value to change\n  it. <strong>ƒ</strong> opens the zoo.exe code behind a stat.\n- <strong>Pins</strong> (⊙ beside a stat): an animal&#x27;s or guest&#x27;s value held where it&#x27;s\n  set, after every update. Pin a lion&#x27;s hunger at 100 and watch what the\n  engine does about it. Its steps still show what the engine tries each\n  tick, so you see the rule fire and the pin hold.\n- <strong>Actions</strong> (the inspector): buttons for what&#x27;s picked.\n  - <strong>animals:</strong> hungry, tired, sick, heal, happy, unhappy, dung, dart,\n    crate, plus any of its animations (<strong>▶ Play</strong>) or behaviour sets\n    (<strong>▶ Do</strong>), on demand\n  - <strong>guests:</strong> hungry, thirsty, bathroom, tired, leave, their animations\n  - <strong>staff:</strong> their animations\n  - <strong>exhibits and tanks:</strong> the game&#x27;s console commands: food, no food,\n    dung, wear and break fences, dirty water</p>\n<p>Each press is a step in the Steps dock.\n- <strong>Clock</strong> (bottom left): run or pause (Space), 1–8×, or +1 s / +1 m of\n  game time. A running mod shows its steps there instead.\n- <strong>Overlays</strong> (top right): the grid, exhibits&#x27; outlines and names, routes,\n  where staff are blocked, footprints. Footprints also marks each door (a\n  building&#x27;s <code>entrpos</code>, turned with it): green on a path, yellow off one. The\n  console&#x27;s <code>doors</code> lists them. The console&#x27;s <code>filters</code> shows each filter&#x27;s\n  state and sound, and <code>drain</code> / <code>fill</code> empty or refill a tank. A selected\n  tour guide shows its followers; a selected guest, the guide it follows. Under the cursor (bottom right) you see the tile, its\n  height and ground.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps</strong> (the dock under the view): the engine&#x27;s code as it runs.\nThe engine&#x27;s code reports its steps through trace points (<code>src/LabTrace.hpp</code>:\n<code>ZT_STEP</code>, <code>ZT_FN</code>, <code>ZT_WHO</code>). Each step gives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the file and line</li>\n<li>the zoo.exe function that code is ours of</li>\n<li>what it read, and what it changed (before → after)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The steps are listed on the left (drag the divider to widen it). Beside them,\nthe step picked: what, who, when, its zoo.exe function, and all its values,\nthen panes, one or two side by side (<strong>◫ Split</strong>; <strong>⤢</strong> makes the dock tall):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Our code:</strong> the engine&#x27;s line (lit, its values under it, what ran since\n  the last step tinted).</li>\n<li><strong>zoo.exe C:</strong> the function it copies, decompiled.</li>\n<li><strong>zoo.exe binary:</strong> that function&#x27;s own machine code run on what&#x27;s picked\n  (as <strong>Try zoo.exe</strong> runs it): every instruction, the registers, and what\n  each read and wrote, by field name; red where it touched something nobody&#x27;s\n  mapped. It runs on the values as they are now (stopped at the step: as they\n  were then).</li>\n<li><strong>Compare:</strong> the step&#x27;s values beside what the original read or wrote in\n  the field of the same name: same, or differs.</li>\n<li><strong>Errors:</strong> what the original reached for and couldn&#x27;t find (fields not\n  mapped, empty vtables, null pointers), each with <strong>Name it</strong> and <strong>Give a\n  value</strong>; it runs again after.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tabs show counts: values that differ, and errors.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trace:</strong> what&#x27;s picked (an exhibit includes its animals, and using a tool\n  traces the tool), everything, or nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Stopping:</strong> <strong>Stop at each step</strong>, <strong>Stop at the next</strong>, or a breakpoint\n  (click a line number, or F9). The engine really stops there: the world\n  frozen mid-update, still drawn, the one it&#x27;s stopped on ringed in cyan.\n  <strong>Continue</strong> (F8), <strong>Over</strong> (F10), <strong>Step</strong> (F11), <strong>Out</strong> (Shift+F11).</li>\n<li>What has trace points so far:</li>\n<li><strong>animals:</strong> status checks, behaviour choices, eating, adopting, exhibit\n    ratings</li>\n<li><strong>guests:</strong> arriving, needs, deciding where to go, watching and what they\n    think of it, donations, prices and buying, leaving, every thought</li>\n<li><strong>staff:</strong> looking for work, keepers&#x27; visits (food, healing, raking),\n    maintenance jobs, catching escaped animals, tour guides</li>\n<li><strong>the tools:</strong> placing, fences, paths, bulldozing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More code gets them as it&#x27;s worked on.</p>\n<p><strong>Try zoo.exe</strong> (the dock): run one of the original game&#x27;s functions on\nwhat&#x27;s picked. Find a function in Code, Functions or Steps and press <strong>Try it\nin the Scene</strong> (or type its address or name). <code>re/lab/zoorun.py</code> runs zoo.exe&#x27;s\nown x86 code (Unicorn), sandboxed. What&#x27;s picked is laid out as the knowledge\nbase says its struct is, filled with live values: the animal as ZTAnimal, its\nspecies as ZTAnimalType from its <code>.ai</code>. You see:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>in red, what it wanted and couldn&#x27;t find:</strong> an offset nobody&#x27;s mapped,\n  an empty pointer or vtable. Each says <strong>where</strong> (the instruction), with\n  <strong>Name it</strong> (saved to the knowledge base; use the engine&#x27;s name for it and\n  it&#x27;s filled live), <strong>Give a value</strong>, and <strong>Game files</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>what it used:</strong> known fields, with their values (read, or before →\n  after), the text it used, the globals (as in the file), the functions it\n  called (<strong>Skip</strong> them, 0 back, or <strong>Run them</strong> too).</li>\n<li>its instructions as they ran, with the registers and what each touched,\n  and its C with the lines that touched known (green) or unknown (red)\n  fields lit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Run it again after naming or filling something, until it&#x27;s all green. Then\n<strong>Apply what it wrote</strong> puts its results into the scene, and <strong>Make it a\nmod</strong> starts a mod for it. It needs <code>pip install unicorn capstone</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Log</strong> and <strong>Console</strong> (the dock): the engine&#x27;s log, and the game&#x27;s own dev\nconsole (<code>help</code> lists its commands). A command&#x27;s answer always shows, even when the log was scrolled up. Ones for modders: <code>text &lt;id&gt; ...</code> (the game&#x27;s words for ids - cNameID, cUsedThought), <code>doors</code> (each building&#x27;s door tiles, on a path or not), <code>fences</code> (the map&#x27;s fences by type, which are zoo wall, how many tiles are inside) and <code>fences map</code> (inside <code>#</code>, outside <code>.</code>, zoo wall <code>+</code>, drawn - when nothing will build, it shows where the wall leaks).</p>\n<p>The place tool says when the game put nothing down (an iceberg off a tank&#x27;s water, say) instead of saying nothing. The Watch panel empties when a new map or engine starts. A scene load sent while the engine restarts is sent again to the new one; a map name it doesn&#x27;t know says so and what loaded instead (a mod&#x27;s map is listed once the mod is on and the engine has started again). <strong>Save as a map</strong> puts the map&#x27;s description in a new mod&#x27;s About too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-how-it-works\">How it works</h2>\n<p><code>main.js</code> starts our engine twice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Lab&#x27;s engine</strong> (<code>zt1-engine.exe --lab-host 18490</code>) is out of sight\n  and silent. It builds and steps the mods, and sends its view to the app as\n  frames.</li>\n<li><strong>The game</strong> (<code>zt1-engine.exe --embed &lt;window&gt;</code>) is a borderless window\n  kept over the Play view, moving and hiding with the Lab.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both close when the Lab closes.</p>\n<p>Ghidra comes in through the <code>ZT1_LabBridge</code> script (127.0.0.1:18489). The\nLab starts it for itself: when nothing answers there as it opens, it runs a\nheadless Ghidra on the project, read only (<code>ZT1_LabBridgeHeadless.py</code>; the\npill says <strong>Ghidra starting</strong> for a minute or two), and closes it with the\nLab. Where Ghidra and the project are: <code>re/private/ghidra.json</code>\n(<code>{&quot;home&quot;: &quot;...ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC&quot;, &quot;project&quot;: &quot;E:\\\\zt1-ghidra-work&quot;,\n&quot;name&quot;: &quot;zt1&quot;}</code>) if you make one, else <code>E:\\ghidra\\ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC</code> and\n<code>E:\\zt1-ghidra-work</code> (a copy of <code>E:\\zt1-ghidra</code>, so your own Ghidra&#x27;s\nproject isn&#x27;t locked) or <code>E:\\zt1-ghidra</code>. Its output: <code>re/private/ghidra-bridge.log</code>.\nYour own Ghidra with the bridge on is used instead (in Ghidra, with zoo.exe\nopen: Tools &gt; zt1-engine &gt; <strong>Start Lab bridge</strong>, or <strong>Ctrl+Alt+L</strong> on a\nfunction). Everything fetched is saved in <code>re/private/ghidra/</code>, so code\nyou&#x27;ve seen still shows with Ghidra closed.</p>\n<p>What the game prints in Play goes to <code>re/private/play.log</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-engine-code\">Engine code</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Write Code</strong> tab is our engine&#x27;s own source, edited and built in the\nLab. The editor is Monaco (VS Code&#x27;s editor), and clangd (<code>clangd/</code>, the C++\nlanguage server) works on the code behind it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-scripts-new-script\">Scripts (＋ New script)</h3>\n<p><strong>＋ New script</strong> (beside ＋ New file) makes a script in <code>src/scripts/</code>:\nengine code, built in with the rest, with the zoo&#x27;s parts set up for you\n(<code>src/EngineScripts.hpp</code>). Pick its name, what it does and when it runs:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>When it runs</th>\n<th>Its function</th>\n<th>Run it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Run once</td>\n<td><code>void fn(Zoo &amp;zoo)</code></td>\n<td><strong>▶ Build &amp; run</strong> on its bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Every tick</td>\n<td><code>void fn(Zoo &amp;zoo)</code></td>\n<td><strong>▶ Build &amp; switch on</strong> (off again on the same button)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Each animal, every tick</td>\n<td><code>void fn(Zoo &amp;zoo, Animals::Member &amp;m)</code></td>\n<td>the same</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>zoo</code> holds <code>world</code>, <code>animals</code>, <code>guests</code>, <code>staff</code>, <code>fences</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>clock</code>\nand <code>dt</code>, and what&#x27;s picked in the Test Zoo: <code>animal</code> (a pointer, or\nnullptr), <code>guest</code>, <code>staffMember</code>, <code>exhibit</code> (ids, -1 for none), <code>tileX</code>,\n<code>tileY</code>. <code>zoo.say(&quot;...&quot;)</code> writes a line in the engine log and a step in\nSteps; <code>num(x)</code> writes a number as words. Each template starts with a small\nworking example and a list of all of these.</p>\n<p>The bar over an open script shows its state (not built in yet / on) and\nbuilds it first if anything changed. The engine restarts on the build, the\nTest Zoo is made again as it was (with what was picked, and the scripts\nthat were on), then the script runs. Nothing runs unless the Lab switches it\non, so a script never runs in Play. A script is a place to try a piece of\ngame code on the live zoo, then move it where it belongs. Copying zoo.exe?\n<code>ZT_STEP(&quot;0x…&quot;, …)</code> in a script counts as in our engine like anywhere else.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Editing:</strong> highlighting, coloured brackets, folding, a minimap,\n  multi-cursor, and undo.</li>\n<li><strong>Finding:</strong> Ctrl+F finds in the file, Ctrl+H replaces, and Ctrl+G goes to\n  a line. The box on the left searches the whole engine.</li>\n<li><strong>Code intelligence:</strong> autocomplete of the engine&#x27;s own classes, hover to\n  see a type, F12 to go to a definition (in any file, opened in a new tab),\n  and Shift+F12 for every use.</li>\n<li><strong>Errors:</strong> they show as you type, as red squiggles with &quot;did you mean…&quot;\n  fixes. Build errors show the same way.</li>\n<li><strong>Calls:</strong> right-click in a function › <strong>Who calls this</strong> (Shift+Alt+H)\n  shows its callers in the <strong>Calls</strong> tab under the editor; each opens to its\n  own callers, and a click goes to the call. <strong>What it calls</strong> turns it the\n  other way round.</li>\n<li><strong>Right-click</strong> also has: search the engine for the word, <strong>Scene: stop\n  here</strong> (a breakpoint, F9), <strong>Open its zoo.exe function</strong> (on a line whose\n  <code>ZT_STEP</code>/<code>ZT_FN</code> names an address), <strong>Copy file:line</strong> and <strong>Build engine</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The libraries</strong> (<code>vendor/</code>: SDL, libzip, the PE loader) open and change\n  here too: type <code>vendor/</code> and a name in the search box.</li>\n<li><strong>＋ New file</strong> (beside the search box) makes a new <code>.cpp</code>, <code>.hpp</code> or <code>.h</code>\n  in <code>src/</code>; it&#x27;s built with the rest on the next build.</li>\n<li><strong>Game mods.</strong> In Game files, an animation open in the viewer has\n  <strong>✦ Make a mod from this…</strong> (<code>re/lab/animaker.py</code>): pick one of its colours\n  (and, ticked, its other shades), the colours it flickers through a frame\n  each, and its speed, and it writes the new frames and palette in the\n  game&#x27;s own format as a mod: <code>build/Release/mods/&lt;name&gt;/mod.json</code> and\n  <code>content.ztd</code>. <strong>Switch it on</strong> restarts the Lab engine with it loaded.\n  The game loads the mods switched on (<code>mods.cfg</code>) before its own files\n  (<code>src/Mods.cpp</code>), and players switch them in the main menu&#x27;s <strong>Get New\n  Zoo Tycoon Items</strong>, the Mod Menu (<code>src/ModMenu.cpp</code>). Both are switches\n  (<code>mods</code>, <code>modmenu</code> in <code>src/Features.hpp</code>): off, the original&#x27;s files only.</li>\n<li><strong>Testing menus in Play:</strong> the Lab can click inside the game\n  (<code>playCmd</code> action: <code>click &lt;x&gt; &lt;y&gt;</code> at the game window&#x27;s pixels;\n  <code>src/Embed.cpp</code>), and the game holds still while the Play tab isn&#x27;t shown.\n  <code>move &lt;x&gt; &lt;y&gt;</code> rests the mouse there (hovering: tooltips, lit pieces);\n  <code>press &lt;x&gt; &lt;y&gt;</code> / <code>release &lt;x&gt; &lt;y&gt;</code> hold and let go of the left button, so\n  <code>playSteps &quot;press 870 337; move 920 337; wait 700; release 920 337&quot;</code> is a\n  drag at your own pace (the game follows the mouse&#x27;s events, not the real\n  mouse, for drags);\n  <code>snap</code> saves the next frame that draws a tooltip as <code>tooltip.bmp</code> beside\n  the game - its own frame, as a window grab of the GPU window can come\n  back stale. <code>shot &lt;file.bmp&gt;</code> saves the next whole frame - the same line\n  the original&#x27;s hook answers, so <code>lab_play shot</code> (a .png) pictures either\n  game in Play. The game holds still while the Play tab is hidden, so a\n  picture needs the Play tab showing. The dev console&#x27;s\n  <code>tankwalls &lt;ex&gt; &lt;height&gt;</code> sets a tank&#x27;s walls (a raised tank shows its\n  staff climbing in and out).</li>\n<li><strong>Screen size in Play:</strong> the game draws 1:1 at the Play view&#x27;s size, as\n  the original does at any size (menus centred, the in-game screen filling\n  it). The Lab owns the window, so the game&#x27;s own window-size and full\n  screen buttons are greyed in Play. The game prints <code>[Embed] ready</code> once\n  its window exists, and the Lab places it over the Play view then.</li>\n<li><strong>Game Options:</strong> the main menu&#x27;s Game Options (in Get New Zoo Tycoon\n  Items&#x27; place) and the in-game disk button open the Options window\n  (<code>src/ui/UiOptions.cpp</code>): Game (in a zoo), Graphics, Sound, Controls,\n  Gameplay, Mods &amp; Updates, every setting kept in zoo.ini. The\n  <code>optionswindow</code> extra off brings back the original side panel. A\n  checkout&#x27;s loose <code>build/Release/ui/startup.lyt</code> overrides the game&#x27;s;\n  the engine edits its Game Options line by key, so either works.</li>\n<li><strong>Build engine</strong> saves every file and builds the engine. The lab zoo\n  restarts on the new build, and <strong>Restart the game</strong> in Play runs it.</li>\n<li><strong>Make release</strong> builds the engine x64 Release in <code>build-x64/</code> (the C++\n  runtime built in, today&#x27;s date as its version) and its installer,\n  <code>dist/ZT1-Engine-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code>, with its <code>.sha256</code> and\n  <code>dist/latest.yml</code>, the in-game updater&#x27;s feed. It runs the release\n  tooling (<code>engine-build-resources/make_release.py</code>, which needs Inno\n  Setup 6), which is private and only on the release machine; without it\n  the button says so.</li>\n<li>The installer holds the engine, its fonts, the licences and the\n    player&#x27;s README. It holds none of the original game&#x27;s files: the engine\n    reads the player&#x27;s own install where it is.</li>\n<li>A build made here isn&#x27;t official, so it never updates itself. Only\n    published releases are.</li>\n<li>\n<p>Nothing is uploaded: releases are published from the release machine\n    (CONTRIBUTING.md).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Sync</strong> (the tab under the editor) takes the engine to GitHub and CodeRook.\n  GitHub (<code>AlexC1991/zt1-engine</code>) is the home: work goes on branches, and\n  into main by pull request. CodeRook keeps a public mirror of main.\n  1. <strong>Get latest main</strong> merges GitHub&#x27;s main into your branch. Any conflicts\n     are listed: <strong>Take mine</strong>, <strong>Take theirs</strong>, or open the file and fix it\n     by hand, then <strong>Fixed it</strong>.\n  2. <strong>Commit</strong> commits everything changed. <code>.gitignore</code> keeps <code>build/</code>, the\n     game&#x27;s files, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.cache</code> and <code>dist/</code> out.\n  3. <strong>Push</strong> pushes your branch.\n  4. <strong>Propose</strong> opens a pull request into main on this repo, never on the\n     upstream it was forked from.\n  5. <strong>Mirror main to CodeRook</strong> (the owner only) sends main as it is on\n     GitHub to CodeRook. It works from a copy of main in <code>.cache/mirror-main</code>\n     using <code>cbx submit</code>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>▶ Sync</strong> does 1 to 4 in one go, after showing what goes where. What&#x27;s\n  offered follows what GitHub says the person may do:\n  - the owner: everything, including the mirror;\n  - helpers with write access: push their branch, then propose;\n  - anyone else: their fork is made and pushed to, and the proposal comes\n    from there.</p>\n<p>Nothing is ever force-pushed (<code>lab-app/sync.js</code>).</p>\n<p><code>compile_flags.txt</code> at the repo&#x27;s root tells clangd how the engine is\ncompiled. Each time the Lab starts clangd it writes\n<code>.cache/lab-clangd/compile_commands.json</code> from it (every source in <code>src/</code>),\nso clangd indexes the whole engine and <strong>Who calls this</strong> finds callers in\nfiles that aren&#x27;t open.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-mods-the-mods-tabs-mods-section\">Mods (the Mods tab&#x27;s Mods section)</h2>\n<p>Making mods properly, and sharing them. The guide for modders is <code>docs/MODDING.md</code>.</p>\n<p>The section is a <strong>studio</strong> (renderer/studio.js): one workspace for the mod being made.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The top bar</strong>: the mod (click it for <strong>the picker</strong>: every mod in the game&#x27;s mods folder, <strong>Find a mod</strong>, each one&#x27;s on/off switch - switched on, it&#x27;s in the Test Zoo and in Play when they next start - <strong>New mod</strong>, <strong>Bring one in</strong>, <strong>Mods folder</strong>), its switch, <strong>All saved</strong> / <strong>Not saved</strong>, <strong>▶ Test in the zoo</strong> (switched on, the Test Zoo&#x27;s engine restarted with it), <strong>Play with it</strong>, <strong>Publish</strong> and <strong>Folder</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The tree</strong> (left): everything the mod holds, by kind, read by re/lab/studio.py from its .ztd files - its <strong>Animals</strong> (its own, <em>new</em>, and the game&#x27;s it changes, <em>changed</em>), <strong>Buildings</strong>, <strong>Scenery</strong>, <strong>Foliage</strong>, <strong>Fences</strong>, <strong>Paths</strong>, <strong>Staff</strong> and <strong>Maps</strong> (its <code>freeform/*.scn</code>) - with <strong>＋</strong> on a kind to add one; <strong>＋ Make something</strong>, <strong>About this mod</strong>, and under <strong>Tools</strong> <strong>Paint and recolour art</strong>, <strong>Its files</strong> and <strong>Publish</strong>. (Whether a file changes one of the game&#x27;s is read from a list of the game&#x27;s file names kept in <code>%TEMP%\\zt1-lab-names.txt</code>, made again when the archives change.)</li>\n<li><strong>Make something</strong>: cards, each starting from something that already works in the game - <strong>New animal</strong> (a name and an animal it behaves like), <strong>New map</strong> (a blank map of a size you pick, in the Test Zoo with editing on; <strong>Save as a map</strong> offers this mod), <strong>Change a game item</strong> (any of the game&#x27;s animals, buildings, scenery, fences, paths or staff, copied in).</li>\n<li><strong>The middle</strong> is the editor for what&#x27;s picked: an animal&#x27;s animations and the animation editor, a building&#x27;s or a scenery item&#x27;s picture and file, a map&#x27;s preview and <strong>Edit it in the Test Zoo</strong>, and the About, Files and Publish pages below.</li>\n<li><strong>The Inspector</strong> (right): the picked animal&#x27;s, building&#x27;s or item&#x27;s values from its file as controls, grouped and named in plain words - an animal&#x27;s price, food and rest, group and space, babies, what it can do (jumps, climbs, swims...), its habitat wishes, guests and health; a building&#x27;s price and size, guests inside, what they pay and what it does for them - and <strong>All its values</strong> for the rest. A change is saved into the mod&#x27;s file as you leave the box (its line changed in place, the file&#x27;s line ends kept); the file open in Write Code is updated too. Nothing picked: the mod&#x27;s own details.</li>\n<li><strong>An animal&#x27;s Needs</strong> (its <strong>Needs</strong> tab, renderer/needs.js; re/lab/animalmaker.py <code>needs</code>, <code>setneeds</code>, <code>copyneeds</code>): everything the game weighs for it, from its species file. <strong>Kind and care</strong>: its kind (<code>[Member]</code>: a zoo animal, Marine Mania&#x27;s <code>aqua</code>, Dinosaur Digs&#x27; <code>dinosaur</code>), who looks after it (<code>cKeeper</code>: a keeper, the Scientist 9551, the Marine Specialist 9552), what it eats and which food (<code>cKeeperFoodType</code>: herbivore chow, meat, fruit, bamboo, grass, fish chow, insects, dinosaur meat and greens, krill, fish, chum, sea grass, sea urchins - food.cfg&#x27;s order, the expansions&#x27; after), needs a shelter. <strong>Home</strong>: biome, continent (or era), family. <strong>Ground it likes</strong> (<code>[cCompatibleTerrain]</code>, a slider each), <strong>biomes, plants, rocks and shelters it likes</strong> (<code>[cSuitableObjects]</code>, with their icons; + Add, ×) and <strong>animals it lives with</strong> (<code>[cCompatibleAnimals]</code>, families and species). Names come from the game&#x27;s own text (the engine&#x27;s <code>texts</code>) and the catalog (its items&#x27; <code>nameId</code>). Saved into the mod a moment after the last change. <strong>Copy needs from…</strong> another animal: its food, home, ground, likes and neighbours at once (its kind and keeper too, if asked) - a preset. A game animal a mod changes has the Needs tab too.</li>\n<li><strong>New animal</strong> asks its kind first - a zoo animal, a marine animal, a dinosaur - and then offers only animals of that kind to behave like (its behaviour, home, keeper and, for a dinosaur, its egg come with it). Problems says when a kind doesn&#x27;t fit its behaviour: a dinosaur with no egg (<code>egg_idle</code>, <code>egg_hatch</code>, <code>egg_break</code>), a marine animal with no underwater behaviour.</li>\n<li><strong>The dock</strong> (bottom): <strong>Problems</strong> - what&#x27;s missing or wrong, each one a link to where it&#x27;s fixed (an animal&#x27;s animations that play nothing, an animal still all linked to another&#x27;s, no picture, no About, a map without a description, a .ztd the game can&#x27;t read) - and the mod&#x27;s <strong>Log</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>New mod</strong> starts one from a template: a starter kit, game files (a .ztd), a shader, or empty.\n    The <strong>starter kit</strong> (re/lab/modstarter.py) is a working example of everything a mod can\n    have, made from the player&#x27;s own game so no game art ships with the Lab: a new rock (a\n    gold copy of the Large Rock, as a <code>.ucs</code> with its map art, icons and palettes) in the\n    Habitat panel&#x27;s Rocks under the Mods filter, its HD art (<code>hd/</code>, 4x), an art shader (off\n    at first), a mod.json setting the shader reads, and a README.md walking through each file.</li>\n<li><strong>Bring one in</strong> takes an old Zoo Tycoon download (.ztd), a .zip, or a mod&#x27;s folder, and makes it a proper mod (dropping one on the page works too). This uses the engine&#x27;s own importer (<code>zt1-engine --import-mod &lt;path&gt;</code>, the same as the game&#x27;s Mod Menu › Add a mod...).</li>\n<li><strong>The pages</strong> the tree opens:</li>\n<li><strong>About this mod</strong>: its mod.json details (name, id, author, version, about), and a short guide.</li>\n<li><strong>Its files</strong>: what it adds or changes - each game thing in its .ztd files (every <code>.ai</code>, <code>.uca</code>, <code>.ucs</code>, <code>.ucb</code>, by kind), its .ztd files and what kinds of file they hold, its shaders, its HD art, what its mod.json says it replaces, and its settings. <strong>Click a thing</strong> to open its file in Write Code, to change: it opens as config text with the mod&#x27;s name on its tab, and <strong>Save</strong> (Ctrl+S) puts it back inside the mod&#x27;s .ztd (re/lab/ztdedit.py). Its bar has <strong>Open the mod</strong> and <strong>Try in the Test Zoo</strong> (saved, switched on, the Test Zoo restarted).</li>\n<li><strong>About this mod</strong> › <strong>Its picture</strong>: the picture the game&#x27;s Mod Menu shows beside the mod (367 x 276), set from an image you choose or the Test Zoo&#x27;s view as it is (another shape is cut to fill it, from the middle). It&#x27;s <code>picture.png</code> in the mod&#x27;s folder, named by mod.json&#x27;s <code>picture</code>; the mod list and the mod&#x27;s header show it too.</li>\n<li><strong>An animal</strong> - the animal maker (renderer/pixeled.js, re/lab/animalmaker.py): a <strong>New animal</strong> from scratch - a name and an animal it behaves like (its needs, biome, behaviour and sounds, all in its species file <code>animals/&lt;type&gt;.uca</code> to change; <strong>Its species file</strong> opens it) - with no art of its own. Its animations are listed one a row: <strong>Linked</strong> (its line in <code>[m/Animations]</code> is a whole path to an animation that exists, the way the game&#x27;s own <code>box_idle = objects/box/idle/idle.ani</code> is - at first the one it behaves like, so it works in the game straight away), <strong>Drawn</strong> (its own) or <strong>Missing</strong>. <strong>Link…</strong> points any row at any animation, the game&#x27;s or one drawn here; <strong>Draw</strong> opens the editor; <strong>+ A new animation</strong> draws one of a new name (its behaviour sets play it once you add it to one).<ul>\n<li><strong>The editor</strong>, as Piskel&#x27;s: transparent layers (a layer marked <strong>Shadow</strong> is the ground shadow - the game&#x27;s colour 1), frames on a strip with onion skin, one set of frames for each way it faces (N, NE, E, SE, S: the game mirrors the other three; <strong>Copy from…</strong> another direction to start), a faint <strong>guide</strong> - the linked animation, by its ground point - under the drawing, or start from a copy of it. Tools: pencil (1-4 px, mirror pen), eraser, fill, line, box, oval, picker, move, the ground point (where it stands) and the height band; keys B E G L R O I M A H, [ ], arrows, space, Ctrl+Z/Y. <strong>Import PNG</strong> (one picture, or a strip of frames) and <strong>Export strip</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The direction ring</strong> (over the drawing): this frame in all eight ways it faces - N, NE, E, SE and S as drawn, and SW, W and NW as the game mirrors them from SE, E and NE (about the ground point, dashed). Click one to draw that way; a mirrored one draws its source.</li>\n<li><strong>Starting a drawing: Remake, Stencil or Blank.</strong> A new drawing opens <strong>the stencil browser</strong>: anything in the game - <strong>Animals</strong>, <strong>People</strong> (staff, and the guests: man, woman, boy, girl), <strong>Buildings</strong>, <strong>Trees and plants</strong>, <strong>Scenery</strong>, <strong>Fences</strong> - with icons and a search across them all, its animations as chips, and the picked one playing in all its directions (the engine&#x27;s own frames, so it&#x27;s quick). It only sets what you see while drawing; it isn&#x27;t the kind of thing you&#x27;re making. <strong>Remake</strong> copies its art in, every frame and direction, to change and recolour. <strong>Stencil</strong> lays it over the canvas: its <strong>outline</strong> (cyan, over the drawing) and its <strong>inside</strong> as faint as the <strong>Inside</strong> slider says (0%: the outline alone, 100%: all of it), with as many blank frames as it has, each drawn over its own. <strong>Blank</strong> is an empty canvas. (animalmaker.py <code>stencils</code> lists any item&#x27;s animations: an animal&#x27;s by its <code>[x/Animations]</code>, anything else every animation in its art folder - <code>objects/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, <code>fences/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, <code>staff/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, <code>guests/&lt;kind&gt;/</code> - or the folders its file names.)</li>\n<li><strong>The Stencil card</strong>: the one in use (click it to choose another, or <strong>Remake</strong> from it - Ctrl+Z goes back), show or hide it, its outline on or off, <strong>paint only inside</strong> the outline (the eraser and the shadow layer go anywhere; a fill stops at the outline), <strong>fill the stencil</strong> (the pen colour on this layer, in every frame and direction - a solid body to shade and detail), and the <strong>Inside</strong> slider. The stencil is kept with the drawing (<code>stencil</code> in its project) and comes back when it&#x27;s opened again. The mod keeps only your drawing, never the game&#x27;s art.</li>\n<li><strong>The panels</strong>: on the right, cards of icon buttons (each says what it does on hover) - <strong>Stencil</strong>, <strong>Layers</strong> (new, up, down, rename, shadow, delete; the eye hides one), <strong>Every direction</strong>, <strong>This direction</strong> (copy from another, flip, import PNG, export strip) and <strong>View</strong> (onion skin, mirror pen). <strong>Zoom</strong>: the bar on the canvas (out, in, fit) or Ctrl + scroll, about the pixel under the mouse. <strong>The frame strip</strong>: play, the frame before and after, ms a frame, the frames (+ adds one), then duplicate, move earlier or later, delete.</li>\n<li><strong>Every direction</strong> (each changes every frame of every direction): <strong>Size</strong> (a tenth bigger or smaller, about the ground point), <strong>Body</strong> (longer or shorter along the way it faces in each direction, from the middle of the body), <strong>Band</strong> (the height band picked with ⇕ - a neck, legs, a shell - taller or shorter; heights are the same in every direction, so one band works for all of them, and the ground stays put) and <strong>Recolour a colour…</strong> (one colour and the shades near it turned into another, each keeping how much lighter or darker it was). The canvas grows to fit; Ctrl+Z undoes each in one step.</li>\n<li><strong>Save</strong>: what&#x27;s drawn is kept as drawn (layers, full colour) in the mod&#x27;s <code>.art/animals/&lt;type&gt;/&lt;sub&gt;/&lt;anim&gt;.json</code> (left out of uploads), and made into the game&#x27;s art in content.ztd - every drawn animation of the animal on one 256-colour palette made from all of them (<code>animals/&lt;type&gt;/&lt;type&gt;.pal</code>: 0 clear, 1 the shadow, up to 254 colours; more are merged and it says so), each frame cut to what&#x27;s drawn, its <code>.ani</code> box worked out - and its row linked to it. The <code>edStroke</code> action (&quot;x,y;x,y&quot;) draws a stroke with the tool in hand, for an agent.</li>\n<li>(A frame header&#x27;s first offset is its ground point&#x27;s <strong>y</strong>, the second its <strong>x</strong> - src/Animation.cpp; the art maker read them the other way round, now fixed.)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Art</strong> - the art maker (renderer/artmaker.js, re/lab/artmaker.py): the mod&#x27;s animations, and for each thing it changes or adds, the game&#x27;s own art for it (marked <em>game</em>: a change saved puts a copy in the mod). One plays in every direction, zoomable, on a checkered, grass or dark background. Its palette is down the right (one palette colours all of an animal&#x27;s animations, so a change there recolours the whole animal):<ul>\n<li><strong>Pick colours</strong>: click the art or a swatch (Shift adds; the picked colours blink magenta on the art). <strong>By shade</strong> picks every colour used that&#x27;s <strong>Darker</strong> or <strong>Lighter</strong> than a slider - a whole mane, a set of stripes or a coat&#x27;s bright shades in one go (Shift adds). <strong>Set</strong> gives them a colour (several keep their own shades), <strong>Hue/Light</strong> and <strong>Shift</strong> move them; after a change the blink pauses a few seconds so you see the result. Mind colour 1: it&#x27;s the ground shadow under an animal (pure black), and picking dark shades takes it too - Shift-click it to drop it.</li>\n<li><strong>Paint</strong>: a pencil in the brush colour (Alt-click a colour), right button to clear, on the frame shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Pattern: fade along the body</strong>: the picked colours turn into a <strong>To</strong> colour from the head back - starting part way, all of them further back - in every frame of every animation of the animal, worked out for each way it faces, with a fixed dither so it doesn&#x27;t crawl. The shadow (the picked colours touching the bottom of the frame) is left as it is.</li>\n<li><strong>Pattern: stripes across the body</strong>: bands of the picked colours, each made its own darker shade, across the back (From/To along the body, Stripes, Width, Darker, Down the body) in every frame of every animation, for each way it faces; a stripe never repeats in under 4 pixels, so it stays a band on a diagonal facing.</li>\n<li>The game&#x27;s art index is kept between steps (<code>%TEMP%\\zt1-lab-artindex.json</code>, made again when the game&#x27;s archives change), so each step takes under a second.</li>\n<li><strong>Save into the mod</strong>, <strong>Undo</strong>, <strong>Frames out (PNG)</strong> (to the mod&#x27;s <code>.art</code> folder, left out of uploads, to paint in another program) and <strong>Frames in</strong> (each pixel the palette&#x27;s nearest colour). A saved file with one of the game&#x27;s paths is listed in mod.json&#x27;s <code>replaces</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Publish</strong>: below.</li>\n<li><strong>Write Code &gt; Game files</strong>, an animal&#x27;s <code>.ai</code> open: <strong>New species from this</strong> (re/lab/newspecies.py) makes a new animal as a mod: the animal&#x27;s whole art folder copied from your own game under the new name (animations pointed at the new folder, frames at the new palettes, palettes dyed a colour you give - white takes the colour, black stays black), its <code>.ai</code> as a <code>.uca</code> with its own type, number and <code>[1033]</code> name. Into a new mod, or one there is (a pack of animals; or this one made again - its species file kept). Any text file open there: <strong>Change it in a mod</strong> copies it into a mod&#x27;s content.ztd at the same path (so it takes the game&#x27;s place while the mod&#x27;s on), adds it to the mod&#x27;s <code>replaces</code> and opens the copy to change. Saving into a mod the Test Zoo has open stops its engine for the write and starts it again (Windows won&#x27;t replace an open file).</li>\n<li><strong>Opening files by name (MCP)</strong>: <code>lab_game_file</code> and <code>lab_open_engine_file</code> take a game path (<code>animals/tiger.ai</code>, opened read only at a line) or a mod&#x27;s own file, <code>mod:&lt;folder&gt;|&lt;ztd&gt;|&lt;entry&gt;</code> (opened in the editor to change; Save puts it back in the .ztd).</li>\n<li><strong>Names in a mod</strong>: a <code>[1033]</code> section with <code>cName</code> in any <code>.ai</code> names it in the game (as the original reads it, zoo.exe 0x597337); add <code>[defaultLCID]</code> <code>LCID = 1033</code> so players with another Windows language see it too.</li>\n<li><strong>Game files&#x27; search</strong>: <code>type:</code> takes an extension too (<code>type:uca</code>), and a mod&#x27;s files have their own <strong>Mods</strong> chip.</li>\n<li><strong>Publish</strong>: <strong>Check it</strong> applies the same rules the repository&#x27;s checks use (game data only, no paths outside the mod, at most 200,000 files, a mod.json with an id and a name). <strong>Upload it</strong> zips the mod and sends it in chunks to the mod repository (<code>https://mods.coderook.com/v1/modrepo/zt1</code>); there it&#x27;s checked again, including a malware scan, and approved automatically, then it&#x27;s in every official game&#x27;s Mod Menu › Mod repository. Uploading again updates it. Where it&#x27;s up already, its status and <strong>Take it down</strong> show here too. <strong>Your uploads</strong> lists every mod you&#x27;ve uploaded and what their checks said. You sign in with your CodeRook account (<code>cbx sign-in</code>, or a pasted personal token, kept in the Lab&#x27;s own state). Each mod belongs to whoever uploaded it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-making-mods-with-lab-scripts-the-toolkit\">Making mods with Lab scripts (the toolkit)</h2>\n<p>A Lab script can make things as well as test them: mods, art packs, anything a program on your PC makes. Its <code>Lab *lab</code> has a small toolkit for this (<code>re/lab/lab_api.h</code>, &quot;Making things&quot;), so you can build your own without changing the Lab:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>start(program, args, cwd)</code> runs a program in the background and returns a job number. Poll <code>running(job)</code> from <code>lab_step</code>, then read <code>exitCode(job)</code> when it&#x27;s done; <code>stop(job)</code> ends it. Its output goes to the log, except lines that are only a percentage.</li>\n<li><code>engineExe()</code> is the engine itself, for its tools:</li>\n<li><code>--hd-export &lt;folder&gt; [prefix…]</code> writes map art&#x27;s frames out as PNGs.</li>\n<li><code>--hd-finish &lt;frames&gt; &lt;upscaled&gt; &lt;out&gt;</code> tidies upscaled frames into a mod&#x27;s <code>hd/</code> folder.</li>\n<li><code>--hd-survey [prefix…]</code> logs how many frames and pixels there are.</li>\n<li><code>makeDir</code>, <code>removeDir</code> (it never removes a drive or a top-level folder), <code>writeFile</code>, <code>exists</code> and <code>countFiles(dir, &quot;.png&quot;)</code> handle files and folders.</li>\n<li><code>writeMod(folder, name, author, about)</code> writes a mod&#x27;s <code>mod.json</code>, unless it already has one. A mod&#x27;s own settings can go in that file too; see <code>mods/zt1-toon/README.md</code>.</li>\n<li><code>useModsFolder(folder)</code> points the game at a mods folder, so it finds what you made. <code>modsFolder()</code> says where it looks now.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-the-hd-art-lab-script-relabscriptshd_artcpp\">The HD art Lab script (<code>re/lab/scripts/hd_art.cpp</code>)</h3>\n<p>This makes a mod of AI-upscaled map art: the animals, people, fences and objects drawn while the map is zoomed in. The game shows the original art until a player switches the mod on in the Mod Menu or Game Options › Mods &amp; Updates, and the switch takes effect at once. It&#x27;s an ordinary Lab script, so copy it to make your own version with another upscaler, model or set of art.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its settings are at the top of the file: the mod&#x27;s name and folder, the mods folder, the art to upscale (folder prefixes such as <code>animals/lion staff/keeper fences/chainlnk</code>, or empty for all of it), and the upscaler and its model.</li>\n<li>Running it does three steps:\n  1. The engine writes out the original frames (<code>--hd-export</code>). Each frame&#x27;s clear edge pixels are filled with neighbouring colours so the upscaler doesn&#x27;t draw dark fringes.\n  2. <a href=\"#\" data-help-web=\"https://github.com/xinntao/Real-ESRGAN\" title=\"Open in your browser\">Real-ESRGAN ncnn-vulkan</a> (BSD-3, works on any Vulkan graphics card) makes them 4x. The inspector shows how many frames are done.\n  3. The engine puts them in the mod&#x27;s <code>hd/</code> folder (<code>--hd-finish</code>). Sizes are checked against the originals, the original outline is kept, and the texture&#x27;s fine detail is put back.\n  The work folders are deleted afterwards.</li>\n<li>Recoloured art comes out in the right colours. A building&#x27;s chosen colours, staff hair and skin, and guests&#x27; clothes are swapped in when the HD frame loads (<code>HdArt::recolour</code>), because the pack is made from each frame&#x27;s own palette.</li>\n<li><strong>In the game&#x27;s dev console:</strong> <code>hdart</code> lists the HD art mods with their counts: frames in use, frames missing, and the zoom the map was last drawn at. <code>hdart on|off</code> switches them.</li>\n<li><strong>Size and time:</strong> the lion, zookeeper and chain-link fence came to 20,630 frames and 792 MB, and took about 3 minutes on an RX 6600. The scientist&#x27;s 1,785 frames took 64 s. All the map art is about a million frames.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-driving-the-lab-from-an-ai-agent-mcp\">Driving the Lab from an AI agent (MCP)</h2>\n<p><code>mcp/server.js</code> is an MCP server that drives the running Lab through its\ncontrol port (127.0.0.1:18492, this PC only). Every tool acts through the\nLab&#x27;s own UI, so you watch it happen and your mouse and keyboard stay free.\nIt needs only Node, with no packages to install.</p>\n<p>How it answers (its design follows <a href=\"#\" data-help-web=\"https://github.com/morluto/rea\" title=\"Open in your browser\">rea</a>,\nMIT):\n- <strong>Errors say what to do.</strong> Each has a <code>code</code> (<code>lab_not_running</code>,\n  <code>timeout</code>, <code>invalid_args</code>, <code>lab_error</code>, ...), <code>retryable</code> and a <code>hint</code>. A\n  slow Lab is reported as a timeout, not as a Lab that isn&#x27;t running.\n- <strong>Inputs are checked first.</strong> A missing required input, or a value that\n  isn&#x27;t one of a field&#x27;s choices, is refused before the Lab is asked.\n- <strong>Long answers come a page at a time.</strong> Lists take <code>offset</code>/<code>limit</code>, texts\n  (decompiled C, a game file) <code>from</code>/<code>to</code>; <code>paging</code> gives the totals and the\n  next page&#x27;s arguments.\n- <strong>Each tool is marked</strong> read-only, destructive (a reload, a sync, removing\n  something) and whether it reaches the internet.\n- <strong>Text from zoo.exe, the game&#x27;s files and the shared knowledge base</strong> is\n  marked as data (<code>_meta.untrusted_content</code>), never instructions.\n- <strong>Deeper questions about zoo.exe</strong> (Ghidra, through the bridge&#x27;s newer\n  queries - <code>re/ghidra_scripts/ZT1_LabBridge.py</code>, ideas from rea&#x27;s own\n  bridge): <code>lab_data_type</code> (a struct&#x27;s layout as Ghidra has it, to check\n  against the knowledge base), <code>lab_function_blocks</code> (basic blocks and each\n  switch table&#x27;s cases), <code>lab_call_targets</code> (every call&#x27;s target; a class-table\n  call such as <code>call [eax+0x16c]</code> matched against the knowledge base&#x27;s class\n  tables), <code>lab_value_flow</code> (where a value goes or comes from inside a\n  function), <code>lab_search_bytes</code> (a byte pattern with <code>??</code> wildcards) and\n  <code>lab_noreturn_functions</code>.\n- <strong>Guided workflows</strong> (MCP prompts): <code>reverse_a_function</code>,\n  <code>copy_behaviour_into_engine</code>, <code>test_in_the_test_zoo</code>, <code>fix_build_errors</code>,\n  <code>map_a_struct_field</code>.</p>\n<p>Setting it up:\n- <strong>Claude Code:</strong> it&#x27;s registered for this repo in <code>.mcp.json</code> (<code>zt1-lab</code>),\n  and Claude Code asks you to approve it the first time.\n- <strong>Any other MCP client</strong> (Codex, Cursor, VS Code / Copilot, Gemini CLI,\n  Claude Desktop…): add a stdio server to that client&#x27;s MCP settings. It&#x27;s\n  the command <code>node</code> with the argument\n  <code>&lt;repo&gt;/lab-app/mcp/server.js</code>:</p>\n<p><code>json\n  { &quot;mcpServers&quot;: { &quot;zt1-lab&quot;: { &quot;command&quot;: &quot;node&quot;, &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;C:/path/to/zt1-engine/lab-app/mcp/server.js&quot;] } } }</code></p>\n<p>(Each client keeps this in its own file, e.g. <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>, or\n  <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> under <code>&quot;servers&quot;</code>. See the client&#x27;s own docs.)</p>\n<p>Start the Lab first (<code>ZT1 Lab.cmd</code>). Without it, every tool answers that the\nLab isn&#x27;t running.</p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-your-own-paths\">Your own paths</h3>\n<p>The Lab&#x27;s settings for your PC live in <code>re/private/</code>, which is never\ncommitted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>original.json</code>:</strong> <code>{ &quot;exe&quot;: &quot;&lt;your zoo.exe&gt;&quot;, &quot;iso&quot;: &quot;&lt;a Marine\n  Mania disc image, if the original asks for one&gt;&quot; }</code>. The exe defaults to\n  the standard install folder.</li>\n<li><strong><code>ghidra.json</code>:</strong> <code>{ &quot;home&quot;: &quot;&lt;Ghidra&#x27;s folder&gt;&quot;, &quot;projects&quot;: [&quot;&lt;your\n  Ghidra project folder&gt;&quot;], &quot;name&quot;: &quot;zt1&quot; }</code>. <code>home</code> can also come from the\n  <code>GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR</code> environment variable — and you can leave <code>home</code> out\n  entirely: an installed Lab runs the Ghidra it carries. To make a project:\n  import <code>zoo.exe</code> into a new Ghidra project, then run <strong>ZT1_Apply</strong> to bring\n  in everything the knowledge base knows (see <code>re/README.md</code>). The views about\n  the original&#x27;s code need Ghidra. The engine, game files, test zoo and\n  Play views don&#x27;t.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>The Lab does not make a project for you yet.</em> It ships Ghidra with the JDK\n  it runs on, but a Ghidra project is built out of <code>zoo.exe</code> — the game, which\n  is nobody&#x27;s to hand out — so the first project has to come from your own\n  copy. Until the Lab grows a button for it (a headless import of your\n  <code>zoo.exe</code>), the decompiled views start working once you point <code>ghidra.json</code>\n  at a project with <code>zoo.exe</code> in it, as above.</p>\n<p>Two things that help when driving it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>lab_state</strong> includes <code>pageErrors</code>: the Lab page&#x27;s own errors (a script that\n  threw, a promise nobody caught, console.error), newest last. An empty or\n  half-drawn view usually has its reason there.</li>\n<li><strong>lab_scene_place</strong> takes a short item name when only one item matches it\n  (<code>dzbar</code> → <code>fences/dzbar.ai</code>); otherwise it says which ones it might mean.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tools:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Tools</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Looking at the Lab</td>\n<td><code>lab_state</code>, <code>lab_screenshot</code> (the window as an image), <code>lab_read</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Moving around</td>\n<td><code>lab_tab</code>, <code>lab_click</code>, <code>lab_type</code>, <code>lab_action</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>zoo.exe&#x27;s functions</td>\n<td><code>lab_search_functions</code> (names, plus the game&#x27;s text), <code>lab_open_function</code> (decompiled C, callers), <code>lab_where_used</code>, <code>lab_name_function</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Game files</td>\n<td><code>lab_game_files</code>, <code>lab_game_file</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine code</td>\n<td><code>lab_search_engine</code>, <code>lab_open_engine_file</code>, <code>lab_read_engine_file</code>, <code>lab_edit_engine_file</code>, <code>lab_save_engine</code>, <code>lab_problems</code>, <code>lab_engine_calls</code> (who calls a function, or what it calls)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sync</td>\n<td><code>lab_sync_status</code>, <code>lab_sync</code> (pull, resolve, commit, push, propose, mirror, branch: only when asked)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Building and running</td>\n<td><code>lab_build_engine</code> (waits; returns ok or the problems), <code>lab_make_release</code> (waits; the zip, its sha256, what was left out), <code>lab_run_mod</code>, <code>lab_engine_log</code>, <code>lab_play</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The Scene</td>\n<td><code>lab_scene</code> (its state and what&#x27;s selected), <code>lab_scene_act</code> (an action on what&#x27;s selected), <code>lab_scene_pin</code> (hold a value), <code>lab_scene_route</code> (can a guest, staff or the selected animal get from here to there, and why not), <code>lab_scene_maps</code>, <code>lab_scene_load</code>, <code>lab_scene_outline</code>, <code>lab_scene_items</code>, <code>lab_scene_place</code> (a tool at a tile, or dragged), <code>lab_scene_select</code>, <code>lab_scene_set</code>, <code>lab_scene_remove</code>, <code>lab_scene_time</code>, <code>lab_scene_view</code> (also the art viewer: a species&#x27; animation, its layers, held on a frame), <code>lab_scene_console</code>, <code>lab_species_check</code> (a species, or every tank animal, in bulk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stepping</td>\n<td><code>lab_scene_trace</code> (what&#x27;s traced, stop at each step, breakpoints), <code>lab_scene_step</code> (step, over, out, continue: returns where it stopped), <code>lab_scene_steps</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>zoo.exe&#x27;s own code</td>\n<td><code>lab_try_function</code> (run a function on what&#x27;s selected: what it used, what&#x27;s missing, a value for a missing offset)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The Lab itself</td>\n<td><code>lab_reload</code> (the page, after changing <code>renderer/</code>), <code>lab_restart</code> (the whole app, after changing <code>main.js</code> or <code>preload.js</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Lab has to be running for the tools to work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-original-game-in-play\">The original game in Play</h2>\n<p><strong>Play original</strong> runs the real <code>zoo.exe</code> inside the Play view, the way\nOpenZT runs it: a small launcher starts it paused and loads our DLL into it\nbefore its first instruction, and the DLL replaces a few of the Windows calls\nit makes. Both are built by <strong>Build engine</strong> (32-bit, like zoo.exe, from the\n<code>build/</code> tree: CMakeLists&#x27; part 12), from <code>src/original/</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Launch.cpp</code> → <code>zt1-original.exe --zoo &lt;zoo.exe&gt; --embed &lt;Lab window&gt; --size &lt;w&gt;x&lt;h&gt; --log &lt;file&gt;</code></li>\n<li>Original Game&#x27;s search also takes <code>value:10282</code> (or <code>value:0x282a</code>): every instruction using that number as itself - a message id, a key&#x27;s number - by function (re/lab/zoobin.py <code>value</code>); code Ghidra has no function for opens at the line asked for.</li>\n<li><code>Hook.cpp</code> → <code>zt1-hook.dll</code>, which:</li>\n<li>answers zoo.ini&#x27;s <code>[user] fullscreen</code> as 0 and <code>screenwidth</code>/<code>screenheight</code> as the Play view&#x27;s size, so the game takes its own windowed path (<code>GXVidMan::setupWindowed</code>, 0x52f1d0) at that size, and never writes those back: your zoo.ini keeps its own;</li>\n<li>keeps its window borderless, owned by the Lab&#x27;s and over the Play view (CreateWindowExA, SetWindowPos, SetWindowLongA, AdjustWindowRectEx), with the same stdin lines as our engine (<code>rect</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>hide</code>, <code>focus</code>, <code>quit</code>);</li>\n<li>stops the Windows &quot;DirectPlay&quot; prompt: the game&#x27;s DirectX version check loads <code>dpnhpast.dll</code> only to tell 8.1 from 8.0, and it&#x27;s refused, exactly as when the prompt is skipped;</li>\n<li>hooks its DirectDraw (made with CoCreateInstance then Initialize, 0x533d0a) to see the surface it draws on: <code>shot &lt;file.bmp&gt;</code>, which the Lab turns into a .png (<code>lab_play</code> with <code>shot</code>);</li>\n<li>logs every setting read, window, DLL and COM object, and any crash with its zoo.exe address and callers, to <code>re/private/original-hook.log</code>.</li>\n<li>the recorder (<code>arm &lt;file&gt;</code>, <code>disarm</code>: &quot;hit&quot; lines) and the Lab&#x27;s mouse, below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Windows&#x27; own dialogs.</strong> Saving and loading open Windows&#x27; file dialog\n(&quot;Save a zoo...&quot;), which isn&#x27;t drawn on the game&#x27;s surface, and the game waits\non it. <code>lab_action origDialog</code> names the dialog that&#x27;s open, <code>origDialog &quot;text\nmy-zoo&quot;</code> fills in the file name, and <code>origDialog ok</code> / <code>origDialog cancel</code>\npresses its button, and <code>origDialog &quot;press Yes&quot;</code> presses a button by its text\n(Windows&#x27; &quot;Confirm Save As&quot; has Yes/No with no fixed ids). <code>origKey 27</code> sends a key (a virtual-key code: Esc).</p>\n<p><strong>Recording from an AI agent</strong> comes in three calls, checked between:\n<code>recDo &quot;&lt;label&gt;|&lt;playSteps lines&gt;&quot;</code> starts it and does the steps, then you\nlook at a <code>shot</code>, then <code>recKeep</code> saves it (with the names its debug text\ngives) or <code>recDrop</code> sets it aside in <code>recordings/discarded/</code>. For a long one,\nleave the steps out and wait outside the call: a <code>lab_action</code> gives up after\nabout a minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Volume.</strong> The Play bar&#x27;s slider sets the running game&#x27;s own volume (either\ngame; Windows&#x27; mixer has it on its own: <code>volume &lt;0-100&gt;</code>, src/SessionVolume.hpp).</p>\n<p><strong>The Lab&#x27;s mouse.</strong> <code>click</code>/<code>rclick</code>/<code>move &lt;x&gt; &lt;y&gt;</code> and <code>drag &lt;x0&gt; &lt;y0&gt; &lt;x1&gt; &lt;y1&gt;</code>\n(the game&#x27;s own pixels, as in a <code>shot</code>) click in the original without anyone&#x27;s\nmouse: the hook answers GetCursorPos, GetAsyncKeyState and GetKeyState for the\nmoment it takes, then hands the mouse back (<code>lab_action playCmd &quot;click 952 462&quot;</code>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-recording-what-runs-the-originals-recorder\">Recording what runs (the original&#x27;s recorder)</h3>\n<p>While the original runs, the Play bar has <strong>Learn idle</strong>, <strong>Record</strong> and\n<strong>Recordings</strong>. The hook puts a one-time breakpoint (int3) on the first byte of\nevery function Ghidra knows in zoo.exe (~7,300); the first time one runs, its\nbyte goes back and the game carries on, so it&#x27;s full speed once warmed up.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Learn idle</strong> (at the screen you&#x27;ll record on: the main menu, or in a zoo):\n   what runs with nothing being done is &quot;always running&quot; and left out after\n   (<code>re/private/original-quiet.json</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Record</strong>: name what you&#x27;re about to do (&quot;Placing a fence&quot;), do just that,\n   <strong>Stop recording</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>What ran</strong>: each function that ran, in order, with what&#x27;s known - its\n   name (the knowledge base&#x27;s, Ghidra&#x27;s), our engine&#x27;s code citing it (&quot;in our\n   engine&quot;), the game&#x27;s own debug text naming it (<code>GXVidMan::createSurface -\n   ...</code>: ticked to name it so), the original&#x27;s source file it&#x27;s from (the\n   developers&#x27; paths were left in: <code>snd/dx8sound.cpp</code>), the text it uses, where\n   it was called from, and the other recordings it ran in. Click an address to\n   read it in Original Game.</li>\n<li><strong>Save to knowledge base</strong>: every function in it gets &quot;Ran when: ...&quot; in\n   <code>re/knowledge.json</code>&#x27;s new <code>seen</code> (kb.py <code>seen</code> / <code>seen-file</code>), and the ticked\n   names (provisional, by &quot;Lab recorder&quot;); <strong>ZT1_Apply</strong> shows them in Ghidra -\n   plate comments and <code>zt1-seen</code> bookmarks. The more that&#x27;s recorded, the more\n   functions run in only one thing: that&#x27;s what they&#x27;re for.</li>\n<li><strong>Only what ran in no other recording</strong> (a tick box over the list): leaves\n   out everything another recording has too, so what&#x27;s left is this action&#x27;s\n   own code. Placing a zebra outside an exhibit ran 136 functions, and 8 of them\n   ran nowhere else: one chain (0x49ccc3 → 0x49cd46 → 0x49c6d7, the red\n   &quot;Animals can only be placed in exhibits&quot; message, → 0x41176c → 0x411797).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A label that turns out wrong (the zebra was refused, not adopted): <strong>Close</strong>\nwithout saving and record it again under the right name.</p>\n<p><strong>Stop the game</strong> ends it even inside a zoo, where the original asks whether\nto quit rather than closing: whatever&#x27;s still running 4 seconds on is ended,\nwith its launcher.</p>\n<p>Recordings are kept in <code>re/private/recordings/</code> and matched again when opened\n(what&#x27;s been learnt since shows). The bridge&#x27;s <code>/debugnames</code> and <code>/fnstrings</code>\ngive the debug names and each function&#x27;s text in one go.</p>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-the-originals-steps-live-the-scenes-step-log-for-zooexe\">The original&#x27;s steps, live (the Scene&#x27;s step log, for zoo.exe)</h3>\n<p><strong>Steps</strong> in the Play bar opens a panel beside the original, the way the\nScene shows our engine&#x27;s steps. While the game runs it streams what zoo.exe\nis doing.</p>\n<p>How it watches: the hook puts a one-time breakpoint on every function except\nthe idle ones, and the Lab re-arms the ones that ran every second (the\nhook&#x27;s <code>rearm</code>). So each function shows each time it runs, at most once a\nsecond.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Known</strong> functions read in words: their name, what they do, and our\n  engine&#x27;s code for them (click to open it).</li>\n<li><strong>Unknown</strong> ones say &quot;unknown&quot;, with a guess and its evidence, strongest\n  first:</li>\n<li>a specific known caller (<em>high</em>);</li>\n<li>the text it uses, such as <code>ui/sharedui/question</code> (the &quot;are you sure?&quot;\n    dialog) (<em>medium</em>);</li>\n<li>&quot;ran for nothing else recorded&quot;: that action&#x27;s own (<em>medium</em>);</li>\n<li>what the few actions it ran in share, such as selling, bulldozing and\n    undo: the refund (<em>medium</em>);</li>\n<li>the known functions it calls (<em>medium</em>);</li>\n<li><strong>the same class slot named in a related class</strong> (<em>high</em>). Name\n    <code>ZTFoodType::isKindOf</code> at slot 0x1c, and every other type&#x27;s slot 0x1c\n    is guessed <code>isKindOf</code>. &quot;Related&quot; means the two tables share at least 30%\n    of their slots;</li>\n<li>a function called from many places, in the window or program-wide\n    (Ghidra&#x27;s <code>/fncallers</code>), is &quot;a helper&quot;, never &quot;part of&quot; one caller;</li>\n<li>a neighbour is only a clue when it&#x27;s specific: not a helper, not code\n    seen in many recordings, and not a method every type has (className,\n    isKindOf - called through the class table, so Ghidra counts no callers,\n    yet they run for everything), nor a getter or setter, nor anything in\n    three or more class tables. Ghidra&#x27;s own labels (<code>caseD_1</code>, <code>switchD_</code>,\n    <code>LAB_</code>) aren&#x27;t names. A slot&#x27;s name only spreads from a function that&#x27;s\n    in that one slot (not <code>returns1</code>, which fills dozens);</li>\n<li>an unknown caller&#x27;s guess, inherited a step weaker;</li>\n<li>the known code that ran either side of it (<em>low</em>).</li>\n<li>Live, each function shows <strong>once</strong>, with how often it ran (×12) and who\n  called it. <strong>Every call</strong> shows them all in order.</li>\n<li><strong>General code</strong> (input, drawing, sound: anything that ran in half the\n  recordings or more) is hidden unless you ask for it.</li>\n<li><strong>❚❚ Pause game and log</strong> presses the game&#x27;s own pause button and holds\n  the list still. <strong>▶ Resume</strong> starts both again. It checks the game&#x27;s clock\n  first, so it stays right even if you used the game&#x27;s button yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Click any line</strong> to open its form:</li>\n<li><strong>Name</strong> (the guess&#x27;s name to start with), <strong>Class</strong>, <strong>What it does</strong>\n    (the guess to start with), and <strong>How sure</strong> (sure, likely, a guess; a\n    guess stays provisional).</li>\n<li><strong>Save</strong> puts it in the knowledge base under your name. The Lab asks\n    your name the first time.</li>\n<li>A name that&#x27;s still a guess (marked <em>guess</em>) gets <strong>Confirm</strong>, which\n    keeps it as sure.</li>\n<li><strong>Read its code</strong> opens it in Original Game.</li>\n<li>While a line is open, the list holds still too.</li>\n<li>Whatever you save shows as known in the panel straight away. ZT1_Apply\n    puts it in Ghidra, and the map in Explore picks it up when rebuilt.</li>\n<li><strong>Record</strong> switches the panel to that recording: each function the first\n  time it runs, with the guesses using the action&#x27;s name. A recording&#x27;s\n  <strong>Its steps</strong> button replays it in the panel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-the-originals-state-live-and-finding-where-it-keeps-things\">The original&#x27;s state, live (and finding where it keeps things)</h3>\n<p>While a zoo is open in the original, the Play bar reads its own state from its\nmemory every 2 s: the date, cash, its animals (with their average happiness)\nand guests, and how many things are in the zoo. Click it for the <strong>Census</strong>:\nevery entity by class and species. What it reads (re/knowledge.json, found\nwith the tools below):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>game manager</td>\n<td>global <code>0x638048</code></td>\n<td>cash: float at +0xc (spent by <code>spendCash</code> 0x41ef68); the date: a SYSTEMTIME at +0x1194 (a real calendar from Mon 1 Jan 2001, a day each 12 s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>world</td>\n<td>global <code>0x638040</code></td>\n<td>map size +0x34/+0x38, tiles +0x40, every entity in a vector at +0x80/+0x84</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an entity</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>its class = its function table (+0): animals <code>0x62ff54</code>, guests <code>0x630f88</code>, fences <code>0x62d808</code>, tank walls <code>0x62dbc0</code>, scenery <code>0x62d950</code>, paths <code>0x62da88</code>, buildings <code>0x62e0b0</code>, ambient animals <code>0x62d6ec</code>; its type +0x128</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a type</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>short name +0xa4 (elm, zebra, boy), kind +0x98 (foliage, building, zoowall)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Find a value</strong> (Play bar) finds the next one, the way a cheat finder does:\nscan for a value you can see (<code>float 75000</code>), change it in the game, rescan\nfor the new one; then <strong>Watch writes</strong> (a hardware watchpoint) reports each\ninstruction that writes it, in its function, with the registers - the object\nit&#x27;s written through. That&#x27;s how the cash and <code>spendCash</code> were found. The\nhook&#x27;s lines: <code>scan</code>, <code>rescan</code>, <code>peek &lt;addr&gt; &lt;n&gt;</code>, <code>each &lt;list&gt; &lt;offsets&gt;</code>\n(a vector of objects&#x27; fields at once), <code>strs &lt;addr&gt; &lt;n&gt;</code> (the text an object\npoints at: its name), <code>watch</code>/<code>unwatch</code>. Lab actions for them: <code>memScan</code>,\n<code>memRescan</code>, <code>memPeek</code>, <code>memEach</code>, <code>memStrs</code>, <code>memWatch</code>, <code>memWrites</code>,\n<code>origLive</code>, <code>origCensus</code>, and <code>playSteps</code> (&quot;click 952 462; wait 1500; ...&quot;)\nto get somewhere in the game without the mouse. Actions now give back what\nthey return (<code>lab_action</code>).</p>\n<p><code>poke &lt;addr&gt; &lt;value&gt;</code> (Lab action <code>memPoke &quot;0xADDRESS value&quot;</code>) writes a 32-bit\nvalue into the original, to push it somewhere for a test: births are proved by\nsetting a species&#x27; cReproductionChance (type +0x278), cReproductionInterval\n(+0x27c) and cHappyReproduceThreshold (+0x378) - on the male&#x27;s subtype, the\ntype at entity +0x128 whose +0xa4 is &quot;m&quot; - then putting them back.\n<code>origFrames &quot;&lt;folder&gt; [count] [ms]&quot;</code> films the original: a picture every\n<code>ms</code> (default 500), saved f000.png, f001.png... to look through frame by\nframe. Popups (the adoption ban, new items) pause the original; OK is at\nabout 712,500 at the Play view&#x27;s size.</p>\n<p>More for reading what the code refers to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>memText &quot;&lt;address&gt; [bytes]&quot;</code>: the text stored right at an address (a name\n  the code compares against: <code>fWalk</code>, <code>fRun</code>). <code>memStrs</code> follows pointers\n  instead.</li>\n<li><code>memTables &quot;&lt;slots&gt; &lt;address&gt;...&quot;</code>: class function tables, each entry as an\n  address, to see which slots a class replaces; <code>memTables &quot;uniq ...&quot;</code> gives\n  each different entry once.</li>\n<li><code>gameText &quot;10287-10290 0x2972&quot;</code>: the game&#x27;s own text by number, from its\n  language files (the engine&#x27;s <code>texts</code> command). The numbers in zoo.exe&#x27;s\n  code - thoughts, messages, status lines - say what the code is for:\n  10287 is &quot;I&#x27;ve never seen such a lousy show!&quot;.</li>\n<li><code>numberUsers &quot;10028&quot;</code> (or <code>0x75</code>): every function with that number in an\n  instruction - a message (&quot;%s is not happy.&quot; is used by animalUpdate), a\n  task, a UI element. Ghidra&#x27;s bridge (<code>/scalar</code>) builds the index the first\n  time, in a few seconds. Small numbers also turn up as other things (the\n  shows reuse 10026-10029 as event codes), so read the match.</li>\n<li><code>uiElement &quot;0x11cb 7000&quot;</code>: which layout and element a UI number is\n  (<code>infocom:ItemCost_Cost</code>). The code finds elements by number\n  (<code>uiFind(0x11cb)</code>). The map is <code>re/private/ui_ids.json</code>, made from the\n  game&#x27;s layouts by <code>python re/lab/uiids.py</code> (it also looks numbers up).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And for naming many at once:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>makeFns &quot;0x52d94f 0x43eb4f ...&quot;</code>: makes functions where Ghidra&#x27;s analysis\n  missed them (class-table entries usually), kept in\n  <code>re/made_functions.json</code> so ZT1_Apply makes them in your project too.</li>\n<li><code>namesSave</code>: a list of <code>address|name|summary|sure/likely/guess|by|class</code>\n  lines (a first line <code>by: &lt;who&gt;</code> sets everyone&#x27;s by). Paste a family read off\n  one table: every handler a dispatcher calls by name.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The families named this way (2026-10-12) are the quickest way into the code:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What</th>\n<th>Where it starts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Behaviour functions (<code>f = fWalk(...)</code> in the .ai files)</td>\n<td><code>behaviourRunFn</code> 0x436065, <code>behaviourRunFnMoves</code> 0x437ccd: each name to its handler (<code>fMoveSlow</code>, <code>fTargetBuilding</code>, ...)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit tasks (going to a building, eating, chasing, the helicopter&#x27;s landing)</td>\n<td><code>unitMakeTask</code> 0x413acd: <code>Task67_GoToBuilding</code>, <code>Task74_Chase</code>...; their shared <code>Task::tick</code> 0x43679e</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Staff jobs (the status line&#x27;s number: placing food, sweeping, fixing fences)</td>\n<td><code>unitMakeJob</code> 0x413a23: <code>Job2_PutDownFood</code>, <code>Job17_FixFence</code>...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UI handlers (every button, panel shown / hidden, tab let go)</td>\n<td><code>uiRegisterHandler</code> 0x512687 from <code>register_&lt;layout&gt;_handlers</code>; <code>infocom_up_Spinner</code>&#x27;s is <code>onPriceUp</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A unit&#x27;s class slots</td>\n<td>0x10 handleEvents, 0x2c tick, 0x38 chooseTask, 0x48 startBehaviour, 0x64 debugText, 0x68 save, 0x6c load, 0x10c setup, 0x1d8 setTask, 0x24c habitat, 0x2a4 sellValue, 0x318 statusText</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-side-by-side-the-original-and-our-engine-day-by-day\">Side by side (the original and our engine, day by day)</h3>\n<p><code>lab_action sideStart &quot;&lt;days&gt; &lt;label&gt;&quot;</code> records both games every game day from\nthe same moment: the original&#x27;s first animal (happiness, +0x2a8) and its\nexhibit (+0xe8: its animals&#x27; average happiness), and the Test Zoo&#x27;s selected\nanimal (happiness, hunger, suitability); it starts the Test Zoo&#x27;s clock too.\n<code>sideStop</code> ends it, <code>sideShow</code> charts it (the last 20 runs are kept). Put the\nsame thing in both first - an exhibit and an animal in each - and start it as\nthe original&#x27;s goes in. The first run (a lone zebra, bare 5x5 chain-link,\nBasic Grass Map): both from 75, the same within the original&#x27;s random 20-40\ntick check for 16 days; then the original paused for its adoption ban, which\nthe Test Zoo didn&#x27;t have - it does now (below).</p>\n<p><strong>The original keeps running</strong> in the Play view when the Lab is the window\nin front (left to itself it pauses in the background, as a fullscreen game\ndoes): the hook tells its window it&#x27;s still active, and its\nGetForegroundWindow answers its own window - 12 s a game day, measured.</p>\n<p>Since then it also records guests: their count and average happiness in\nboth, the original&#x27;s first guest&#x27;s happiness and needs (+0x2a8, +0x2b0\nhunger, +0x2b8 thirst, +0x2c0 energy, +0x2c8 bathroom) beside a guest\nselected in the Test Zoo, and the original&#x27;s zoo rating (its game manager&#x27;s\n+0x2c), which it gives the Test Zoo as it goes (<code>economy</code>: the lab zoo keeps\nno books) so both get their guests by the same rating. What it found: the\noriginal&#x27;s trash check takes 5 from every guest each cTrashCheck unless it&#x27;s\noff to a trash can, holding litter or not (zoo.exe 0x43aedc) - now ours too.\nPut both exhibits the same distance from paths: guests gawk only from them.</p>\n<p><strong>Goals in the Test Zoo.</strong> The lab zoo runs its map&#x27;s goals as the game does\n(Lab.cpp <code>setupGoals</code>, the same rules as main.cpp&#x27;s): freeform&#x27;s donation,\naward and unlock scripts - the adoption ban when the animals&#x27; average\nhappiness is under 25 of 100, lifted over 45. A popup is a note in the log,\nand <code>pause=1</code> pauses the zoo (and stops a time skip) as the game does; the\nUI elements a goal disables are noted. Goals on cash or the zoo rating are\nleft out: the lab zoo keeps no books.</p>\n<p>The Marine Mania disc image is mounted first if no drive has it (paths in\n<code>re/private/original.json</code>: <code>{ &quot;exe&quot;: ..., &quot;iso&quot;: ... }</code>). The game is drawn\nat the Play view&#x27;s size when it starts; it isn&#x27;t resized after.</p>","toc":[{"id":"the-shared-knowledge-base","title":"The shared knowledge base","level":2},{"id":"explore-start-here","title":"Explore (start here)","level":2},{"id":"code-11-with-zooexe-explores-right-side","title":"Code 1:1 with zoo.exe (Explore's right side)","level":3},{"id":"the-views","title":"The views","level":2},{"id":"finding-things-the-lab-couldnt-before","title":"Finding things the Lab couldn't before","level":3},{"id":"the-scene","title":"The Scene","level":2},{"id":"how-it-works","title":"How it works","level":2},{"id":"engine-code","title":"Engine code","level":2},{"id":"scripts-new-script","title":"Scripts (＋ New script)","level":3},{"id":"mods-the-mods-tabs-mods-section","title":"Mods (the Mods tab's Mods section)","level":2},{"id":"making-mods-with-lab-scripts-the-toolkit","title":"Making mods with Lab scripts (the toolkit)","level":2},{"id":"the-hd-art-lab-script-relabscriptshd_artcpp","title":"The HD art Lab script (re/lab/scripts/hd_art.cpp)","level":3},{"id":"driving-the-lab-from-an-ai-agent-mcp","title":"Driving the Lab from an AI agent (MCP)","level":2},{"id":"your-own-paths","title":"Your own paths","level":3},{"id":"the-original-game-in-play","title":"The original game in Play","level":2},{"id":"recording-what-runs-the-originals-recorder","title":"Recording what runs (the original's recorder)","level":3},{"id":"the-originals-steps-live-the-scenes-step-log-for-zooexe","title":"The original's steps, live (the Scene's step log, for zoo.exe)","level":3},{"id":"the-originals-state-live-and-finding-where-it-keeps-things","title":"The original's state, live (and finding where it keeps things)","level":3},{"id":"side-by-side-the-original-and-our-engine-day-by-day","title":"Side by side (the original and our engine, day by day)","level":3}],"text":"# ZT1 Lab (the app)\n\n**Help:** the tab beside Mods contains searchable guides, illustrated tutorials\nand nine bundled videos. Start with **Start with the Lab**, or search for an\nanimal, OBJ import, a tank, a map or publishing. Press **F1** from any Lab tab.\nThe library works offline and includes the earlier overview, labelled as an\nearlier interface. Current lessons cover the redesigned Mods studio.\n\nThe friendly face of the Lab, built with Electron to the design in\n`re/lab/design/`.\n\n**Item builder:** Mods → Make something → Build an item creates separate\nbuildings, fences, staff, tank filters and scenery, or customizes a selected\ngame item. Draw with Blank and external PNG/OBJ import, or Remake existing art.\nGuest appearances target the game's four existing guest kinds; staff inherit\nexisting duties. Check service, damage and work poses as well as idle/walk.\nSee [the modding guide](../docs/MODDING.md#build-buildings-fences-people-and-objects).\n\n**From a checkout:** double-click **`ZT1 Lab.cmd`**, or run:\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\nIt needs `build\\Release\\zt1-engine.exe` built first. The first time, it\ninstalls its packages (Electron) into `node_modules/`, which isn't\ncommitted.\n\n**Installed:** `dist/ZT1-Lab-Setup-<version>.exe` (a release's third\ndownload), which is this app - Electron and\nall - with the engine and what the Lab needs inside it, and no admin\nprompt. It installs into `%LocalAppData%\\Programs\\ZT1-Lab`:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `ZT1 Lab.exe` | the app (the page inside `resources/app.asar`) |\n| `tree\\engine\\` | `zt1-engine.exe`, built for release, and the hook for the original game |\n| `tree\\re\\` | the knowledge base and the Lab's Python tools |\n| `tree\\clangd\\` | the code intelligence |\n| `tree\\ghidra\\` | Ghidra itself, and `tree\\java\\` the JDK it runs on |\n| `tree\\python\\` | a Python of its own, with what the Lab's tools need |\n\nNothing here asks the player to install Python or Java: `tree\\python`\nis an interpreter with `capstone`, `pefile` and `unicorn` beside it, and\n`tree\\ghidra` is Ghidra with the JDK it runs on. `tree\\ghidra` is the\nprogram, not a project of the game: a Ghidra project is built out of\n`zoo.exe` itself - the game, which is nobody's to hand out - so the Lab\nmakes one from the player's own copy.\n\nThe installer asks for the engine's checkout (and remembers it in\n`%LocalAppData%\\zt1-engine\\lab-roots.json`). With one, the Lab reads and\nedits the real tree - Write Code, Build engine, Game Files, Sync, the\ncode intelligence - exactly as running from a checkout does. Without\none, it runs on what it carries: the lab zoo, Explore, Original Game's\nfunctions and code, the game's own files, Play. What needs a checkout\nsays so when you click it.\n\n**One thing it does not carry, and will not: the game.** *Zoo Tycoon* is\nnot free, and the Lab never ships it. It reads a copy you already own and\nhave installed - the original `zoo.exe` for Original Game, Play original\nand the traces, and the game's own files for the Scene and Game Files. It\nfinds it by itself, or you point it at yours:\n\n- `re/private/original.json` — `{ \"exe\": \"<your zoo.exe>\", \"iso\": \"<a Marine Mania disc image, if your copy needs the disc>\" }`\n- `ZT1_EXE` for the Scene's **Try zoo.exe**\n- Game Files and the Terrain painter find the install by themselves (the usual\n  `Microsoft Games\\Zoo Tycoon`, or the Complete Collection)\n\nEverything the Lab *does* carry is ours or third-party under its own\nlicence: the engine (`tree\\engine`), the Lab's own tools and its knowledge\nbase (`tree\\re`), clangd, an embeddable Python with `capstone`, `pefile` and\n`unicorn`, and Ghidra with the JDK it runs on. No part of the game is in\nthe installer, and `re/private` - your recordings, your game's location -\nnever leaves your machine.\n\nEach of those carries its licence with it: the build writes\n`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt` from the licence text each project ships (and says\nso when one does not), and `SOURCE-OFFER.txt` is the written offer of\ncorresponding source for the GPL-covered ones — the Temurin JDK and unicorn —\nbecause that source is not in the installer. Both sit beside `LICENSE.txt` in\nthe install; `engine-build-resources/notices.py` writes them, and\n`fetch_licenses.py` keeps the one text no package provides (unicorn's GPLv2).\n\nThe installer is built on the release machine. Its packing and the\nbackend it carries (a Python with `capstone`, `pefile` and `unicorn`, and\nGhidra with its JDK) are part of the release tooling, which isn't in this\nrepository.\n\nThe **checkout** needs none of that: it runs on the machine's own\nPython 3, so that still needs `unicorn`, `capstone` and `pefile`.\n\n## The shared knowledge base\n\nEverything the Lab works out about the original's `zoo.exe` - what\n`0x4378df` is, a struct's fields, which functions ran in a recording - is\nthe same answer for every install, because it is all about one build of\none game. So it can be shared: `re/cloud/` is a Cloudflare Worker and a\ndatabase that moves findings between checkouts, and `re/knowledge.json`\nstays the copy that ships and that you review in git.\n\n```bash\npython re/cloud/sync.py pull        # bring in what others have found\npython re/cloud/sync.py contribute  # send what this checkout worked out\n```\n\nThe released Lab and checkout include the shared endpoint, so no per-user\nsetup is needed. `re/private/cloud.json` or `ZT1_KB_API` can override it.\n**Sharing** in the Lab's header turns it on (off until you do): the Lab then\nreceives and sends in the background - at start and every half hour, sending\nonly what changed - and its dialog shows when it last did and **Share now**.\nThe Lab can sync from its control port too, so an AI agent can\n(`lab_kb_cloud`). Anything contributed lands **provisional** until it is\nreviewed, and a contribution from a `zoo.exe` build other than the 2002\nComplete Collection is refused - its addresses would land on the wrong\nfunctions. Recordings never leave the machine; the addresses they found\ndo. See **[`re/cloud/README.md`](../re/cloud/README.md)**.\n\n## Explore (start here)\n\nThe original game has no source code and no documentation. What's known\nabout it is spread across the knowledge base, the recordings, Ghidra and our\nengine's comments. **Explore** joins all of it into one map you can ask in\nplain words. Nothing is sent anywhere: the search runs in the Lab.\n\n- **Ask** \"selling animals\", \"show tank\", \"where is the zoo saved\",\n  \"cHungerCheck\" or an address like `0x4378df`. Each answer says what it is\n  (a function, a recorded action, a config key, a struct, our engine's\n  code), why it matched, and where to go next:\n  - **Open its code** shows the function in Original Game.\n  - **Open in Write Code** jumps to the engine line.\n  - **Which game files set it** runs a `has:` search for a key.\n- **What's missing** lists every action recorded in the original, against\n  our engine:\n  - **Copied from the original** and **Partly traced** come from what our\n    engine's code cites, so they're reliable.\n  - **Probably in our engine, not traced** and **Not found - check** are\n    guesses from the action's words, until someone checks.\n  - **Not in our engine** only ever comes from a person's checked verdict.\n\n  Open an action's **Details** to see:\n  - our engine's closest code;\n  - the functions that run only for that action, green where our engine\n    cites them (click one to read it);\n  - everything that ran in the recording.\n\n  Checked it? Press **Our engine does this** or **Not in our engine**. The\n  verdict is saved in `re/lab/verdicts.json` with your name.\n- **The map** is `re/private/atlas.json`, built by `re/lab/atlas.py` from\n  the knowledge base, the recordings, Ghidra's function list and texts, and\n  every comment and function in `src/`. When you record or name something,\n  Explore says the map is stale; **Rebuild the map** takes a few seconds.\n- From an AI agent (MCP): `lab_action exAsk \"<question>\"`, `exMissing [status]`,\n  `exOpenAction \"<label>\"`, `exVerdict \"<label>|<status>|<file:line>|<note>\"`\n  and `atlasBuild`.\n\nThe usual loop for a modder:\n1. Play the original (Play › Play original) and record an action.\n2. Rebuild the map, and look the action up in Explore.\n3. Read its own functions (Open its code), write the feature (Write Code),\n   and cite the addresses in a comment.\n4. Rebuild: it turns green.\n\n### Code 1:1 with zoo.exe (Explore's right side)\n\nOur engine is meant to be the original, 1:1. **Code 1:1 with zoo.exe**\n(next to Recorded actions) shows how far it is, both ways round. It's built\nfresh each time by `re/lab/fidelity.py`, and the answer is saved to\n`re/private/fidelity.json`.\n\n- **Formula checks** (`re/lab/formulas.json`) run the original's function\n  in zoorun and ours on the same numbers, case by case:\n  - On the original's side, zoorun lays the objects out from each case's\n    inputs, and reads back ST0 for a float, eax, or a field.\n  - On our side, the engine's `formula` command calls the very function\n    the game calls.\n  - The checks run again by themselves after every build that works, and\n    a toast says if any case differs.\n  - To add one, make our formula a function the game and the check both\n    call. Then add it to `formulas.json` and to the `formula` command in\n    `src/Lab.cpp`.\n- **The original's gameplay, by system**: every function the knowledge\n  base names, grouped by system (money, tasks, jobs, guests, animals,\n  staff…). Each is green if our engine cites its address and red if it\n  doesn't. Click one to open it.\n- **Our code, by file**: every function in our gameplay, UI and drawing\n  code, and whether it says where it came from. A function counts as\n  marked when its comment or body has one of:\n  - a `zoo.exe 0x…` address (copied);\n  - `measured:` (measured from the original's behaviour, saying how);\n  - `ours:` (the original has nothing like it);\n  - `addition:` or a `Features::` switch (a new feature).\n\n  The rest are listed to go through. **#** counts the *bare numbers*: a\n  number with a point in it on a line that doesn't say where it's from.\n  Those belong in `src/OriginalConstants.hpp` with their address, or come\n  from the game's files (economy.cfg's keys are read into\n  `Original::economy()`).\n- From an AI agent (MCP):\n  - `lab_action exMode fidelity`\n  - `exFidOpen \"o:<system>\"`, `\"f:src/<file>\"` or `\"x:<check id>\"`\n  - `fxRun`\n\n## The views\n\nThe tabs are named for what you do in each: **Original Game** studies the\noriginal zoo.exe; **Write Code**, **Test Zoo** and **Play Game** are our\nengine; then **Mods**.\n\n| Tab | Was | What it's for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Original Game** | Functions + Code | One window for the original zoo.exe. Left: find a function - search names, notes and the game's text (with the functions using each), `field:0x3d6`, filter by system. Middle: its code - decompiled C, or raw instructions where Ghidra made no function. Right: name it and note what it does (saved to the knowledge base and Ghidra), what calls it, the fields it uses, where our engine copies it. |\n| **Write Code** | Engine code | Our engine's own source (and, on the left, the game's files): write the feature, **Build engine**. |\n| **Test Zoo** | Scene | Our engine running a test zoo, as a scene editor and debugger (below). |\n| **Play Game** | Play | **Play ZT1 Engine**: the whole game as our engine runs it now. **Play original**: the real zoo.exe, here in the same view, windowed and at the view's size by our hook (see *The original game in Play* below). |\n| **Mods** | Experiments | Three sections, switched at the top of its left column. **Mods**: make, test and share mods (see *Mods* below). **Game files**: every file the game has. **Lab scripts**: small C++ scripts that run in the Test Zoo without changing the engine - checking one of the original's functions against ours, or making things such as the HD art mod (see *Making mods with Lab scripts*); edit, see what it will do in plain words, **Build & test**. |\n| **Help** | New | Search the bundled guides, follow the current studio tutorials and play the recordings. Article history, section links and enlarged pictures keep longer lessons easy to navigate. |\n\n**Laid out as you like:** Original Game (Find · Code · Name & details), Write\nCode (Files · Editor · Build) and Test Zoo are made of panels. Drag a panel's\ntab onto another's side to dock it there, onto its middle to stack them, or\nout to float it; drag the edges to resize. **▦ Layout** (top right) brings a\nclosed panel back or resets the tab to its default. Each tab's layout is\nremembered.\n\n### Finding things the Lab couldn't before\n\n- **Game files, `has:text`** looks *inside* the game's text files (.ai, .cfg,\n  .lyt…), the copies the game really uses (a patch's over the original).\n  Spaces and case don't count: `has:cpreattack=1` lists every animal with\n  `cPreattack = 1`, with the matching lines under each file, and opening one\n  jumps to that line. Combine it with words: `animals/trex.ai has:bpreattack]`.\n  (A section is written `[m\\BehaviorSet\\bPreattack]`, so search the name with\n  just its closing bracket.) An open game file's bar says what our engine\n  makes of it: where our code names it, or - for a species' .ai, which goes\n  through the one loader - how many of its keys our engine reads (`107 of\n  its 193 keys: Class, Type…`, each a link to where it's read).\n- **Functions, `field:0x3d6`** lists every instruction in zoo.exe that\n  touches that offset - read or written, and the function it's in (or the\n  bare code). It's how you find who uses a value the species loader filled.\n  Add a size to keep to it: `field:0x3b0 byte` (or `word`, `dword`). The\n  function each is in comes from Ghidra (a function's code can be in\n  pieces). Above the list, a form names that offset in a struct\n  (`ZTAnimal` + `0x3b0` = `rampaging`, bool) - saved to the knowledge base,\n  then shown as Known and suggested in Write Code.\n- **Where used on a global** (an address from 0x600000, a value in the\n  game's data): the box has **Name it** - `0x641560` = `cDefenseRadius` -\n  saved to the knowledge base's constants. From then on the decompiled C\n  shows the name beside it: `DAT_00641560 cDefenseRadius`.\n- **Find in the code**: the Code panel's header (it stays in view) has a Find\n  box - the lines holding the words lit, Enter for the next, Shift+Enter\n  back, `1 of 3`. It's kept as you open other functions, and a function\n  newly opened goes to its first match.\n- **Where used on text nobody uses**: a string marked \"not used by any\n  function (yet found)\" has a **Where used** button. Its places are often in\n  code Ghidra never made a function of (reached through a table).\n- **Raw instructions**: open such an address and Code shows zoo.exe's\n  instructions there (re/lab/zoobin.py, capstone), from where that code\n  starts (a guess: just after the code before it ends), the address marked,\n  and the text each instruction points at named (`\"Started preattack\"`).\n  The page is titled `Code at 0x…` with **no function yet**, and\n  **Make it a function** (in the header, always in view) turns that code into a Ghidra function from where it\n  starts, and Original Game then shows its decompiled C, callers and all. The\n  starts are kept in `re/made_functions.json`: the Lab's Ghidra bridge makes\n  them again each time it starts (it opens zoo.exe read only), and\n  `ZT1_Apply` makes them for good in your own Ghidra project.\n- **Engine code suggests the binary**, beside clangd's C++ suggestions:\n  inside `ZT_STEP(\"` or `ZT_FN(\"` type a name, part of what a function does\n  or an address, and pick from zoo.exe's mapped functions (the knowledge\n  base's first, then Ghidra's named ones) - the address goes in. After `+0x`,\n  the mapped struct fields (`ZTAnimal+0x3` → zapCheck, egg, hatchIn…). Inside\n  `num(\"` / `set(m, \"` / `pushSet(m, \"`, the game's own .ai keys and\n  behaviour sets, with how many files use each (`num(\"cpre` → cPreattack in\n  24 files). Hover an address for its name and summary, or a game key for\n  where it's set. Name what you find (Functions › Name) and it's suggested\n  from then on.\n- **Scene › Watch** (a panel): follow animals and the engine logs a row each\n  time one starts doing something else or goes a tile, in game time - paused,\n  playing or skipping time. A hunt reads like a script: `roar_up_left`,\n  `shake`, `snort`, `dustball`, `(gone)`. Pick an animal, then **Follow**.\n- **Steps during a time skip**: skipping 200 s keeps the steps the engine\n  reports on the way (the last 1500), so a rampage that starts at 140 s is\n  there to read afterwards. A skip from an agent (`lab_scene_time`) waits\n  till the engine says the skip is done.\n- **Grow the database** (top of Find, in Original Game): what zoo.exe says\n  about itself, beside what `re/knowledge.json` has, saved in one click. Each\n  save is under the tool's name (\"Lab loader mapper\"), so it's told apart\n  from what you name yourself.\n  - **Config loaders**: every key the game reads (`cHungerCheck`) and where it\n    goes, a struct's field or a global, grouped by the function reading them.\n    The readers it looks for are in `re/lab/loaders.json` (int, string,\n    float). Name the struct a loader fills (`ZTKeeperType`) and **Save**: each\n    key becomes a field at its offset where nothing's named yet, each global\n    is labelled with its key, and the loader itself is named\n    (`ZTKeeperType::loadConfig`, or `loadAnimalSettings` for a loader of\n    globals only). \"N in the base\" counts what's mapped already.\n  - **Class tables**: the 223 tables of functions (vtables) zoo.exe's\n    constructors store. Open one to see each slot's function and which named\n    classes share it: shared slots are the class they come from (ZTAnimal and\n    ZTGuest share 112 of 197). Name it and **Save**, and `kb.py show` then\n    says which tables a function is in (`ZTAnimal (slot 0x2c)`).\n  - **The game's text**: functions named by their own debug text\n    (`GXVidMan::flip`) and source files from the developers' paths\n    (`gx/gxvidman.cpp`). **Save all** gives provisional names where nothing's\n    named; a function with only a file gets `gxvidman_4d64ab` till it has a\n    name. One text in several functions is numbered (`unlockSurface2`).\n  - **Refresh the Lab's Ghidra** (top right of the window): the Lab's Ghidra\n    reads a copy of your project (`E:\\zt1-ghidra-work`), so after ZT1_Apply,\n    or naming things in your own Ghidra, this copies your project over it and\n    restarts the bridge. Close your project in Ghidra first: it's locked while\n    it's open.\n  - **Naming a class table:** let the census (Play › the bar's readout) say\n    what its objects are (`helicopter`, `herbchow`). A type's table has its\n    config loader at slot 0x60, so `kbClassCompare` names the type tables.\n    Comparing slots finds the base classes: a table no object uses that\n    shares most of its slots with a whole family (ZTStaff, ZTUnit). The census\n    uses the knowledge base's class names.\n  - From an AI agent (MCP), `lab_action`: `kbTab loaders|classes|text`,\n    `kbLoaderBrief all` (every loader's keys, a line each), `kbLoaderKeys`,\n    `kbLoaderSave \"0x4c34ae|ZTKeeperType\"` (add `|likely` for a guess), `kbClassSlots`,\n    `kbClassCompare \"0x62ed84 0x62f0b4\"` (each table's slots shared with every\n    named class, its own, and its known functions), `ghidraRefresh`, `kbClassSave\n    \"0x62ff54|ZTAnimal\"`, `kbTextSave`, and `kbOrphans` / `kbMakeOrphans` (the\n    engine's zoo.exe references that fall where Ghidra made no function, each\n    with where its function would start and what comes before it; check the\n    raw code before making one, because a piece of a bigger function or a\n    jump table can look like a start).\n- **The Test Zoo survives a build**: Build engine restarts the engine, and\n  the Lab then makes the zoo again - the map, every fence, animal and object\n  placed with the mouse, the pins, the Watch follows - and runs it back to\n  the same time. The engine log says so (\"made again after the build: 17\n  steps of set-up, back to 199 s\"). Loading a map starts the list afresh.\n\n## The Scene\n\nThe Scene is our engine's real world, the same code the game runs. You can\nbuild in it, watch its code run step by step, and try zoo.exe's own code on\nanything in it.\n\n**Laid out as you like:** every piece of the Scene tab is a panel. The pieces\nare the Scene view, Zoo, Inspector, Steps, Step, Our code, zoo.exe C, zoo.exe\nbinary, Compare, Errors, Try zoo.exe, Log and Console. You can:\n\n- drag a panel's tab onto another panel's side to dock it there, onto its\n  middle to put it in with that panel as a tab, or off to float it;\n- drag the edges between panels to size them;\n- close a panel with its ×.\n\n**▦ Layout** (top bar) brings panels back and switches the Scene view's\nfloating pieces on and off: the tools, the map and view bar, play and time,\nthe status box (what's under the cursor), and the exhibit names. It also\nsaves layouts by name, loads them, and resets to the default. The layout is\nkept between runs. **Classic** goes back to the fixed layout (`layout.js`,\nusing dockview-core).\n\n**The zoo**\n\n- **Maps:** the map name (top right, or top left) loads any of the game's\n  freeform maps, as it is or made flat (all grass, nothing in it). The game's\n  files are what the engine reads, expansions included. The Lab is where\n  unfinished things get tried.\n- **Save as a map** (beside the map name): the zoo as a freeform map of your\n  own, in a mod - its name, description, size, and which mod. The engine\n  writes the design (`SaveGame::saveMap`: its ground, paths, walkways,\n  fences, exhibits and objects over the map it was loaded from, and the view\n  it starts on) and re/lab/mapmaker.py packs it: `freeform/<id>.scn` (the map\n  it's over's .scn, with `[start] design=` and `[freeform] title=`),\n  `freeform/<id>.zt1map`, its description and a 367x276 picture of the view\n  you're looking at. Switched on, the mod's map is in the game's freeform map\n  list (the \"custommaps\" switch).\n- **A blank map** (the map list's first entries): flat grass with nothing on\n  it, Small 75 x 75, Medium 125 x 125 or Large 150 x 150 as the game's maps\n  are, or any size from 16 to 512 a side. It starts in **Edit the map**\n  (beside the map name; **✎ Editing the map** while on): walls, entrances\n  and scenery go anywhere, not only inside the zoo. Paint the ground and\n  paths, draw the zoo's boundary with the Zoo Fence, and put down an\n  entrance - the Zoo Entrance, Dinosaur Digs' or Marine Mania's (`aqgate`:\n  the game has no name for it), and their admission booths, all under\n  Buildings - where guests should come in: the wall opens across its\n  doorway. Switch editing off to see the zoo\n  as the game will (its inside is what the wall and entrance close in; the\n  Exhibits view and the Terrain view show the walls, the zoo wall in red).\n  Saved as a map, it's packed with `[start] blank = W H` and no map of the\n  game's under it; the game's map view draws what you made.\n- **Terrain** (the view): the whole map from above - each tile its ground's\n  colour, lit by its height, paths pale, objects as dots - to design a map\n  by. The tools work in the Scene view (the Lab's own place tool switches\n  back to it).\n- **Zoo outline** (left): every exhibit, animal, staff member, guest,\n  building and kind of scenery. Click one to select it and centre on it. The\n  **Mods** tab beside it holds the mods.\n- **Placing** (**＋ Place**, or P): the buy panels' items by tab, with the\n  game's icons, plus terrain paint and height tools. Each is put down with\n  the game's own tool, so the same rules apply. Fences and paths have the\n  drag modes (Bend, Straight, Rectangle: Tab). Animals can be male or\n  female, objects turn (R), and the terrain brush has a size.\n- **Other tools:** **Bulldoze** (B), **Undo** (Ctrl+Z), and right-click to\n  stop a tool.\n- **Animal** (the view): a species' art on its own. Pick any of its\n  animations; **Layers** draws a Marine Mania animation's layers together\n  (\"shadow\" + its art, \"under\" + its art, then the animation, at one anchor:\n  the red cross); ◀ ▶ hold every layer on one frame and step it. **Check**\n  goes through the species in bulk: art missing or not loading, layers missing\n  or with other frame counts, behaviour-set steps the engine skips (not done\n  yet), moves to places it doesn't know, and animations or sets named but not\n  there. **Check marine** does it for every tank animal.\n- **Route** (T): can it get there? Pick **Guest** or **Staff** and click\n  where from, then where to (each click after is a new where to). Or pick\n  **Picked animal** and click where to. The search runs by that kind's own\n  rules: guests on paths and not into exhibits, staff out through gates with\n  paths cheaper, an animal on its exhibit's ground. It's drawn on the ground:\n  - what it looked at, fainter first\n  - what was still open (yellow)\n  - tiles it couldn't go onto (red)\n  - steps refused, such as a fence or cliff edge (red edges)\n  - the route (green), the start (blue) and the goal\n\n  The chip says how it went.\n- **Routes** (an overlay): where everyone is walking (animals orange, guests\n  blue, staff green, goals as diamonds), and the picked one's last route\n  search drawn as above. Its inspector has a **Route** section: where it's\n  going, and how its last search went. If it failed, it says why: no way\n  there, gave up at its limit, or off the map. Each search is also a step\n  (\"Route search done\") you can stop on. **Blocked** is the engine's older\n  staff overlay: where staff can't walk.\n- **Inspector** (right): click anything (an animal, guest, keeper, building,\n  rock, exhibit, the ground) for its live values. Type a new value to change\n  it. **ƒ** opens the zoo.exe code behind a stat.\n- **Pins** (⊙ beside a stat): an animal's or guest's value held where it's\n  set, after every update. Pin a lion's hunger at 100 and watch what the\n  engine does about it. Its steps still show what the engine tries each\n  tick, so you see the rule fire and the pin hold.\n- **Actions** (the inspector): buttons for what's picked.\n  - **animals:** hungry, tired, sick, heal, happy, unhappy, dung, dart,\n    crate, plus any of its animations (**▶ Play**) or behaviour sets\n    (**▶ Do**), on demand\n  - **guests:** hungry, thirsty, bathroom, tired, leave, their animations\n  - **staff:** their animations\n  - **exhibits and tanks:** the game's console commands: food, no food,\n    dung, wear and break fences, dirty water\n\n  Each press is a step in the Steps dock.\n- **Clock** (bottom left): run or pause (Space), 1–8×, or +1 s / +1 m of\n  game time. A running mod shows its steps there instead.\n- **Overlays** (top right): the grid, exhibits' outlines and names, routes,\n  where staff are blocked, footprints. Footprints also marks each door (a\n  building's `entrpos`, turned with it): green on a path, yellow off one. The\n  console's `doors` lists them. The console's `filters` shows each filter's\n  state and sound, and `drain` / `fill` empty or refill a tank. A selected\n  tour guide shows its followers; a selected guest, the guide it follows. Under the cursor (bottom right) you see the tile, its\n  height and ground.\n\n**Steps** (the dock under the view): the engine's code as it runs.\nThe engine's code reports its steps through trace points (`src/LabTrace.hpp`:\n`ZT_STEP`, `ZT_FN`, `ZT_WHO`). Each step gives:\n\n- the file and line\n- the zoo.exe function that code is ours of\n- what it read, and what it changed (before → after)\n\nThe steps are listed on the left (drag the divider to widen it). Beside them,\nthe step picked: what, who, when, its zoo.exe function, and all its values,\nthen panes, one or two side by side (**◫ Split**; **⤢** makes the dock tall):\n\n- **Our code:** the engine's line (lit, its values under it, what ran since\n  the last step tinted).\n- **zoo.exe C:** the function it copies, decompiled.\n- **zoo.exe binary:** that function's own machine code run on what's picked\n  (as **Try zoo.exe** runs it): every instruction, the registers, and what\n  each read and wrote, by field name; red where it touched something nobody's\n  mapped. It runs on the values as they are now (stopped at the step: as they\n  were then).\n- **Compare:** the step's values beside what the original read or wrote in\n  the field of the same name: same, or differs.\n- **Errors:** what the original reached for and couldn't find (fields not\n  mapped, empty vtables, null pointers), each with **Name it** and **Give a\n  value**; it runs again after.\n\nThe tabs show counts: values that differ, and errors.\n\n- **Trace:** what's picked (an exhibit includes its animals, and using a tool\n  traces the tool), everything, or nothing.\n- **Stopping:** **Stop at each step**, **Stop at the next**, or a breakpoint\n  (click a line number, or F9). The engine really stops there: the world\n  frozen mid-update, still drawn, the one it's stopped on ringed in cyan.\n  **Continue** (F8), **Over** (F10), **Step** (F11), **Out** (Shift+F11).\n- What has trace points so far:\n  - **animals:** status checks, behaviour choices, eating, adopting, exhibit\n    ratings\n  - **guests:** arriving, needs, deciding where to go, watching and what they\n    think of it, donations, prices and buying, leaving, every thought\n  - **staff:** looking for work, keepers' visits (food, healing, raking),\n    maintenance jobs, catching escaped animals, tour guides\n  - **the tools:** placing, fences, paths, bulldozing\n\n  More code gets them as it's worked on.\n\n**Try zoo.exe** (the dock): run one of the original game's functions on\nwhat's picked. Find a function in Code, Functions or Steps and press **Try it\nin the Scene** (or type its address or name). `re/lab/zoorun.py` runs zoo.exe's\nown x86 code (Unicorn), sandboxed. What's picked is laid out as the knowledge\nbase says its struct is, filled with live values: the animal as ZTAnimal, its\nspecies as ZTAnimalType from its `.ai`. You see:\n\n- **in red, what it wanted and couldn't find:** an offset nobody's mapped,\n  an empty pointer or vtable. Each says **where** (the instruction), with\n  **Name it** (saved to the knowledge base; use the engine's name for it and\n  it's filled live), **Give a value**, and **Game files**.\n- **what it used:** known fields, with their values (read, or before →\n  after), the text it used, the globals (as in the file), the functions it\n  called (**Skip** them, 0 back, or **Run them** too).\n- its instructions as they ran, with the registers and what each touched,\n  and its C with the lines that touched known (green) or unknown (red)\n  fields lit.\n\nRun it again after naming or filling something, until it's all green. Then\n**Apply what it wrote** puts its results into the scene, and **Make it a\nmod** starts a mod for it. It needs `pip install unicorn capstone`.\n\n**Log** and **Console** (the dock): the engine's log, and the game's own dev\nconsole (`help` lists its commands). A command's answer always shows, even when the log was scrolled up. Ones for modders: `text <id> ...` (the game's words for ids - cNameID, cUsedThought), `doors` (each building's door tiles, on a path or not), `fences` (the map's fences by type, which are zoo wall, how many tiles are inside) and `fences map` (inside `#`, outside `.`, zoo wall `+`, drawn - when nothing will build, it shows where the wall leaks).\n\nThe place tool says when the game put nothing down (an iceberg off a tank's water, say) instead of saying nothing. The Watch panel empties when a new map or engine starts. A scene load sent while the engine restarts is sent again to the new one; a map name it doesn't know says so and what loaded instead (a mod's map is listed once the mod is on and the engine has started again). **Save as a map** puts the map's description in a new mod's About too.\n\n## How it works\n\n`main.js` starts our engine twice:\n\n- **The Lab's engine** (`zt1-engine.exe --lab-host 18490`) is out of sight\n  and silent. It builds and steps the mods, and sends its view to the app as\n  frames.\n- **The game** (`zt1-engine.exe --embed <window>`) is a borderless window\n  kept over the Play view, moving and hiding with the Lab.\n\nBoth close when the Lab closes.\n\nGhidra comes in through the `ZT1_LabBridge` script (127.0.0.1:18489). The\nLab starts it for itself: when nothing answers there as it opens, it runs a\nheadless Ghidra on the project, read only (`ZT1_LabBridgeHeadless.py`; the\npill says **Ghidra starting** for a minute or two), and closes it with the\nLab. Where Ghidra and the project are: `re/private/ghidra.json`\n(`{\"home\": \"...ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC\", \"project\": \"E:\\\\zt1-ghidra-work\",\n\"name\": \"zt1\"}`) if you make one, else `E:\\ghidra\\ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC` and\n`E:\\zt1-ghidra-work` (a copy of `E:\\zt1-ghidra`, so your own Ghidra's\nproject isn't locked) or `E:\\zt1-ghidra`. Its output: `re/private/ghidra-bridge.log`.\nYour own Ghidra with the bridge on is used instead (in Ghidra, with zoo.exe\nopen: Tools > zt1-engine > **Start Lab bridge**, or **Ctrl+Alt+L** on a\nfunction). Everything fetched is saved in `re/private/ghidra/`, so code\nyou've seen still shows with Ghidra closed.\n\nWhat the game prints in Play goes to `re/private/play.log`.\n\n## Engine code\n\nThe **Write Code** tab is our engine's own source, edited and built in the\nLab. The editor is Monaco (VS Code's editor), and clangd (`clangd/`, the C++\nlanguage server) works on the code behind it.\n\n### Scripts (＋ New script)\n\n**＋ New script** (beside ＋ New file) makes a script in `src/scripts/`:\nengine code, built in with the rest, with the zoo's parts set up for you\n(`src/EngineScripts.hpp`). Pick its name, what it does and when it runs:\n\n| When it runs | Its function | Run it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run once | `void fn(Zoo &zoo)` | **▶ Build & run** on its bar |\n| Every tick | `void fn(Zoo &zoo)` | **▶ Build & switch on** (off again on the same button) |\n| Each animal, every tick | `void fn(Zoo &zoo, Animals::Member &m)` | the same |\n\n`zoo` holds `world`, `animals`, `guests`, `staff`, `fences`, `map`, `clock`\nand `dt`, and what's picked in the Test Zoo: `animal` (a pointer, or\nnullptr), `guest`, `staffMember`, `exhibit` (ids, -1 for none), `tileX`,\n`tileY`. `zoo.say(\"...\")` writes a line in the engine log and a step in\nSteps; `num(x)` writes a number as words. Each template starts with a small\nworking example and a list of all of these.\n\nThe bar over an open script shows its state (not built in yet / on) and\nbuilds it first if anything changed. The engine restarts on the build, the\nTest Zoo is made again as it was (with what was picked, and the scripts\nthat were on), then the script runs. Nothing runs unless the Lab switches it\non, so a script never runs in Play. A script is a place to try a piece of\ngame code on the live zoo, then move it where it belongs. Copying zoo.exe?\n`ZT_STEP(\"0x…\", …)` in a script counts as in our engine like anywhere else.\n\n- **Editing:** highlighting, coloured brackets, folding, a minimap,\n  multi-cursor, and undo.\n- **Finding:** Ctrl+F finds in the file, Ctrl+H replaces, and Ctrl+G goes to\n  a line. The box on the left searches the whole engine.\n- **Code intelligence:** autocomplete of the engine's own classes, hover to\n  see a type, F12 to go to a definition (in any file, opened in a new tab),\n  and Shift+F12 for every use.\n- **Errors:** they show as you type, as red squiggles with \"did you mean…\"\n  fixes. Build errors show the same way.\n- **Calls:** right-click in a function › **Who calls this** (Shift+Alt+H)\n  shows its callers in the **Calls** tab under the editor; each opens to its\n  own callers, and a click goes to the call. **What it calls** turns it the\n  other way round.\n- **Right-click** also has: search the engine for the word, **Scene: stop\n  here** (a breakpoint, F9), **Open its zoo.exe function** (on a line whose\n  `ZT_STEP`/`ZT_FN` names an address), **Copy file:line** and **Build engine**.\n- **The libraries** (`vendor/`: SDL, libzip, the PE loader) open and change\n  here too: type `vendor/` and a name in the search box.\n- **＋ New file** (beside the search box) makes a new `.cpp`, `.hpp` or `.h`\n  in `src/`; it's built with the rest on the next build.\n- **Game mods.** In Game files, an animation open in the viewer has\n  **✦ Make a mod from this…** (`re/lab/animaker.py`): pick one of its colours\n  (and, ticked, its other shades), the colours it flickers through a frame\n  each, and its speed, and it writes the new frames and palette in the\n  game's own format as a mod: `build/Release/mods/<name>/mod.json` and\n  `content.ztd`. **Switch it on** restarts the Lab engine with it loaded.\n  The game loads the mods switched on (`mods.cfg`) before its own files\n  (`src/Mods.cpp`), and players switch them in the main menu's **Get New\n  Zoo Tycoon Items**, the Mod Menu (`src/ModMenu.cpp`). Both are switches\n  (`mods`, `modmenu` in `src/Features.hpp`): off, the original's files only.\n- **Testing menus in Play:** the Lab can click inside the game\n  (`playCmd` action: `click <x> <y>` at the game window's pixels;\n  `src/Embed.cpp`), and the game holds still while the Play tab isn't shown.\n  `move <x> <y>` rests the mouse there (hovering: tooltips, lit pieces);\n  `press <x> <y>` / `release <x> <y>` hold and let go of the left button, so\n  `playSteps \"press 870 337; move 920 337; wait 700; release 920 337\"` is a\n  drag at your own pace (the game follows the mouse's events, not the real\n  mouse, for drags);\n  `snap` saves the next frame that draws a tooltip as `tooltip.bmp` beside\n  the game - its own frame, as a window grab of the GPU window can come\n  back stale. `shot <file.bmp>` saves the next whole frame - the same line\n  the original's hook answers, so `lab_play shot` (a .png) pictures either\n  game in Play. The game holds still while the Play tab is hidden, so a\n  picture needs the Play tab showing. The dev console's\n  `tankwalls <ex> <height>` sets a tank's walls (a raised tank shows its\n  staff climbing in and out).\n- **Screen size in Play:** the game draws 1:1 at the Play view's size, as\n  the original does at any size (menus centred, the in-game screen filling\n  it). The Lab owns the window, so the game's own window-size and full\n  screen buttons are greyed in Play. The game prints `[Embed] ready` once\n  its window exists, and the Lab places it over the Play view then.\n- **Game Options:** the main menu's Game Options (in Get New Zoo Tycoon\n  Items' place) and the in-game disk button open the Options window\n  (`src/ui/UiOptions.cpp`): Game (in a zoo), Graphics, Sound, Controls,\n  Gameplay, Mods & Updates, every setting kept in zoo.ini. The\n  `optionswindow` extra off brings back the original side panel. A\n  checkout's loose `build/Release/ui/startup.lyt` overrides the game's;\n  the engine edits its Game Options line by key, so either works.\n- **Build engine** saves every file and builds the engine. The lab zoo\n  restarts on the new build, and **Restart the game** in Play runs it.\n- **Make release** builds the engine x64 Release in `build-x64/` (the C++\n  runtime built in, today's date as its version) and its installer,\n  `dist/ZT1-Engine-Setup-<version>.exe`, with its `.sha256` and\n  `dist/latest.yml`, the in-game updater's feed. It runs the release\n  tooling (`engine-build-resources/make_release.py`, which needs Inno\n  Setup 6), which is private and only on the release machine; without it\n  the button says so.\n  - The installer holds the engine, its fonts, the licences and the\n    player's README. It holds none of the original game's files: the engine\n    reads the player's own install where it is.\n  - A build made here isn't official, so it never updates itself. Only\n    published releases are.\n  - Nothing is uploaded: releases are published from the release machine\n    (CONTRIBUTING.md).\n\n- **Sync** (the tab under the editor) takes the engine to GitHub and CodeRook.\n  GitHub (`AlexC1991/zt1-engine`) is the home: work goes on branches, and\n  into main by pull request. CodeRook keeps a public mirror of main.\n  1. **Get latest main** merges GitHub's main into your branch. Any conflicts\n     are listed: **Take mine**, **Take theirs**, or open the file and fix it\n     by hand, then **Fixed it**.\n  2. **Commit** commits everything changed. `.gitignore` keeps `build/`, the\n     game's files, `node_modules`, `.cache` and `dist/` out.\n  3. **Push** pushes your branch.\n  4. **Propose** opens a pull request into main on this repo, never on the\n     upstream it was forked from.\n  5. **Mirror main to CodeRook** (the owner only) sends main as it is on\n     GitHub to CodeRook. It works from a copy of main in `.cache/mirror-main`\n     using `cbx submit`.\n\n  **▶ Sync** does 1 to 4 in one go, after showing what goes where. What's\n  offered follows what GitHub says the person may do:\n  - the owner: everything, including the mirror;\n  - helpers with write access: push their branch, then propose;\n  - anyone else: their fork is made and pushed to, and the proposal comes\n    from there.\n\n  Nothing is ever force-pushed (`lab-app/sync.js`).\n\n`compile_flags.txt` at the repo's root tells clangd how the engine is\ncompiled. Each time the Lab starts clangd it writes\n`.cache/lab-clangd/compile_commands.json` from it (every source in `src/`),\nso clangd indexes the whole engine and **Who calls this** finds callers in\nfiles that aren't open.\n\n## Mods (the Mods tab's Mods section)\n\nMaking mods properly, and sharing them. The guide for modders is `docs/MODDING.md`.\n\nThe section is a **studio** (renderer/studio.js): one workspace for the mod being made.\n\n- **The top bar**: the mod (click it for **the picker**: every mod in the game's mods folder, **Find a mod**, each one's on/off switch - switched on, it's in the Test Zoo and in Play when they next start - **New mod**, **Bring one in**, **Mods folder**), its switch, **All saved** / **Not saved**, **▶ Test in the zoo** (switched on, the Test Zoo's engine restarted with it), **Play with it**, **Publish** and **Folder**.\n- **The tree** (left): everything the mod holds, by kind, read by re/lab/studio.py from its .ztd files - its **Animals** (its own, *new*, and the game's it changes, *changed*), **Buildings**, **Scenery**, **Foliage**, **Fences**, **Paths**, **Staff** and **Maps** (its `freeform/*.scn`) - with **＋** on a kind to add one; **＋ Make something**, **About this mod**, and under **Tools** **Paint and recolour art**, **Its files** and **Publish**. (Whether a file changes one of the game's is read from a list of the game's file names kept in `%TEMP%\\zt1-lab-names.txt`, made again when the archives change.)\n- **Make something**: cards, each starting from something that already works in the game - **New animal** (a name and an animal it behaves like), **New map** (a blank map of a size you pick, in the Test Zoo with editing on; **Save as a map** offers this mod), **Change a game item** (any of the game's animals, buildings, scenery, fences, paths or staff, copied in).\n- **The middle** is the editor for what's picked: an animal's animations and the animation editor, a building's or a scenery item's picture and file, a map's preview and **Edit it in the Test Zoo**, and the About, Files and Publish pages below.\n- **The Inspector** (right): the picked animal's, building's or item's values from its file as controls, grouped and named in plain words - an animal's price, food and rest, group and space, babies, what it can do (jumps, climbs, swims...), its habitat wishes, guests and health; a building's price and size, guests inside, what they pay and what it does for them - and **All its values** for the rest. A change is saved into the mod's file as you leave the box (its line changed in place, the file's line ends kept); the file open in Write Code is updated too. Nothing picked: the mod's own details.\n- **An animal's Needs** (its **Needs** tab, renderer/needs.js; re/lab/animalmaker.py `needs`, `setneeds`, `copyneeds`): everything the game weighs for it, from its species file. **Kind and care**: its kind (`[Member]`: a zoo animal, Marine Mania's `aqua`, Dinosaur Digs' `dinosaur`), who looks after it (`cKeeper`: a keeper, the Scientist 9551, the Marine Specialist 9552), what it eats and which food (`cKeeperFoodType`: herbivore chow, meat, fruit, bamboo, grass, fish chow, insects, dinosaur meat and greens, krill, fish, chum, sea grass, sea urchins - food.cfg's order, the expansions' after), needs a shelter. **Home**: biome, continent (or era), family. **Ground it likes** (`[cCompatibleTerrain]`, a slider each), **biomes, plants, rocks and shelters it likes** (`[cSuitableObjects]`, with their icons; + Add, ×) and **animals it lives with** (`[cCompatibleAnimals]`, families and species). Names come from the game's own text (the engine's `texts`) and the catalog (its items' `nameId`). Saved into the mod a moment after the last change. **Copy needs from…** another animal: its food, home, ground, likes and neighbours at once (its kind and keeper too, if asked) - a preset. A game animal a mod changes has the Needs tab too.\n- **New animal** asks its kind first - a zoo animal, a marine animal, a dinosaur - and then offers only animals of that kind to behave like (its behaviour, home, keeper and, for a dinosaur, its egg come with it). Problems says when a kind doesn't fit its behaviour: a dinosaur with no egg (`egg_idle`, `egg_hatch`, `egg_break`), a marine animal with no underwater behaviour.\n- **The dock** (bottom): **Problems** - what's missing or wrong, each one a link to where it's fixed (an animal's animations that play nothing, an animal still all linked to another's, no picture, no About, a map without a description, a .ztd the game can't read) - and the mod's **Log**.\n  - **New mod** starts one from a template: a starter kit, game files (a .ztd), a shader, or empty.\n    The **starter kit** (re/lab/modstarter.py) is a working example of everything a mod can\n    have, made from the player's own game so no game art ships with the Lab: a new rock (a\n    gold copy of the Large Rock, as a `.ucs` with its map art, icons and palettes) in the\n    Habitat panel's Rocks under the Mods filter, its HD art (`hd/`, 4x), an art shader (off\n    at first), a mod.json setting the shader reads, and a README.md walking through each file.\n  - **Bring one in** takes an old Zoo Tycoon download (.ztd), a .zip, or a mod's folder, and makes it a proper mod (dropping one on the page works too). This uses the engine's own importer (`zt1-engine --import-mod <path>`, the same as the game's Mod Menu › Add a mod...).\n- **The pages** the tree opens:\n  - **About this mod**: its mod.json details (name, id, author, version, about), and a short guide.\n  - **Its files**: what it adds or changes - each game thing in its .ztd files (every `.ai`, `.uca`, `.ucs`, `.ucb`, by kind), its .ztd files and what kinds of file they hold, its shaders, its HD art, what its mod.json says it replaces, and its settings. **Click a thing** to open its file in Write Code, to change: it opens as config text with the mod's name on its tab, and **Save** (Ctrl+S) puts it back inside the mod's .ztd (re/lab/ztdedit.py). Its bar has **Open the mod** and **Try in the Test Zoo** (saved, switched on, the Test Zoo restarted).\n  - **About this mod** › **Its picture**: the picture the game's Mod Menu shows beside the mod (367 x 276), set from an image you choose or the Test Zoo's view as it is (another shape is cut to fill it, from the middle). It's `picture.png` in the mod's folder, named by mod.json's `picture`; the mod list and the mod's header show it too.\n  - **An animal** - the animal maker (renderer/pixeled.js, re/lab/animalmaker.py): a **New animal** from scratch - a name and an animal it behaves like (its needs, biome, behaviour and sounds, all in its species file `animals/<type>.uca` to change; **Its species file** opens it) - with no art of its own. Its animations are listed one a row: **Linked** (its line in `[m/Animations]` is a whole path to an animation that exists, the way the game's own `box_idle = objects/box/idle/idle.ani` is - at first the one it behaves like, so it works in the game straight away), **Drawn** (its own) or **Missing**. **Link…** points any row at any animation, the game's or one drawn here; **Draw** opens the editor; **+ A new animation** draws one of a new name (its behaviour sets play it once you add it to one).\n    - **The editor**, as Piskel's: transparent layers (a layer marked **Shadow** is the ground shadow - the game's colour 1), frames on a strip with onion skin, one set of frames for each way it faces (N, NE, E, SE, S: the game mirrors the other three; **Copy from…** another direction to start), a faint **guide** - the linked animation, by its ground point - under the drawing, or start from a copy of it. Tools: pencil (1-4 px, mirror pen), eraser, fill, line, box, oval, picker, move, the ground point (where it stands) and the height band; keys B E G L R O I M A H, [ ], arrows, space, Ctrl+Z/Y. **Import PNG** (one picture, or a strip of frames) and **Export strip**.\n    - **The direction ring** (over the drawing): this frame in all eight ways it faces - N, NE, E, SE and S as drawn, and SW, W and NW as the game mirrors them from SE, E and NE (about the ground point, dashed). Click one to draw that way; a mirrored one draws its source.\n    - **Starting a drawing: Remake, Stencil or Blank.** A new drawing opens **the stencil browser**: anything in the game - **Animals**, **People** (staff, and the guests: man, woman, boy, girl), **Buildings**, **Trees and plants**, **Scenery**, **Fences** - with icons and a search across them all, its animations as chips, and the picked one playing in all its directions (the engine's own frames, so it's quick). It only sets what you see while drawing; it isn't the kind of thing you're making. **Remake** copies its art in, every frame and direction, to change and recolour. **Stencil** lays it over the canvas: its **outline** (cyan, over the drawing) and its **inside** as faint as the **Inside** slider says (0%: the outline alone, 100%: all of it), with as many blank frames as it has, each drawn over its own. **Blank** is an empty canvas. (animalmaker.py `stencils` lists any item's animations: an animal's by its `[x/Animations]`, anything else every animation in its art folder - `objects/<name>/`, `fences/<name>/`, `staff/<name>/`, `guests/<kind>/` - or the folders its file names.)\n    - **The Stencil card**: the one in use (click it to choose another, or **Remake** from it - Ctrl+Z goes back), show or hide it, its outline on or off, **paint only inside** the outline (the eraser and the shadow layer go anywhere; a fill stops at the outline), **fill the stencil** (the pen colour on this layer, in every frame and direction - a solid body to shade and detail), and the **Inside** slider. The stencil is kept with the drawing (`stencil` in its project) and comes back when it's opened again. The mod keeps only your drawing, never the game's art.\n    - **The panels**: on the right, cards of icon buttons (each says what it does on hover) - **Stencil**, **Layers** (new, up, down, rename, shadow, delete; the eye hides one), **Every direction**, **This direction** (copy from another, flip, import PNG, export strip) and **View** (onion skin, mirror pen). **Zoom**: the bar on the canvas (out, in, fit) or Ctrl + scroll, about the pixel under the mouse. **The frame strip**: play, the frame before and after, ms a frame, the frames (+ adds one), then duplicate, move earlier or later, delete.\n    - **Every direction** (each changes every frame of every direction): **Size** (a tenth bigger or smaller, about the ground point), **Body** (longer or shorter along the way it faces in each direction, from the middle of the body), **Band** (the height band picked with ⇕ - a neck, legs, a shell - taller or shorter; heights are the same in every direction, so one band works for all of them, and the ground stays put) and **Recolour a colour…** (one colour and the shades near it turned into another, each keeping how much lighter or darker it was). The canvas grows to fit; Ctrl+Z undoes each in one step.\n    - **Save**: what's drawn is kept as drawn (layers, full colour) in the mod's `.art/animals/<type>/<sub>/<anim>.json` (left out of uploads), and made into the game's art in content.ztd - every drawn animation of the animal on one 256-colour palette made from all of them (`animals/<type>/<type>.pal`: 0 clear, 1 the shadow, up to 254 colours; more are merged and it says so), each frame cut to what's drawn, its `.ani` box worked out - and its row linked to it. The `edStroke` action (\"x,y;x,y\") draws a stroke with the tool in hand, for an agent.\n    - (A frame header's first offset is its ground point's **y**, the second its **x** - src/Animation.cpp; the art maker read them the other way round, now fixed.)\n  - **Art** - the art maker (renderer/artmaker.js, re/lab/artmaker.py): the mod's animations, and for each thing it changes or adds, the game's own art for it (marked *game*: a change saved puts a copy in the mod). One plays in every direction, zoomable, on a checkered, grass or dark background. Its palette is down the right (one palette colours all of an animal's animations, so a change there recolours the whole animal):\n    - **Pick colours**: click the art or a swatch (Shift adds; the picked colours blink magenta on the art). **By shade** picks every colour used that's **Darker** or **Lighter** than a slider - a whole mane, a set of stripes or a coat's bright shades in one go (Shift adds). **Set** gives them a colour (several keep their own shades), **Hue/Light** and **Shift** move them; after a change the blink pauses a few seconds so you see the result. Mind colour 1: it's the ground shadow under an animal (pure black), and picking dark shades takes it too - Shift-click it to drop it.\n    - **Paint**: a pencil in the brush colour (Alt-click a colour), right button to clear, on the frame shown.\n    - **Pattern: fade along the body**: the picked colours turn into a **To** colour from the head back - starting part way, all of them further back - in every frame of every animation of the animal, worked out for each way it faces, with a fixed dither so it doesn't crawl. The shadow (the picked colours touching the bottom of the frame) is left as it is.\n    - **Pattern: stripes across the body**: bands of the picked colours, each made its own darker shade, across the back (From/To along the body, Stripes, Width, Darker, Down the body) in every frame of every animation, for each way it faces; a stripe never repeats in under 4 pixels, so it stays a band on a diagonal facing.\n    - The game's art index is kept between steps (`%TEMP%\\zt1-lab-artindex.json`, made again when the game's archives change), so each step takes under a second.\n    - **Save into the mod**, **Undo**, **Frames out (PNG)** (to the mod's `.art` folder, left out of uploads, to paint in another program) and **Frames in** (each pixel the palette's nearest colour). A saved file with one of the game's paths is listed in mod.json's `replaces`.\n  - **Publish**: below.\n- **Write Code > Game files**, an animal's `.ai` open: **New species from this** (re/lab/newspecies.py) makes a new animal as a mod: the animal's whole art folder copied from your own game under the new name (animations pointed at the new folder, frames at the new palettes, palettes dyed a colour you give - white takes the colour, black stays black), its `.ai` as a `.uca` with its own type, number and `[1033]` name. Into a new mod, or one there is (a pack of animals; or this one made again - its species file kept). Any text file open there: **Change it in a mod** copies it into a mod's content.ztd at the same path (so it takes the game's place while the mod's on), adds it to the mod's `replaces` and opens the copy to change. Saving into a mod the Test Zoo has open stops its engine for the write and starts it again (Windows won't replace an open file).\n- **Opening files by name (MCP)**: `lab_game_file` and `lab_open_engine_file` take a game path (`animals/tiger.ai`, opened read only at a line) or a mod's own file, `mod:<folder>|<ztd>|<entry>` (opened in the editor to change; Save puts it back in the .ztd).\n- **Names in a mod**: a `[1033]` section with `cName` in any `.ai` names it in the game (as the original reads it, zoo.exe 0x597337); add `[defaultLCID]` `LCID = 1033` so players with another Windows language see it too.\n- **Game files' search**: `type:` takes an extension too (`type:uca`), and a mod's files have their own **Mods** chip.\n  - **Publish**: **Check it** applies the same rules the repository's checks use (game data only, no paths outside the mod, at most 200,000 files, a mod.json with an id and a name). **Upload it** zips the mod and sends it in chunks to the mod repository (`https://mods.coderook.com/v1/modrepo/zt1`); there it's checked again, including a malware scan, and approved automatically, then it's in every official game's Mod Menu › Mod repository. Uploading again updates it. Where it's up already, its status and **Take it down** show here too. **Your uploads** lists every mod you've uploaded and what their checks said. You sign in with your CodeRook account (`cbx sign-in`, or a pasted personal token, kept in the Lab's own state). Each mod belongs to whoever uploaded it.\n\n## Making mods with Lab scripts (the toolkit)\n\nA Lab script can make things as well as test them: mods, art packs, anything a program on your PC makes. Its `Lab *lab` has a small toolkit for this (`re/lab/lab_api.h`, \"Making things\"), so you can build your own without changing the Lab:\n\n- `start(program, args, cwd)` runs a program in the background and returns a job number. Poll `running(job)` from `lab_step`, then read `exitCode(job)` when it's done; `stop(job)` ends it. Its output goes to the log, except lines that are only a percentage.\n- `engineExe()` is the engine itself, for its tools:\n  - `--hd-export <folder> [prefix…]` writes map art's frames out as PNGs.\n  - `--hd-finish <frames> <upscaled> <out>` tidies upscaled frames into a mod's `hd/` folder.\n  - `--hd-survey [prefix…]` logs how many frames and pixels there are.\n- `makeDir`, `removeDir` (it never removes a drive or a top-level folder), `writeFile`, `exists` and `countFiles(dir, \".png\")` handle files and folders.\n- `writeMod(folder, name, author, about)` writes a mod's `mod.json`, unless it already has one. A mod's own settings can go in that file too; see `mods/zt1-toon/README.md`.\n- `useModsFolder(folder)` points the game at a mods folder, so it finds what you made. `modsFolder()` says where it looks now.\n\n### The HD art Lab script (`re/lab/scripts/hd_art.cpp`)\n\nThis makes a mod of AI-upscaled map art: the animals, people, fences and objects drawn while the map is zoomed in. The game shows the original art until a player switches the mod on in the Mod Menu or Game Options › Mods & Updates, and the switch takes effect at once. It's an ordinary Lab script, so copy it to make your own version with another upscaler, model or set of art.\n\n- Its settings are at the top of the file: the mod's name and folder, the mods folder, the art to upscale (folder prefixes such as `animals/lion staff/keeper fences/chainlnk`, or empty for all of it), and the upscaler and its model.\n- Running it does three steps:\n  1. The engine writes out the original frames (`--hd-export`). Each frame's clear edge pixels are filled with neighbouring colours so the upscaler doesn't draw dark fringes.\n  2. [Real-ESRGAN ncnn-vulkan](https://github.com/xinntao/Real-ESRGAN) (BSD-3, works on any Vulkan graphics card) makes them 4x. The inspector shows how many frames are done.\n  3. The engine puts them in the mod's `hd/` folder (`--hd-finish`). Sizes are checked against the originals, the original outline is kept, and the texture's fine detail is put back.\n  The work folders are deleted afterwards.\n- Recoloured art comes out in the right colours. A building's chosen colours, staff hair and skin, and guests' clothes are swapped in when the HD frame loads (`HdArt::recolour`), because the pack is made from each frame's own palette.\n- **In the game's dev console:** `hdart` lists the HD art mods with their counts: frames in use, frames missing, and the zoom the map was last drawn at. `hdart on|off` switches them.\n- **Size and time:** the lion, zookeeper and chain-link fence came to 20,630 frames and 792 MB, and took about 3 minutes on an RX 6600. The scientist's 1,785 frames took 64 s. All the map art is about a million frames.\n\n## Driving the Lab from an AI agent (MCP)\n\n`mcp/server.js` is an MCP server that drives the running Lab through its\ncontrol port (127.0.0.1:18492, this PC only). Every tool acts through the\nLab's own UI, so you watch it happen and your mouse and keyboard stay free.\nIt needs only Node, with no packages to install.\n\nHow it answers (its design follows [rea](https://github.com/morluto/rea),\nMIT):\n- **Errors say what to do.** Each has a `code` (`lab_not_running`,\n  `timeout`, `invalid_args`, `lab_error`, ...), `retryable` and a `hint`. A\n  slow Lab is reported as a timeout, not as a Lab that isn't running.\n- **Inputs are checked first.** A missing required input, or a value that\n  isn't one of a field's choices, is refused before the Lab is asked.\n- **Long answers come a page at a time.** Lists take `offset`/`limit`, texts\n  (decompiled C, a game file) `from`/`to`; `paging` gives the totals and the\n  next page's arguments.\n- **Each tool is marked** read-only, destructive (a reload, a sync, removing\n  something) and whether it reaches the internet.\n- **Text from zoo.exe, the game's files and the shared knowledge base** is\n  marked as data (`_meta.untrusted_content`), never instructions.\n- **Deeper questions about zoo.exe** (Ghidra, through the bridge's newer\n  queries - `re/ghidra_scripts/ZT1_LabBridge.py`, ideas from rea's own\n  bridge): `lab_data_type` (a struct's layout as Ghidra has it, to check\n  against the knowledge base), `lab_function_blocks` (basic blocks and each\n  switch table's cases), `lab_call_targets` (every call's target; a class-table\n  call such as `call [eax+0x16c]` matched against the knowledge base's class\n  tables), `lab_value_flow` (where a value goes or comes from inside a\n  function), `lab_search_bytes` (a byte pattern with `??` wildcards) and\n  `lab_noreturn_functions`.\n- **Guided workflows** (MCP prompts): `reverse_a_function`,\n  `copy_behaviour_into_engine`, `test_in_the_test_zoo`, `fix_build_errors`,\n  `map_a_struct_field`.\n\nSetting it up:\n- **Claude Code:** it's registered for this repo in `.mcp.json` (`zt1-lab`),\n  and Claude Code asks you to approve it the first time.\n- **Any other MCP client** (Codex, Cursor, VS Code / Copilot, Gemini CLI,\n  Claude Desktop…): add a stdio server to that client's MCP settings. It's\n  the command `node` with the argument\n  `<repo>/lab-app/mcp/server.js`:\n\n  ```json\n  { \"mcpServers\": { \"zt1-lab\": { \"command\": \"node\", \"args\": [\"C:/path/to/zt1-engine/lab-app/mcp/server.js\"] } } }\n  ```\n\n  (Each client keeps this in its own file, e.g. `.cursor/mcp.json`, or\n  `.vscode/mcp.json` under `\"servers\"`. See the client's own docs.)\n\nStart the Lab first (`ZT1 Lab.cmd`). Without it, every tool answers that the\nLab isn't running.\n\n### Your own paths\n\nThe Lab's settings for your PC live in `re/private/`, which is never\ncommitted:\n\n- **`original.json`:** `{ \"exe\": \"<your zoo.exe>\", \"iso\": \"<a Marine\n  Mania disc image, if the original asks for one>\" }`. The exe defaults to\n  the standard install folder.\n- **`ghidra.json`:** `{ \"home\": \"<Ghidra's folder>\", \"projects\": [\"<your\n  Ghidra project folder>\"], \"name\": \"zt1\" }`. `home` can also come from the\n  `GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR` environment variable — and you can leave `home` out\n  entirely: an installed Lab runs the Ghidra it carries. To make a project:\n  import `zoo.exe` into a new Ghidra project, then run **ZT1_Apply** to bring\n  in everything the knowledge base knows (see `re/README.md`). The views about\n  the original's code need Ghidra. The engine, game files, test zoo and\n  Play views don't.\n\n  *The Lab does not make a project for you yet.* It ships Ghidra with the JDK\n  it runs on, but a Ghidra project is built out of `zoo.exe` — the game, which\n  is nobody's to hand out — so the first project has to come from your own\n  copy. Until the Lab grows a button for it (a headless import of your\n  `zoo.exe`), the decompiled views start working once you point `ghidra.json`\n  at a project with `zoo.exe` in it, as above.\n\nTwo things that help when driving it:\n\n- **lab_state** includes `pageErrors`: the Lab page's own errors (a script that\n  threw, a promise nobody caught, console.error), newest last. An empty or\n  half-drawn view usually has its reason there.\n- **lab_scene_place** takes a short item name when only one item matches it\n  (`dzbar` → `fences/dzbar.ai`); otherwise it says which ones it might mean.\n\nThe tools:\n\n| Area | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Looking at the Lab | `lab_state`, `lab_screenshot` (the window as an image), `lab_read` |\n| Moving around | `lab_tab`, `lab_click`, `lab_type`, `lab_action` |\n| zoo.exe's functions | `lab_search_functions` (names, plus the game's text), `lab_open_function` (decompiled C, callers), `lab_where_used`, `lab_name_function` |\n| Game files | `lab_game_files`, `lab_game_file` |\n| Engine code | `lab_search_engine`, `lab_open_engine_file`, `lab_read_engine_file`, `lab_edit_engine_file`, `lab_save_engine`, `lab_problems`, `lab_engine_calls` (who calls a function, or what it calls) |\n| Sync | `lab_sync_status`, `lab_sync` (pull, resolve, commit, push, propose, mirror, branch: only when asked) |\n| Building and running | `lab_build_engine` (waits; returns ok or the problems), `lab_make_release` (waits; the zip, its sha256, what was left out), `lab_run_mod`, `lab_engine_log`, `lab_play` |\n| The Scene | `lab_scene` (its state and what's selected), `lab_scene_act` (an action on what's selected), `lab_scene_pin` (hold a value), `lab_scene_route` (can a guest, staff or the selected animal get from here to there, and why not), `lab_scene_maps`, `lab_scene_load`, `lab_scene_outline`, `lab_scene_items`, `lab_scene_place` (a tool at a tile, or dragged), `lab_scene_select`, `lab_scene_set`, `lab_scene_remove`, `lab_scene_time`, `lab_scene_view` (also the art viewer: a species' animation, its layers, held on a frame), `lab_scene_console`, `lab_species_check` (a species, or every tank animal, in bulk) |\n| Stepping | `lab_scene_trace` (what's traced, stop at each step, breakpoints), `lab_scene_step` (step, over, out, continue: returns where it stopped), `lab_scene_steps` |\n| zoo.exe's own code | `lab_try_function` (run a function on what's selected: what it used, what's missing, a value for a missing offset) |\n| The Lab itself | `lab_reload` (the page, after changing `renderer/`), `lab_restart` (the whole app, after changing `main.js` or `preload.js`) |\n\nThe Lab has to be running for the tools to work.\n\n## The original game in Play\n\n**Play original** runs the real `zoo.exe` inside the Play view, the way\nOpenZT runs it: a small launcher starts it paused and loads our DLL into it\nbefore its first instruction, and the DLL replaces a few of the Windows calls\nit makes. Both are built by **Build engine** (32-bit, like zoo.exe, from the\n`build/` tree: CMakeLists' part 12), from `src/original/`:\n\n- `Launch.cpp` → `zt1-original.exe --zoo <zoo.exe> --embed <Lab window> --size <w>x<h> --log <file>`\n- Original Game's search also takes `value:10282` (or `value:0x282a`): every instruction using that number as itself - a message id, a key's number - by function (re/lab/zoobin.py `value`); code Ghidra has no function for opens at the line asked for.\n- `Hook.cpp` → `zt1-hook.dll`, which:\n  - answers zoo.ini's `[user] fullscreen` as 0 and `screenwidth`/`screenheight` as the Play view's size, so the game takes its own windowed path (`GXVidMan::setupWindowed`, 0x52f1d0) at that size, and never writes those back: your zoo.ini keeps its own;\n  - keeps its window borderless, owned by the Lab's and over the Play view (CreateWindowExA, SetWindowPos, SetWindowLongA, AdjustWindowRectEx), with the same stdin lines as our engine (`rect`, `show`, `hide`, `focus`, `quit`);\n  - stops the Windows \"DirectPlay\" prompt: the game's DirectX version check loads `dpnhpast.dll` only to tell 8.1 from 8.0, and it's refused, exactly as when the prompt is skipped;\n  - hooks its DirectDraw (made with CoCreateInstance then Initialize, 0x533d0a) to see the surface it draws on: `shot <file.bmp>`, which the Lab turns into a .png (`lab_play` with `shot`);\n  - logs every setting read, window, DLL and COM object, and any crash with its zoo.exe address and callers, to `re/private/original-hook.log`.\n  - the recorder (`arm <file>`, `disarm`: \"hit\" lines) and the Lab's mouse, below.\n\n**Windows' own dialogs.** Saving and loading open Windows' file dialog\n(\"Save a zoo...\"), which isn't drawn on the game's surface, and the game waits\non it. `lab_action origDialog` names the dialog that's open, `origDialog \"text\nmy-zoo\"` fills in the file name, and `origDialog ok` / `origDialog cancel`\npresses its button, and `origDialog \"press Yes\"` presses a button by its text\n(Windows' \"Confirm Save As\" has Yes/No with no fixed ids). `origKey 27` sends a key (a virtual-key code: Esc).\n\n**Recording from an AI agent** comes in three calls, checked between:\n`recDo \"<label>|<playSteps lines>\"` starts it and does the steps, then you\nlook at a `shot`, then `recKeep` saves it (with the names its debug text\ngives) or `recDrop` sets it aside in `recordings/discarded/`. For a long one,\nleave the steps out and wait outside the call: a `lab_action` gives up after\nabout a minute.\n\n**Volume.** The Play bar's slider sets the running game's own volume (either\ngame; Windows' mixer has it on its own: `volume <0-100>`, src/SessionVolume.hpp).\n\n**The Lab's mouse.** `click`/`rclick`/`move <x> <y>` and `drag <x0> <y0> <x1> <y1>`\n(the game's own pixels, as in a `shot`) click in the original without anyone's\nmouse: the hook answers GetCursorPos, GetAsyncKeyState and GetKeyState for the\nmoment it takes, then hands the mouse back (`lab_action playCmd \"click 952 462\"`).\n\n### Recording what runs (the original's recorder)\n\nWhile the original runs, the Play bar has **Learn idle**, **Record** and\n**Recordings**. The hook puts a one-time breakpoint (int3) on the first byte of\nevery function Ghidra knows in zoo.exe (~7,300); the first time one runs, its\nbyte goes back and the game carries on, so it's full speed once warmed up.\n\n1. **Learn idle** (at the screen you'll record on: the main menu, or in a zoo):\n   what runs with nothing being done is \"always running\" and left out after\n   (`re/private/original-quiet.json`).\n2. **Record**: name what you're about to do (\"Placing a fence\"), do just that,\n   **Stop recording**.\n3. **What ran**: each function that ran, in order, with what's known - its\n   name (the knowledge base's, Ghidra's), our engine's code citing it (\"in our\n   engine\"), the game's own debug text naming it (`GXVidMan::createSurface -\n   ...`: ticked to name it so), the original's source file it's from (the\n   developers' paths were left in: `snd/dx8sound.cpp`), the text it uses, where\n   it was called from, and the other recordings it ran in. Click an address to\n   read it in Original Game.\n4. **Save to knowledge base**: every function in it gets \"Ran when: ...\" in\n   `re/knowledge.json`'s new `seen` (kb.py `seen` / `seen-file`), and the ticked\n   names (provisional, by \"Lab recorder\"); **ZT1_Apply** shows them in Ghidra -\n   plate comments and `zt1-seen` bookmarks. The more that's recorded, the more\n   functions run in only one thing: that's what they're for.\n5. **Only what ran in no other recording** (a tick box over the list): leaves\n   out everything another recording has too, so what's left is this action's\n   own code. Placing a zebra outside an exhibit ran 136 functions, and 8 of them\n   ran nowhere else: one chain (0x49ccc3 → 0x49cd46 → 0x49c6d7, the red\n   \"Animals can only be placed in exhibits\" message, → 0x41176c → 0x411797).\n\nA label that turns out wrong (the zebra was refused, not adopted): **Close**\nwithout saving and record it again under the right name.\n\n**Stop the game** ends it even inside a zoo, where the original asks whether\nto quit rather than closing: whatever's still running 4 seconds on is ended,\nwith its launcher.\n\nRecordings are kept in `re/private/recordings/` and matched again when opened\n(what's been learnt since shows). The bridge's `/debugnames` and `/fnstrings`\ngive the debug names and each function's text in one go.\n\n### The original's steps, live (the Scene's step log, for zoo.exe)\n\n**Steps** in the Play bar opens a panel beside the original, the way the\nScene shows our engine's steps. While the game runs it streams what zoo.exe\nis doing.\n\nHow it watches: the hook puts a one-time breakpoint on every function except\nthe idle ones, and the Lab re-arms the ones that ran every second (the\nhook's `rearm`). So each function shows each time it runs, at most once a\nsecond.\n\n- **Known** functions read in words: their name, what they do, and our\n  engine's code for them (click to open it).\n- **Unknown** ones say \"unknown\", with a guess and its evidence, strongest\n  first:\n  - a specific known caller (*high*);\n  - the text it uses, such as `ui/sharedui/question` (the \"are you sure?\"\n    dialog) (*medium*);\n  - \"ran for nothing else recorded\": that action's own (*medium*);\n  - what the few actions it ran in share, such as selling, bulldozing and\n    undo: the refund (*medium*);\n  - the known functions it calls (*medium*);\n  - **the same class slot named in a related class** (*high*). Name\n    `ZTFoodType::isKindOf` at slot 0x1c, and every other type's slot 0x1c\n    is guessed `isKindOf`. \"Related\" means the two tables share at least 30%\n    of their slots;\n  - a function called from many places, in the window or program-wide\n    (Ghidra's `/fncallers`), is \"a helper\", never \"part of\" one caller;\n  - a neighbour is only a clue when it's specific: not a helper, not code\n    seen in many recordings, and not a method every type has (className,\n    isKindOf - called through the class table, so Ghidra counts no callers,\n    yet they run for everything), nor a getter or setter, nor anything in\n    three or more class tables. Ghidra's own labels (`caseD_1`, `switchD_`,\n    `LAB_`) aren't names. A slot's name only spreads from a function that's\n    in that one slot (not `returns1`, which fills dozens);\n  - an unknown caller's guess, inherited a step weaker;\n  - the known code that ran either side of it (*low*).\n- Live, each function shows **once**, with how often it ran (×12) and who\n  called it. **Every call** shows them all in order.\n- **General code** (input, drawing, sound: anything that ran in half the\n  recordings or more) is hidden unless you ask for it.\n- **❚❚ Pause game and log** presses the game's own pause button and holds\n  the list still. **▶ Resume** starts both again. It checks the game's clock\n  first, so it stays right even if you used the game's button yourself.\n- **Click any line** to open its form:\n  - **Name** (the guess's name to start with), **Class**, **What it does**\n    (the guess to start with), and **How sure** (sure, likely, a guess; a\n    guess stays provisional).\n  - **Save** puts it in the knowledge base under your name. The Lab asks\n    your name the first time.\n  - A name that's still a guess (marked *guess*) gets **Confirm**, which\n    keeps it as sure.\n  - **Read its code** opens it in Original Game.\n  - While a line is open, the list holds still too.\n  - Whatever you save shows as known in the panel straight away. ZT1_Apply\n    puts it in Ghidra, and the map in Explore picks it up when rebuilt.\n- **Record** switches the panel to that recording: each function the first\n  time it runs, with the guesses using the action's name. A recording's\n  **Its steps** button replays it in the panel.\n\n### The original's state, live (and finding where it keeps things)\n\nWhile a zoo is open in the original, the Play bar reads its own state from its\nmemory every 2 s: the date, cash, its animals (with their average happiness)\nand guests, and how many things are in the zoo. Click it for the **Census**:\nevery entity by class and species. What it reads (re/knowledge.json, found\nwith the tools below):\n\n| | Where | |\n|---|---|---|\n| game manager | global `0x638048` | cash: float at +0xc (spent by `spendCash` 0x41ef68); the date: a SYSTEMTIME at +0x1194 (a real calendar from Mon 1 Jan 2001, a day each 12 s) |\n| world | global `0x638040` | map size +0x34/+0x38, tiles +0x40, every entity in a vector at +0x80/+0x84 |\n| an entity | | its class = its function table (+0): animals `0x62ff54`, guests `0x630f88`, fences `0x62d808`, tank walls `0x62dbc0`, scenery `0x62d950`, paths `0x62da88`, buildings `0x62e0b0`, ambient animals `0x62d6ec`; its type +0x128 |\n| a type | | short name +0xa4 (elm, zebra, boy), kind +0x98 (foliage, building, zoowall) |\n\n**Find a value** (Play bar) finds the next one, the way a cheat finder does:\nscan for a value you can see (`float 75000`), change it in the game, rescan\nfor the new one; then **Watch writes** (a hardware watchpoint) reports each\ninstruction that writes it, in its function, with the registers - the object\nit's written through. That's how the cash and `spendCash` were found. The\nhook's lines: `scan`, `rescan`, `peek <addr> <n>`, `each <list> <offsets>`\n(a vector of objects' fields at once), `strs <addr> <n>` (the text an object\npoints at: its name), `watch`/`unwatch`. Lab actions for them: `memScan`,\n`memRescan`, `memPeek`, `memEach`, `memStrs`, `memWatch`, `memWrites`,\n`origLive`, `origCensus`, and `playSteps` (\"click 952 462; wait 1500; ...\")\nto get somewhere in the game without the mouse. Actions now give back what\nthey return (`lab_action`).\n\n`poke <addr> <value>` (Lab action `memPoke \"0xADDRESS value\"`) writes a 32-bit\nvalue into the original, to push it somewhere for a test: births are proved by\nsetting a species' cReproductionChance (type +0x278), cReproductionInterval\n(+0x27c) and cHappyReproduceThreshold (+0x378) - on the male's subtype, the\ntype at entity +0x128 whose +0xa4 is \"m\" - then putting them back.\n`origFrames \"<folder> [count] [ms]\"` films the original: a picture every\n`ms` (default 500), saved f000.png, f001.png... to look through frame by\nframe. Popups (the adoption ban, new items) pause the original; OK is at\nabout 712,500 at the Play view's size.\n\nMore for reading what the code refers to:\n\n- `memText \"<address> [bytes]\"`: the text stored right at an address (a name\n  the code compares against: `fWalk`, `fRun`). `memStrs` follows pointers\n  instead.\n- `memTables \"<slots> <address>...\"`: class function tables, each entry as an\n  address, to see which slots a class replaces; `memTables \"uniq ...\"` gives\n  each different entry once.\n- `gameText \"10287-10290 0x2972\"`: the game's own text by number, from its\n  language files (the engine's `texts` command). The numbers in zoo.exe's\n  code - thoughts, messages, status lines - say what the code is for:\n  10287 is \"I've never seen such a lousy show!\".\n- `numberUsers \"10028\"` (or `0x75`): every function with that number in an\n  instruction - a message (\"%s is not happy.\" is used by animalUpdate), a\n  task, a UI element. Ghidra's bridge (`/scalar`) builds the index the first\n  time, in a few seconds. Small numbers also turn up as other things (the\n  shows reuse 10026-10029 as event codes), so read the match.\n- `uiElement \"0x11cb 7000\"`: which layout and element a UI number is\n  (`infocom:ItemCost_Cost`). The code finds elements by number\n  (`uiFind(0x11cb)`). The map is `re/private/ui_ids.json`, made from the\n  game's layouts by `python re/lab/uiids.py` (it also looks numbers up).\n\nAnd for naming many at once:\n\n- `makeFns \"0x52d94f 0x43eb4f ...\"`: makes functions where Ghidra's analysis\n  missed them (class-table entries usually), kept in\n  `re/made_functions.json` so ZT1_Apply makes them in your project too.\n- `namesSave`: a list of `address|name|summary|sure/likely/guess|by|class`\n  lines (a first line `by: <who>` sets everyone's by). Paste a family read off\n  one table: every handler a dispatcher calls by name.\n\nThe families named this way (2026-10-12) are the quickest way into the code:\n\n| What | Where it starts |\n|---|---|\n| Behaviour functions (`f = fWalk(...)` in the .ai files) | `behaviourRunFn` 0x436065, `behaviourRunFnMoves` 0x437ccd: each name to its handler (`fMoveSlow`, `fTargetBuilding`, ...) |\n| Unit tasks (going to a building, eating, chasing, the helicopter's landing) | `unitMakeTask` 0x413acd: `Task67_GoToBuilding`, `Task74_Chase`...; their shared `Task::tick` 0x43679e |\n| Staff jobs (the status line's number: placing food, sweeping, fixing fences) | `unitMakeJob` 0x413a23: `Job2_PutDownFood`, `Job17_FixFence`... |\n| UI handlers (every button, panel shown / hidden, tab let go) | `uiRegisterHandler` 0x512687 from `register_<layout>_handlers`; `infocom_up_Spinner`'s is `onPriceUp` |\n| A unit's class slots | 0x10 handleEvents, 0x2c tick, 0x38 chooseTask, 0x48 startBehaviour, 0x64 debugText, 0x68 save, 0x6c load, 0x10c setup, 0x1d8 setTask, 0x24c habitat, 0x2a4 sellValue, 0x318 statusText |\n\n### Side by side (the original and our engine, day by day)\n\n`lab_action sideStart \"<days> <label>\"` records both games every game day from\nthe same moment: the original's first animal (happiness, +0x2a8) and its\nexhibit (+0xe8: its animals' average happiness), and the Test Zoo's selected\nanimal (happiness, hunger, suitability); it starts the Test Zoo's clock too.\n`sideStop` ends it, `sideShow` charts it (the last 20 runs are kept). Put the\nsame thing in both first - an exhibit and an animal in each - and start it as\nthe original's goes in. The first run (a lone zebra, bare 5x5 chain-link,\nBasic Grass Map): both from 75, the same within the original's random 20-40\ntick check for 16 days; then the original paused for its adoption ban, which\nthe Test Zoo didn't have - it does now (below).\n\n**The original keeps running** in the Play view when the Lab is the window\nin front (left to itself it pauses in the background, as a fullscreen game\ndoes): the hook tells its window it's still active, and its\nGetForegroundWindow answers its own window - 12 s a game day, measured.\n\nSince then it also records guests: their count and average happiness in\nboth, the original's first guest's happiness and needs (+0x2a8, +0x2b0\nhunger, +0x2b8 thirst, +0x2c0 energy, +0x2c8 bathroom) beside a guest\nselected in the Test Zoo, and the original's zoo rating (its game manager's\n+0x2c), which it gives the Test Zoo as it goes (`economy`: the lab zoo keeps\nno books) so both get their guests by the same rating. What it found: the\noriginal's trash check takes 5 from every guest each cTrashCheck unless it's\noff to a trash can, holding litter or not (zoo.exe 0x43aedc) - now ours too.\nPut both exhibits the same distance from paths: guests gawk only from them.\n\n**Goals in the Test Zoo.** The lab zoo runs its map's goals as the game does\n(Lab.cpp `setupGoals`, the same rules as main.cpp's): freeform's donation,\naward and unlock scripts - the adoption ban when the animals' average\nhappiness is under 25 of 100, lifted over 45. A popup is a note in the log,\nand `pause=1` pauses the zoo (and stops a time skip) as the game does; the\nUI elements a goal disables are noted. Goals on cash or the zoo rating are\nleft out: the lab zoo keeps no books.\n\nThe Marine Mania disc image is mounted first if no drive has it (paths in\n`re/private/original.json`: `{ \"exe\": ..., \"iso\": ... }`). The game is drawn\nat the Play view's size when it starts; it isn't resized after.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"binary-reference","title":"Original-game investigation reference","summary":"Knowledge-base tools and the earlier binary Lab interface.","group":"Reference","tabs":[],"legacy":true,"notice":"This reference includes the earlier binary Lab interface and command-line tools. Use Find your way around the Lab for the current app tabs.","source":"../../re/README.md","html":"<p>The original game is the spec. Every rule the engine copies is read out of\nzoo.exe, and its source says where it came from:</p>\n<pre><code>// Hunger (zoo.exe 0x438a79): ...\n</code></pre>\n<p>This folder is where what we know about zoo.exe is kept: <strong>the knowledge\nbase</strong>, <code>knowledge.json</code>. Ghidra scripts read it, add to it, and send\nquestions on, so the work can be done, checked and kept by anyone, with or\nwithout an AI helping.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>File</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Committed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lab/</code></td>\n<td><strong>ZT1 Lab</strong>: the live view on our engine, its scripts and their interface (below)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>knowledge.json</code></td>\n<td><strong>The knowledge base</strong>: functions, struct fields, numbers, notes, questions and answers, and a log of who added what</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>engine_refs.csv</code></td>\n<td>Every zoo.exe address the engine&#x27;s source cites (made by <code>scan_engine.py</code>)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ghidra_scripts/</code></td>\n<td>The Ghidra side (below)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kb.py</code></td>\n<td>The knowledge base from the command line</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>scan_engine.py</code></td>\n<td>Rebuilds <code>engine_refs.csv</code> from <code>src/</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>check.py</code></td>\n<td>ZT1 Check: struct fields checked against zoo.exe&#x27;s loader code, without the game (below)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rd.py</code></td>\n<td>Disassembles one function, its calls labelled with known names</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>msg.py</code></td>\n<td>The game&#x27;s text: a message&#x27;s id from its words, or the reverse</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>private/</code></td>\n<td>Other people&#x27;s names, game text, your report, your name. <strong>Never committed</strong></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-zt1-lab\">ZT1 Lab</h2>\n<p>ZT1 Lab is an editor in the style of Unity, built on our own engine, with\nGhidra as its window into zoo.exe. Start it with <code>zt1-engine.exe --lab</code>\n(from <code>build\\Release</code>), or from Ghidra with <strong>Ctrl+Alt+L</strong> (Show in Lab).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part of the window</th>\n<th>What it shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Left: Scripts</strong></td>\n<td>Your scripts, in <code>re/lab/scripts/</code>. Double-click one to build and run it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Left: Binary</strong></td>\n<td>What&#x27;s in the knowledge base (functions, structs, numbers), and every one of zoo.exe&#x27;s 7,304 functions straight from Ghidra. Pick one to inspect it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Middle: the live view</strong></td>\n<td><code>scene</code>: the lab zoo, our engine&#x27;s real World with a fenced exhibit and its animals (drag to pan, wheel to zoom). <code>heightmap</code>: the map&#x27;s heights from above. <code>animal</code> / <code>sprite</code>: a species or an animation, drawn and playing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Right: Inspector</strong></td>\n<td>The picked function or struct. What your script watches (each value labelled with the offset it maps to), its checks in green or red, its graphs, and the lab animals&#x27; state in the engine.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bottom tabs</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Log</strong> (your script&#x27;s and the build&#x27;s). <strong>Decompiled</strong> and <strong>Assembly</strong>, both from Ghidra; the decompiled view lists the mapped fields and known numbers it uses beside it. <strong>Calls &amp; uses</strong>: what calls it and what it calls (click to go there), and the strings and numbers it uses. <strong>Engine code</strong>: our source that implements it, with Open in code.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Toolbar</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Build &amp; Run</strong>, <strong>Step</strong>, <strong>Auto</strong>/<strong>Pause</strong>, Stop, steps per second, Play world (the engine running freely), the view, and whether Ghidra is connected.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Ghidra as the backend.</strong> With zoo.exe open in Ghidra, run Tools &gt;\nzt1-engine &gt; <strong>Start Lab bridge</strong> (Show in Lab starts it too). The Lab then\nasks Ghidra for code, calls and references on 127.0.0.1:18489, which is this\nPC only. Everything it fetches is saved in <code>re/private/ghidra/</code>, so code\nyou&#x27;ve looked at still shows when Ghidra is closed. <strong>Show in Ghidra</strong> moves\nGhidra&#x27;s cursor to what you&#x27;re looking at in the Lab.</p>\n<p><strong>Scripts.</strong> A script is one small C++ file. <strong>Build &amp; Run</strong> compiles it into\na DLL with Visual Studio. The first build of a session sets up the compiler\n(about 30 s); after that a build takes about 1 s. The Lab then runs it:\n<code>lab_start</code> once, then <code>lab_step</code> each step until it returns false. A crash in\na script stops the script, not the Lab. What a script can do is all in\n<a>lab/lab_api.h</a>:\n- build an exhibit, put animals and objects in it\n- run the engine for so many seconds\n- read and set an animal&#x27;s state\n- read a species&#x27; <code>.ai</code> values, zoo.exe&#x27;s own values and the game&#x27;s text\n- look up a mapped offset\n- log, watch, plot, and <code>expect</code> (a check)</p>\n<p>Here is the happiness script, cut down:</p>\n<pre><code>#include &quot;lab_api.h&quot;\n// zoo.exe 0x4378df          &lt;- what it&#x27;s about: Show in Lab and the Binary list find it\nLAB_EXPORT void lab_start(Lab *lab) {\n  lab-&gt;exhibit(5, 5, &quot;chainlnk&quot;);\n  zebra = lab-&gt;animal(&quot;zebra&quot;, false);\n}\nLAB_EXPORT bool lab_step(Lab *lab, int step) {\n  double before = lab-&gt;get(zebra, &quot;happiness&quot;), pending = lab-&gt;get(zebra, &quot;pending&quot;);\n  lab-&gt;runEngine(1.0f);                                    // one tick of the engine\n  double mine = clamp(before + pending, -100, 100);         // my reading of 0x4378df\n  lab-&gt;expect(&quot;engine == my reading&quot;, lab-&gt;get(zebra, &quot;happiness&quot;) == mine);\n  lab-&gt;plot(&quot;happiness&quot;, lab-&gt;get(zebra, &quot;happiness&quot;));\n  return step &lt; 90;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>To start one for any function, pick it in Binary and press <strong>New script for\nit</strong>. That writes a skeleton and opens it in your editor.</p>\n<p>The three that come with it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Script</th>\n<th>What it checks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>happiness</code></td>\n<td><code>0x4378df</code>: happiness += pending, kept to -100..100, every tick. It passes on all 90 ticks; a lone zebra drops from 75 to 20.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hunger</code></td>\n<td><code>0x438a79</code>: hunger rises by the species&#x27; <code>cHungerIncrement</code> every 30 ticks. On its first run this caught an engine bug: lions got 10, not their 5, because the loader read only the male <code>m/</code> section and the lion&#x27;s values are under <code>f/</code>. Fixed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>species_tour</code></td>\n<td>Each species drawn, with its <code>.ai</code> values and the offsets they&#x27;re mapped at, then the map&#x27;s heights.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-whats-in-the-knowledge-base\">What&#x27;s in the knowledge base</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>functions</td>\n<td><code>0x4378df</code>: <code>ZTAnimal::updateHappiness</code>, what it does, the engine code implementing it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>structs</td>\n<td><code>ZTAnimalType</code> + <code>0x20c</code> = <code>cHungerThreshold</code> (int): an animal species&#x27; data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>constants</td>\n<td><code>10560</code> = the message &quot;This object must be placed in a deeper tank.&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>notes</td>\n<td><code>0x5cda56</code>: &quot;the deeper-tank refusal&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>questions</td>\n<td>yours for your AI assistant, or leads it leaves for you, and their answers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>log</td>\n<td>2026-10-10 Batty251: Field ZTAnimal+0x2a8 = happiness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Everything in it says <strong>who</strong> added it (<code>by</code>) and <strong>how sure</strong> they were\n(<code>sure</code>, <code>likely</code>, <code>guess</code>).</p>\n<p>It started from the engine&#x27;s earlier work, credited as\n<code>zt1-engine (batty251, earlier sessions)</code>. That&#x27;s 74 functions whose behaviour\nis known and implemented, about 130 fields of the animal species data (from\nits .ai loader), the tank and scenery offsets, and the known message numbers.</p>\n<p>Those 74 functions have <strong>provisional</strong> names: we know what they do, but\nnot what the original programmers called them. A provisional name gives way\nto a real one, either yours (rename it) or OpenZT&#x27;s (import their names).</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-ghidra-scripts\">The Ghidra scripts</h2>\n<p>They&#x27;re under <strong>Tools &gt; zt1-engine</strong>, once you&#x27;ve put them there. This is a\none-time step in each Ghidra (each user profile):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open Window &gt; Script Manager.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>zt1-engine</strong> category on the left.</li>\n<li>Tick the <strong>In Tool</strong> box of each of the scripts.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Ghidra only gives a script its menu item and its key once that box is ticked.\nIf you change a script&#x27;s key, press the Script Manager&#x27;s Refresh button (the\ngreen arrows).</p>\n<p><strong>Ctrl+Alt+M</strong>, not Ctrl+Shift+M: another program on this PC has\nCtrl+Shift+M as a global hotkey (as Teams and Discord do, for mute), so\nGhidra never gets it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Script</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_ShowInLab</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Alt+L</code>)</td>\n<td>Sends the function under the cursor to the ZT1 Lab, starting the Lab and the bridge if needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_LabBridge</strong></td>\n<td>Starts (or, run again, stops) the bridge the Lab reads Ghidra through.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_Check</strong></td>\n<td>Opens ZT1 Check: struct fields against the loader code.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_Map</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Alt+M</code>)</td>\n<td>Records what&#x27;s under the cursor, in the listing or the decompiler, and puts it in Ghidra at once. See below.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_Apply</strong></td>\n<td>Puts the whole knowledge base into Ghidra: names, plate comments, structs (Data Type Manager &gt; zoo.exe &gt; zt1), notes, questions as bookmarks, and optionally each known number marked where it&#x27;s used. Run it after pulling the repo, or after your assistant answers you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_Sync</strong></td>\n<td>Brings in what you did straight in Ghidra instead of through Map: renames (<code>L</code>), plate comments, signatures, fields edited in the /zt1 structs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_Report</strong></td>\n<td>Writes <code>re/private/report.md</code> for your AI assistant (or anyone helping).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_EngineRefs</strong></td>\n<td>Bookmarks every address the engine cites (category zt1-engine) and tags those functions <code>zt1:implemented</code>. Run <code>python re/scan_engine.py</code> first.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ZT1_ImportNames</strong></td>\n<td>Names functions from someone else&#x27;s CSV, e.g. OpenZT&#x27;s, tagged <code>src:openzt</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-map-this-ctrlaltm\">Map this (Ctrl+Alt+M)</h3>\n<p>Put the cursor on something and press <code>Ctrl+Alt+M</code>, then pick one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Name this function</strong>: its name, its class (e.g. <code>ZTAnimal</code>), what it does,\n  and how sure you are. It becomes yours (<code>by:</code> you), even if it had a\n  provisional name.</li>\n<li><strong>A struct field</strong>: put the cursor on an offset (<code>param_1 + 0x2a8</code>, on the\n  <code>0x2a8</code>). Pick the struct, name the field and give its type. It goes into\n  the /zt1 struct in Ghidra at once.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then click the variable holding that kind of thing (<code>param_1</code>), press\n  <code>Ctrl+L</code> and type e.g. <code>ZTAnimal *</code>. The decompiler then shows\n  <code>param_1-&gt;happiness</code> in place of <code>*(int *)(param_1 + 0x2a8)</code>, in every\n  function you do that in. This is how the C gets readable.\n- <strong>What a number means</strong>: put the cursor on a number (<code>10560</code>) and say what it\n  is. <code>python re/msg.py 10560</code> gives a message&#x27;s text.\n- <strong>A note here</strong>: anything worth remembering about this spot.\n- <strong>Ask your AI assistant about this</strong>: your question, kept with where you asked it.\n- <strong>Answer a question or lead here</strong>: answer one of the leads left for you, or close\n  one of your own questions.\n- <strong>Show what&#x27;s known here</strong>: everything about this function.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-zt1-check-is-whats-mapped-right\">ZT1 Check: is what&#x27;s mapped right?</h2>\n<p>To open it, use Tools &gt; zt1-engine &gt; Open checker, or run\n<code>python re/check.py</code>. It has three tabs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loader check</strong> (no game needed). It checks every struct field named for a\n  config key (<code>cHungerThreshold</code>...) against zoo.exe&#x27;s own loading code. The\n  loader takes the field&#x27;s address (<code>lea ecx, [ebp + 0x20c]</code>) just before it\n  pushes the key&#x27;s name, so the code itself says where each value goes.</li>\n<li><strong>right</strong>: the mapped offset is where the code stores it.</li>\n<li><strong>WRONG</strong>: the code stores it somewhere else, and it shows where.</li>\n<li><strong>can&#x27;t tell</strong>: the value goes into a temporary first, so the code\n    doesn&#x27;t settle it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On 10 Oct 2026: 123 right, 0 wrong, 6 can&#x27;t tell.\n- <strong>Species data</strong>: a species&#x27; values, straight from its .ai (its default\n  subtype, with <code>f</code>/<code>y</code> over it), at each mapped offset.\n- <strong>Live</strong> reads the original game&#x27;s memory while it runs. You don&#x27;t need it:\n  the ZT1 Lab is the place to see what&#x27;s mapped working. It&#x27;s there if you\n  ever want to compare against the 2001 game itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-working-with-an-ai-assistant-or-anyone\">Working with an AI assistant (or anyone)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>When you&#x27;re stuck, use <strong>Map &gt; Ask your AI assistant about this</strong> on the spot.</li>\n<li>Run <strong>ZT1_Report</strong>. It writes <code>re/private/report.md</code>, with each open\n   question, its function&#x27;s decompiled C and what&#x27;s known about it, plus\n   everything added since the last report.</li>\n<li>Give your assistant the report, or just say &quot;read the report&quot; (it&#x27;s in\n   the repo folder). With the Lab&#x27;s MCP server it can read the original&#x27;s\n   code itself.</li>\n<li>It answers in the knowledge base (<code>kb.py answer</code>), may leave you leads\n   (<code>kb.py lead</code>), and may add what it works out, under your name.</li>\n<li>Run <strong>ZT1_Apply</strong>. Answers show as bookmarks (Window &gt; Bookmarks, category\n   zt1-answer), and leads as zt1-lead.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-the-loop-how-a-rule-gets-from-zooexe-into-the-engine\">The loop (how a rule gets from zoo.exe into the engine)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pick something</strong>: a lead (Bookmarks &gt; zt1-lead), something the engine\n   doesn&#x27;t do like the original yet, or <code>re/private/todo.txt</code> (named functions\n   no engine code cites yet, from ZT1_EngineRefs).</li>\n<li><strong>Find it.</strong> You can start from:\n   - a config key: Search &gt; Program Text &gt; <code>cKeeperFoodType</code>\n   - a message: <code>python re/msg.py deeper tank</code> gives 10560; then\n     Search &gt; For Scalars &gt; 10560\n   - a struct field: once a variable is typed (<code>ZTAnimalType *</code>), right-click\n     a field in the decompiler, e.g. <code>cHungerThreshold</code>, and choose Find Uses\n     of. That lists every function reading it, as long as the variables\n     there are typed too.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the decompiler.</strong> Name variables (<code>L</code>), retype them (<code>Ctrl+L</code>), and\n   map the offsets and numbers you work out with <code>Ctrl+Alt+M</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Name the function</strong> (Map &gt; Name this function).</li>\n<li><strong>Implement it</strong> in <code>src/</code> and cite it: <code>// Hunger (zoo.exe 0x438a79): ...</code>.\n   Any 6-digit address after <code>0x</code> in a <code>//</code> comment is picked up.</li>\n<li><strong>Test it.</strong> Add a check to the panel-shots suite in <code>src/main.cpp</code>, run\n   <code>zt1-engine-dev.exe --panel-shots E:/zt1-shots/shots</code>, then look at\n   <code>timings.txt</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Record it.</strong> Run <code>python re/scan_engine.py</code>, then in Ghidra run\n   <strong>ZT1_EngineRefs</strong>. If you renamed anything with <code>L</code>, also run\n   <strong>ZT1_Sync</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Commit</strong> <code>src/</code>, <code>re/knowledge.json</code> and <code>re/engine_refs.csv</code> together.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Save the Ghidra project as you go (<code>Ctrl+S</code>), and back up <code>E:\\zt1-ghidra</code>\nnow and then. The knowledge base is the part that matters, though: with it\nand <code>ZT1_Apply</code>, a fresh project gets everything back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-doing-it-alone-a-playbook\">Doing it alone (a playbook)</h2>\n<p>Everything below works without an AI assistant. Start in the Lab&#x27;s <strong>Explore</strong> tab:\nask where something is in plain words, and see which recorded actions our\nengine does, partly does, or doesn&#x27;t do yet (lab-app/README.md, Explore). Each step&#x27;s results go into\n<code>re/knowledge.json</code> under your name; run <strong>ZT1_Apply</strong> in Ghidra afterwards to\nsee them there.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>What the game reads.</strong> Original Game › <strong>Grow the database</strong> › Config\n   loaders. Open a loader with no struct name yet and read its keys. They say\n   what it fills (<code>cCleanTrashRadius</code>, <code>cFixFenceModifier</code> → maintenance\n   workers). Look at the offsets too: a type&#x27;s own fields start past its\n   base&#x27;s. ZTEntityType has <code>cIconZoom</code> at +0x50, ZTUnitType has <code>cSlowRate</code>\n   at +0x100, and an animal or guest type&#x27;s own keys start around +0x1b4.\n   Type a struct name and press <strong>Save</strong>. A loader that reads only globals\n   takes a plain name (<code>AnimalSettings</code>).\n   - A loader may read a key and then store it by hand\n     (<code>cSlowAnimSpeed</code> into a local, then copied). Its row shows &quot;a local&quot;:\n     read the raw code and add the field with <code>kb.py field</code>.\n   - A key whose reader isn&#x27;t listed won&#x27;t show at all. If a loader&#x27;s C calls\n     another function with (&quot;file&quot;, &quot;cKey&quot;, this + 0x..), add that function to\n     <code>re/lab/loaders.json</code>&#x27;s readers. That&#x27;s how the float reader 0x4b37c3\n     was found (cPctSick, the admission prices).</li>\n<li><strong>Values no key reads.</strong> In Find, search <code>field:0x22c</code>. Look for a write\n   of a plain number beside the loaders (<code>mov [ebp+0x22c], 0xffffff9c</code>). That&#x27;s\n   the type&#x27;s defaults, set before its file is read. Then see what reads the\n   field (animalUpdateHappiness clamps to it), and name it with <code>kb.py field</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Classes.</strong> Grow the database › Class tables. Name a table from what the\n   live census (Play › the bar&#x27;s readout) says its objects are, and press\n   <strong>Save</strong>. Put things in the zoo first (staff, buildings, an exhibit and\n   animals) so the census has them. Use <code>kbClassCompare</code> for tables no\n   object uses: a type&#x27;s config loader at slot 0x60 names it, and a base\n   class shares most of its slots with its whole family. When two tables\n   look the same (both have the staff loader), leave them unnamed rather\n   than guess. Slots shared with a named class come from their common base class.\n   A function&#x27;s slot often names it: slot 0x30 is &quot;apply pending happiness&quot;\n   for both animals and guests.</li>\n<li><strong>The game&#x27;s text.</strong> Grow the database › The game&#x27;s text › <strong>Save all</strong>.\n   Do it again whenever Ghidra has new functions.</li>\n<li><strong>Record.</strong> Play › Play original. Use the checklist below.</li>\n<li><strong>Close the gaps.</strong> <code>kbOrphans</code> lists the engine&#x27;s zoo.exe references\n   that have no Ghidra function. Open each one&#x27;s raw code first. If something\n   that ends code (<code>ret</code>, <code>jmp</code> away, padding) comes right before it, and it\n   starts like a function (<code>sub esp</code>, pushes, <code>mov eax, [esp+4]</code>), press\n   <strong>Make it a function</strong>. If it jumps back into code before it, it&#x27;s a piece\n   of a bigger function. If it disassembles into nonsense, it&#x27;s a table. Leave\n   both alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Questions.</strong> Run <code>python re/kb.py questions --all</code>. A lead &quot;for user&quot; is\n   yours to chase. Answer it with <code>kb.py answer</code>, saying how you found it.</li>\n<li><strong>OpenZT&#x27;s names</strong>, when crusavor sends them (below). The import path was\n   tested on a scratch copy of the project (2026-10-12). It named functions\n   that had no name and replaced provisional names, recording each in the\n   knowledge base as <code>name from openzt</code>. It kept a name already set, and listed\n   an address that isn&#x27;t code. A ZT1_Apply afterwards didn&#x27;t put the old\n   provisional name back.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After <strong>ZT1_Apply</strong> (or naming things) in your own Ghidra, press <strong>Refresh the\nLab&#x27;s Ghidra</strong> in Grow the database, so the Lab shows the same.</p>\n<p>To test a script without touching your project, copy <code>E:\\zt1-ghidra</code>\n(zt1.gpr and zt1.rep) and <code>re/</code> (ghidra_scripts, knowledge.json,\nmade_functions.json) into a scratch folder, and run it headless there.\nEach <code>ask</code> takes the next script argument:</p>\n<pre><code>analyzeHeadless.bat &lt;scratch&gt;\\project zt1 -process zoo.exe -noanalysis -scriptPath &lt;scratch&gt;\\repo\\re\\ghidra_scripts -postScript ZT1_Apply.py false -postScript ZT1_ImportNames.py &lt;csv&gt; openzt false\n</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"help-section-recording-checklist\">Recording checklist</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Play original and get to the screen you&#x27;ll record on: the main menu, or a\n   zoo. The game takes the Play view&#x27;s size, so click by what a <code>shot</code> shows.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Learn idle</strong> there, and leave the game alone for the 10 seconds.</li>\n<li>Record one action at a time, named as it&#x27;ll be read later. Say what\n   happened, including when the game refused: &quot;Placing a zebra outside an\n   exhibit (refused: Animals can only be placed in exhibits)&quot;.</li>\n<li>Before you save, check the game did what the label says (take a shot). If\n   it didn&#x27;t, press <strong>Close</strong>, and record it again under the right name.</li>\n<li><strong>Save to knowledge base</strong>, then tick <strong>Only what ran in no other\n   recording</strong>: that&#x27;s the action&#x27;s own code. Put a <code>kb.py note</code> on the best\n   lead.</li>\n<li>Record pairs that differ by one thing: opening a panel against using it,\n   placing that works against placing that&#x27;s refused, one animal against\n   another. Each pair narrows the other.</li>\n<li>Watch the date while you record. If a month ends during a recording,\n   wages and the month&#x27;s reports run too: discard it and record again.</li>\n<li>Already recorded, 57 actions (re/private/recordings/):\n   - <strong>Menus and files:</strong> main menu → Freeform, starting a zoo, saving\n     (60 functions of its own), loading a save (112), Options;\n   - <strong>Panels:</strong> Construction, Hire Staff, Adopt Animal, Research and\n     Conservation, Scenario information, the message list, the Animal and\n     Exhibit/Show lists, Zoo Status with all six of its tabs (raising the\n     admission price and marketing too), an animal&#x27;s info, an exhibit&#x27;s info\n     and donations tabs, Modify tank;\n   - <strong>Building:</strong> a path, a tree, a rock, painting terrain, raising land,\n     fences (inside the zoo, and refused outside the wall), a dinosaur\n     paddock, a tank (one wall, and closing it: the tank forms and fills, 142\n     of its own), a tank toy, the show grandstand, buildings, raising a tank&#x27;s\n     wall and lowering its base, deleting (bulldozing) an object;\n   - <strong>Animals and staff:</strong> a zebra into an exhibit and refused outside one,\n     a porpoise into a tank and refused on land, an allosaurus into a paddock\n     (it arrives as an egg), hiring a keeper, marine specialist and dinosaur\n     scientist, a DRT base;\n   - <strong>Running:</strong> the zoo running a minute, the adoption-ban popup&#x27;s OK, an\n     escaped dinosaur loose (it broke its paddock), the DRT base while it&#x27;s\n     loose, a month ending (exhibitBooksMonthly), pausing, and a show object\n     refused in a shallow tank (&quot;This object must be placed in a deeper\n     tank&quot;).</li>\n<li>Added since (82 actions in all):\n   - <strong>Shows (from Marine Mania Tutorial 2):</strong> a show animal into its exhibit\n     tank, building its <strong>show tank</strong> beside it (show tank walls: the gate\n     forms), grandstands facing it, opening the Show Script, adding tricks,\n     changing the show frequency, the orca ball in the show tank (it unlocks\n     Play with Ball), funding trick research;\n   - <strong>Combined exhibits (Tutorial 3):</strong> closing a land exhibit beside a tank;\n   - <strong>Guests:</strong> the Guest List, a guest&#x27;s info, thoughts and favourites\n     tabs, guests buying from vending machines (22 of its own), raising a\n     building&#x27;s price (priceAdjustB);\n   - <strong>Running:</strong> two and a half minutes with idle learnt while running\n     (keeperPickFoodSpot), draining a tank;\n   - <strong>Scenarios:</strong> the scenario list, starting a scenario (123 of its own),\n     a tutorial popup&#x27;s OK, a goal completing, going on to the next tutorial,\n     undo (undoLastAction).</li>\n<li>Rules learnt the hard way:<ul>\n<li><strong>Show objects only go in a show tank</strong>, built with the show tank walls\n  (blue, starred) beside the animal&#x27;s own exhibit tank, at the same height.\n  In a plain tank they&#x27;re refused as &quot;must be placed in a deeper tank&quot;,\n  however deep it is.</li>\n<li><strong>A combined land and tank exhibit forms when the tank wall&#x27;s top is\n  level with the land.</strong> The shared wall turns grey-and-white striped.</li>\n<li>A tutorial blocks clicks on anything but its current step.</li>\n<li><strong>The show tank comes first.</strong> Built alone it&#x27;s named &quot;Show Tank&quot;; an\n  exhibit tank then built against it gets the gate between them. Built\n  the other way round, the second one is just another tank.</li>\n<li><strong>A show needs a marine specialist assigned</strong> (Staff Information&#x27;s\n  assign button, then click the animal&#x27;s tank): the show tank&#x27;s info says\n  &quot;Staff Assigned: No&quot; until then. Marine specialists can only be\n  assigned to tanks with marine animals.</li>\n<li><strong>Trick depth:</strong> each trick has a minimumdepth (orca.ai: Play with Ball\n  8, Wave to Crowd 2). That&#x27;s where &quot;This object must be placed in a\n  deeper tank&quot; comes from (loadTricks, 0x4b9690).</li>\n<li>An escaped animal can&#x27;t be sold (its Sell button greys out).</li>\n<li>Shows run every shows.cfg interval: continuous 65, frequent 90,\n  infrequent 120.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Also recorded: building a show tank alone and an exhibit tank against\n    it, selling an animal (&quot;Refund: $320&quot;, 40%), assigning a marine\n    specialist to a tank, and a trainer waiting at a show platform for an\n    unhappy lone orca (no show came).</li>\n<li>Next to record: a show being performed. It needs a happy show animal:\n    an orca wants 2-3 of its kind, and that zoo (Saved Games\\lab-show-zoo)\n    is under the adoption ban. Start a zoo, adopt two orcas at once, assign\n    the specialist, and record. Also dung, rubble (a rampage), and a\n    scenario won or lost.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-whose-work-is-whose\">Whose work is whose</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Everything you add is under your name: Ghidra asks for it once and keeps\n  it in <code>re/private/me.txt</code>. The log says when.</li>\n<li>The starting set says <code>zt1-engine (batty251, earlier sessions)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>OpenZT&#x27;s names</strong> come in tagged <code>src:openzt</code>. When one replaces a\n  provisional name, the knowledge base keeps the behaviour (ours) and notes\n  the name is theirs (<code>name from openzt</code>). Their full list only goes to\n  <code>re/private/</code>. Don&#x27;t publish it in this repo unless crusavor says that&#x27;s\n  fine, and credit OpenZT when it helps.</li>\n<li>Report&#x27;s &quot;Who&#x27;s done what&quot; section counts each person&#x27;s functions, fields\n  and numbers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-getting-openzts-names\">Getting OpenZT&#x27;s names</h2>\n<p>You can get them two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Through crusavor&#x27;s Ghidra server</strong> (they offered an account). Choose\n  File &gt; New Project &gt; Shared, enter their server, and open their zoo.exe.\n  Check it&#x27;s the same build: the scripts warn you if it isn&#x27;t. Run\n  <strong>ZT1_Sync</strong> there and say <strong>yes</strong> to &quot;Everything too?&quot;. That writes\n  <code>re/private/all_names.csv</code>. Back in your own project, run\n  <strong>ZT1_ImportNames</strong> on that file and label it <code>openzt</code>. Leave their project\n  as it is unless they ask you to add to it.</li>\n<li><strong>From a CSV they send you</strong> (their <code>function_exporter.py</code> writes\n  <code>Class, Name, Address, ...</code>). Put it in <code>re/private/</code>, then run\n  <strong>ZT1_ImportNames</strong> on it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Either way, ImportNames never overwrites a name you set yourself unless you\nsay so. It only replaces provisional names.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-without-ghidra-open\">Without Ghidra open</h2>\n<pre><code>python re/kb.py stats\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>kb.py</code> commands:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py stats</code></td>\n<td>Counts of everything in the knowledge base</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py show 0x4378df</code></td>\n<td>Everything known about a function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py show ZTAnimalType</code></td>\n<td>A struct&#x27;s fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py show 10560</code></td>\n<td>What a number means</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py show hunger</code></td>\n<td>Searches names and fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py questions</code></td>\n<td>Open questions and leads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py field ZTAnimal 0x2b0 energy int &quot;Its energy&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Adds a field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/kb.py log</code></td>\n<td>Recent additions, with who and when</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>python re/kb.py</code> with no arguments lists every command.</p>\n<p>The other tools:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/rd.py 4df688</code></td>\n<td>One function&#x27;s disassembly, calls labelled with known names</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python re/msg.py deeper tank</code></td>\n<td>A message&#x27;s id from its words, or the text of an id</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Ghidra can also run the scripts with no window (headless). A script&#x27;s\nquestions are answered by the arguments after its name, in order, and\nyes/no questions take <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Close the project in the Ghidra\nwindow first. For example:</p>\n<pre><code>E:/ghidra/ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC/support/analyzeHeadless.bat E:/zt1-ghidra zt1 -process zoo.exe -noanalysis -postScript ZT1_Apply.py false\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-one-time-setup\">One-time setup</h2>\n<p>On this PC, all of this was done on 10 Oct 2026:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ghidra 12.1.4 is in <code>E:\\ghidra\\ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC</code>. Start it with\n  <code>ghidraRun.bat</code> there.</li>\n<li>The project is <code>E:\\zt1-ghidra\\zt1.gpr</code>, with zoo.exe analysed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These steps are for a new PC:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ghidra.</strong> Download the latest release from\n   https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/releases and unzip it on\n   E: (C: is nearly full). It needs a 64-bit JDK 21 or newer; Temurin 25\n   works. Ghidra 12 no longer has Jython built in, and the scripts need it.\n   To install it, unzip <code>Extensions\\Ghidra\\ghidra_*_Jython.zip</code> into\n   <code>Ghidra\\Extensions\\</code> (make that folder).</li>\n<li><strong>Project.</strong> File &gt; New Project &gt; Non-Shared, in <code>E:\\zt1-ghidra</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Import zoo.exe.</strong> Use the 2002 Complete Collection build\n   (2,449,432 bytes). The scripts check its SHA-256, because other builds&#x27;\n   addresses don&#x27;t match. Say yes to Analyze, and keep the defaults.</li>\n<li><strong>Scripts.</strong> Ghidra reads scripts from <code>%USERPROFILE%\\ghidra_scripts</code>. On\n   this PC that&#x27;s a link to <code>&lt;repo&gt;\\re\\ghidra_scripts</code>, made with\n   <code>mklink /J %USERPROFILE%\\ghidra_scripts &lt;repo&gt;\\re\\ghidra_scripts</code>.\n   You can instead add the folder in Script Manager &gt; Manage Script\n   Directories.</li>\n<li><strong>Put the scripts in the menu.</strong> In Window &gt; Script Manager &gt; zt1-engine,\n   tick <strong>In Tool</strong> for each.</li>\n<li><strong>Fill it in.</strong> Run <code>python re/scan_engine.py</code>, then in Ghidra run\n   <strong>ZT1_EngineRefs</strong> and <strong>ZT1_Apply</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Python tools.</strong> Run <code>pip install pefile capstone</code>. Copy\n   <code>E:\\zt1-shots\\tools\\strings.pkl</code> into <code>re\\private\\</code>, then run\n   <code>python re/msg.py 1</code> once, so Map can fill in a message&#x27;s text.</li>\n</ol>","toc":[{"id":"zt1-lab","title":"ZT1 Lab","level":2},{"id":"whats-in-the-knowledge-base","title":"What's in the knowledge base","level":2},{"id":"the-ghidra-scripts","title":"The Ghidra scripts","level":2},{"id":"map-this-ctrlaltm","title":"Map this (Ctrl+Alt+M)","level":3},{"id":"zt1-check-is-whats-mapped-right","title":"ZT1 Check: is what's mapped right?","level":2},{"id":"working-with-an-ai-assistant-or-anyone","title":"Working with an AI assistant (or anyone)","level":2},{"id":"the-loop-how-a-rule-gets-from-zooexe-into-the-engine","title":"The loop (how a rule gets from zoo.exe into the engine)","level":2},{"id":"doing-it-alone-a-playbook","title":"Doing it alone (a playbook)","level":2},{"id":"recording-checklist","title":"Recording checklist","level":3},{"id":"whose-work-is-whose","title":"Whose work is whose","level":2},{"id":"getting-openzts-names","title":"Getting OpenZT's names","level":2},{"id":"without-ghidra-open","title":"Without Ghidra open","level":2},{"id":"one-time-setup","title":"One-time setup","level":2}],"text":"# Reverse engineering zoo.exe\n\nThe original game is the spec. Every rule the engine copies is read out of\nzoo.exe, and its source says where it came from:\n\n```cpp\n// Hunger (zoo.exe 0x438a79): ...\n```\n\nThis folder is where what we know about zoo.exe is kept: **the knowledge\nbase**, `knowledge.json`. Ghidra scripts read it, add to it, and send\nquestions on, so the work can be done, checked and kept by anyone, with or\nwithout an AI helping.\n\n| File | What it is | Committed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lab/` | **ZT1 Lab**: the live view on our engine, its scripts and their interface (below) | yes |\n| `knowledge.json` | **The knowledge base**: functions, struct fields, numbers, notes, questions and answers, and a log of who added what | yes |\n| `engine_refs.csv` | Every zoo.exe address the engine's source cites (made by `scan_engine.py`) | yes |\n| `ghidra_scripts/` | The Ghidra side (below) | yes |\n| `kb.py` | The knowledge base from the command line | yes |\n| `scan_engine.py` | Rebuilds `engine_refs.csv` from `src/` | yes |\n| `check.py` | ZT1 Check: struct fields checked against zoo.exe's loader code, without the game (below) | yes |\n| `rd.py` | Disassembles one function, its calls labelled with known names | yes |\n| `msg.py` | The game's text: a message's id from its words, or the reverse | yes |\n| `private/` | Other people's names, game text, your report, your name. **Never committed** | no |\n\n## ZT1 Lab\n\nZT1 Lab is an editor in the style of Unity, built on our own engine, with\nGhidra as its window into zoo.exe. Start it with `zt1-engine.exe --lab`\n(from `build\\Release`), or from Ghidra with **Ctrl+Alt+L** (Show in Lab).\n\n| Part of the window | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| **Left: Scripts** | Your scripts, in `re/lab/scripts/`. Double-click one to build and run it. |\n| **Left: Binary** | What's in the knowledge base (functions, structs, numbers), and every one of zoo.exe's 7,304 functions straight from Ghidra. Pick one to inspect it. |\n| **Middle: the live view** | `scene`: the lab zoo, our engine's real World with a fenced exhibit and its animals (drag to pan, wheel to zoom). `heightmap`: the map's heights from above. `animal` / `sprite`: a species or an animation, drawn and playing. |\n| **Right: Inspector** | The picked function or struct. What your script watches (each value labelled with the offset it maps to), its checks in green or red, its graphs, and the lab animals' state in the engine. |\n| **Bottom tabs** | **Log** (your script's and the build's). **Decompiled** and **Assembly**, both from Ghidra; the decompiled view lists the mapped fields and known numbers it uses beside it. **Calls & uses**: what calls it and what it calls (click to go there), and the strings and numbers it uses. **Engine code**: our source that implements it, with Open in code. |\n| **Toolbar** | **Build & Run**, **Step**, **Auto**/**Pause**, Stop, steps per second, Play world (the engine running freely), the view, and whether Ghidra is connected. |\n\n**Ghidra as the backend.** With zoo.exe open in Ghidra, run Tools >\nzt1-engine > **Start Lab bridge** (Show in Lab starts it too). The Lab then\nasks Ghidra for code, calls and references on 127.0.0.1:18489, which is this\nPC only. Everything it fetches is saved in `re/private/ghidra/`, so code\nyou've looked at still shows when Ghidra is closed. **Show in Ghidra** moves\nGhidra's cursor to what you're looking at in the Lab.\n\n**Scripts.** A script is one small C++ file. **Build & Run** compiles it into\na DLL with Visual Studio. The first build of a session sets up the compiler\n(about 30 s); after that a build takes about 1 s. The Lab then runs it:\n`lab_start` once, then `lab_step` each step until it returns false. A crash in\na script stops the script, not the Lab. What a script can do is all in\n[lab/lab_api.h](lab/lab_api.h):\n- build an exhibit, put animals and objects in it\n- run the engine for so many seconds\n- read and set an animal's state\n- read a species' `.ai` values, zoo.exe's own values and the game's text\n- look up a mapped offset\n- log, watch, plot, and `expect` (a check)\n\nHere is the happiness script, cut down:\n\n```cpp\n#include \"lab_api.h\"\n// zoo.exe 0x4378df          <- what it's about: Show in Lab and the Binary list find it\nLAB_EXPORT void lab_start(Lab *lab) {\n  lab->exhibit(5, 5, \"chainlnk\");\n  zebra = lab->animal(\"zebra\", false);\n}\nLAB_EXPORT bool lab_step(Lab *lab, int step) {\n  double before = lab->get(zebra, \"happiness\"), pending = lab->get(zebra, \"pending\");\n  lab->runEngine(1.0f);                                    // one tick of the engine\n  double mine = clamp(before + pending, -100, 100);         // my reading of 0x4378df\n  lab->expect(\"engine == my reading\", lab->get(zebra, \"happiness\") == mine);\n  lab->plot(\"happiness\", lab->get(zebra, \"happiness\"));\n  return step < 90;\n}\n```\n\nTo start one for any function, pick it in Binary and press **New script for\nit**. That writes a skeleton and opens it in your editor.\n\nThe three that come with it:\n\n| Script | What it checks |\n|---|---|\n| `happiness` | `0x4378df`: happiness += pending, kept to -100..100, every tick. It passes on all 90 ticks; a lone zebra drops from 75 to 20. |\n| `hunger` | `0x438a79`: hunger rises by the species' `cHungerIncrement` every 30 ticks. On its first run this caught an engine bug: lions got 10, not their 5, because the loader read only the male `m/` section and the lion's values are under `f/`. Fixed. |\n| `species_tour` | Each species drawn, with its `.ai` values and the offsets they're mapped at, then the map's heights. |\n\n## What's in the knowledge base\n\n| Section | Example |\n|---|---|\n| functions | `0x4378df`: `ZTAnimal::updateHappiness`, what it does, the engine code implementing it |\n| structs | `ZTAnimalType` + `0x20c` = `cHungerThreshold` (int): an animal species' data |\n| constants | `10560` = the message \"This object must be placed in a deeper tank.\" |\n| notes | `0x5cda56`: \"the deeper-tank refusal\" |\n| questions | yours for your AI assistant, or leads it leaves for you, and their answers |\n| log | 2026-10-10 Batty251: Field ZTAnimal+0x2a8 = happiness |\n\nEverything in it says **who** added it (`by`) and **how sure** they were\n(`sure`, `likely`, `guess`).\n\nIt started from the engine's earlier work, credited as\n`zt1-engine (batty251, earlier sessions)`. That's 74 functions whose behaviour\nis known and implemented, about 130 fields of the animal species data (from\nits .ai loader), the tank and scenery offsets, and the known message numbers.\n\nThose 74 functions have **provisional** names: we know what they do, but\nnot what the original programmers called them. A provisional name gives way\nto a real one, either yours (rename it) or OpenZT's (import their names).\n\n## The Ghidra scripts\n\nThey're under **Tools > zt1-engine**, once you've put them there. This is a\none-time step in each Ghidra (each user profile):\n\n1. Open Window > Script Manager.\n2. Click the **zt1-engine** category on the left.\n3. Tick the **In Tool** box of each of the scripts.\n\nGhidra only gives a script its menu item and its key once that box is ticked.\nIf you change a script's key, press the Script Manager's Refresh button (the\ngreen arrows).\n\n**Ctrl+Alt+M**, not Ctrl+Shift+M: another program on this PC has\nCtrl+Shift+M as a global hotkey (as Teams and Discord do, for mute), so\nGhidra never gets it.\n\n| Script | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **ZT1_ShowInLab** (`Ctrl+Alt+L`) | Sends the function under the cursor to the ZT1 Lab, starting the Lab and the bridge if needed. |\n| **ZT1_LabBridge** | Starts (or, run again, stops) the bridge the Lab reads Ghidra through. |\n| **ZT1_Check** | Opens ZT1 Check: struct fields against the loader code. |\n| **ZT1_Map** (`Ctrl+Alt+M`) | Records what's under the cursor, in the listing or the decompiler, and puts it in Ghidra at once. See below. |\n| **ZT1_Apply** | Puts the whole knowledge base into Ghidra: names, plate comments, structs (Data Type Manager > zoo.exe > zt1), notes, questions as bookmarks, and optionally each known number marked where it's used. Run it after pulling the repo, or after your assistant answers you. |\n| **ZT1_Sync** | Brings in what you did straight in Ghidra instead of through Map: renames (`L`), plate comments, signatures, fields edited in the /zt1 structs. |\n| **ZT1_Report** | Writes `re/private/report.md` for your AI assistant (or anyone helping). |\n| **ZT1_EngineRefs** | Bookmarks every address the engine cites (category zt1-engine) and tags those functions `zt1:implemented`. Run `python re/scan_engine.py` first. |\n| **ZT1_ImportNames** | Names functions from someone else's CSV, e.g. OpenZT's, tagged `src:openzt`. |\n\n### Map this (Ctrl+Alt+M)\n\nPut the cursor on something and press `Ctrl+Alt+M`, then pick one:\n\n- **Name this function**: its name, its class (e.g. `ZTAnimal`), what it does,\n  and how sure you are. It becomes yours (`by:` you), even if it had a\n  provisional name.\n- **A struct field**: put the cursor on an offset (`param_1 + 0x2a8`, on the\n  `0x2a8`). Pick the struct, name the field and give its type. It goes into\n  the /zt1 struct in Ghidra at once.\n\n  Then click the variable holding that kind of thing (`param_1`), press\n  `Ctrl+L` and type e.g. `ZTAnimal *`. The decompiler then shows\n  `param_1->happiness` in place of `*(int *)(param_1 + 0x2a8)`, in every\n  function you do that in. This is how the C gets readable.\n- **What a number means**: put the cursor on a number (`10560`) and say what it\n  is. `python re/msg.py 10560` gives a message's text.\n- **A note here**: anything worth remembering about this spot.\n- **Ask your AI assistant about this**: your question, kept with where you asked it.\n- **Answer a question or lead here**: answer one of the leads left for you, or close\n  one of your own questions.\n- **Show what's known here**: everything about this function.\n\n## ZT1 Check: is what's mapped right?\n\nTo open it, use Tools > zt1-engine > Open checker, or run\n`python re/check.py`. It has three tabs:\n\n- **Loader check** (no game needed). It checks every struct field named for a\n  config key (`cHungerThreshold`...) against zoo.exe's own loading code. The\n  loader takes the field's address (`lea ecx, [ebp + 0x20c]`) just before it\n  pushes the key's name, so the code itself says where each value goes.\n  - **right**: the mapped offset is where the code stores it.\n  - **WRONG**: the code stores it somewhere else, and it shows where.\n  - **can't tell**: the value goes into a temporary first, so the code\n    doesn't settle it.\n\n  On 10 Oct 2026: 123 right, 0 wrong, 6 can't tell.\n- **Species data**: a species' values, straight from its .ai (its default\n  subtype, with `f`/`y` over it), at each mapped offset.\n- **Live** reads the original game's memory while it runs. You don't need it:\n  the ZT1 Lab is the place to see what's mapped working. It's there if you\n  ever want to compare against the 2001 game itself.\n\n## Working with an AI assistant (or anyone)\n\n1. When you're stuck, use **Map > Ask your AI assistant about this** on the spot.\n2. Run **ZT1_Report**. It writes `re/private/report.md`, with each open\n   question, its function's decompiled C and what's known about it, plus\n   everything added since the last report.\n3. Give your assistant the report, or just say \"read the report\" (it's in\n   the repo folder). With the Lab's MCP server it can read the original's\n   code itself.\n4. It answers in the knowledge base (`kb.py answer`), may leave you leads\n   (`kb.py lead`), and may add what it works out, under your name.\n5. Run **ZT1_Apply**. Answers show as bookmarks (Window > Bookmarks, category\n   zt1-answer), and leads as zt1-lead.\n\n## The loop (how a rule gets from zoo.exe into the engine)\n\n1. **Pick something**: a lead (Bookmarks > zt1-lead), something the engine\n   doesn't do like the original yet, or `re/private/todo.txt` (named functions\n   no engine code cites yet, from ZT1_EngineRefs).\n2. **Find it.** You can start from:\n   - a config key: Search > Program Text > `cKeeperFoodType`\n   - a message: `python re/msg.py deeper tank` gives 10560; then\n     Search > For Scalars > 10560\n   - a struct field: once a variable is typed (`ZTAnimalType *`), right-click\n     a field in the decompiler, e.g. `cHungerThreshold`, and choose Find Uses\n     of. That lists every function reading it, as long as the variables\n     there are typed too.\n3. **Read the decompiler.** Name variables (`L`), retype them (`Ctrl+L`), and\n   map the offsets and numbers you work out with `Ctrl+Alt+M`.\n4. **Name the function** (Map > Name this function).\n5. **Implement it** in `src/` and cite it: `// Hunger (zoo.exe 0x438a79): ...`.\n   Any 6-digit address after `0x` in a `//` comment is picked up.\n6. **Test it.** Add a check to the panel-shots suite in `src/main.cpp`, run\n   `zt1-engine-dev.exe --panel-shots E:/zt1-shots/shots`, then look at\n   `timings.txt`.\n7. **Record it.** Run `python re/scan_engine.py`, then in Ghidra run\n   **ZT1_EngineRefs**. If you renamed anything with `L`, also run\n   **ZT1_Sync**.\n8. **Commit** `src/`, `re/knowledge.json` and `re/engine_refs.csv` together.\n\nSave the Ghidra project as you go (`Ctrl+S`), and back up `E:\\zt1-ghidra`\nnow and then. The knowledge base is the part that matters, though: with it\nand `ZT1_Apply`, a fresh project gets everything back.\n\n## Doing it alone (a playbook)\n\nEverything below works without an AI assistant. Start in the Lab's **Explore** tab:\nask where something is in plain words, and see which recorded actions our\nengine does, partly does, or doesn't do yet (lab-app/README.md, Explore). Each step's results go into\n`re/knowledge.json` under your name; run **ZT1_Apply** in Ghidra afterwards to\nsee them there.\n\n1. **What the game reads.** Original Game › **Grow the database** › Config\n   loaders. Open a loader with no struct name yet and read its keys. They say\n   what it fills (`cCleanTrashRadius`, `cFixFenceModifier` → maintenance\n   workers). Look at the offsets too: a type's own fields start past its\n   base's. ZTEntityType has `cIconZoom` at +0x50, ZTUnitType has `cSlowRate`\n   at +0x100, and an animal or guest type's own keys start around +0x1b4.\n   Type a struct name and press **Save**. A loader that reads only globals\n   takes a plain name (`AnimalSettings`).\n   - A loader may read a key and then store it by hand\n     (`cSlowAnimSpeed` into a local, then copied). Its row shows \"a local\":\n     read the raw code and add the field with `kb.py field`.\n   - A key whose reader isn't listed won't show at all. If a loader's C calls\n     another function with (\"file\", \"cKey\", this + 0x..), add that function to\n     `re/lab/loaders.json`'s readers. That's how the float reader 0x4b37c3\n     was found (cPctSick, the admission prices).\n2. **Values no key reads.** In Find, search `field:0x22c`. Look for a write\n   of a plain number beside the loaders (`mov [ebp+0x22c], 0xffffff9c`). That's\n   the type's defaults, set before its file is read. Then see what reads the\n   field (animalUpdateHappiness clamps to it), and name it with `kb.py field`.\n3. **Classes.** Grow the database › Class tables. Name a table from what the\n   live census (Play › the bar's readout) says its objects are, and press\n   **Save**. Put things in the zoo first (staff, buildings, an exhibit and\n   animals) so the census has them. Use `kbClassCompare` for tables no\n   object uses: a type's config loader at slot 0x60 names it, and a base\n   class shares most of its slots with its whole family. When two tables\n   look the same (both have the staff loader), leave them unnamed rather\n   than guess. Slots shared with a named class come from their common base class.\n   A function's slot often names it: slot 0x30 is \"apply pending happiness\"\n   for both animals and guests.\n4. **The game's text.** Grow the database › The game's text › **Save all**.\n   Do it again whenever Ghidra has new functions.\n5. **Record.** Play › Play original. Use the checklist below.\n6. **Close the gaps.** `kbOrphans` lists the engine's zoo.exe references\n   that have no Ghidra function. Open each one's raw code first. If something\n   that ends code (`ret`, `jmp` away, padding) comes right before it, and it\n   starts like a function (`sub esp`, pushes, `mov eax, [esp+4]`), press\n   **Make it a function**. If it jumps back into code before it, it's a piece\n   of a bigger function. If it disassembles into nonsense, it's a table. Leave\n   both alone.\n7. **Questions.** Run `python re/kb.py questions --all`. A lead \"for user\" is\n   yours to chase. Answer it with `kb.py answer`, saying how you found it.\n8. **OpenZT's names**, when crusavor sends them (below). The import path was\n   tested on a scratch copy of the project (2026-10-12). It named functions\n   that had no name and replaced provisional names, recording each in the\n   knowledge base as `name from openzt`. It kept a name already set, and listed\n   an address that isn't code. A ZT1_Apply afterwards didn't put the old\n   provisional name back.\n\nAfter **ZT1_Apply** (or naming things) in your own Ghidra, press **Refresh the\nLab's Ghidra** in Grow the database, so the Lab shows the same.\n\nTo test a script without touching your project, copy `E:\\zt1-ghidra`\n(zt1.gpr and zt1.rep) and `re/` (ghidra_scripts, knowledge.json,\nmade_functions.json) into a scratch folder, and run it headless there.\nEach `ask` takes the next script argument:\n\n```bash\nanalyzeHeadless.bat <scratch>\\project zt1 -process zoo.exe -noanalysis -scriptPath <scratch>\\repo\\re\\ghidra_scripts -postScript ZT1_Apply.py false -postScript ZT1_ImportNames.py <csv> openzt false\n```\n\n### Recording checklist\n\n1. Play original and get to the screen you'll record on: the main menu, or a\n   zoo. The game takes the Play view's size, so click by what a `shot` shows.\n2. Press **Learn idle** there, and leave the game alone for the 10 seconds.\n3. Record one action at a time, named as it'll be read later. Say what\n   happened, including when the game refused: \"Placing a zebra outside an\n   exhibit (refused: Animals can only be placed in exhibits)\".\n4. Before you save, check the game did what the label says (take a shot). If\n   it didn't, press **Close**, and record it again under the right name.\n5. **Save to knowledge base**, then tick **Only what ran in no other\n   recording**: that's the action's own code. Put a `kb.py note` on the best\n   lead.\n6. Record pairs that differ by one thing: opening a panel against using it,\n   placing that works against placing that's refused, one animal against\n   another. Each pair narrows the other.\n7. Watch the date while you record. If a month ends during a recording,\n   wages and the month's reports run too: discard it and record again.\n8. Already recorded, 57 actions (re/private/recordings/):\n   - **Menus and files:** main menu → Freeform, starting a zoo, saving\n     (60 functions of its own), loading a save (112), Options;\n   - **Panels:** Construction, Hire Staff, Adopt Animal, Research and\n     Conservation, Scenario information, the message list, the Animal and\n     Exhibit/Show lists, Zoo Status with all six of its tabs (raising the\n     admission price and marketing too), an animal's info, an exhibit's info\n     and donations tabs, Modify tank;\n   - **Building:** a path, a tree, a rock, painting terrain, raising land,\n     fences (inside the zoo, and refused outside the wall), a dinosaur\n     paddock, a tank (one wall, and closing it: the tank forms and fills, 142\n     of its own), a tank toy, the show grandstand, buildings, raising a tank's\n     wall and lowering its base, deleting (bulldozing) an object;\n   - **Animals and staff:** a zebra into an exhibit and refused outside one,\n     a porpoise into a tank and refused on land, an allosaurus into a paddock\n     (it arrives as an egg), hiring a keeper, marine specialist and dinosaur\n     scientist, a DRT base;\n   - **Running:** the zoo running a minute, the adoption-ban popup's OK, an\n     escaped dinosaur loose (it broke its paddock), the DRT base while it's\n     loose, a month ending (exhibitBooksMonthly), pausing, and a show object\n     refused in a shallow tank (\"This object must be placed in a deeper\n     tank\").\n9. Added since (82 actions in all):\n   - **Shows (from Marine Mania Tutorial 2):** a show animal into its exhibit\n     tank, building its **show tank** beside it (show tank walls: the gate\n     forms), grandstands facing it, opening the Show Script, adding tricks,\n     changing the show frequency, the orca ball in the show tank (it unlocks\n     Play with Ball), funding trick research;\n   - **Combined exhibits (Tutorial 3):** closing a land exhibit beside a tank;\n   - **Guests:** the Guest List, a guest's info, thoughts and favourites\n     tabs, guests buying from vending machines (22 of its own), raising a\n     building's price (priceAdjustB);\n   - **Running:** two and a half minutes with idle learnt while running\n     (keeperPickFoodSpot), draining a tank;\n   - **Scenarios:** the scenario list, starting a scenario (123 of its own),\n     a tutorial popup's OK, a goal completing, going on to the next tutorial,\n     undo (undoLastAction).\n10. Rules learnt the hard way:\n    - **Show objects only go in a show tank**, built with the show tank walls\n      (blue, starred) beside the animal's own exhibit tank, at the same height.\n      In a plain tank they're refused as \"must be placed in a deeper tank\",\n      however deep it is.\n    - **A combined land and tank exhibit forms when the tank wall's top is\n      level with the land.** The shared wall turns grey-and-white striped.\n    - A tutorial blocks clicks on anything but its current step.\n    - **The show tank comes first.** Built alone it's named \"Show Tank\"; an\n      exhibit tank then built against it gets the gate between them. Built\n      the other way round, the second one is just another tank.\n    - **A show needs a marine specialist assigned** (Staff Information's\n      assign button, then click the animal's tank): the show tank's info says\n      \"Staff Assigned: No\" until then. Marine specialists can only be\n      assigned to tanks with marine animals.\n    - **Trick depth:** each trick has a minimumdepth (orca.ai: Play with Ball\n      8, Wave to Crowd 2). That's where \"This object must be placed in a\n      deeper tank\" comes from (loadTricks, 0x4b9690).\n    - An escaped animal can't be sold (its Sell button greys out).\n    - Shows run every shows.cfg interval: continuous 65, frequent 90,\n      infrequent 120.\n11. Also recorded: building a show tank alone and an exhibit tank against\n    it, selling an animal (\"Refund: $320\", 40%), assigning a marine\n    specialist to a tank, and a trainer waiting at a show platform for an\n    unhappy lone orca (no show came).\n12. Next to record: a show being performed. It needs a happy show animal:\n    an orca wants 2-3 of its kind, and that zoo (Saved Games\\lab-show-zoo)\n    is under the adoption ban. Start a zoo, adopt two orcas at once, assign\n    the specialist, and record. Also dung, rubble (a rampage), and a\n    scenario won or lost.\n\n## Whose work is whose\n\n- Everything you add is under your name: Ghidra asks for it once and keeps\n  it in `re/private/me.txt`. The log says when.\n- The starting set says `zt1-engine (batty251, earlier sessions)`.\n- **OpenZT's names** come in tagged `src:openzt`. When one replaces a\n  provisional name, the knowledge base keeps the behaviour (ours) and notes\n  the name is theirs (`name from openzt`). Their full list only goes to\n  `re/private/`. Don't publish it in this repo unless crusavor says that's\n  fine, and credit OpenZT when it helps.\n- Report's \"Who's done what\" section counts each person's functions, fields\n  and numbers.\n\n## Getting OpenZT's names\n\nYou can get them two ways:\n\n- **Through crusavor's Ghidra server** (they offered an account). Choose\n  File > New Project > Shared, enter their server, and open their zoo.exe.\n  Check it's the same build: the scripts warn you if it isn't. Run\n  **ZT1_Sync** there and say **yes** to \"Everything too?\". That writes\n  `re/private/all_names.csv`. Back in your own project, run\n  **ZT1_ImportNames** on that file and label it `openzt`. Leave their project\n  as it is unless they ask you to add to it.\n- **From a CSV they send you** (their `function_exporter.py` writes\n  `Class, Name, Address, ...`). Put it in `re/private/`, then run\n  **ZT1_ImportNames** on it.\n\nEither way, ImportNames never overwrites a name you set yourself unless you\nsay so. It only replaces provisional names.\n\n## Without Ghidra open\n\n```bash\npython re/kb.py stats\n```\n\nThe `kb.py` commands:\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `python re/kb.py stats` | Counts of everything in the knowledge base |\n| `python re/kb.py show 0x4378df` | Everything known about a function |\n| `python re/kb.py show ZTAnimalType` | A struct's fields |\n| `python re/kb.py show 10560` | What a number means |\n| `python re/kb.py show hunger` | Searches names and fields |\n| `python re/kb.py questions` | Open questions and leads |\n| `python re/kb.py field ZTAnimal 0x2b0 energy int \"Its energy\"` | Adds a field |\n| `python re/kb.py log` | Recent additions, with who and when |\n\n`python re/kb.py` with no arguments lists every command.\n\nThe other tools:\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `python re/rd.py 4df688` | One function's disassembly, calls labelled with known names |\n| `python re/msg.py deeper tank` | A message's id from its words, or the text of an id |\n\nGhidra can also run the scripts with no window (headless). A script's\nquestions are answered by the arguments after its name, in order, and\nyes/no questions take `true` or `false`. Close the project in the Ghidra\nwindow first. For example:\n\n```bash\nE:/ghidra/ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC/support/analyzeHeadless.bat E:/zt1-ghidra zt1 -process zoo.exe -noanalysis -postScript ZT1_Apply.py false\n```\n\n## One-time setup\n\nOn this PC, all of this was done on 10 Oct 2026:\n\n- Ghidra 12.1.4 is in `E:\\ghidra\\ghidra_12.1.4_PUBLIC`. Start it with\n  `ghidraRun.bat` there.\n- The project is `E:\\zt1-ghidra\\zt1.gpr`, with zoo.exe analysed.\n\nThese steps are for a new PC:\n\n1. **Ghidra.** Download the latest release from\n   https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/releases and unzip it on\n   E: (C: is nearly full). It needs a 64-bit JDK 21 or newer; Temurin 25\n   works. Ghidra 12 no longer has Jython built in, and the scripts need it.\n   To install it, unzip `Extensions\\Ghidra\\ghidra_*_Jython.zip` into\n   `Ghidra\\Extensions\\` (make that folder).\n2. **Project.** File > New Project > Non-Shared, in `E:\\zt1-ghidra`.\n3. **Import zoo.exe.** Use the 2002 Complete Collection build\n   (2,449,432 bytes). The scripts check its SHA-256, because other builds'\n   addresses don't match. Say yes to Analyze, and keep the defaults.\n4. **Scripts.** Ghidra reads scripts from `%USERPROFILE%\\ghidra_scripts`. On\n   this PC that's a link to `<repo>\\re\\ghidra_scripts`, made with\n   `mklink /J %USERPROFILE%\\ghidra_scripts <repo>\\re\\ghidra_scripts`.\n   You can instead add the folder in Script Manager > Manage Script\n   Directories.\n5. **Put the scripts in the menu.** In Window > Script Manager > zt1-engine,\n   tick **In Tool** for each.\n6. **Fill it in.** Run `python re/scan_engine.py`, then in Ghidra run\n   **ZT1_EngineRefs** and **ZT1_Apply**.\n7. **Python tools.** Run `pip install pefile capstone`. Copy\n   `E:\\zt1-shots\\tools\\strings.pkl` into `re\\private\\`, then run\n   `python re/msg.py 1` once, so Map can fill in a message's text.\n","videos":[]},{"id":"earlier-overview","title":"Earlier Lab overview","summary":"The original overview recording and fourteen illustrated workspace examples.","group":"Recorded tutorials","tabs":[],"legacy":true,"notice":"Earlier interface: this video predates the redesigned Mods studio. Use Start with the Lab and the current studio tutorials for today’s controls.","videos":[{"file":"../../renderer/help/media/21e9fd1762507685b9.mp4","title":"Earlier Lab overview","caption":"Original overview recording. Some tab layouts and the old Mods menu have changed.","src":"help/media/21e9fd1762507685b9.mp4","poster":"help/media/0ded7cf7ac32b92d8d.webp"}],"html":"<p>This recording is retained alongside the current guides. It demonstrates the earlier interface. For the current Mods studio, use <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"animal-imports\">Three animal import workflows</a> and <a href=\"#\" data-help-link=\"habitat-tutorial\">Habitat items, maps and theme packs</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-scene\">Scene</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0ded7cf7ac32b92d8d.webp\" alt=\"Scene\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-palette\">Palette</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/3be7d29f752bd82bfe.webp\" alt=\"Palette\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-layout\">Layout</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/894a13e0c5248977ba.webp\" alt=\"Layout\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-steps-binary\">Steps binary</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/0f29cc9d7ae6dfe903.webp\" alt=\"Steps binary\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-functions\">Functions</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/293308cbdd1f22c9b2.webp\" alt=\"Functions\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-code\">Code</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/1512f7b3ea17a6dbc0.webp\" alt=\"Code\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-mods\">Mods</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/163b556e314134d631.webp\" alt=\"Mods\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-gamefiles-reader\">Gamefiles reader</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/ce4e5e294acd4d232e.webp\" alt=\"Gamefiles reader\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-ani-viewer\">Ani viewer</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/8f34898bd381d12314.webp\" alt=\"Ani viewer\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-sync\">Sync</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/cef725a5dd79ca4f31.webp\" alt=\"Sync\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-rightclick\">Rightclick</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/fe2cb683a0b303c3e2.webp\" alt=\"Rightclick\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-calls\">Calls</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/47e92f89a77baf4995.webp\" alt=\"Calls\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-play\">Play</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/16c63902b1c48a01e8.webp\" alt=\"Play\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"help-section-species-check\">Species check</h2>\n<p><img src=\"help/media/1ebba82ce62234ea8e.webp\" alt=\"Species check\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" title=\"Enlarge picture\"></p>","toc":[{"id":"scene","title":"Scene","level":2},{"id":"palette","title":"Palette","level":2},{"id":"layout","title":"Layout","level":2},{"id":"steps-binary","title":"Steps binary","level":2},{"id":"functions","title":"Functions","level":2},{"id":"code","title":"Code","level":2},{"id":"mods","title":"Mods","level":2},{"id":"gamefiles-reader","title":"Gamefiles reader","level":2},{"id":"ani-viewer","title":"Ani viewer","level":2},{"id":"sync","title":"Sync","level":2},{"id":"rightclick","title":"Rightclick","level":2},{"id":"calls","title":"Calls","level":2},{"id":"play","title":"Play","level":2},{"id":"species-check","title":"Species check","level":2}],"text":"# Earlier Lab overview\n\nThis recording is retained alongside the current guides. It demonstrates the earlier interface. For the current Mods studio, use [Three animal import workflows](help:animal-imports) and [Habitat items, maps and theme packs](help:habitat-tutorial).\n\n## Scene\n\n\n\n## Palette\n\n\n\n## Layout\n\n\n\n## Steps binary\n\n\n\n## Functions\n\n\n\n## Code\n\n\n\n## Mods\n\n\n\n## Gamefiles reader\n\n\n\n## Ani viewer\n\n\n\n## Sync\n\n\n\n## Rightclick\n\n\n\n## Calls\n\n\n\n## Play\n\n\n\n## Species check\n\n\n\n"}];
